"""Multi-domain integrity corpus - kept SEPARATE from the core DueCare
human-exploitation RAG_CORPUS so the anti-trafficking prompts and retrieval
never commingle with off-domain content. Each pack is one integrity vertical
(id-prefix <domain>_<topic>); see docs/domain_pack_framework.md. Exposed at
/api/harness-catalog/multidomain and browsable in the Knowledge Atlas
"Integrity verticals" tab. New packs are appended by
reports/_scratch/merge_rag.py with DEST=multidomain."""

MULTIDOMAIN_CORPUS = [
    ('corruption_uncac_overview',
     'UN Convention Against Corruption (UNCAC)  -  Core Framework',
     'United Nations General Assembly Resolution 58/4, 31 October 2003; entered into force 14 December 2005; UNODC, Vienna; source: https://www.unodc.org/documents/brussels/UN_Convention_Against_Corruption.pdf',
     "The UN Convention Against Corruption (UNCAC) is the only legally binding multilateral anti-corruption treaty, adopted in 2003 and ratified by 191 States Parties as of 2024. Its 71 articles across 8 chapters set mandatory and discretionary obligations covering prevention, criminalization, international cooperation, and asset recovery.\n\nMandatory criminalization (Chapter III) requires States Parties to criminalize: (Art. 15) bribery of national public officials; (Art. 16) bribery of foreign public officials and officials of international organizations; (Art. 17) embezzlement, misappropriation, or other diversion of property by a public official; (Art. 21) bribery in the private sector; and (Art. 25) obstruction of justice. Discretionary offences include trading in influence (Art. 18) and illicit enrichment (Art. 20).\n\nPreventive measures (Chapter II) require States to establish anti-corruption bodies (Art. 6), apply merit-based civil service recruitment (Art. 7), require asset and interest declarations from public officials (Art. 8), ensure government transparency and public procurement integrity (Arts. 9-10), and support civil society participation (Art. 13).\n\nAsset recovery (Chapter V) is a 'fundamental principle' of UNCAC (Art. 51): it obligates States to recognize each other's property rights in corruptly obtained assets and cooperate in confiscation and return of assets, including through mutual legal assistance (Art. 55) and international cooperation (Arts. 56-59).\n\nWhistleblower and victim protection (Arts. 32-33) requires States to protect witnesses, experts, and reporting persons from retaliation. The Conference of States Parties (CoSP) and the Implementation Review Mechanism (IRM) monitor compliance through peer review cycles. UNCAC is the legal anchor for most domestic anti-corruption reforms globally and aligns with SDG 16 (peace, justice, and strong institutions)."),
    ('corruption_oecd_anti_bribery_convention',
     'OECD Convention on Combating Bribery of Foreign Public Officials (Anti-Bribery Convention)',
     'OECD Convention on Combating Bribery of Foreign Public Officials in International Business Transactions, signed 17 December 1997; entered into force 15 February 1999; 44 adherents as of 2024; source: https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/topics/policy-sub-issues/fighting-foreign-bribery/Convention%20and%20commentaries%20booklet%202024.pdf',
     "The OECD Anti-Bribery Convention (1997) establishes legally binding obligations for its 44 adherent countries to criminalize bribery of foreign public officials in international business transactions. Unlike UNCAC's broad mandate, this convention focuses exclusively on the supply side of foreign bribery  -  the person or company paying the bribe.\n\nCore obligations: Art. 1 requires each Party to make it a criminal offence when any person intentionally offers, promises, or gives any undue pecuniary or other advantage to a foreign public official in order to obtain or retain business. Corporate criminal or equivalent functional liability must exist alongside individual liability. Sanctions must be 'effective, proportionate and dissuasive.' Art. 8 prohibits off-books accounts, false invoices, and inadequate internal controls specifically designed to conceal bribery payments.\n\nKey red flags addressed: facilitation payments to foreign officials are not a defence under the Convention. Disguising bribes as legitimate business expenses  -  consulting fees, commissions, or marketing costs  -  is prohibited under the accounting provisions.\n\nMonitoring mechanism: The OECD Working Group on Bribery conducts a rigorous four-phase peer review process. Phase 4 examines whether laws are being actively enforced. As of 2024, only approximately one-third of Parties are rated as actively enforcing the Convention, creating an uneven compliance landscape.\n\nCompliance tools: the 2009 OECD Anti-Bribery Recommendation supplements the Convention with guidance on internal controls, ethics, and compliance programmes. Companies operating internationally use the Convention's standards to design anti-bribery compliance including third-party due diligence, gifts and hospitality policies, and books-and-records controls."),
    ('corruption_fcpa_us',
     'US Foreign Corrupt Practices Act (FCPA)  -  Anti-Bribery and Accounting Provisions',
     'Foreign Corrupt Practices Act of 1977, 15 U.S.C. sections 78dd-1 et seq., as amended 1988 and 1998; jointly enforced by DOJ Criminal Division and SEC; source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_Corrupt_Practices_Act | https://carpenterwellington.com/post/foreign-corrupt-practices-act-fcpa-introduction/',
     "The US Foreign Corrupt Practices Act (FCPA, 1977, amended 1988 and 1998) prohibits US persons, US issuers, and foreign companies listed on US exchanges from bribing foreign government officials to obtain or retain business. It has two components with separate enforcement regimes.\n\nAnti-bribery provisions (15 U.S.C. section 78dd-1/2/3): it is unlawful to corruptly offer, pay, promise, or authorize payment of anything of value to a foreign official, foreign political party, or candidate for the purpose of influencing an official act or decision to obtain or retain business. The DOJ brings criminal enforcement; individuals face up to 5 years imprisonment and fines up to $250,000 per count; companies face fines up to $2 million per count or twice the gain or loss under the Alternative Fines Act. A narrow statutory exception exists for 'routine governmental action' (facilitation payments), but many compliance programmes prohibit them entirely.\n\nAccounting provisions (15 U.S.C. sections 78m(b)(2)-(7)): issuers must (a) keep books and records that accurately and fairly reflect transactions and (b) maintain an adequate system of internal accounting controls. These provisions are enforced by the SEC independently of whether a bribery offence occurred; they catch disguised bribe payments booked as consulting fees or agent commissions.\n\nFCPA red flags include: agent recommended by a foreign official; agent with close ties to government decision-makers; unusual payment routes such as numbered accounts or third-country transfers; requests for excessive commissions; inability to verify agent qualifications; pressure to use specific subcontractors; and transactions in jurisdictions with high Corruption Perceptions Index scores. The DOJ and SEC joint Resource Guide (2012, updated 2020) details compliance expectations including third-party due diligence and self-disclosure credit."),
    ('corruption_uk_bribery_act_s7',
     'UK Bribery Act 2010  -  Including Section 7 Corporate Failure to Prevent Bribery',
     'Bribery Act 2010, c.23 (UK); Ministry of Justice Guidance (2011); Crime and Courts Act 2013, Schedule 17 (Deferred Prosecution Agreements); source: https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/23/section/7 | https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bribery-act-2010-guidance',
     "The UK Bribery Act 2010 (in force 1 July 2011) is widely regarded as one of the most stringent anti-bribery statutes globally. It creates four criminal offences: (s.1) active bribery  -  offering, promising, or giving a financial or other advantage; (s.2) passive bribery  -  requesting or accepting such an advantage; (s.6) bribing a foreign public official; and (s.7) the corporate strict-liability offence of failing to prevent bribery.\n\nSection 7  -  Failure to Prevent Bribery: A relevant commercial organisation is guilty of an offence if a person associated with it  -  employee, agent, subsidiary, joint venture partner, or other third party acting on its behalf  -  pays a bribe intending to obtain or retain business or a business advantage for the organisation. The prosecution need not prove knowledge, direction, or intent at the leadership level. The sole statutory defence is proof that the organisation had 'adequate procedures' in place. The Ministry of Justice Guidance identifies six principles: proportionate procedures; top-level commitment; risk assessment; due diligence on third parties; communication and training; and monitoring and review.\n\nJurisdiction: applies to any commercial organisation incorporated or carrying on business in the UK, regardless of where the bribery occurs globally  -  including via offshore subsidiaries.\n\nFacilitation payments: unlike the FCPA, the UK Bribery Act contains no facilitation payment exception; all grease payments are prohibited.\n\nPenalties: unlimited fines for organisations; individuals face up to 10 years imprisonment. The Serious Fraud Office (SFO) uses Deferred Prosecution Agreements (DPAs) under Schedule 17 of the Crime and Courts Act 2013, allowing corporates to avoid conviction in exchange for financial penalties, disgorgement of profits, and compliance reforms. Notable DPAs include Rolls-Royce (approximately 671 million, 2017) and Glencore (approximately 280 million, 2022)."),
    ('corruption_eu_anti_corruption_directive',
     'EU Anti-Corruption Directive  -  COM(2023)234 and Provisional Agreement December 2025',
     'European Commission Proposal COM(2023) 234, 3 May 2023; provisional agreement European Parliament and Council, 2 December 2025; replaces Council Framework Decision 2003/568/JHA; source: https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex%3A52023PC0234 | https://epthinktank.eu/2024/09/24/directive-on-combating-corruption-eu-legislation-in-progress/',
     "The EU Anti-Corruption Directive (provisional agreement December 2025; general transposition deadline approximately 24 months after entry into force) replaces the fragmented 2003 framework and the 1997 EU officials convention with a single comprehensive instrument. It constitutes the most thorough harmonization of EU anti-corruption criminal law to date.\n\nOffences covered (public and private sectors): (1) bribery  -  promising, offering, or giving advantages to influence officials or private actors; (2) misappropriation  -  intentional diversion of entrusted property; (3) trading in influence  -  exchanging influence over decision-making for undue advantages; (4) abuse of functions  -  violating legal duties for private gain; (5) obstruction of justice  -  interfering with corruption proceedings; (6) enrichment from corruption  -  possessing property derived from corruption; (7) incitement, aiding, and abetting all of the above.\n\nMinimum maximum sentences for natural persons: 4-6 years imprisonment for bribery of public officials, misappropriation, abuse of functions, and obstruction; 4 years for private bribery and trading in influence. Additional penalties include disqualification from public office and exclusion from public procurement.\n\nCorporate liability (Art. 16): Member States must establish criminal or equivalent liability for legal persons where offences are committed for the organization's benefit by persons in leading positions or persons under their supervision. Sanctions include dissolution, supervision orders, procurement exclusion, and unlimited fines.\n\nPreventive obligations: Member States must maintain specialized anti-corruption bodies and develop national anti-corruption strategies. Limitation periods of 8-15 years apply. The Directive aligns with UNCAC and GRECO recommendations while harmonizing minimum standards to prevent jurisdiction shopping across the EU."),
    ('corruption_schemes_red_flags',
     'Corruption Schemes and Red Flags  -  Grand vs. Petty Corruption, State Capture, Procurement Fraud, and Bid Rigging',
     'Transparency International Glossary on Grand Corruption and State Capture; OECD Guidelines for Fighting Bid Rigging in Public Procurement (2025 Update); Open Contracting Partnership, Red Flags in Public Procurement (2024); source: https://www.transparency.org/en/our-priorities/grand-corruption | https://www.open-contracting.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/OCP2024-RedFlagProcurement-1.pdf | https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-guidelines-for-fighting-bid-rigging-in-public-procurement-2025-update_cbe05a56-en.html',
     "Corruption manifests in distinct scheme types, each with characteristic red flags that inform due diligence and integrity monitoring.\n\nGrand corruption: misuse of high-level power involving large sums or systemic harm, typically by senior officials, ministers, or executives. Hallmarks include infrastructure contracts awarded to politically connected companies, diversion of development aid, and legislative or regulatory capture. Red flags: single-source or sole-supplier contracting for high-value infrastructure; contract prices anomalously above regional benchmarks; contracts awarded to companies with government ownership ties; repeated amendment of contracts after award to increase value.\n\nPetty corruption: low-level officials demand unofficial payments to perform routine duties (licensing, permits, customs clearance, healthcare access). Disproportionately burdens lower-income individuals. Red flag: unofficial fees demanded outside any published schedule; services delayed without explanation until a payment is made.\n\nState capture: private actors systematically shape laws, policies, and regulations to serve private interests, effectively capturing the legislative and regulatory environment. Red flag: repeated legislative or regulatory changes that favour a specific company or sector with no plausible public-interest rationale; revolving-door patterns between regulator and regulated industry.\n\nProcurement fraud and bid rigging (OECD, 2025): collusion among bidders to predetermine contract outcomes through cover bidding, bid suppression, bid rotation, or market allocation. Specific red flags: only one responsive bid received; winning bidder subcontracts to losing bidders; bids submitted by companies incapable of delivering; identical mathematical errors across multiple bids; prices clustered far above market estimates; contract specifications only one vendor can meet; consistent rotating win patterns.\n\nConflict of interest: a decision-maker's private interests interfere with their public duties. Red flag: procurement official has undisclosed ownership or family ties to a bidding company. Embezzlement red flag: payments to ghost employees, fictitious vendors, or duplicate invoices."),
    ('corruption_beneficial_ownership_transparency',
     'Beneficial Ownership Transparency  -  Registers, FATF Standards, and Shell Company Red Flags',
     'FATF Recommendation 24 (revised March 2022); FATF Guidance on Beneficial Ownership of Legal Persons (March 2023); EU AMLD4 (Directive 2015/849) and AMLD5 (Directive 2018/843); source: https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/Guidance-Beneficial-Ownership-Legal-Persons.html | https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/topics/beneficial-ownership.html',
     'Beneficial ownership (BO) transparency refers to publicly identifying the natural person(s) who ultimately own or control a legal entity  -  the company, trust, or foundation  -  regardless of the nominal or legal owner on any corporate register. Concealment of beneficial ownership is a primary enabler of grand corruption, money laundering, and sanctions evasion.\n\nFATF Recommendation 24 (revised 2022) requires countries to ensure that competent authorities have timely access to adequate, accurate, and up-to-date BO information on all companies incorporated domestically. The revised standard requires a multi-pronged approach combining: centralised BO registers accessible to authorities; information held by companies themselves; and information filed with regulated entities such as banks, lawyers, and notaries. Countries with centralised and publicly accessible BO registers are rated more effective under FATF mutual evaluations.\n\nRed flags indicating shell company abuse for corrupt purposes include: complex cross-border ownership chains with no apparent commercial rationale; nominee directors or shareholders acting as straw men; legal persons in secrecy jurisdictions as intermediate holders; bearer shares or warrants to bearer; frequent unexplained transfers of ownership; companies with no operational presence, employees, or real assets; and hundreds of companies registered at the same agent address.\n\nAt the EU level, the 4th and 5th Anti-Money Laundering Directives (AMLD4/5) required Member States to establish BO registers accessible to persons with a legitimate interest. The UK established the People with Significant Control (PSC) register in 2016. Under UNCAC Art. 52, States are obligated to require financial institutions to verify and record BO information for politically exposed persons (PEPs) and apply enhanced due diligence to high-risk relationships.'),
    ('corruption_asset_declarations_integrity_due_diligence',
     'Asset and Interest Declarations, Conflict-of-Interest Management, and Integrity Due Diligence',
     'UNCAC Arts. 7-8, 52; UNODC and World Bank StAR Initiative, Income and Asset Declarations: Tools and Trade-offs (2012); Transparency International Topic Guide on Interest and Asset Disclosure; source: https://www.unodc.org/documents/corruption/Publications/StAR/StAR_Publication_-_Income_and_Asset_Declarations.pdf | https://knowledgehub.transparency.org/guide/topic-guide-on-interest-and-asset-disclosure/5867',
     'Asset and interest declarations (AIDs)  -  also called financial disclosure systems  -  require public officials to periodically report their income, assets, liabilities, and outside interests to an oversight body. When effectively implemented and verified, AIDs serve three functions: deterrence (officials know their wealth is on record), detection (unexplained wealth gains trigger investigation), and conflict-of-interest management (recusal obligations can be enforced). Over 160 countries have enacted financial disclosure systems according to World Bank research, though quality, verification rigor, and public accessibility vary enormously.\n\nUNCAC legal basis: Art. 7.4 requires each State Party to consider establishing systems requiring public officials to declare outside activities, employment, investments, and assets where a conflict of interest may arise. Art. 8 requires codes of conduct for public officials. Art. 52 extends disclosure requirements to politically exposed persons (PEPs) in financial systems, requiring enhanced due diligence and ongoing monitoring.\n\nConflict-of-interest red flags: an official who votes on a contract benefiting a company they or a family member own; a regulator who accepts gifts from regulated entities; a procurement officer who steers specifications to a former employer. Best-practice conflict-of-interest regimes combine: mandatory pre-employment and annual AIDs with independent verification; cooling-off restrictions on post-government employment (revolving-door rules); real-time declaration requirements when a conflict arises during decision-making; and oversight bodies with investigative powers and sanction authority.\n\nIntegrity due diligence (IDD): the private-sector equivalent, conducted before engaging with public officials, high-risk counterparties, or entities in high-Corruption-Perceptions-Index-risk jurisdictions. IDD scope includes: politically exposed person screening; adverse media searches; verification of beneficial ownership; review of procurement relationships; and assessment of unexplained wealth red flags. Failure to conduct adequate IDD is itself a red flag in FCPA and UK Bribery Act compliance reviews.'),
    ('corruption_whistleblower_protections',
     'Whistleblower Protection  -  EU Directive 2019/1937, US Dodd-Frank, SOX, and False Claims Act',
     'Directive (EU) 2019/1937 of the European Parliament and of the Council, 23 October 2019, OJ L 305/17; US Sarbanes-Oxley Act 2002 (18 U.S.C. section 1514A); Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act 2010 (15 U.S.C. section 78u-6); False Claims Act 31 U.S.C. sections 3729-3733; sources: https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/1937/oj/eng | https://www.whistleblowers.org/what-is-the-dodd-frank-act/',
     "Whistleblower protection regimes are a primary mechanism for detecting and prosecuting corruption that would otherwise remain hidden. Three major frameworks operate across jurisdictions.\n\nEU Directive 2019/1937 (transposition deadline December 2021 for large entities): creates minimum standards for persons reporting breaches of EU law. Material scope covers public procurement, financial services, anti-money laundering, competition, corporate tax, and environmental law. Personal scope extends beyond employees to volunteers, trainees, contractors, subcontractors, suppliers, shareholders, job applicants, and former workers. Key protections: confidential internal and external reporting channels must be established; retaliation including dismissal, demotion, harassment, and blacklisting is prohibited; the burden of proof reverses once the worker shows they reported a breach and suffered a detriment  -  the employer must prove the adverse treatment was unrelated to the report. Remedies include reinstatement, compensation, and interim injunctive relief.\n\nUS Dodd-Frank Act (2010, SEC and CFTC programs): whistleblowers who provide original information leading to enforcement actions with sanctions exceeding $1 million are entitled to 10-30% of the monetary sanction. Reports may be filed anonymously through counsel. Anti-retaliation provisions allow direct civil action if an employer retaliates. The SEC's Office of the Whistleblower has paid over $2 billion in awards since inception. FCPA violations are expressly within scope.\n\nUS False Claims Act (qui tam provisions, 31 U.S.C. sections 3729-3733): private citizens as relators can file suit on behalf of the government against contractors who defraud federal programs; the relator receives 15-30% of recovery if the government intervenes. The primary tool for domestic procurement fraud against the US government.\n\nUS Sarbanes-Oxley (2002, section 806): protects employees of publicly traded companies who report securities fraud; criminal penalties of up to 10 years for employers who retaliate."),
    ('corruption_trafficking_nexus',
     'The Corruption-Trafficking Nexus  -  How Official Corruption Enables Human Trafficking and Recruitment Fraud',
     "UNODC, 'The Role of Corruption in Trafficking in Persons,' Issue Paper (2011); UNODC, 'Understanding the Hidden Links between Human Trafficking and Corruption,' analysis of 120+ cases across approximately 80 countries, January 2026; UN News report, January 2026; sources: https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/01/1166797 | https://www.unodc.org/documents/human-trafficking/2011/Issue_Paper_-_The_Role_of_Corruption_in_Trafficking_in_Persons.pdf",
     'Official corruption is not incidental to human trafficking  -  it is structural. UNODC research analyzing over 120 cases across approximately 80 countries (2026) establishes that corruption permeates and facilitates every stage of the trafficking cycle. Without corruption, trafficking networks would struggle to recruit, move, or exploit victims at scale.\n\nRecruitment and documentation stage: corrupt staff in recruitment agencies or immigration offices issue false work permits; consular workers accept bribes to deliver fraudulent visas or passports; labour inspectors are paid to overlook agencies placing workers in exploitative conditions. Red flags: recruitment agencies operating with anomalously low inspection rates; consular visa approval rates inconsistent with legitimate migration patterns; groups with identical travel itineraries whose documents are held by a handler.\n\nTransport and border crossing stage: border guards and immigration officials accept bribes or are themselves extorted to allow victims to cross undetected. Forged travel documents authenticated by paid officials enable legal-appearing movement across corridors. Red flags: passports with implausible visa stamp sequences; same-nationality groups on identical itineraries; handlers in possession of all group travel documents.\n\nExploitation and control stage: police officers, labour inspectors, and local officials accept payments to shield exploitation sites  -  farms, fishing vessels, factories, domestic premises, commercial sex venues  -  from investigation. Victims are threatened that reporting is futile because officials are paid. Red flags: businesses operating without routine labour inspections over extended periods in high-risk sectors; repeated complaints against a venue that consistently result in no enforcement action.\n\nInvestigative and judicial stage: corruption can obstruct prosecutions  -  evidence disappears, witnesses are intimidated with official assistance, charges are reduced or dropped. UNODC recommends strengthened penalties when public officials are trafficking perpetrators; specialized joint anti-corruption and anti-trafficking investigative units; and victim-centred reporting channels that bypass potentially corrupt local officers, such as national-level reporting lines or civil-society intermediaries. UNCAC Art. 25 (obstruction of justice) and Palermo Protocol Art. 11 (border controls and document security) together frame the joint legal duty to prosecute both the corruption and the underlying trafficking offences.'),
    ('fincrime_fatf_40_recommendations_rba',
     'FATF 40 Recommendations and the Risk-Based Approach (RBA)',
     'Financial Action Task Force (FATF), The FATF Recommendations, adopted 1990, substantially revised 2012, updated 2023; source: https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/Fatf-recommendations.html',
     'The FATF 40 Recommendations are the internationally agreed standard for combating money laundering (ML), terrorist financing (TF), and proliferation financing. They are organized into five thematic pillars: (1) Risk Assessment and Policy Foundation (R1-8); (2) Customer Due Diligence (R9-23); (3) Transparency of Beneficial Ownership (R24-25); (4) Regulatory Oversight and Enforcement (R26-35); and (5) International Cooperation (R36-40). The cornerstone is the Risk-Based Approach (RBA) under Recommendation 1: countries and financial institutions must identify, assess, and understand their ML/TF risks and apply controls proportionate to those risks - enhanced measures for higher-risk customers, jurisdictions, or products; simplified measures only where risk is demonstrably low. Recommendation 10 mandates Customer Due Diligence (CDD): verify customer identity, identify beneficial owners, understand the purpose of the business relationship, and conduct ongoing monitoring. Recommendation 16 (the Travel Rule) requires wire-transfer originators and beneficiary institutions to transmit and retain full originator and beneficiary information for transfers at or above USD/EUR 1,000. Recommendation 20 requires immediate filing of Suspicious Transaction Reports (STRs) with the national Financial Intelligence Unit (FIU) when a transaction is suspected to involve ML or TF proceeds - without tipping off the customer. Recommendations 24-25 require central registries of beneficial ownership of legal persons and legal arrangements, preventing shell-company concealment. Red flags triggering enhanced due diligence include: high-risk jurisdictions on FATF grey/black lists, complex or opaque ownership structures, PEP involvement, and transactions inconsistent with stated business purpose. Non-compliant jurisdictions are publicly grey-listed or black-listed, facing countermeasures including enhanced scrutiny from correspondent banks and international financial institutions.'),
    ('fincrime_palermo_convention_ml_articles',
     'UN Convention Against Transnational Organized Crime (Palermo Convention)  -  Money Laundering Articles 6, 7, 12-14',
     'United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime, UNGA Res. 55/25, 15 November 2000, in force 29 September 2003, 193 parties as of 2025; source: https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/organized-crime/intro/UNTOC.html',
     "The Palermo Convention is the primary international legal instrument against transnational organized crime and provides the foundational treaty framework for money laundering criminalization. Article 6 ('Criminalization of the laundering of proceeds of crime') obligates States Parties to criminalize: (a) converting, transferring, concealing, or disguising property knowing it is proceeds of crime; (b) acquiring, possessing, or using such property; and (c) participating in, associating with, or conspiring to commit these acts. Article 6 requires each State to apply these offences to the widest range of predicate offences possible, including all serious crimes carrying a maximum deprivation of liberty of at least four years. Article 7 ('Measures to combat money laundering') requires States to establish a comprehensive regulatory and supervisory regime for banks, non-bank financial institutions, and other vulnerable entities, emphasizing customer identification, record-keeping, and suspicious transaction reporting. States must establish Financial Intelligence Units (FIUs) to receive, analyze, and disseminate ML-related information. Articles 12-14 address confiscation of proceeds of crime, requiring domestic legal frameworks permitting seizure and forfeiture of criminal proceeds and instrumentalities, and mandating international cooperation for cross-border asset recovery. Red flags and legal duties: financial institutions must report transactions linked to organized crime, apply CDD to all customers, and cooperate with competent authorities. Remedies include domestic criminalization of all ML conduct, mandatory confiscation regimes, international mutual legal assistance (MLAT) treaties, and the return of recovered assets to originating states. The Convention is supplemented by three Protocols: on trafficking in persons, smuggling of migrants, and illicit firearms trafficking, each of which generates predicate offences for ML purposes."),
    ('fincrime_eu_6amld_directive',
     'EU Sixth Anti-Money Laundering Directive (6AMLD)  -  Directive (EU) 2018/1673',
     'Directive (EU) 2018/1673 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 23 October 2018 on combating money laundering by criminal law (6AMLD); EUR-Lex ref. 32018L1673; transposition deadline 3 December 2020, application from 3 June 2021; source: https://www.ondato.com/blog/6th-anti-money-laundering-directive/',
     "The Sixth Anti-Money Laundering Directive (6AMLD) harmonizes criminal law definitions of money laundering across EU member states. It defines 22 predicate offences whose proceeds can constitute money laundering: terrorism, drug trafficking, human trafficking, arms trafficking, kidnapping, extortion, corruption, fraud, forgery, tax crimes, organized crime, environmental crimes, cybercrime, sexual exploitation, trafficking of cultural goods, trafficking of nuclear materials, insider trading, market manipulation, copyright infringement, currency counterfeiting, illicit trafficking of hormonal substances and precursors, and bribery. Key provisions: (1) Expanded criminalization: aiding, abetting, inciting, and attempting money laundering are now fully criminal, eliminating safe harbors for facilitators. (2) Dual criminality removal: prosecution may proceed even if the predicate offence is not illegal in the jurisdiction where it was committed. (3) Corporate criminal liability: legal entities - companies, partnerships, organizations - can face criminal charges where leadership failed to prevent ML or lacked adequate AML controls. (4) Minimum penalties: at least four years' imprisonment for individuals (raised from one year under previous directives); corporate sanctions include fines, exclusion from public contracts, temporary or permanent business closure. (5) Cross-border cooperation: member states must recognize predicate offences committed in other EU jurisdictions and coordinate investigations and prosecutions. Red flags: any transaction generating proceeds from the 22 predicate offences; corporate vehicles used by entities in jurisdictions not implementing 6AMLD standards; facilitation by professionals (lawyers, accountants) without adequate CDD. Remedies include prosecution of both direct perpetrators and corporate enablers, asset forfeiture, and enhanced penalties for repeat or aggravated offences."),
    ('fincrime_bsa_amla2020_fincen',
     'US Bank Secrecy Act (BSA), Anti-Money Laundering Act 2020 (AMLA), and FinCEN Beneficial Ownership Registry',
     'Bank Secrecy Act, 31 U.S.C.  5311-5336, enacted 1970; Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020, Div. F of the National Defense Authorization Act for FY2021, Pub. L. 116-283, signed January 2021; Corporate Transparency Act (part of AMLA); FinCEN CDD Final Rule 2016; source: https://www.fincen.gov/resources/statutes-and-regulations/bank-secrecy-act and https://www.avyse.co.uk/insights/us-anti-money-laundering-act-2020',
     "The Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) is the foundational US AML law, requiring financial institutions to maintain records and file reports that assist law enforcement in detecting ML and other financial crimes. Key BSA obligations: Currency Transaction Reports (CTRs) for cash transactions exceeding $10,000; Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) for transactions of $5,000 or more where the institution knows, suspects, or has reason to suspect ML; record-keeping of wire transfers exceeding $3,000; and customer identification programs (CIPs). The Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020 (AMLA) - the most significant BSA reform since the USA PATRIOT Act 2001 - introduced: (1) Eight national AML/CFT priorities published by FinCEN in 2021, including corruption, cybercrime, human trafficking, drug trafficking, and proliferation financing. (2) Corporate Transparency Act (CTA): entities with up to 20 employees must report beneficial ownership information (name, date of birth, address, government ID number) to FinCEN's non-public registry; effective 1 January 2024 for new entities. Non-compliance penalties: up to 2 years imprisonment, $10,000 criminal fines, $500/day civil penalties. (3) Enhanced subpoena authority: Treasury and DOJ may subpoena foreign FIs with US correspondent accounts for ML-related records, with a pilot for international information-sharing. (4) SAR sharing pilot: FIs may share SARs with foreign affiliates (excluding Russia, China, sanctioned jurisdictions). (5) Whistleblower awards: up to 30% of recovered funds exceeding $1 million for original information on BSA violations, with anti-retaliation protections. (6) Enhanced penalties: transactions exceeding $1 million involving PEPs or ML entities carry 10 years imprisonment and $1 million fines; egregious violators face 10-year board exclusion. Red flags: beneficial owner concealment, structuring deposits below $10,000, PEP-linked transactions, and high-volume cash-intensive businesses."),
    ('fincrime_wolfsberg_principles',
     'Wolfsberg Group Principles  -  AML Standards for Correspondent Banking and Financial Crime Compliance',
     'Wolfsberg Group, Financial Crime Principles for Correspondent Banking (2022); Wolfsberg Financial Crime Compliance Questionnaire (FCCQ) v1.2 (2023); Correspondent Banking Due Diligence Questionnaire (CBDDQ) v1.4 (2023); source: https://wolfsberg-group.org/resources/correspondent-banking',
     'The Wolfsberg Group is a non-governmental association of thirteen major global private banks established in 2000 to develop and promote AML and financial crime compliance standards that complement FATF rules. The Wolfsberg Principles are not legally binding but are widely adopted as global best practice and are referenced in FATF mutual evaluations. Core AML principle areas: (1) Know Your Customer (KYC): verify identities and assess risk profiles before and throughout a customer relationship. (2) Customer Due Diligence (CDD): ongoing monitoring of customer activity against expected transaction profiles. (3) Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD): heightened procedures for high-risk clients, especially Politically Exposed Persons (PEPs) and customers in high-risk jurisdictions. (4) Correspondent Banking: the 2022 Financial Crime Principles for Correspondent Banking require banks to apply risk-based due diligence to respondent institutions, assessing ownership structure, beneficial ownership, sanctions screening, and AML/CFT program quality. The Correspondent Banking Due Diligence Questionnaire (CBDDQ v1.4, 2023) is the global standard for interbank due diligence; it must be reviewed at least annually. The Financial Crime Compliance Questionnaire (FCCQ v1.2, 2023) provides a standardized compliance assessment framework for correspondent relationships. (5) Payment Transparency (2024 guidance): banks must identify the debtor and creditor behind each payment to support transaction monitoring and STR filing. Key risk indicators in correspondent banking: geographic risk (FATF-listed jurisdictions), state-owned entities, PEP-linked ownership or management, inadequate respondent AML programs, and off-market pricing in interbank transactions. Red flags: rapid correspondent account volume growth, transactions routed through multiple intermediary banks without clear business purpose, and respondents with deficient KYC programs. Remedy: risk-based enhanced monitoring, relationship de-risking, or termination of correspondent relationship.'),
    ('fincrime_three_stage_model_placement_layering_integration',
     'The Three-Stage Money Laundering Model: Placement, Layering, and Integration',
     'FATF typologies reports (1990-present); UNODC Global Programme against Money Laundering; UNODC estimates $800 billion-$2 trillion laundered annually (2%-5% of global GDP); source: https://www.lexisnexis.com/en-gb/glossary/money-laundering-stages and https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/press/releases/2011/October/unodc-estimates-that-criminals-may-have-laundered-usdollar-1.6-trillion-in-2009.html',
     "Money laundering is the process by which proceeds of crime are given the appearance of legitimate origin. UNODC estimates that between $800 billion and $2 trillion (2%-5% of global GDP) is laundered annually; a 2011 UNODC analysis estimated $1.6 trillion (2.7% of GDP) in a single year. The three-stage model - placement, layering, integration - is the canonical framework for detection and prosecution. Stage 1  -  Placement: introducing illicit cash into the financial system. Methods include smurfing (structuring deposits below reporting thresholds across multiple accounts or institutions), cash-intensive business commingling, casino chip purchases, real estate purchases in cash, and use of money service businesses. Red flags: large round-number cash deposits just below $10,000; multiple same-day branch deposits; customers unfamiliar with the stated source of funds. Stage 2  -  Layering: obscuring the origin through complex transactions to make tracing difficult. Methods include wire transfers between multiple jurisdictions and shell companies, conversion to financial instruments (cashier's checks, money orders), purchase and resale of luxury assets, use of nominee accounts, and trade-based money laundering techniques. Red flags: wire transfers to or from high-risk jurisdictions without business rationale; rapid movement through multiple accounts; transactions inconsistent with the customer's declared business or income. Stage 3  -  Integration: re-introducing funds as apparently legitimate wealth. Methods include real estate investment, purchase of businesses, luxury goods, inflated service invoices, and professional service fees. Red flags: property purchases significantly above market value; inflated invoices between related parties; unexplained business growth without corresponding economic activity. Legal duties at each stage: CDD/KYC at account opening, continuous transaction monitoring, SAR filing upon suspicion, and record retention for at least five years. Remedies include freezing and forfeiture of assets at any stage, prosecution under both predicate offence and ML statutes, and international MLAT cooperation."),
    ('fincrime_trade_based_ml_tbml',
     'Trade-Based Money Laundering (TBML)  -  Typologies and Red-Flag Indicators',
     'FATF, Trade-Based Money Laundering: Risk Indicators, 2021; FATF/Egmont Group, Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments, 2020; FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering Typologies, 2006; source: https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/Trade-based-money-laundering-indicators.html',
     "Trade-Based Money Laundering (TBML) is the use of international trade transactions to transfer value and obscure the origin of criminal proceeds. FATF estimates TBML constitutes approximately 80-85% of global ML and terrorist financing by volume, exploiting the complexity of international supply chains, high trade documentation volumes, and multiple cross-border parties. Core TBML typologies: (1) Over-invoicing: seller overstates value of goods or services; buyer transfers excess value to seller, effectively moving criminal funds. (2) Under-invoicing: seller understates value; buyer under-pays, transferring value to the buyer's jurisdiction. (3) Multiple invoicing: same shipment invoiced more than once to enable multiple payments for a single delivery. (4) Falsely described goods or services: goods mis-described as high- or low-value to manipulate the transfer amount. (5) Short-shipping or phantom shipments: payment made for goods never shipped or only partially shipped. (6) Misuse of free trade zones (FTZs): reduced documentation requirements and oversight in FTZs exploited to conceal TBML. (7) Carousel fraud: goods imported and re-exported through multiple entities to generate fraudulent VAT refund claims. Red-flag indicators for banks and trade finance institutions: significant price discrepancy versus published market benchmarks; buyer and seller in same or related ownership; multiple amendments to letters of credit; vague descriptions of goods; atypical trade routes or mismatched transport documentation; transactions involving counterparts in FATF high-risk jurisdictions; mismatch between country of origin and routing. Legal duties: financial institutions extending trade finance must apply CDD to all parties, verify trade documentation consistency, and file STRs when indicators appear. Remedies: cross-agency sharing of trade and financial intelligence; enhanced scrutiny of FTZ transactions; FATF mutual evaluations assessing TBML controls nationally."),
    ('fincrime_structuring_shell_companies_beneficial_ownership',
     'Structuring / Smurfing, Shell Companies, and Beneficial-Ownership Concealment',
     'US 31 U.S.C.  5324 (anti-structuring statute); FinCEN CDD Final Rule 2016, 31 C.F.R.  1010.230; FATF Recommendations 24-25; US Corporate Transparency Act 2021 (effective 1 January 2024); source: https://www.fincen.gov/resources/statutes-and-regulations/cdd-final-rule',
     "Structuring (US: 31 U.S.C.  5324) is the deliberate breaking up of financial transactions to avoid currency reporting thresholds - a federal crime regardless of whether the underlying funds are licit or illicit. Smurfing is a network variant in which multiple 'money mules' coordinate deposits across accounts and institutions. Red flags: repeated cash deposits just below $10,000 at multiple branches; same-day deposits at different locations; customers who appear nervous about bank reporting rules; round-number deposits at high frequency. Shell companies are legal entities with no genuine business operations, used to hold assets or conduct transactions anonymously. Layered shell-company structures - often spanning multiple jurisdictions - separate beneficial owners from visible asset ownership. Front companies commingle illicit and legitimate revenues to conceal the criminal origin of funds. Beneficial-ownership concealment is the core vulnerability: FATF Recommendations 24-25 require member countries to maintain central registries disclosing the natural persons who ultimately own or control legal entities with 25% or more equity, or a controlling management interest. FinCEN's 2016 CDD Final Rule requires US financial institutions to identify and verify beneficial owners for all new legal-entity customers. The US Corporate Transparency Act (CTA, effective 1 January 2024) extends this to a FinCEN-maintained national registry accessible to law enforcement. Red flags for shell or front-company abuse: same registered address for multiple unrelated entities; directors who are nominees with no operating role; unusual inter-company transfer volume; no visible employees or business activity; incorporation in secrecy jurisdictions (British Virgin Islands, Cayman Islands, Delaware without disclosure). Remedies: beneficial-ownership registries, SAR filing, cross-border asset freezing and forfeiture under FATF Recommendation 38, and international information sharing under R40."),
    ('fincrime_hawala_ivts_sanctions_evasion',
     'Hawala / Informal Value Transfer Systems (IVTS) and Sanctions Evasion',
     'FATF, The Role of Hawala and Other Similar Service Providers in ML/TF, 2013; FATF Recommendation 14 (money/value transfer services); OFAC Executive Order 14114 (December 2023); US Treasury OFAC guidance on secondary sanctions; source: https://www.fatf-gafi.org/content/dam/fatf/documents/reports/Role-of-hawala-and-similar-in-ml-tf.pdf and https://ofac.treasury.gov/faqs/topic/1501',
     "Hawala is an informal value transfer system (IVTS) in which brokers (hawaladars) settle obligations through a network of trusted counterparts rather than physical cash movement. The network operates largely outside formal financial channels, making it attractive for laundering proceeds and evading sanctions. FATF Recommendation 14 requires all countries to register and regulate money/value transfer services, including hawala operators, imposing CDD, record-keeping, and STR obligations equivalent to those of licensed money service businesses. FATF's 2013 typologies report identified structuring (breaking large payments into smaller hawala transactions), coded language, courier intermediaries, and lack of KYC documentation as common hawala ML patterns. Red flags for hawala abuse: round-number or high-velocity remittances by the same customer; customers aware of law enforcement surveillance; use of coded language or courier settlement; inability to identify the ultimate beneficiary; no documented business rationale for international transfers. Sanctions evasion is a closely related risk. OFAC's Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) List and sectoral sanctions prohibit US persons and entities from transacting with designated individuals, entities, and jurisdictions. Post-2022 Russia sanctions under EO 14024 and EO 14114 (signed December 2023) impose secondary sanctions on foreign financial institutions that conduct or facilitate significant transactions involving Russia's military-industrial base. OFAC has identified joining Russia's SPFS interbank payment network as a sanctions-evasion red flag. Additional red flags: routing payments through non-sanctioned third-country intermediaries; atypical commodity exports to sanctioned jurisdictions via indirect routes; correspondent accounts held at banks in jurisdictions with weak AML enforcement. Legal duties: real-time screening against OFAC's SDN and consolidated lists; enhanced due diligence for correspondent accounts in high-risk jurisdictions; SAR filing for suspected evasion. Remedies: civil and criminal penalties under the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA); correspondent account termination; OFAC-led global coordination."),
    ('fincrime_virtual_assets_travel_rule_crypto',
     'Virtual Asset Money Laundering and the FATF Travel Rule (Recommendation 15)',
     'FATF, Virtual Assets Red Flag Indicators of ML and TF, 2020; FATF, Targeted Update on Implementation of FATF Standards on VAs and VASPs, June 2023; FATF, Targeted Update on VAs and VASPs, June 2024; FATF Recommendation 15 and Interpretive Note; source: https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/Virtual-assets-red-flag-indicators.html and https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/targeted-update-virtual-assets-vasps-2023.html',
     "Virtual assets (VA) - cryptocurrencies, stablecoins, and tokens - present novel ML/TF typologies and detection challenges. FATF Recommendation 15 and its Interpretive Note require countries to regulate Virtual Asset Service Providers (VASPs: exchanges, custodial wallets, and applicable DeFi platforms) as financial institutions subject to the full FATF framework: registration or licensing, CDD/KYC, transaction monitoring, SAR filing, and the Travel Rule. The Travel Rule requires VASPs to transmit and retain originator and beneficiary information (name, account or wallet address, transaction amount) alongside transfers at or above USD/EUR 1,000. FATF's June 2024 targeted update found that approximately 75% of jurisdictions remain only partially or non-compliant with Travel Rule requirements; over 30% have passed no implementing legislation. Illicit on-chain activity was estimated at approximately $51 billion in 2024, dominated by fraud, scams, and state-sponsored theft (notably the $1.5 billion Bybit hack attributed to North Korea's Lazarus Group). Key red flags for virtual asset ML: use of cryptocurrency mixers, tumblers, or privacy coins (e.g., Monero) to break the transaction trail; rapid conversion between asset types; use of unhosted or self-custody wallets without clear beneficial-owner identification; peer-to-peer (P2P) transfers bypassing regulated VASPs; structuring VA transactions below Travel Rule thresholds; transfers to or from OFAC-sanctioned addresses or FATF-listed jurisdictions; NFT wash trading to create false trading histories; layering through decentralized finance (DeFi) protocols. Legal duties for VASPs: customer identification at onboarding, Travel Rule compliance for qualifying transfers, suspicious transaction monitoring, and SAR filing. Remedies: VASP licensing revocation, on-chain asset freezing via court order and exchange cooperation, international MLAT cooperation, and FATF mutual evaluations of VA regulatory frameworks."),
    ('fincrime_pep_sar_cdd_iff_sdg164',
     'PEPs, SARs/STRs, Illicit Financial Flows (IFFs), Asset Recovery  -  SDG 16.4 and the StAR Initiative',
     'FATF Guidance on Politically Exposed Persons (Recommendations 12 and 22), June 2013; GFI, Illicit Financial Flows from Developing Countries; UNCTAD SDG Indicator 16.4.1; World Bank/UNODC Stolen Asset Recovery (StAR) Initiative, launched 2007; source: https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/Peps-r12-r22.html and https://gfintegrity.org/issue/illicit-financial-flows/ and https://star.worldbank.org',
     "Politically Exposed Persons (PEPs) under FATF Recommendation 12 are individuals entrusted with prominent public functions - heads of state, senior politicians, senior government, military, or judicial officials, senior executives of state-owned enterprises, and senior officials of international organizations - plus their immediate family members and known close associates. PEPs present elevated ML/TF risk because their positions may be abused for corruption or bribery. Required enhanced due diligence for PEPs: senior management approval before establishing or continuing a business relationship; reasonable measures to establish source of wealth and source of funds; and enhanced ongoing monitoring of all transactions. Red flags: corporate vehicles concealing PEP ownership; declared income inconsistent with transaction volumes; PEP-linked transactions in high-risk sectors; asset declarations inconsistent with lifestyle. Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) or Suspicious Transaction Reports (STRs) under FATF Recommendation 20 must be filed with the national FIU whenever an institution suspects a transaction involves ML or TF proceeds; tipping off the customer is a criminal offence. Illicit Financial Flows (IFFs): Global Financial Integrity (GFI) estimates that trade-related IFFs represent approximately 20% of developing-country trade with advanced economies, exceeding combined official development assistance and foreign direct investment flowing into many low-income nations. UN SDG 16.4 targets significantly reducing illicit financial and arms flows, strengthening recovery and return of stolen assets, and combating organized crime by 2030. UNCTAD and UNODC co-manage SDG Indicator 16.4.1. The World Bank/UNODC Stolen Asset Recovery (StAR) Initiative, launched 2007, supports recovery of corruption-related stolen assets under UNCAC Chapter V; estimates indicate $20-40 billion per year is stolen by public officials. Non-Conviction Based (NCB) asset forfeiture allows confiscation without a criminal conviction where prosecution is impossible, and is a key StAR-promoted remedy. StAR's Asset Recovery Watch database tracks over 52 countries of origin and 34 jurisdictions from which assets have been recovered since 1980."),
    ('fincrime_fatf_human_trafficking_financial_flows',
     'FATF Typology: Financial Flows from Human Trafficking  -  Intersection of AML and Anti-Trafficking Frameworks',
     'FATF/APG, Financial Flows from Human Trafficking, July 2018; FATF, Money Laundering Risks Arising from Trafficking of Human Beings and Smuggling of Migrants, 2011; source: https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/Human-trafficking.html',
     "Human trafficking generates significant illicit financial flows that are laundered through a mix of general ML techniques and trafficking-specific methods. FATF's 2018 typology report covers money laundering of proceeds from forced labor, sexual exploitation, and organ trafficking. Sectors commonly used to launder trafficking proceeds: cash-intensive businesses (restaurants, nail salons, car washes, massage parlors) used for revenue commingling; real estate purchased with cash proceeds; hawala and money remittance services for moving proceeds to origin countries; online payment platforms and escort-service front companies; and structured cash withdrawals feeding street-level cash circulation. Key financial red flags specific to human trafficking: multiple unrelated individuals sharing the same bank account or residential address; large numbers of low-value cash deposits to accounts controlled by a single person or network; frequent transfers to foreign nationals in source countries inconsistent with the account holder's declared income; purchase of prepaid debit cards, bus tickets, or accommodation for third parties with no apparent family connection; business bank accounts receiving regular deposits primarily from cash-intensive services alongside structured withdrawals; and individuals unfamiliar with transactions conducted in their name. General red flags shared with other ML typologies include inconsistent KYC information, use of nominees, use of shell companies, and unusual geographic transaction patterns linking known trafficking corridors. Legal duties: financial institutions must apply CDD including source-of-funds inquiry, monitor for trafficking-specific red flags, and file SARs; coordination with law enforcement on patterns linked to specific geographic corridors or industries is recommended. FATF recommends joint AML and anti-trafficking task forces to share intelligence among FIUs, law enforcement, and labor inspectors, and to treat trafficking proceeds as predicate offences under domestic ML statutes. Remedies include financial investigation of trafficking networks, freezing and forfeiture of trafficking proceeds, and international asset-recovery cooperation."),
    ('counterfeit_trips',
     'WTO TRIPS Agreement: Minimum Standards for IP Enforcement (Part III)',
     'WTO TRIPS Agreement, Enforcement of intellectual property rights, Part III (wto.org/english/tratop_e/trips_e/intel2b_e.htm, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'TRIPS Part III obliges WTO members to provide enforcement procedures permitting effective action against infringement without becoming barriers to legitimate trade. Article 41 sets general obligations of fair, reasoned, written decisions. Civil remedies include injunctions (Art 44), adequate damages and costs (Art 45), and disposal or destruction of infringing goods outside commerce (Art 46), plus supply-chain information orders (Art 47). Article 50 requires prompt provisional measures, including ex parte where delay risks irreparable harm. Articles 51-60 require border measures letting customs detain suspect counterfeit-trademark and pirated-copyright goods on a rights-holder application. Article 61 mandates criminal procedures for wilful trademark counterfeiting or copyright piracy on a commercial scale. Recognition: these are the floor; weaker national law signals an enforcement gap.'),
    ('counterfeit_wco_customs',
     'WCO Customs Border Enforcement Against Counterfeit and Pirated Goods',
     'World Customs Organization, IPR, Health and Safety Programme (wcoomd.org/en/topics/enforcement-and-compliance/activities-and-programmes/ipr.aspx, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "The WCO frames customs as a frontline defence against counterfeiting to prevent unfair competition and protect public health, citing pharmaceuticals with poisonous or inferior constituents and unsafe auto parts and toys. Its IPR programme accredits trained experts, runs regional seminars, and conducts diagnostic missions reviewing national legislation and country-specific risk. Coordinated border operations use simultaneous enhanced controls with real-time information sharing and risk-analysis tools. Private-sector partnership gives customs commercial data and intelligence from legitimate rights holders. The WCO also publishes guidance on environmentally safe destruction of seized counterfeits. Recognition: goods diverging from a rights holder's authentic-product profile in a customs risk database are flagged; the remedy is detention, seizure, and lawful destruction."),
    ('counterfeit_us_trademark',
     'US Criminal Trademark Counterfeiting (18 USC 2320) and Lanham Act Civil Liability',
     '18 U.S.C. 2320 and 15 U.S.C. 1114 (Lanham Act), via Cornell LII (law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2320 and law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/1114, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     '18 USC 2320 makes it a crime to intentionally traffic in goods or services using a counterfeit mark, defined as a spurious mark identical with or substantially indistinguishable from a federally registered mark and likely to cause confusion, mistake, or deception. Individual first offenders face up to a $2,000,000 fine or 10 years; repeat offenders up to $5,000,000 or 20 years; entities up to $5,000,000 then $15,000,000. Counterfeit military goods or drugs carry up to 20-30 years, and death can reach life. Courts order forfeiture and destruction. Lanham Act 15 USC 1114 imposes civil liability for unauthorized commercial use of a reproduction, counterfeit, copy, or colorable imitation of a registered mark likely to cause confusion, with damages and profits. Recognition: an identical-mark good from an unauthorized channel is the core legal trigger.'),
    ('counterfeit_eu_customs',
     'EU Regulation 608/2013: Customs Enforcement of Intellectual Property Rights',
     'Regulation (EU) No 608/2013 concerning customs enforcement of IP rights (eur-lex.europa.eu, CELEX 32013R0608, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Regulation 608/2013 lets EU customs act against goods under customs supervision suspected of infringing an IP right (Art 1). Covered rights (Art 2) include trade marks, designs, copyright, geographical indications, and patents. Rights holders file an Application for Action with technical data on authentic goods (Arts 3-6). On identifying suspect goods, customs suspend release or detain them and notify the declarant and rights holder, generally within one working day (Arts 17-19). Goods may be destroyed without a court finding where the rights holder confirms infringement in writing (10 working days, or 3 for perishables) and the holder agrees or stays silent (Art 23). A small-consignments procedure (Art 26) covers fakes in parcels of three units or under two kilograms. Recognition: detained goods diverging from the authentic-product dossier face destruction.'),
    ('counterfeit_goods_redflags',
     'Counterfeit-Goods Buyer Red Flags: Price, Seller, Packaging, Serial Anomalies',
     'U.S. Customs and Border Protection, The Truth Behind Counterfeits (cbp.gov/trade/fakegoodsrealdangers, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'CBP warns that the dangers of counterfeits are not always obvious, especially online, and urges buying authentic products from trusted, verified sources. Recognition signals that a good is likely counterfeit rather than genuine include: a price far below the normal market rate for the brand; a seller who is not an authorized retailer or distributor; listings on unverified marketplaces or social channels; packaging, labels, logos, or holograms with spelling, color, or print-quality anomalies; missing or duplicated serial numbers, batch codes, or authentication features; and absent warranty, manuals, or safety certifications. Over 90% of seizures occur in mail and express channels carrying small e-commerce parcels. Remedy framing: importing counterfeit merchandise is unlawful and may expose even an unwitting consumer to a fine, while CBP targets and seizes such imports.'),
    ('counterfeit_product_safety',
     'Counterfeit and Substandard Product Safety Harm: Drugs, Auto Parts, Cosmetics, Toys',
     'U.S. Customs and Border Protection, The Truth Behind Counterfeits (cbp.gov/trade/fakegoodsrealdangers, fetched 2026-06-05); OECD-EUIPO, Mapping Global Trade in Fakes 2025 (oecd.org, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "Counterfeit goods are frequently made with substandard materials and can be hazardous. CBP cites counterfeit pharmaceuticals that lack necessary active ingredients or contain fentanyl or other opioids; counterfeit cosmetics and personal-care items such as toothpaste, shampoo, makeup, and perfume; and counterfeit automotive parts such as airbags and brake pads that can cause catastrophic failure. In FY2023 counterfeit pharmaceuticals were nearly half of all health-and-safety products CBP seized. The OECD-EUIPO 2025 study finds counterfeiters expanding into hazardous categories including automotive parts, medicines, cosmetics, toys, and food. Recognition: a safety-critical product (electronics, vehicle parts, medicine, children's toys) offered through an unauthorized or anonymous channel is a high-risk fake. Remedy routes are customs seizure, recall, and market-surveillance enforcement."),
    ('counterfeit_online_marketplace',
     'Online-Marketplace Counterfeit Recognition and the US INFORM Consumers Act',
     'Federal Trade Commission, Informing Businesses about the INFORM Consumers Act (ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/INFORMAct, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The INFORM Consumers Act, effective June 27, 2023, deters criminals from selling stolen, counterfeit, or unsafe goods on online marketplaces by adding seller transparency. Marketplaces must collect, verify, and sometimes disclose information about high-volume third-party sellers, defined as sellers with 200 or more new or unused consumer-product transactions and $5,000 or more in gross revenue in a 12-month period within the prior 24 months. The marketplace has 10 days to collect bank account, business tax ID, and contact information, then 10 days to verify it as valid and not falsified. Sellers at $20,000+ annual revenue must have name, address, and contact details disclosed on listings, which must offer a consumer reporting mechanism. Recognition: an opaque, unverifiable high-volume seller is a red flag. The FTC and State AGs enforce, with civil penalties up to $53,088 per violation.'),
    ('counterfeit_organized_crime_nexus',
     'Counterfeiting, Organized Crime, and the Forced-Labor Production Nexus',
     "UNODC, Counterfeit: Don't buy into organized crime campaign (unodc.org/unodc/en/frontpage/2014/January/counterfeit-dont-buy-into-organized-crime.html, fetched 2026-06-05)",
     'UNODC frames counterfeiting as a roughly USD 250 billion-a-year transnational business that funds organized crime. Groups including the Mafia and Camorra in Europe and the Triads and Yakuza in Asia have diversified into counterfeit trafficking while also engaged in drug and human trafficking, extortion, and money laundering, often using the same routes and methods. Proceeds from fake-goods sales provide income and help launder other illicit profits, which can be recycled into further production. Production frequently occurs in unregulated, exploitative settings where low-paid workers face unsafe conditions, a forced-labor dimension. Recognition: a deeply discounted, unauthorized-channel good can indicate proceeds flowing to criminal networks. Remedy routes are seizure, financial-intelligence tracing, and prosecution under organized-crime and anti-money-laundering law.'),
    ('counterfeit_gray_market',
     'Gray-Market and Parallel-Import Diversion Recognition (Material Differences)',
     'International Trademark Association, Parallel Imports / Gray Market (inta.org/topics/parallel-imports and inta.org/fact-sheets/parallel-imports-gray-market-goods, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Gray-market or parallel-import goods are genuine branded products lawfully made by or for the brand owner, then imported into another market and sold without the trademark owner consent. They are authentic, not counterfeit, and arise from exhaustion of IP rights or the first-sale doctrine: once the owner authorizes a sale into a jurisdiction, downstream resale there generally cannot be controlled. Exhaustion regimes differ; the EU applies regional exhaustion, while the US first-sale defense applies domestically but not to all foreign-first sales. The material-differences doctrine is a key limit: most US courts treat distribution as unlawful where imports differ materially from authorized goods. Recognition: authentic-looking goods sold outside the authorized network, lacking local warranty or compliance, signal diversion; the remedy is civil trademark and customs action.'),
    ('counterfeit_oecd_euipo_trade',
     'OECD-EUIPO Trade-in-Fakes Scale and Trends (Mapping Global Trade in Fakes 2025)',
     'OECD-EUIPO, Mapping Global Trade in Fakes 2025 (oecd.org press release, 2025-05-07, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The OECD-EUIPO 2025 study estimates global trade in counterfeit goods at USD 467 billion in 2021, about 2.3% of world imports; for the EU about USD 117 billion, roughly 4.7% of EU imports. Clothing, footwear, and leather goods jointly accounted for about 62% of seized counterfeits, while counterfeiters expanded into hazardous categories such as auto parts, medicines, cosmetics, toys, and food. China remained the dominant source at about 45% of reported seizures in 2021, with other Asian, Middle Eastern, and Latin American economies involved. Around 65% of seizures involved small parcels and mail, and counterfeiters increasingly use localization, shipping unassembled parts to assemble fakes near end markets. Recognition: these patterns flag high-risk categories and channels. The report urges real-time information sharing among customs, police, and financial units.'),
    ('counterfeit_gi_fraud',
     'Geographical Indications and Food, Wine, and Origin Fraud (TRIPS Art 22-23)',
     'WTO TRIPS Agreement, Articles 22-23 on geographical indications, and EU GI schemes PDO/PGI/TSG (wto.org TRIPS overview and EU GI policy, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Geographical indications protect product names tied to a place whose qualities or reputation derive from that origin. TRIPS Article 22.2 requires members to prevent designations suggesting a good originates somewhere other than its true place of origin in a way that misleads the public. Article 23 gives wines and spirits enhanced protection: members must prevent using a GI for wines or spirits not originating in the indicated place even where the true origin is shown or the name carries expressions like type or style. The EU operates PDO, PGI, and TSG schemes for foods, wines, and spirits and has strengthened protection against fraud. Recognition: a protected name (such as Champagne or a PDO olive oil) applied to product from the wrong region, or false provenance labeling, is origin fraud. Remedy: cancellation of misuse, customs action, and enforcement against deceptive labeling.'),
    ('counterfeit_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: Customs Seizure, Recordation, Civil/Criminal IP Action',
     'U.S. Customs and Border Protection, IPR Seizures (cbp.gov/newsroom/stats/intellectual-property-rights-ipr-seizures, fetched 2026-06-05); 18 U.S.C. 2320; 15 U.S.C. 1114',
     'Counterfeiting remedies stack across border, civil, and criminal channels. At the border, CBP targets and seizes counterfeit and pirated goods; in FY2024 nearly 90% of IPR seizures came from China and Hong Kong, with top commodities by value including jewelry, watches, and handbags/wallets, and over 90% in mail and express environments. Rights holders strengthen interdiction by recording registered trademarks and copyrights with CBP through IPR e-Recordation, letting officers detain and seize matching goods. Civil remedies under the Lanham Act (15 USC 1114) include injunctions, damages, and profits; criminal liability under 18 USC 2320 reaches up to $2,000,000 and 10 years for an individual first offense, with forfeiture and destruction. Recognition: a seized or recordation-matched good triggers detention, then destruction or litigation.'),
    ('envcrime_cites_wildlife_trade',
     'CITES: Permit System and Appendices I/II/III for Endangered Species Trade',
     'CITES Convention, How CITES works and The CITES Appendices (cites.org/eng/disc/how.php and cites.org/eng/app/index.php, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'CITES subjects international trade in listed species to a licensing system. Each Party designates a Management Authority to issue permits and a Scientific Authority to assess trade impact. Appendix I covers species threatened with extinction: commercial trade is prohibited and exceptional trade needs both an import and an export permit. Appendix II covers species that could become threatened; an export permit or re-export certificate is required and may issue only if the specimen was legally obtained and trade is not detrimental to survival. Appendix III covers species a Party protects domestically and seeks help controlling. Recognition signal: a CITES specimen crossing a border without the correct, valid permit or certificate, or with a forged or altered one, is a red flag. The remedy route is refusal of clearance at the port and referral to enforcement.'),
    ('envcrime_basel_hazardous_waste',
     'Basel Convention: Prior Informed Consent and the Definition of Illegal Traffic',
     'Basel Convention Overview and Illegal Traffic Overview (basel.int/TheConvention/Overview and basel.int/Implementation/LegalMatters/IllegalTraffic, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The Basel Convention controls transboundary movements of hazardous and other wastes. Under the Prior Informed Consent procedure (Articles 6-7), the State of export must notify the States of import and transit in writing, and movement may proceed only after all concerned States give written consent. Hazardous waste may not be exported to non-Parties or to States that have banned imports. Article 9 defines illegal traffic as movement without notification or consent, with fraudulently obtained consent, in material non-conformance with documents, or resulting in deliberate disposal contrary to the Convention. Recognition signal: a waste shipment lacking a matching, consented notification document is presumptively illegal traffic. The remedy is the duty to take back or otherwise ensure environmentally sound disposal, plus national prosecution.'),
    ('envcrime_eudr_deforestation',
     'EU Deforestation Regulation (2023/1115): Due Diligence and Geolocation',
     'Regulation (EU) 2023/1115 on deforestation-free products (eur-lex.europa.eu, CELEX 32023R1115, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The EU Deforestation Regulation covers seven commodities and their derived products: cattle, cocoa, coffee, oil palm, rubber, soya and wood. Operators and large traders must run a due-diligence system (information gathering, risk assessment, risk mitigation) and may place products on the market only when risk of non-compliance is negligible. Products must be deforestation-free, meaning produced on land not subject to deforestation after 31 December 2020, and wood must not induce forest degradation after that cut-off. Operators must submit geolocation coordinates (latitude and longitude to six decimals) for plots above four hectares. The Commission benchmarks countries as low, standard or high risk. Recognition signal: missing or implausible geolocation, or sourcing traced to land cleared after the cut-off date, indicates non-compliance and triggers enforcement.'),
    ('envcrime_lacey_act',
     'US Lacey Act and the 2008 Plant Amendment: Due Care and Import Declarations',
     'US Fish & Wildlife Service Lacey Act page and USDA APHIS Lacey Act declaration requirements (fws.gov/law/lacey-act and aphis.usda.gov/plant-imports/file-lacey-act-declaration, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The Lacey Act prohibits the import, export, transport, sale, receipt, acquisition or purchase of any fish, wildlife or plant taken, possessed, transported or sold in violation of any underlying law, treaty or regulation, US or foreign. The 2008 Farm Bill amendment extended coverage to plants and plant products, including timber and paper, and made it unlawful to trade in illegally harvested plants. Importers must file a declaration stating the plant scientific name, value, quantity and country of harvest; where origin is unknown or mixed, all possible countries must be listed. The standard is due care: a trafficker who, exercising due care, should have known goods were illegally sourced commits a crime. Recognition signal: a false or evasive species or country-of-harvest declaration is a primary red flag, enforced by the FWS Office of Law Enforcement.'),
    ('envcrime_eu_iuu_regulation',
     'EU IUU Regulation (1005/2008): Catch Certification and the Carding System',
     'EU IUU Regulation overview, European Commission Oceans and Fisheries (oceans-and-fisheries.ec.europa.eu/fisheries/rules/illegal-fishing_en, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Council Regulation (EC) No 1005/2008 establishes a system to prevent, deter and eliminate illegal, unreported and unregulated (IUU) fishing. Marine fishery products may be imported into the EU only when accompanied by a catch certificate validated by the flag State of the vessel that made the catch; certificates travel with the product through the supply chain to allow checks. The digital CATCH system became mandatory in January 2026. For non-cooperating third countries the Commission runs a carding process: a yellow card warning requiring reform, escalating to a red card that designates a country non-cooperating and bans its fisheries products from the EU market. The EU also maintains an IUU vessel list. Recognition signal: product offered for import without a valid flag-State catch certificate, or sourced from a red-carded country or listed vessel, is a red flag for market refusal.'),
    ('envcrime_psma_port_state',
     'FAO Port State Measures Agreement: Denying Port to IUU Vessels',
     'FAO Agreement on Port State Measures (PSMA) overview, Food and Agriculture Organization (fao.org/iuu-fishing/international-framework/psma/en, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The Agreement on Port State Measures (PSMA), approved by the FAO Conference in November 2009 and in force since June 2016, is the first binding international agreement that specifically targets IUU fishing by denying port access and use to foreign vessels engaged in or supporting it. It sets a minimum set of standard measures Parties apply when foreign vessels seek entry to, or are in, their ports, including advance requests for port entry, designated ports, inspections, and denial of port services and landing where IUU activity is found. Parties share information about vessels denied entry. The Agreement binds dozens of Parties covering over a hundred States. Recognition signal: a vessel that avoids designated ports, gives inconsistent advance-entry information, or appears on an IUU list warrants inspection and possible denial of landing as the remedy.'),
    ('envcrime_fatf_money_laundering',
     'FATF: Money Laundering from Environmental Crime - Typologies and Red Flags',
     'FATF report Money Laundering from Environmental Crime, 28 June 2021 (fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Environmentalcrime/Money-laundering-from-environmental-crime.html, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'FATF finds environmental crime (forestry crime, illegal mining, waste trafficking, illegal wildlife trade) is a low-risk, high-reward enterprise generating billions and converging with corruption, tax fraud, drug trafficking and forced labour. Criminals make profits by using front companies to mix legal and illegal goods and payments early in resource supply chains, and rely on corruption, trade-based fraud and offshore corporate structures to conceal beneficial owners. Red-flag indicators include commingled legal/illegal commodity flows, shell companies in the supply chain, trade-based mismatches between declared and shipped goods, and proceeds routed through trade and financial centres far from the resource. Remedy route: strengthen cooperation between financial investigators and environmental agencies, pursue financial investigations, and recover assets held abroad.'),
    ('envcrime_wildlife_redflags',
     'Wildlife Trafficking Recognition Red Flags in Trade and Permits',
     'TRAFFIC, Red Flag Indicators for wildlife and timber trafficking in containerized sea cargo (traffic.org/publications/reports/how-to-spot-the-warning-signs-of-wildlife-crime-in-the-maritime-industry, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Most wildlife products are trafficked by sea, hidden within legitimate trade. Documentary red flags include falsified or tampered CITES permits and certificates, and discrepancies between declared and actual value, weight, species or appearance. Species laundering is a recurring scheme: wild-caught animals are passed off as captive-bred through facilities or quotas, sometimes set far above what stock could biologically produce, giving illegal specimens legal cover. Behavioural red flags include consignments split across multiple shipments, last-minute clearance requests, and sudden changes to routes or destinations. Corruption producing officials who falsely certify wild animals as captive-bred is a known enabler. Recognition framing only; remedy routes are seizure, refusal of clearance, and prosecution under CITES-implementing national law.'),
    ('envcrime_timber_laundering',
     'Timber Laundering Recognition: False Origin, Shell Exporters, Mixed Loads',
     'TRAFFIC, Red Flag Indicators for wildlife and timber trafficking in containerized sea cargo (traffic.org/publications/reports/how-to-spot-the-warning-signs-of-wildlife-crime-in-the-maritime-industry, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Illegally logged timber is laundered into legal trade through documentary manipulation. Recognition red flags include false or vague species and country-of-harvest declarations, declared species that does not match the physical load, and protected species mislabelled as common commercial timber. Newly formed or shell exporters with no verifiable harvesting history, abrupt jumps in export volume, and mixed loads that combine legally sourced wood with illegal stock to dilute scrutiny are further signals. Routing anomalies and transshipment through a country with no matching forest resource suggest origin-laundering. These signals align with the EU Deforestation Regulation due-diligence test and the US Lacey Act plant declaration. Recognition framing only: the remedy route is document verification, physical inspection, seizure of non-conforming consignments, and referral to enforcement.'),
    ('envcrime_iuu_forced_labour_nexus',
     'Forced-Labour Nexus in IUU Fishing: Recognition Framing',
     'ILO, Forced labour in fishing: how big is the problem, and EJF distant-water fleet briefings (ilo.org/resource/article/forced-labour-fishing-how-big-problem and ejfoundation.org, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'IUU fishing frequently converges with forced labour and human trafficking at sea. Distant-water fleets raise vulnerability because long voyages, isolation, and at-sea transshipment keep crews beyond the reach of inspection and let vessels avoid port oversight. Reported indicators of abuse include retention of identity documents, deception during recruitment, debt and unlawful wage deductions, withheld food and water, and threats of violence; thousands of migrant fishers have been repatriated from exploitative vessels. ILO Convention No. 188 (Work in Fishing) sets minimum labour standards to protect fishers. Recognition framing only: an IUU indicator combined with crew-isolation, document-retention, or recruitment-debt signals warrants a combined fisheries and anti-trafficking response.'),
    ('envcrime_ewaste_trafficking',
     'E-Waste Trafficking Recognition Red Flags Under the Basel Convention',
     'Basel Convention Illegal Traffic overview and E-waste Amendments FAQs (basel.int/Implementation/LegalMatters/IllegalTraffic and basel.int/Implementation/Ewaste, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The 2022 Basel e-waste amendments, effective 1 January 2025, bring all electrical and electronic waste, hazardous and non-hazardous, under the Prior Informed Consent procedure, so cross-border e-waste shipments require advance notification and consent. A common evasion is disguising waste electronics as used or working equipment; the rules require testing for functionality and documentation before export. Recognition red flags include false declarations misrepresenting contents, concealment or double-layering of materials, mislabelled containers, untested used equipment, and mixed loads hiding broken devices among working ones. Illegal traffic under Article 9 covers movement without notification or consent or with fraudulent documents. Remedy route: customs scrutiny, refusal or take-back of the shipment, and national prosecution.'),
    ('envcrime_enforcement_remedy',
     'INTERPOL and the Enforcement and Remedy Landscape for Environmental Crime',
     'INTERPOL, Environmental crime programme (interpol.int/en/Crimes/Environmental-crime, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "INTERPOL's environmental security work addresses five crime areas: wildlife crime, forestry crime, fisheries crime, illegal mining, and pollution (including illegal waste). It supports member countries through coordinated joint operations, criminal-intelligence analysis, capacity building, and dedicated projects such as Project LEAP on deforestation; recent global operations produced hundreds of arrests and large seizures of live animals. INTERPOL emphasises that environmental crime occurs hand in hand with passport fraud, corruption and money laundering. The enforcement and remedy landscape combines treaty controls (CITES, Basel, PSMA), national prosecution, financial investigation to seize proceeds, and cross-border cooperation to recover assets held abroad. These channels escalate a grounded suspicion of environmental crime for investigation and remedy."),
    ('fraud_pig_butchering_recognition',
     'Pig-Butchering / Romance-Investment Fraud: Recognition Signals',
     'FTC Consumer Advice, What To Know About Romance Scams (consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-about-romance-scams, fetched 2026-06-05); FBI/OHCHR corroborate the romance-investment pattern',
     'Pig-butchering blends a romance scam with a fake investment. Recognition signals: a stranger meets the target on a dating app or social media, then quickly moves the chat off-platform and builds trust over weeks or months of daily contact (long grooming). The persona always has a reason they cannot meet or video-call in person (overseas work, oil rig, military). Once trust is built they introduce a crypto or trading opportunity, steer the target to a slick but fake platform showing fabricated gains, and pressure deposits. The decisive red flag is the withdrawal block: the victim cannot cash out, or is told to pay taxes or fees first. Never mix online dating with investment advice; that combination is treated as a scam.'),
    ('fraud_scam_compound_labour_nexus',
     'The Scam-Compound Forced-Labour Nexus (Victim, Not Criminal)',
     'UN Human Rights Office (OHCHR) press release, 29 Aug 2023 (ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2023/08/hundreds-thousands-trafficked-work-online-scammers-se-asia-says-un-report, fetched 2026-06-05); UNODC SE Asia estimates',
     'Many online scams are run from fortified compounds in Southeast Asia staffed by trafficked workers. The UN Human Rights Office found hundreds of thousands of people coerced into online criminality, with credible estimates of at least 120,000 held in Myanmar and around 100,000 in Cambodia. People are lured by fake job adverts, then confined and forced to run romance-investment, crypto, and other frauds under threat, including torture and arbitrary detention. The recognition frame is dual victimhood: the person operating the scam may themselves be a trafficking victim. The UN warns such people are wrongly treated as criminals or immigration offenders rather than protected. Identify coercion indicators and route to protection and remedy, not prosecution.'),
    ('fraud_bec_redflags',
     'Business Email Compromise (BEC): Red Flags',
     'FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3), Business Email Compromise (ic3.gov/CrimeInfo/BEC, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'BEC is a scam targeting organizations that move funds, carried out by compromising a legitimate business email account through social engineering or intrusion, then redirecting a payment. Recognition signals: an emailed request to change payment type (often check to wire) or to send funds to a new bank account; a spoofed or lookalike sender domain that does not exactly match the real party; a reply in an existing thread claiming vendor banking details have changed; and urgency that pressures the recipient to act before verifying. Treat any unexpected change of payment instructions as suspect. The control is out-of-band verification: confirm changes via a known channel, never the contact details supplied inside the request itself.'),
    ('fraud_advance_fee',
     'Advance-Fee Fraud (419 / Fake Fees to Release a Prize or Inheritance)',
     'FTC Consumer Advice, Fake Prize, Sweepstakes, and Lottery Scams (consumer.ftc.gov/articles/fake-prize-sweepstakes-and-lottery-scams, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Advance-fee fraud, also called the 419 scam, promises a large payout (a prize, lottery win, loan, or inheritance) that supposedly requires a small upfront payment to release. Recognition signals per the FTC: if you must pay a fee for taxes, shipping, processing, or customs to get a prize, it is a scam; real prizes are free. Other red flags are claims you won a lottery you never entered, pressure to act now, impersonation of a government body or known company, instructions to pay by wire, gift card, or cryptocurrency, and a mailed check you are told to deposit and partly send back (a fake-check variant). No legitimate prize or windfall is conditioned on paying money first to receive it.'),
    ('fraud_crypto_investment_redflags',
     'Crypto / Fake-Investment Fraud: Red Flags',
     'FTC Consumer Advice, What To Know About Cryptocurrency and Scams (consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-about-cryptocurrency-and-scams, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Crypto investment fraud is among the top ways scammers extract money, and crypto is both the lure and the payment. Recognition signals from the FTC: only scammers guarantee profits or big returns; an out-of-the-blue investment manager promises to grow your money but only if you buy crypto and move it to their account; the investment website looks real but is fake. The decisive red flag is the withdrawal block: you cannot take money out at all, or only after paying high fees. Other markers are unsolicited contact, celebrity-endorsement claims, promises of free money, and big claims with no verifiable detail. No legitimate business demands payment in cryptocurrency in advance, and crypto payments are typically irreversible.'),
    ('fraud_fake_job_offer',
     'Fraudulent Job-Offer Recruitment Scams (Bridge to Trafficking)',
     'FTC Consumer Advice, Job Scams (consumer.ftc.gov/articles/job-scams, fetched 2026-06-05); UN/OHCHR on fake-job recruitment into scam compounds',
     "Fraudulent job offers promise easy money but seek the applicant's money or identity, and the same fake-recruitment pattern is used to lure people into trafficking and scam compounds. Recognition signals from the FTC: any demand to pay upfront for a job, training, certification, equipment, or placement (honest employers and placement firms do not charge candidates); offers to make a lot of money fast with little work; and the fake-check tell, where a new employer sends a check to deposit and asks you to send part of the money back or buy gift cards. With overseas offers, pressure around visa, ticket, or processing fees and an unrealistically high salary are red flags. Search the employer name plus the word scam before engaging."),
    ('fraud_money_mule_recruitment',
     'Money-Mule Recruitment: Recognition Signals',
     'FBI, Money Mules (fbi.gov/scams-and-safety/common-frauds-and-scams/money-mules, via IC3, fetched 2026-06-05); US DOJ Money Mule Initiative',
     'A money mule moves illegally obtained funds for someone else, laundering proceeds of scams and trafficking. Recruitment often arrives as a work-from-home offer to be a payment processor or transfer agent, framed as easy money. Recognition signals from the FBI: an employer who communicates only through free web-based email; a request to receive funds in your personal bank account and then process or transfer them by wire, ACH, mail, or money-service business, keeping a cut; and instructions to open accounts or move money for people you do not know. The frame is critical: acting as a mule is a crime even if unwitting, and it can carry money-laundering and fraud charges. Treat any job that uses your own account to move money as a stop signal.'),
    ('fraud_ftc_act_section5',
     'US FTC Act Section 5: Unfair or Deceptive Acts or Practices',
     'Federal Trade Commission Act, 15 U.S.C. Sec. 45 (Section 5); FTC legal-library statute page (ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/statutes/federal-trade-commission-act, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Section 5 of the FTC Act, 15 U.S.C. Sec. 45, is the core US legal authority against consumer fraud: it prohibits unfair or deceptive acts or practices in or affecting commerce. A practice is deceptive if a representation, omission, or practice is likely to mislead a consumer acting reasonably and is material to a buying decision; the FTC weighs the overall net impression, not isolated words. A practice is unfair if it causes substantial consumer injury that is not reasonably avoidable and not outweighed by benefits. Under Section 5 the Commission can investigate, bring administrative or court actions, seek redress for harmed consumers, and write rules defining specific unfair or deceptive conduct. This is the statutory anchor for grounding a US deception or unfairness judgement.'),
    ('fraud_eu_ucpd',
     'EU Unfair Commercial Practices Directive 2005/29/EC',
     'Directive 2005/29/EC on unfair business-to-consumer commercial practices; European Commission UCPD page (commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/consumer-protection-law/unfair-commercial-practices-law/unfair-commercial-practices-directive_en, fetched 2026-06-05); EUR-Lex CELEX:32005L0029',
     'Directive 2005/29/EC is the overarching EU law on unfair business-to-consumer commercial practices, covering conduct before, during, and after a transaction. It bans practices that are unfair under a general clause (contrary to professional diligence and likely to distort the economic behaviour of the average consumer), and two main sub-categories: misleading practices (false or deceptive information, or misleading omissions) and aggressive practices (using harassment, coercion, or undue influence). Annex I is a blacklist of practices banned in all circumstances, such as fake prize claims and false scarcity. It was modernised by Directive (EU) 2019/2161 to cover online platforms, fake reviews, and dark patterns. National enforcers use it to curb a broad range of deceptive and aggressive selling, the EU counterpart to US FTC Section 5.'),
    ('fraud_phishing_recognition',
     'Phishing / Smishing / Vishing: Recognition Signals',
     'FTC Consumer Advice, How To Recognize and Avoid Phishing Scams (consumer.ftc.gov/articles/how-recognize-and-avoid-phishing-scams, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Phishing uses email, text (smishing), or calls (vishing) impersonating a trusted company to steal passwords, account numbers, or identity data. Recognition signals from the FTC: an unexpected message that looks like a known bank, card issuer, utility, or payment app and tells a story to make you click a link or open an attachment; claims of suspicious activity or a login attempt that did not happen; claims of an account or payment problem that does not exist; a request to confirm personal or financial information; an unfamiliar invoice; or a link to make a payment. A generic greeting and a manufactured account-on-hold pretext are common tells. Legitimate companies do not email or text a link to update payment details. Verify only through a channel you independently know is real.'),
    ('fraud_elder_exploitation',
     'Elder Financial Exploitation: Recognition Signals',
     'US Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB), Protecting Older Adults from Fraud and Financial Exploitation (consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/educator-tools/resources-for-older-adults/protecting-against-fraud, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Elder financial exploitation is a tracked, growing harm where older adults are defrauded or coerced into transferring money, often unable to earn it back. Recognition signals: unexplained or unusual withdrawals, especially from dormant accounts with large balances; uncharacteristic asset transfers, including sudden moves into cryptocurrency, to people with no prior relationship; new unrecognized charges; mail from unfamiliar firms offering protection or monitoring; and reluctance or anxiety about discussing finances. Isolation is a control tactic, cutting the person off from family. Common scripts include tech-support pop-ups demanding payment and the grandparent scam, where an imposter posing as a relative in trouble pleads for urgent secret money. Financial institutions are encouraged to flag suspicious activity and alert trusted contacts.'),
    ('fraud_victim_remedy',
     'Victim Remedy and Reporting Landscape (Generalized)',
     'FTC Consumer Advice, What To Do if You Were Scammed (consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-do-if-you-were-scammed, fetched 2026-06-05); FBI IC3 BEC recall guidance (ic3.gov/CrimeInfo/BEC)',
     'Victims should act fast; remedy depends on the payment rail. Wire or bank transfer: contact the sending bank or transfer company immediately, report fraud, and request a recall or reversal; for cross-border wires, banks can pursue a SWIFT recall and a hold-harmless or indemnity letter. Card payment: ask the card issuer to reverse the charge and dispute it (chargeback). Money-app or ACH: report to the app or bank and ask for reversal. Cryptocurrency is typically irreversible, but still notify the exchange used. Reporting routes generalize across borders: in the US, file with the FTC reportfraud portal and the FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3); identity loss goes to the identity-theft recovery site; elsewhere, report to the national consumer-protection agency, financial regulator, and police. Reporting builds cases and may enable fund recovery.'),
    ('health_false_claims_act',
     'US False Claims Act and Qui Tam (Healthcare Billing Fraud)',
     'False Claims Act, 31 U.S.C. ss 3729-3733; US DOJ Civil Division (https://www.justice.gov/civil/false-claims-act)',
     "Legal authority: The False Claims Act (31 U.S.C. 3729-3733), enacted 1863, makes anyone who knowingly submits or causes a false claim to a federal program (Medicare, Medicaid, TRICARE) liable for treble damages plus per-claim penalties. Liability also attaches to using a false record material to a claim or improperly avoiding an obligation to repay. Recognition: false billing, kickback-tainted claims, and reverse false claims are core triggers; DOJ recovered over $2.9 billion from fraud and false-claims cases in FY2024. Remedy: the qui tam provision lets a private relator (whistleblower) sue on the government's behalf and share in the recovery; conduct may be reported to DOJ or HHS-OIG."),
    ('health_anti_kickback',
     'US Anti-Kickback Statute (42 U.S.C. 1320a-7b)',
     'Anti-Kickback Statute, 42 U.S.C. 1320a-7b; HHS-OIG Fraud & Abuse Laws (https://oig.hhs.gov/compliance/physician-education/fraud-abuse-laws/)',
     'Legal authority: The Anti-Kickback Statute (42 U.S.C. 1320a-7b) criminalizes knowing and willful payment or receipt of remuneration to induce or reward referrals of items or services reimbursed by federal health programs. Remuneration is broad: cash, free rent, lavish hotel stays and meals, or excessive compensation. Recognition red flags: payments tracking referral volume, sham consulting or medical-director arrangements, and waived copays used as inducements. Statutory safe harbors (e.g., bona fide employment, qualifying personal-services and space rentals) protect arrangements only if every condition is met. Remedy: criminal fines and imprisonment, civil penalties up to $50,000 per violation plus three times the remuneration, and exclusion from federal programs; DOJ and OIG enforce.'),
    ('health_stark_law',
     'US Stark Law - Physician Self-Referral (42 U.S.C. 1395nn)',
     'Physician Self-Referral Law (Stark Law), 42 U.S.C. 1395nn; HHS-OIG Fraud & Abuse Laws (https://oig.hhs.gov/compliance/physician-education/fraud-abuse-laws/)',
     'Legal authority: The Stark Law (42 U.S.C. 1395nn) bars a physician from referring Medicare/Medicaid patients for designated health services (clinical labs, imaging, physical therapy, DME, home health, hospital services) to an entity with which the physician or an immediate family member has a financial relationship, unless an exception applies. It is a strict-liability statute: no proof of intent is required. Recognition red flags: referral patterns tied to ownership or compensation, lease or service payments not at fair market value, and volume- or value-based arrangements without a qualifying exception. Remedy: tainted claims cannot be billed or must be refunded; violations bring civil monetary penalties and program exclusion, enforced by CMS and OIG.'),
    ('health_billing_fraud_redflags',
     'Healthcare Billing-Fraud Recognition Red Flags',
     'False Claims Act, 31 U.S.C. 3729; HHS-OIG enforcement patterns (https://oig.hhs.gov/compliance/physician-education/fraud-abuse-laws/)',
     'Recognition framing for common billing-fraud schemes investigated by HHS-OIG under the False Claims Act. Upcoding: submitting a higher-paying diagnosis or procedure code than what was actually documented or performed. Unbundling (fragmentation): splitting a bundled procedure into separately billed components to inflate reimbursement. Phantom or ghost billing: claiming services, tests, or equipment never ordered or provided. Medically unnecessary services: ordering tests or procedures (e.g., excessive nerve-conduction or genetic panels) solely to generate payment. Red flags: mismatched documentation, identical claims across patients, and billing volumes inconsistent with capacity. Remedy: internal compliance review, refund of overpayments, and reporting via the FCA qui tam route or OIG hotline.'),
    ('health_who_falsified_medicines',
     'WHO Substandard and Falsified (SF) Medical Products Programme',
     'WHO Fact Sheet: Substandard and Falsified Medical Products (https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/substandard-and-falsified-medical-products)',
     'Legal/policy authority: WHO defines substandard products as authorized items failing quality standards through poor manufacturing or quality control, and falsified products as those that deliberately misrepresent identity, composition, or source to deceive. WHO estimates at least 1 in 10 medical products in low- and middle-income countries is substandard or falsified, with annual global spending near $30.5 billion. Recognition: treatment failure, poisoning, and accelerated antimicrobial resistance; heightened risk when buying from unauthorized or online sources or amid disrupted supply chains. Remedy: the WHO Global Surveillance and Monitoring System (GSMS, since 2013) and Member State mechanism collect reports and issue rapid alerts; suspected products should be reported to national regulators.'),
    ('health_eu_fmd',
     'EU Falsified Medicines Directive 2011/62/EU',
     'Directive 2011/62/EU (Falsified Medicines Directive), EUR-Lex (https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32011L0062)',
     'Legal authority: EU Directive 2011/62/EU defines a falsified medicinal product as one falsely representing its identity, source/manufacturer, or history/distribution records (excluding unintentional quality defects and IP disputes). It mandates safety features on prescription medicines: a unique identifier verified against a repositories system, and an anti-tampering device. Recognition tools: a common EU logo identifies legally authorized online pharmacies and links to the competent-authority register; wholesalers and pharmacists must verify safety features before supply or dispensing. Supply-chain controls require distributors to confirm supplier authorization and manufacturers to audit active-substance suppliers. Remedy: packs failing verification are quarantined and reported to national authorities.'),
    ('health_counterfeit_medicine_supply',
     'Counterfeit-Medicine Supply-Chain Recognition',
     'Drug Supply Chain Security Act (DSCSA), Drug Quality and Security Act 2013; US FDA (https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-supply-chain-integrity/drug-supply-chain-security-act-dscsa)',
     'Legal authority: The US Drug Supply Chain Security Act (DSCSA, part of the 2013 Drug Quality and Security Act) builds an interoperable, package-level electronic track-and-trace system to keep counterfeit, diverted, stolen, or contaminated drugs out of the supply chain. Recognition red flags for an illegitimate product: missing or inconsistent transaction information (product name, strength, lot number, National Drug Code, container count, prior owners), relabeled or repackaged stock, broken serialization, and sourcing outside authorized trading partners. Remedy: trading partners must investigate suspect product, quarantine it, notify trading partners, and notify FDA within 24 hours of confirming a product is illegitimate; the verification ladder enables rapid recall.'),
    ('health_organ_trafficking',
     'Organ Trafficking - Council of Europe Convention and WHO Guiding Principles',
     'Council of Europe Convention against Trafficking in Human Organs (CETS No. 216) and WHO Guiding Principles on Human Cell, Tissue and Organ Transplantation (WHA63.22); CoE financial-gain guide (https://rm.coe.int/guide-financial-gain/16807bfc9a)',
     'Legal authority: The Council of Europe Convention against Trafficking in Human Organs (CETS 216) criminalizes illicit removal of organs from living or deceased donors where consent is absent or a financial gain/comparable advantage is offered, plus illicit transplantation and use of such organs. WHO Guiding Principles (WHA63.22, 2010) require free, informed, uncoerced donation and prohibit payment for cells, tissues, or organs (allocation by clinical criteria only). Recognition (human-trafficking nexus): inducements to vulnerable donors, transplant tourism, brokered matches, and altered consent records. Remedy: clinicians must not participate where organs were obtained by exploitation, coercion, or payment; reimbursement of verifiable donor expenses remains lawful, keeping donation financially neutral.'),
    ('health_clinical_trial_integrity',
     'Clinical-Trial Integrity and Data Fabrication (ICH GCP, FDA)',
     'ICH E6(R3) Guideline for Good Clinical Practice (2025), database.ich.org (https://database.ich.org/sites/default/files/ICH_E6(R3)_Step4_FinalGuideline_2025_0106.pdf)',
     'Legal/standards authority: ICH E6(R3) Good Clinical Practice (Step 4, 2025), adopted by FDA, requires data integrity from collection through analysis, with a dedicated data-governance section and quality-by-design to protect factors critical to trial reliability. Recognition red flags from FDA/EMA clinical-investigator inspections: fabricated or altered source data, missing essential records (informed consent, case report forms, monitoring and serious-adverse-event reports), protocol non-adherence, and data inconsistencies or monitoring lapses; data-integrity citations have risen in recent inspection cycles. Remedy: sponsors and investigators must keep essential records inspection-ready; deficiencies trigger FDA inspectional findings, data rejection, and potential disqualification of the investigator.'),
    ('health_controlled_substance_diversion',
     'Controlled-Substance Diversion Recognition (Pill Mills, Opioids)',
     'Controlled Substances Act and DEA corresponding-responsibility doctrine (21 C.F.R. 1306.04); DEA Diversion Control Division (https://www.deadiversion.usdoj.gov/pharmacy.html)',
     "Legal authority: The Controlled Substances Act and DEA regulation (21 C.F.R. 1306.04) impose a corresponding responsibility on pharmacists to dispense controlled substances only for a legitimate medical purpose, alongside the prescriber's duty. A 'pill mill' is a prescriber or pharmacy dispensing large volumes of medically unnecessary controlled substances. Recognition red flags: cash payment for high-dose opioids when insurance is on file, the opioid+benzodiazepine+carisoprodol 'trinity' cocktail, multiple prescribers in the PDMP, out-of-area prescribers, early refills with escalating doses, and altered or inconsistent prescriptions. Remedy: pharmacists must resolve red flags before dispensing; unresolved flags can prove CSA and False Claims Act violations, exposing prescribers and pharmacies to civil, criminal, and licensure consequences."),
    ('health_medical_identity_theft',
     'Medical Identity Theft and Patient-Data Fraud (HIPAA)',
     'HIPAA Privacy Rule, 45 C.F.R. Part 160 and Part 164 Subparts A & E; HHS Summary of the HIPAA Privacy Rule (https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/laws-regulations/index.html)',
     'Legal authority: The HIPAA Privacy Rule (45 C.F.R. Parts 160 and 164) sets national standards protecting individually identifiable health information held by covered entities and business associates, limiting use and disclosure to the minimum necessary and granting patients access rights. Recognition: medical identity theft surfaces when a patient sees services, charges, or diagnoses on their record that they never received, signaling a third party used their protected health information for fraudulent claims; breaches that expose names, SSNs, dates of birth, and insurance data enable downstream Medicare fraud. Remedy: patients may exercise access rights to detect misuse; HHS Office for Civil Rights enforces via corrective action and civil monetary penalties, and breaches trigger Breach Notification Rule reporting.'),
    ('health_patient_safety',
     'Patient Safety, Never Events and Adverse-Event Reporting (WHO)',
     'WHO Global Patient Safety Action Plan 2021-2030; WHO Patient Safety Fact Sheet (https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/patient-safety)',
     'Policy authority: WHO frames patient safety as the absence of preventable harm in health care; about 1 in 10 patients is harmed, over 3 million deaths occur annually from unsafe care, and more than 50% of harm is preventable (half medication-related). Recognition of adverse and never events: medication errors, unsafe surgery and wrong-site procedures, health-care-associated infections, diagnostic errors, patient falls, pressure ulcers, patient misidentification, unsafe transfusions, and unsafe injections. WHO favors a systems approach over blame, attributing most errors to process and design failures. Remedy: incident-reporting systems for learning, the Global Patient Safety Action Plan 2021-2030, and World Patient Safety Day (17 September) drive disclosure, investigation, and prevention.'),
    ('labour_ilo_c087_freedom_association',
     'ILO C087: Freedom of Association and Protection of the Right to Organise (1948)',
     'ILO Convention No. 87 (1948), text via Refworld/ILO (refworld.org/legal/agreements/ilo/1948/en/36193, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'ILO Convention No. 87, adopted 9 July 1948 (in force 4 July 1950), is a fundamental convention. Article 2 grants workers and employers, without distinction, the right to establish and join organisations of their own choosing without previous authorisation. Article 3 protects the right to draw up constitutions, elect representatives in full freedom, and organise activities; public authorities must refrain from interference, and organisations may not be dissolved or suspended by administrative authority. Articles 4-5 protect federations and acquisition of legal personality. A violation is recognised where the state requires prior approval to form a union, bans or dissolves unions administratively, or interferes in union elections. Remedy runs through ILO supervisory bodies (the Committee on Freedom of Association and the CEACR) and national courts ordering reinstatement of association rights.'),
    ('labour_ilo_c098_collective_bargaining',
     'ILO C098: Right to Organise and Collective Bargaining (1949)',
     'ILO Convention No. 98 (1949), text via Refworld/ILO (refworld.org/legal/agreements/ilo/1949/en/30766, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "ILO Convention No. 98, adopted 1 July 1949 (in force 18 July 1951), is a fundamental convention. Article 1 requires adequate protection against anti-union discrimination in employment: it is unlawful to condition hiring on not joining a union, to require relinquishing membership, or to dismiss or prejudice a worker for union membership or participation in union activities. Article 2 protects workers' and employers' organisations against acts of mutual interference, including employer-dominated unions. Article 4 obliges states to promote and encourage voluntary collective bargaining to regulate terms and conditions of employment. A violation is recognised where workers are fired or blacklisted for organising, where bargaining is refused in bad faith, or where an employer funds a sham union. Remedy includes reinstatement, back pay, and ILO supervisory comments."),
    ('labour_ilo_c155_osh',
     'ILO C155 Occupational Safety and Health (1981) and C187 Promotional Framework (2006)',
     'ILO Conventions No. 155 (1981) and No. 187 (2006), summary via ILO (ilo.org/resource/other/occupational-safety-and-health-convention-no-155-fundamental-convention, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'ILO Convention No. 155, adopted 1981 (in force 1983) and now a fundamental convention, requires ratifying states to formulate, implement and periodically review a coherent national policy on occupational safety and health and the working environment. Employers must ensure that workplaces, machinery, equipment and processes under their control are safe and without risk to health, provide protective clothing where needed, give information and training, and arrange for workers to participate in safety measures (Articles 16-19). Workers who remove themselves from imminent and serious danger must be protected from undue consequences. Convention No. 187 (2006) adds a promotional framework: a national OSH policy, system and programme for continuous improvement. A violation is recognised where employers ignore known hazards, withhold protective equipment, or punish workers for reporting danger; remedy runs through labour inspection, penalties, and the ILO supervisory system.'),
    ('labour_ilo_c095_wage_protection',
     'ILO C095: Protection of Wages (1949)',
     'ILO Convention No. 95 (1949), text via ILO/Refworld and UMN HR Library (hrlibrary.umn.edu/instree/ilo95.html; refworld.org/legal/agreements/ilo/1949/en/122505, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "ILO Convention No. 95, adopted 1949 (in force 24 September 1952), protects how wages are paid. Wages in money must be paid in legal tender (Article 3); payment in promissory notes, vouchers or scrip representing legal tender is prohibited. Partial payment in kind may be authorised only for the worker's benefit, must be appropriate, and may not take the form of liquor or noxious drugs (Article 4). Wages must be paid directly to the worker, regularly, and at agreed intervals (Articles 5, 6, 12). Deductions are permitted only under conditions set by law, collective agreement or arbitration, and workers must be informed of them (Article 8). Critically, Article 9 prohibits any deduction to obtain or retain employment paid to an employer, representative or intermediary such as a recruiter. A violation is recognised in unlawful deductions, in-kind wage abuse, withheld or delayed pay, and recruitment-fee deductions; remedy is through labour inspection, recovery of unpaid wages, and ILO supervision."),
    ('labour_ilo_c131_minimum_wage',
     'ILO C131: Minimum Wage Fixing (1970)',
     'ILO Convention No. 131 (1970), summary via ILO (ilo.org/resource/c131-minimum-wage-fixing-convention-1970; refworld.org/legal/agreements/ilo/1970/en/122496, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "ILO Convention No. 131, adopted 22 June 1970 (in force 29 April 1972), protects wage earners against unduly low wages. Ratifying states must establish and maintain a system of minimum wages covering all groups of wage earners whose conditions make coverage appropriate, in consultation with employers' and workers' organisations (Articles 1-2). Minimum wages set under the Convention have the force of law and may not be lowered; failure to apply them exposes the employer to penalties (Article 2). In determining minimum-wage levels, states must consider the needs of workers and their families, the cost of living, social-security benefits, relative living standards, and economic factors such as productivity and employment (Article 3). States must create machinery to fix and adjust minimum wages and to enforce them through inspection. A violation is recognised where workers are paid below the legally fixed minimum or where excluded groups are denied coverage; remedy is recovery of the shortfall plus statutory penalties."),
    ('labour_ilo_working_time',
     'ILO Working-Time Limits: C001 Hours of Work (1919) and C014 Weekly Rest (1921)',
     'ILO Conventions No. 1 (1919) and No. 14 (1921), text via UMN HR Library and ILO NORMLEX (hrlibrary.umn.edu/business/ilo1.html; ilo.org/dyn/normlex, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'ILO Convention No. 1 (Hours of Work, Industry), adopted 1919 (in force 1921), was the first ILO convention and limits hours in industrial undertakings to eight in the day and forty-eight in the week, with narrowly defined exceptions for shift work, emergencies and averaging arrangements; overtime must be paid at a premium rate. ILO Convention No. 14 (Weekly Rest, Industry), adopted 1921, entitles every worker in an industrial undertaking to a rest period of at least twenty-four consecutive hours in every seven-day period, wherever possible fixed to coincide with established local custom. Together they recognise excessive hours and denial of weekly rest as labour-rights violations. A violation is recognised where workers are routinely required to work beyond statutory daily or weekly caps without premium pay, or are denied a weekly rest day; remedy is through labour inspection, payment of owed overtime, and corrective orders, with oversight by the ILO supervisory bodies.'),
    ('labour_ilo_child_labour',
     'ILO C138 Minimum Age (1973) and C182 Worst Forms of Child Labour (1999)',
     'ILO Conventions No. 138 (1973) and No. 182 (1999), summary via ILO IPEC and OAS (ilo.org/sites/default/files/2024-04/C138_at_a_glance_EN.pdf; oas.org/dil/C182_Worst_Forms_of_Child_Labour_Convention_1999.pdf, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'ILO Convention No. 138 (Minimum Age, 1973) requires ratifying states to pursue a national policy to abolish child labour and to set a minimum age for employment not less than the age of compulsory schooling and in any case not below 15 (14 transitionally for less-developed economies). Hazardous work likely to harm health, safety or morals is restricted to those 18 and over; light non-harmful work may be allowed from 13-15. Convention No. 182 (Worst Forms of Child Labour, 1999) requires immediate and effective measures to prohibit and eliminate the worst forms for anyone under 18: hazardous work, the use of children in illicit activities, and (overlapping with trafficking instruments) slavery-like practices and armed conflict. Beyond trafficking, a violation is recognised wherever under-age children are employed below the lawful minimum, or where adolescents perform prohibited hazardous tasks. Remedy includes removal of the child, withdrawal-and-rehabilitation programmes, penalties for employers, and ILO supervision; C182 has achieved universal ratification.'),
    ('labour_us_flsa_wage_theft',
     'US Fair Labor Standards Act: Minimum Wage, Overtime, and Wage Theft',
     'Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938, 29 U.S.C. 201 et seq., via US DOL Wage and Hour Division (dol.gov/agencies/whd/flsa, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 (29 U.S.C. 201-219) sets US minimum wage, overtime, recordkeeping and youth-employment standards for the private sector and government employers. Covered non-exempt workers must receive at least the federal minimum wage (currently 7.25 USD/hour, or the higher applicable state minimum) and overtime at one and one-half times the regular rate for hours worked over 40 in a workweek. Employers must keep accurate records of hours and pay. Wage theft - working off the clock, sub-minimum pay, denied overtime, stolen tips, or deliberate employee misclassification - is recognised as an FLSA violation. The DOL Wage and Hour Division investigates and may recover back wages and liquidated damages; willful or repeated violations carry civil penalties up to 1,000 USD per violation, and willful violations can bring criminal fines up to 10,000 USD and imprisonment on a second conviction. Workers may also sue to recover unpaid wages, and retaliation for asserting FLSA rights is prohibited.'),
    ('labour_us_osha',
     'US OSHA: Employer Duties, Recognized Hazards, and Anti-Retaliation',
     'Occupational Safety and Health Act of 1970, Sections 5 and 11(c), via OSHA (osha.gov/laws-regs/oshact/section5-duties; whistleblowers.gov/statutes/oshact, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "The Occupational Safety and Health Act of 1970 establishes US workplace safety duties enforced by OSHA. Section 5(a)(1), the General Duty Clause, requires each employer to furnish a place of employment free from recognized hazards likely to cause death or serious physical harm; Section 5(a)(2) requires compliance with specific OSHA standards. A General Duty Clause violation is proven where a recognized hazard exists, the employer or industry recognized it, it was likely to cause serious harm, and a feasible means to abate it existed. Workers have the right to report unsafe conditions and request inspections. Section 11(c) prohibits retaliation against employees who exercise these rights; a worker must complain to OSHA within 30 days, after which OSHA investigates and can order reinstatement, back pay and restored benefits, or sue the employer. Remedy for unsafe conditions includes citations, abatement orders, and monetary penalties; OSHA's Whistleblower Protection Program enforces the anti-retaliation provisions."),
    ('labour_eu_working_time_directive',
     'EU Working Time Directive 2003/88/EC: Maximum Hours, Rest, and Paid Leave',
     'Directive 2003/88/EC of the European Parliament and of the Council, via EUR-Lex (eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32003L0088, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "Directive 2003/88/EC sets minimum health-and-safety requirements for the organisation of working time across the EU. Average weekly working time, including overtime, may not exceed 48 hours over a reference period of up to four months (extendable to six, or twelve by collective agreement). Every worker is entitled to a minimum daily rest of 11 consecutive hours per 24-hour period, an uninterrupted weekly rest of 24 hours plus the daily rest, a rest break when the working day exceeds six hours, and at least four weeks of paid annual leave that may not be replaced by payment except on termination. Night work is additionally limited. An individual opt-out from the 48-hour limit is permitted only with the worker's express, freely given consent and protection from detriment. A violation is recognised where employers impose excessive average hours, deny mandated rest or paid leave, or coerce an opt-out. Remedy is through national enforcement authorities and courts, which can order compliance, compensation for lost leave, and penalties."),
    ('labour_supply_chain_due_diligence',
     'Supply-Chain Due Diligence: German LkSG and the EU CSDDD',
     'German Supply Chain Due Diligence Act (LkSG, 2023) and EU Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive (Directive 2024/1760), via CSR Germany and legal analyses (csr-in-deutschland.de/EN/Legislation/German-Supply-Chain-Act/german-supply-chain-act.html, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The German Act on Corporate Due Diligence Obligations in Supply Chains (LkSG), in force 1 January 2023, requires large enterprises (initially 3,000+ employees in Germany, then 1,000+ from 2024) to perform human-rights and environmental due diligence across their own operations, direct suppliers and, on substantiated knowledge, indirect suppliers. Duties include a risk-management system, risk analysis, preventive and remedial measures, a complaints mechanism, and public reporting, anchored in eleven recognised human-rights instruments and the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights. The EU Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive (CSDDD, Directive 2024/1760) extends similar obligations EU-wide, requiring in-scope companies to identify, prevent, mitigate, end and remedy adverse human-rights and environmental impacts in their chains of activities, with civil liability and supervisory enforcement. A violation is recognised where a covered company fails to assess or act on labour-rights risks such as forced or child labour; remedy includes administrative penalties, corrective action, and access to grievance mechanisms. (Scope and timelines remain subject to ongoing EU and German legislative revision.)'),
    ('labour_uk_msa_s54_transparency',
     'UK Modern Slavery Act 2015 s.54: Transparency in Supply Chains',
     'Modern Slavery Act 2015, section 54, and the Transparency in Supply Chains Regulations 2015, via legislation.gov.uk (legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2015/30/section/54, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Section 54 of the UK Modern Slavery Act 2015 imposes a transparency-in-supply-chains duty. A commercial organisation that supplies goods or services and carries on business in the UK with a total annual turnover of at least 36 million GBP (turnover of the organisation plus its subsidiaries, including those wholly outside the UK) must prepare a slavery and human trafficking statement for each financial year. The statement sets out the steps taken to ensure slavery and human trafficking are not occurring in its business or supply chains, or states that no such steps were taken. It must be approved by the board and signed by a director, and, where the organisation has a website, published there with a prominent homepage link; statutory guidance expects publication within six months of the financial year-end. The provision is disclosure-based: a violation is recognised where a qualifying organisation fails to publish or approve a compliant statement. Enforcement is by the Secretary of State seeking an injunction, alongside reputational and investor pressure; later reforms have proposed strengthening mandatory content and penalties.'),
    ('tax_oecd_crs',
     'OECD Common Reporting Standard (CRS): Automatic Exchange of Financial Account Information',
     'OECD Standard for Automatic Exchange of Financial Account Information in Tax Matters / Common Reporting Standard (oecd.org/tax/automatic-exchange/common-reporting-standard, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "The CRS, approved by the OECD Council on 15 July 2014 at the G20's request, requires jurisdictions to obtain financial account information from their financial institutions and automatically exchange it with other jurisdictions annually. It covers bank deposits, custodial assets, life insurance, and investment income, with common due-diligence rules to identify reportable account holders. Over 100 jurisdictions participate; offshore disclosure programmes have raised over EUR 135 billion in tax, interest and penalties. Recognition relevance: undisclosed offshore accounts, nominee holders masking the beneficial owner, and assets shifted to non-CRS jurisdictions are red flags. CRS data supports detection and recovery of evaded tax."),
    ('tax_fatca',
     'US FATCA: Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act Reporting Obligations',
     'Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act (FATCA), enacted under the HIRE Act, U.S. IRS (irs.gov/businesses/corporations/foreign-account-tax-compliance-act-fatca, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'FATCA requires foreign financial institutions (banks, brokers, investment entities, certain insurers) to report to the IRS on accounts held by U.S. taxpayers, or by foreign entities in which U.S. persons hold substantial ownership, or face 30% withholding on certain U.S.-source payments. Registered FFIs receive a Global Intermediary Identification Number (GIIN). U.S. taxpayers report specified foreign assets on Form 8938; failure carries a USD 10,000 penalty (up to USD 50,000 after notice) plus a 40% understatement penalty on undisclosed-asset underpayments. Recognition relevance: concealed U.S.-person ownership behind foreign entities, unreported foreign accounts, and structuring to stay below thresholds signal evasion. FATCA is a legal-authority basis for enforcement.'),
    ('tax_beps_pillar_two',
     'OECD BEPS Pillar Two: 15% Global Minimum Tax (GloBE Rules)',
     'OECD/G20 Global Anti-Base Erosion (GloBE) Model Rules, Pillar Two (oecd.org/en/topics/sub-issues/global-minimum-tax/global-anti-base-erosion-model-rules-pillar-two.html, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'Pillar Two of the OECD/G20 BEPS Project, joined by over 135 jurisdictions in October 2021, sets a 15% minimum effective tax rate for multinational groups with revenue above EUR 750 million. The GloBE Rules impose a top-up tax when the jurisdictional effective tax rate falls below 15%, applied through the Income Inclusion Rule and UTPR, starting from 2024. A Substance-Based Income Exclusion carves out routine returns on real assets and payroll. Recognition relevance: profit booked in low-tax jurisdictions disproportionate to real economic activity, and structures engineered purely to depress effective tax rates, are base-erosion red flags. Pillar Two is the legal-authority counter to profit-shifting, narrowing incentives to relocate paper profits.'),
    ('tax_eu_dac6',
     'EU DAC6: Mandatory Disclosure of Reportable Cross-Border Arrangements',
     'Council Directive (EU) 2018/822 (DAC6), European Commission Taxation and Customs Union (taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/taxation/tax-transparency-cooperation/.../dac6_en, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'DAC6 (Council Directive (EU) 2018/822) requires EU intermediaries, or in some cases taxpayers, to disclose potentially aggressive cross-border arrangements to tax authorities within 30 days, with automatic exchange among Member States. Arrangements are reportable if they meet hallmarks in five categories (A-E): confidentiality or contingent-fee features, standardised structures, loss-buying or income conversion, cross-border deduction/relief mismatches, transfer-pricing safe harbours, and arrangements that undermine beneficial-ownership or CRS reporting. Categories A, B and parts of C also require a main benefit test. By November 2024 over 60,000 arrangements were filed to the DAC6 Central Directory. Recognition relevance: hallmark features serve as an early-alert taxonomy for abusive planning.'),
    ('tax_trade_misinvoicing_iff',
     'Trade Misinvoicing and Illicit Financial Flows (SDG Indicator 16.4.1)',
     'UN SDG Indicator 16.4.1 metadata (UNODC/UNCTAD custodians) and Global Financial Integrity methodology (unstats.un.org/sdgs/metadata/files/Metadata-16-04-01.pdf; gfintegrity.org/reports/methodology, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "SDG Indicator 16.4.1 measures the total value of inward and outward illicit financial flows, defined as flows illicit in origin, transfer or use that cross borders, including tax evasion and aggressive tax avoidance. Trade misinvoicing, a major channel, moves value by falsifying import/export prices, quantities or tariff classifications. GFI's partner-country (mirror-trade) method compares what two trading partners report for the same flow and flags persistent value gaps as misinvoicing risk; UNCTAD's Partner Country and Price Filter methods are analogous. Recognition relevance: under- or over-reported prices, multiple invoicing, and quantity or classification mismatches are red flags. These estimates support recovery of lost domestic revenue."),
    ('tax_shell_company_opacity',
     'Shell Companies and Beneficial-Ownership Opacity in Tax Evasion',
     'FATF Recommendations 24 and 25 on Beneficial Ownership (fatf-gafi.org/en/topics/beneficial-ownership.html, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'FATF set the first global beneficial-ownership standard in 2003 and strengthened Recommendation 24 (legal persons) in March 2022 and Recommendation 25 (legal arrangements/trusts) in February 2023, requiring competent authorities to obtain adequate, accurate and up-to-date information on the true owners of companies. Anonymous shell companies, inactive corporations, bearer shares and nominee shareholders obscure who ultimately controls assets, enabling tax evasion alongside corruption and laundering. Recognition relevance: ownership chains terminating in opaque jurisdictions, nominee directors, and entities with no employees, premises or genuine activity are red flags. Central beneficial-ownership registers with verification underpin enforcement, bridging tax and anti-corruption work.'),
    ('tax_offshore_secrecy',
     'Offshore Secrecy-Jurisdiction Typologies: Lessons from Panama and Pandora Papers',
     'ICIJ Pandora Papers and Panama Papers investigations (icij.org/investigations/pandora-papers; icij.org/investigations/panama-papers, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The ICIJ Pandora Papers (2.94 TB) exposed over 27,000 companies and 29,000 ultimate beneficial owners, linking offshore structures to 35 current and former leaders and over 330 public officials across 91 countries; the earlier Panama Papers exposed the Mossack Fonseca firm. Entities were registered in secrecy jurisdictions such as the British Virgin Islands, Panama, Seychelles, Singapore and Hong Kong, with more than two-thirds of politician-linked companies set up in the BVI. The system lets wealth and assets sit in paper-only companies that provide secrecy from authorities and creditors. Recognition relevance: layered offshore holdings, professional enablers creating thousands of shells, and assets held only to hide ownership are typologies for grounding tax-evasion judgements.'),
    ('tax_vat_carousel_fraud',
     'EU VAT Carousel / Missing-Trader Intra-Community (MTIC) Fraud Recognition',
     'European Commission, VAT Carousel Fraud, Taxation and Customs Union (taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/taxation/vat/fight-against-vat-fraud/vat-carousel-fraud_en, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "MTIC (carousel) fraud exploits the zero-rating of cross-border B2B supplies in the EU: a trader acquires goods VAT-free from another Member State, sells them domestically with VAT charged, then disappears without remitting the collected VAT (the 'missing trader'). 'Buffer' traders are inserted to distance the missing trader from detection, and the same goods may circulate in a loop, re-entering the chain repeatedly. An EU Parliament study identified MTIC/carousel fraud as the most damaging cross-border VAT fraud, with losses on the order of tens of billions of euros annually. Recognition relevance: rapid resale of high-value low-bulk goods, circular trade chains, defaulting traders, and unremitted output VAT are red flags. E-invoicing reforms aim to enable real-time detection."),
    ('tax_transfer_pricing',
     "Transfer-Pricing Manipulation: Abuse of the Arm's-Length Principle",
     'OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises and Tax Administrations (oecd.org/en/topics/transfer-pricing.html, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "The OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines codify the arm's-length principle: cross-border transactions between associated enterprises must be priced as if between independent parties on market terms. Manipulation reallocates taxable profit from high-tax to low-tax jurisdictions by mispricing intra-group sales, services, royalties or loans, or by strategically locating debt and intellectual property. The OECD recognises the principle has been misapplied to shift profits, especially for hard-to-value intangibles and high-risk transactions. Recognition relevance: intra-group prices departing from comparable market terms, profit booked where there is little substance, and outsized related-party interest or royalty payments are red flags. Country-by-Country Reporting and arm's-length adjustments support enforcement and revenue recovery."),
    ('tax_envelope_wages',
     'Envelope/Cash Wages and Payroll-Tax Evasion',
     'European Labour Authority glossary on Envelope Wages and European Platform tackling undeclared work (ela.europa.eu/en/glossary/envelope-wages; ela.europa.eu/en/undeclared-work, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "An envelope wage is undeclared cash paid by a formal employer to a formal employee on top of an officially declared salary to reduce tax and social-security contributions. It is widespread in transition and post-transition labour markets and in sectors such as construction, cleaning, childcare and hospitality. The practice distorts competition against compliant firms, drains public revenue, and erodes workers' social-protection entitlements, pension accrual and access to credit. Enforcement involves labour inspectorates, social-security inspectorates and tax authorities. Recognition relevance: declared pay far below the going rate, cash top-ups, and mismatches between hours worked and reported wages are red flags, bridging tax evasion and labour-rights harm to the worker."),
    ('tax_fatf_predicate_offence',
     'FATF: Tax Crimes as a Money-Laundering Predicate Offence',
     'FATF 40 Recommendations / International Standards on Combating Money Laundering and the Financing of Terrorism, 2012 (fatf-gafi.org/content/dam/fatf-gafi/recommendations/FATF Recommendations 2012.pdf, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     "The 2012 revision of the FATF Recommendations added serious tax crimes, covering both direct and indirect taxes, to the designated categories of predicate offences for money laundering. As a result, financial institutions must detect and report suspected tax evaders among their clients, and proceeds of tax crime can be prosecuted as laundering. Each country defines which tax offences are 'serious' enough to qualify. The designated-offence list also spans corruption, fraud, trafficking and smuggling. Recognition relevance: funds whose origin traces to concealed taxable income, combined with layering or integration into legitimate assets, are red flags. Predicate-offence status is the legal hook linking tax evasion to AML enforcement, freezing and confiscation."),
    ('tax_enforcement_recovery',
     'Enforcement and Asset Recovery: Exchange of Information and StAR',
     'OECD Multilateral Convention on Mutual Administrative Assistance in Tax Matters and World Bank/UNODC Stolen Asset Recovery (StAR) Initiative (oecd.org/en/topics/convention-on-mutual-administrative-assistance-in-tax-matters.html; star.worldbank.org/about-star, fetched 2026-06-05)',
     'The OECD/Council of Europe Multilateral Convention on Mutual Administrative Assistance in Tax Matters (1988, amended 2010), with over 150 participating jurisdictions, provides all forms of cooperation: exchange of information on request (EOIR) of foreseeably relevant data, automatic exchange, assistance in tax collection, joint audits and service of documents. The StAR Initiative, launched in 2007 by the World Bank and UNODC, supports asset tracing, mutual legal assistance and return of stolen assets under UNCAC Chapter V; an estimated USD 20-40 billion is stolen by officials yearly. Recognition relevance: requests for owner identity, foreseeably relevant records, and cross-border tracing operationalise recovery. These instruments are the legal-authority backbone for returning evaded and stolen funds.'),
    ('arms_att',
     'UN Arms Trade Treaty (ATT) - Article 6 Prohibitions and Article 7 Export Risk Assessment',
     'United Nations Arms Trade Treaty (ATT, 2013), Articles 6, 7 and 11 - https://www.thearmstradetreaty.org/hyper-images/file/TheArmsTradeTreaty1/TheArmsTradeTreaty.pdf',
     'The ATT sets common standards for transfers of conventional arms (tanks, armoured vehicles, artillery, combat aircraft, attack helicopters, warships, missiles, and small arms and light weapons) plus ammunition, parts and components. Article 6 bars any transfer that would violate UN Security Council Chapter VII measures, in particular arms embargoes; violate other relevant treaty obligations; or where the State knows the arms would be used in genocide, crimes against humanity, grave breaches of the 1949 Geneva Conventions, attacks on civilians, or other war crimes. If not prohibited, Article 7 requires the exporting State to assess whether the arms would undermine peace and security or facilitate serious violations of international humanitarian or human rights law, terrorism, or transnational organized crime; if an overriding risk remains after mitigation, export must be denied. Article 11 obliges parties to assess and prevent diversion.'),
    ('arms_wassenaar',
     'Wassenaar Arrangement - Dual-Use and Conventional-Arms Export Control Lists',
     'The Wassenaar Arrangement on Export Controls for Conventional Arms and Dual-Use Goods and Technologies - https://www.wassenaar.org/about-us/',
     'The Wassenaar Arrangement is a multilateral export-control regime of 42 participating states that promotes transparency and responsibility in transfers of conventional arms and dual-use goods to prevent destabilizing accumulations and acquisition by terrorists. It maintains two control lists that members implement through national law: the Munitions List (military-designed equipment, including small arms and light weapons, tanks, armoured vehicles, aircraft, and unmanned systems) and the List of Dual-Use Goods and Technologies, organized into nine categories (special materials, electronics, computers, telecommunications, information security, sensors, navigation, marine, and aerospace) with Sensitive and Very Sensitive sub-lists. Members exchange transfer information, report transfers and denials to non-members semi-annually, and decide by consensus. The lists are the technical baseline national authorities use to recognize controlled items requiring an export license.'),
    ('arms_eu_dual_use',
     'EU Dual-Use Regulation 2021/821 - Authorization Requirement and Catch-All Clause',
     'Regulation (EU) 2021/821 setting up a Union regime for the control of exports of dual-use items - https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32021R0821',
     'Regulation (EU) 2021/821 is the Union framework controlling exports, brokering, technical assistance, transit, and certain transfers of dual-use items (goods, software, and technology usable for both civil and military purposes, including for nuclear, chemical, or biological weapons or their delivery systems). Items listed in Annex I require an authorization, available as individual, global, national general, or Union general authorizations. The catch-all clause extends control to non-listed items when an exporter is informed by, or is aware, that the items are or may be intended for WMD-related use, for military end-use in an embargoed destination, or for unauthorized incorporation into military items; the exporter must then notify the competent authority. Exporters are expected to maintain Internal Compliance Programmes and conduct end-use and diversion-risk due diligence, including for cyber-surveillance items linked to repression. This is the EU legal-authority basis for recognizing controlled dual-use exports and unresolved diversion risk.'),
    ('arms_us_ear_itar',
     'US Export Controls - EAR (BIS) and ITAR (DDTC) Overview',
     'Export Administration Regulations (15 CFR 730-774, BIS) - https://www.bis.gov/regulations/ear ; International Traffic in Arms Regulations (22 CFR 120-130, DDTC) - https://www.state.gov/bureaus-offices/under-secretary-for-arms-control-and-international-security-affairs/bureau-of-political-military-affairs/directorate-of-defense-trade-controls-pm-ddtc',
     'The US runs two principal export-control regimes. The Export Administration Regulations (EAR, 15 CFR 730-774), administered by the Bureau of Industry and Security, control dual-use and less-sensitive military items on the Commerce Control List, which has ten categories and five product groups; each item carries an Export Control Classification Number, and license requirements turn on the item, destination, end-use, and end-user, with controls for WMD and military end-use and screening against the Entity List and other denied-party lists. The International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR, 22 CFR 120-130), administered by the State Department Directorate of Defense Trade Controls under the Arms Export Control Act, control defense articles and services on the US Munitions List and require manufacturers, exporters, and brokers to register and license. The distinction (dual-use under Commerce vs. defense articles under State) is central to recognizing the correct jurisdiction and authority for a transfer.'),
    ('arms_un_embargoes',
     'UN Arms Embargoes and Security Council Sanctions Regimes',
     'United Nations Security Council - Sanctions Information - https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/sanctions/information',
     'Acting under Chapter VII (Article 41) of the UN Charter, the Security Council can impose sanctions that do not involve armed force, including arms embargoes prohibiting the supply of arms and related materiel to listed actors or, in country-wide regimes, to a territory as a whole. Such resolutions are binding: all UN Member States are obliged to implement them. Since 1966 the Council has established more than thirty sanctions regimes; each is overseen by a Sanctions Committee (a subsidiary organ of all fifteen Council members) that maintains designation lists, considers exemptions, and monitors implementation, frequently assisted by Panels or Groups of Experts that investigate violations. An embargo is the highest legal-authority bar on a transfer; a transfer to an embargoed actor is prohibited outright and is a primary recognition trigger that any related dealing, brokering, or financing is illicit.'),
    ('arms_end_user_redflags',
     'End-User and End-Use Red Flags - Diversion Indicators and False End-User Certificates',
     "BIS EAR Supplement No. 3 to Part 732, 'Know Your Customer Guidance and Red Flags' - https://www.bis.gov/regulations/ear ; BIS compliance guidance - https://www.bis.gov",
     "Export-control authorities expect exporters to know their customer and to recognize warning signs of diversion before shipping. Classic red flags include: a customer or agent reluctant to give end-use information; a product whose capabilities do not fit the buyer's line of business; an item incompatible with the technical level of the destination; willingness to pay cash for a normally financed item; little or no business background; unfamiliarity with the product yet insistence on buying it; declined installation, training, or maintenance; vague delivery dates or out-of-the-way delivery points; a freight forwarder named as the final destination; abnormal shipping routes; packaging inconsistent with the stated destination; and evasiveness about whether the item is for domestic use, export, or re-export. False or inconsistent end-user certificates are a core indicator. The affirmative duty is decisive: if a red flag cannot be resolved, the exporter must not proceed and should seek guidance or deny the transaction."),
    ('arms_salw_firearms_protocol',
     'Illicit Small Arms and Light Weapons - UN Firearms Protocol and Programme of Action',
     'Protocol against the Illicit Manufacturing of and Trafficking in Firearms, Their Parts and Components and Ammunition (supplementing UNTOC, 2001) - https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/firearms-protocol/the-firearms-protocol.html',
     'The Firearms Protocol, supplementing the UN Convention against Transnational Organized Crime, is the only legally binding global instrument criminalizing illicit firearms activity. Adopted in 2001 and in force from 2005, it requires States to criminalize illicit manufacturing and trafficking in firearms, parts, components, and ammunition and the tampering with markings; to mark firearms (manufacturer, country, and year of import) and keep records for at least ten years for tracing; and to operate an import, export, and transit licensing or authorization system, with deactivation standards and regulation of brokers. It complements the politically binding UN Programme of Action on Small Arms and Light Weapons and the International Tracing Instrument, which support stockpile management, marking, and cooperative tracing. Together they provide the legal-authority and tracing framework for recognizing diverted, unmarked, or trafficked small arms.'),
    ('arms_brokering',
     'Brokering Controls and Recognition of Illicit Arms Brokering',
     "UNIDIR / Stimson Center, 'The Arms Trade Treaty: Regulating Brokering to Reduce the Risk of Diversion' (2025) - https://unidir.org/publication/the-arms-trade-treaty-regulating-brokering-to-reduce-the-risk-of-diversion/",
     "Brokering is the arranging or facilitating of an arms transfer between third parties, often without the goods entering the broker's own country. ATT Article 10 obliges States to regulate brokering under their jurisdiction, which may include requiring brokers to register or obtain written authorization before acting. Unregulated brokering is a leading enabler of diversion: unscrupulous brokers exploit 'third-country' deals to circumvent national controls and have supplied embargoed actors, non-state armed groups, criminals, and listed terrorist procurement networks. Recognition indicators include intermediaries who obscure the true end-user, layered or shell-company arrangements, routing finance and logistics through permissive jurisdictions, and transactions structured to fall between national licensing regimes. Roughly forty States have broker-specific laws, and effective controls extend to extraterritorial conduct by nationals. Gaps in brokering and transport-agent regulation remain a recognized pathway to UN embargo violations."),
    ('arms_sanctions_evasion',
     'Sanctions-Evasion Typologies - Front Companies, Transshipment, Ship-to-Ship Transfers',
     "FATF, 'Complex Proliferation Financing and Sanctions Evasion Schemes' - https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Financingofproliferation/complex-proliferation-financing-sanction-evasion-schemes.html",
     'Sanctioned actors deploy organized schemes designed to defeat name-based screening and jurisdiction-based controls. Recognized typologies include front and shell companies in permissive jurisdictions, nominee directors and opaque or layered ownership, transshipment of restricted goods (such as microelectronics and aerospace components) through third-country hubs for quiet re-export, falsified end-user or commodity-description documents, commodity substitution, and payments routed from third countries not named on end-user forms. In maritime trade, evaders spoof or disable AIS, conduct ship-to-ship transfers (often at night or in high-risk areas), and rapidly change flags or ownership to hide cargo origin and destination. Detection indicators include repeated AIS gaps, unexplained route deviations, rapid flag changes, recurring ship-to-ship activity in high-risk regions, and third-party payments. These patterns are the principal recognition signals that a transfer is structured to breach an embargo or targeted sanction.'),
    ('arms_proliferation_financing',
     'Proliferation Financing - FATF Recommendation 7 and Dual-Use Procurement Networks',
     'FATF Guidance on Counter Proliferation Financing (2018); FATF Recommendation 7 on targeted financial sanctions related to proliferation - https://www.fatf-gafi.org/content/dam/fatf-gafi/guidance/Guidance-Countering-Proliferation-Financing.pdf',
     'Proliferation financing is the provision of funds or financial services used for the manufacture, acquisition, development, export, transshipment, brokering, transport, transfer, stockpiling, or use of nuclear, chemical, or biological weapons and their delivery systems and related materials, including dual-use goods used for non-legitimate purposes. FATF Recommendation 7 requires countries to implement, without delay, the targeted financial sanctions called for by UN Security Council resolutions on proliferation, freezing the assets of designated persons and entities and prohibiting the making available of funds to them. Proliferators source components and technology through procurement networks and front companies and access the financial system through layered channels. Recognition indicators include transactions linked to designated parties or their fronts, dual-use goods routed to high-risk end-users, mismatched commodity and payment trails, and use of intermediaries to obscure the ultimate consignee. The legal-authority frame is the binding UNSC sanctions implemented via Recommendation 7.'),
    ('arms_transshipment_ftz',
     'Strategic-Goods Transshipment and Free-Trade-Zone Diversion',
     "FATF, 'Money Laundering Vulnerabilities of Free Trade Zones' - https://www.fatf-gafi.org/content/dam/fatf-gafi/reports/ML%20vulnerabilities%20of%20Free%20Trade%20Zones.pdf",
     'Free trade zones and transshipment hubs are recognized diversion risks for strategic and dual-use goods. FATF reports that thousands of FTZs benefit from relaxed oversight, minimal duties, and weak transparency: customs authorities often have little visibility into zone activity, goods are rarely inspected, and zone authorities may request little ownership information when registering firms. These conditions let illicit actors store, repackage, relabel, and re-export controlled items, breaking the paper trail between true origin and ultimate consignee and circumventing direct-export controls. Recognition indicators include controlled goods routed to or through an FTZ with no clear commercial logic, re-invoicing or re-labeling inside the zone, consignees that are zone-registered shells, and re-export toward embargoed or higher-risk destinations. The remedy frame emphasizes enhanced zone governance, beneficial-ownership disclosure, goods inspection, and integration of zones into national export-control and customs enforcement.'),
    ('arms_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and Remedy - Prosecutions, Designated-Party Screening, Voluntary Self-Disclosure',
     'Bureau of Industry and Security - Enforcement; DOJ/BIS/OFAC voluntary self-disclosure guidance - https://www.bis.gov/enforcement',
     "Export-control and sanctions violations carry layered civil and criminal consequences. Under the EAR, violations are subject to strict civil liability (lack of knowledge is not a defense), with civil penalties, loss of export privileges, and denial orders; criminal violations can bring fines up to roughly one million dollars per violation and up to twenty years' imprisonment, and criminal penalties can be imposed on top of civil ones. Designated-party screening against the Entity List, Specially Designated Nationals, and consolidated screening lists is the front-line preventive control. Voluntary self-disclosure of potential violations is a recognized mitigation: timely, comprehensive disclosure with full cooperation substantially reduces civil penalties, while failure to disclose is an aggravating factor. Recent enforcement settlements and prosecutions, including for illegal export of sensitive microelectronics and semiconductor equipment, illustrate the remedy path: screen, detect, disclose, remediate, and, where warranted, prosecute."),
    ('cyber_budapest_convention',
     'Budapest Convention on Cybercrime (ETS No. 185) - Scope and International Cooperation',
     'Council of Europe Convention on Cybercrime (Budapest Convention, ETS No. 185, 2001) - https://www.coe.int/en/web/cybercrime/the-budapest-convention',
     'The Budapest Convention is the first international treaty on cybercrime, opened for signature in Budapest on 23 November 2001 and in force from 1 July 2004. It harmonizes domestic offences across parties: illegal access, illegal interception, data and system interference, misuse of devices, computer-related forgery and fraud, child-exploitation content offences, and copyright infringement. It also sets procedural powers (expedited preservation, search and seizure of stored data, interception) and a fast international-cooperation regime including mutual legal assistance and extradition. Article 35 establishes a 24/7 contact-point network so each party can give immediate cross-border assistance and preserve electronic evidence. It is the principal legal-authority baseline for recognizing and prosecuting cyber offences across jurisdictions.'),
    ('cyber_us_cfaa',
     'US Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (18 U.S.C. 1030)',
     'Computer Fraud and Abuse Act, 18 U.S.C. 1030 - https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1030',
     'The Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (18 U.S.C. 1030) is the primary US federal anti-intrusion statute. It prohibits accessing a protected computer without authorization or exceeding authorized access to obtain information; computer espionage involving national-defense data; accessing nonpublic government systems; knowingly accessing to further fraud and obtain value; causing damage by transmitting code; trafficking in passwords or access credentials affecting interstate commerce; and extortion involving threats to damage a computer or expose data. A protected computer includes systems used by financial institutions or the US government, any computer used in or affecting interstate or foreign commerce, and federal voting systems. The statute supplies the legal-authority frame for recognizing unauthorized-access, credential-trafficking, and computer-extortion conduct.'),
    ('cyber_eu_nis2',
     'EU NIS2 Directive - Critical-Infrastructure Cybersecurity Duties',
     'Directive (EU) 2022/2555 (NIS2 Directive) - https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2022/2555/oj',
     'NIS2 (Directive (EU) 2022/2555) sets a high common level of cybersecurity across the Union and replaces the 2016 NIS Directive. It classifies covered organizations as essential or important entities across critical sectors such as energy, transport, water, health, banking, digital infrastructure, cloud services, public administration, and space. Entities must adopt proportionate, all-hazards risk-management measures: risk analysis, incident handling, business continuity and backups, supply-chain security, access control, and cyber hygiene. Significant incidents trigger a two-stage report to the CSIRT or competent authority: an early warning within 24 hours and a fuller notification within 72 hours. National authorities and CSIRTs, ENISA, and the Cooperation Group supervise and enforce. It is the legal-authority baseline for recognizing infrastructure-cybersecurity obligations in the EU.'),
    ('cyber_gdpr_breach_notification',
     'GDPR Personal-Data-Breach Notification (Articles 33 and 34)',
     'Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR) Article 33, Notification of a personal data breach to the supervisory authority - https://gdpr-info.eu/art-33-gdpr/',
     "GDPR Article 33 requires a controller to notify the competent supervisory authority of a personal-data breach without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of becoming aware, unless the breach is unlikely to risk individuals' rights and freedoms; late notifications must explain the delay. The notification must describe the breach nature, the categories and approximate number of affected data subjects and records, a contact point such as the data protection officer, the likely consequences, and the measures taken or proposed. Processors must inform controllers without undue delay. Controllers must document all breaches. Article 34 additionally requires direct communication to affected individuals when a breach is likely to result in high risk. These provisions are the legal-authority baseline for recognizing and reporting data-breach obligations."),
    ('cyber_ransomware_recognition',
     'Ransomware Recognition and Response Posture (CISA #StopRansomware)',
     'CISA #StopRansomware Guide (joint CISA/FBI/NSA/MS-ISAC, Oct 2023) - https://www.cisa.gov/stopransomware/ransomware-guide',
     "CISA's #StopRansomware Guide describes ransomware as malware that encrypts files and the systems relying on them, after which actors demand payment for decryption; many variants also locate and delete or encrypt reachable backups. The recommended defensive posture is preparation, not payment: maintain tested offline backups, keep an incident response plan, patch known-exploited vulnerabilities, enforce multi-factor authentication, segment networks, and train users against social engineering. CISA, the FBI, and partner agencies discourage paying ransom because payment does not guarantee recovery and may embolden further attacks. Recognizing an incident should prompt isolation of affected systems, evidence preservation, and reporting to the FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3) portal or CISA's operations channel. Framing here is defensive and recognition-only."),
    ('cyber_eu_dsa_platform_duties',
     'EU Digital Services Act - Platform Illegal-Content and Transparency Duties',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act) - https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2065/oj',
     'The Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065) creates harmonized rules for a safe, predictable, and trustworthy online environment. Hosting services and online platforms must operate notice-and-action mechanisms so users can flag illegal content and must act on sufficiently substantiated notices, giving affected users a statement of reasons and redress options. Platforms grant priority handling to vetted trusted flaggers (law-enforcement units and specialist NGOs), publish transparency reports on content moderation, and may not use deceptive dark-pattern designs. Very large online platforms and search engines carry added due-diligence duties, including annual systemic-risk assessments, mitigation, and independent audits. The DSA is the legal-authority baseline for online-platform illegal-content and transparency obligations in the EU.'),
    ('cyber_uk_online_safety_act',
     'UK Online Safety Act 2023 - Duty of Care and Illegal-Content Duties',
     'Online Safety Act 2023 (c. 50), UK Government explainer - https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/online-safety-act-explainer/online-safety-act-explainer',
     'The Online Safety Act 2023 places a duty of care on user-to-user and search services with links to the UK, regulated by Ofcom. Providers must assess the risk of users encountering illegal content and, where children are likely to access the service, content harmful to children, and must implement proportionate systems and processes to detect, mitigate, and remove it. The Act defines priority illegal content categories including terrorism, child sexual exploitation and abuse (CSEA), fraud, and the sale of illegal drugs or weapons, plus material promoting suicide or self-harm. Child-protection duties include highly effective age assurance for the most harmful content. Ofcom enforces with powers to investigate, fine up to 10 percent of qualifying worldwide revenue, and in severe cases seek court-ordered service blocking. The Act is the UK legal-authority baseline for online-safety duties.'),
    ('cyber_ocse_recognition',
     'Online Child Sexual Exploitation - Recognition Signals and Grooming Red Flags (Defensive)',
     'National Center for Missing and Exploited Children (NCMEC), Online Enticement - https://www.missingkids.org/theissues/onlineenticement',
     'Online enticement, as defined by NCMEC, is an individual communicating with someone believed to be a child to commit a sexual offense or abduction, and it occurs across social media, messaging apps, and gaming platforms. For recognition only, common grooming red-flag categories that a parent, guardian, educator, or platform may observe include an adult building rapport through excessive flattery or shared interests, attempts to move conversation onto private or disappearing channels, requests for secrecy, offers of gifts or money, and an adult misrepresenting their age. A child becoming secretive about online activity can also be a warning sign. Suspected exploitation should be reported to the NCMEC CyberTipline reporting portal, which works with law enforcement to safeguard victims. This entry is strictly recognition and reporting and contains no instructional or operational detail.'),
    ('cyber_ocse_mandatory_reporting',
     'Mandatory Reporting of Online Child Exploitation (18 U.S.C. 2258A; NCMEC; INHOPE)',
     '18 U.S.C. 2258A (Reporting requirements of providers) - https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2258A',
     'Under 18 U.S.C. 2258A, electronic communication and remote computing service providers must report apparent child sexual abuse material to the NCMEC CyberTipline as soon as reasonably possible after obtaining actual knowledge; the statute also reaches apparent minor sex-trafficking and online enticement. Providers must preserve reported material and related data for one year. NCMEC operates as the centralized clearinghouse, forwarding reports to federal, state, local, and authorized foreign law enforcement. Internationally, INHOPE coordinates national internet hotlines that accept reports, including anonymous reports, from the public, exchange reports across borders, and work with NCMEC, law enforcement, and industry to remove content. The public should report through the NCMEC CyberTipline portal or their national INHOPE hotline portal, referenced by portal, not by phone number.'),
    ('cyber_bec_account_takeover',
     'Business Email Compromise and Account Takeover - Recognition (Computer-Intrusion Angle)',
     'FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3), Business Email Compromise - https://www.ic3.gov/CrimeInfo/BEC',
     'The FBI describes Business Email Compromise (BEC) as a scam targeting organizations and individuals that conduct fund transfers, frequently carried out by compromising legitimate email accounts through social engineering or computer intrusion, or by spoofing addresses. The account-takeover dimension distinguishes BEC from ordinary consumer fraud: once criminals access a mailbox, they can monitor correspondence and time a fraudulent request, such as a vendor invoice that changes payment instructions from check to wire or redirects funds to a new account. Recognition red flags include unexpected changes to payment or banking details, look-alike sender addresses with subtle character variations, urgency around a transfer, and requests to bypass normal verification. Out-of-band verification of any payment change is the recommended control. Victims should report to the FBI IC3 portal.'),
    ('cyber_data_breach_identity_theft',
     'Data-Breach and Identity-Theft Recognition and Remedy',
     'US Federal Trade Commission, IdentityTheft.gov - https://www.identitytheft.gov/',
     "The FTC's IdentityTheft.gov defines identity theft as the unlawful use of a person's personal information to commit fraud. Recognition signs include unfamiliar accounts opened in one's name, unexpected charges or withdrawals, bills for services never requested, and credit applications denied without explanation, which can follow a data breach exposing personal records. The FTC's recommended remedy steps are to report the theft at the IdentityTheft.gov portal to obtain an official report and recovery plan, place fraud alerts or credit freezes with the three nationwide credit bureaus to block new fraudulent accounts, review credit reports for unauthorized activity, and file reports with police, creditors, and financial institutions as needed. Acting quickly limits damage. This entry is recognition and remedy framing for breach-driven identity theft."),
    ('cyber_critical_infrastructure',
     'Critical-Infrastructure and Operational-Technology Threat Recognition (Defensive Posture)',
     'CISA, Industrial Control Systems topic and OT guidance - https://www.cisa.gov/topics/industrial-control-systems',
     'CISA describes industrial control systems (ICS) and operational technology (OT) as the systems that run physical processes in critical-infrastructure sectors such as energy, water, and manufacturing, where a cyber compromise can have physical and safety consequences. The recommended defensive posture centers on layered defense-in-depth: maintain a current asset inventory of hardware, firmware, and protocols; segment IT from OT using zones, industrial firewalls, and a DMZ to contain threats; apply least-privilege access and multi-factor authentication on remote and administrative logins; and monitor traffic to flag deviations or unauthorized access. CISA also points to zero-trust principles and supply-chain risk management for legacy OT. Recognizing that insufficient segmentation, insecure OT protocols, and exposed remote access are common weaknesses guides protective controls.'),
    ('drug_un_1961',
     'UN Single Convention on Narcotic Drugs 1961 (as amended by the 1972 Protocol) - Scheduling System',
     'Single Convention on Narcotic Drugs, 1961, as amended by the 1972 Protocol (INCB) - https://www.incb.org/incb/en/narcotic-drugs/1961_Convention.html',
     'The Single Convention on Narcotic Drugs, 1961, as amended by the 1972 Protocol, is the framework treaty for international control of narcotic drugs. It consolidated earlier treaties and broadened scope to cannabis and similar substances. Narcotic drugs are listed in four Schedules by defined criteria, with Schedule IV reserved for the most dangerous under the strictest controls. The Commission on Narcotic Drugs, advised by WHO, may add, remove, or transfer substances among Schedules. The governing principle is that production, trade, use, and possession are limited to medical and scientific purposes, enforced through licensing, estimates of quantities, reporting, inspection, recordkeeping, and prescription rules. It is the primary legal-authority baseline for recognizing controlled narcotic substances and lawful versus diverted handling.'),
    ('drug_un_1971',
     'UN Convention on Psychotropic Substances 1971 - Four Schedules',
     'Convention on Psychotropic Substances of 1971 (INCB) - https://www.incb.org/incb/en/psychotropics/1971_convention.html',
     'The Convention on Psychotropic Substances, signed at Vienna in 1971, is the framework for international control of psychotropic substances, complementing the 1961 regime by covering substances acting on the central nervous system - hallucinogens, stimulants, and sedative-hypnotics and anxiolytics. It places substances in four Schedules of decreasing control stringency, with Schedule I the most restrictive. Initially 32 substances were scheduled; the lists are periodically updated by the Commission on Narcotic Drugs on WHO assessment. Controls include licensing of manufacture and trade, prescription and dispensing rules, recordkeeping, export-import authorizations for the more dangerous Schedules, and limitation to medical and scientific purposes. It provides the legal-authority frame for recognizing controlled psychotropics and distinguishing lawful pharmaceutical use from diversion.'),
    ('drug_un_1988',
     'UN Convention against Illicit Traffic 1988 - Trafficking Offences and Precursor Control (Article 12)',
     'United Nations Convention against Illicit Traffic in Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances, 1988 (INCB) - https://www.incb.org/incb/en/precursors/1988-convention.html',
     'The 1988 Convention, in force from 11 November 1990, supplies enforcement mechanisms for the 1961 and 1971 treaties. It obliges parties to criminalize illicit production, trafficking, and related conduct, and to treat laundering of drug proceeds as an offence. It enables international cooperation through extradition, mutual legal assistance, transfer of proceedings, and controlled deliveries. Article 12 establishes the framework for international precursor control, requiring parties to take measures to prevent diversion of chemicals in Table I and Table II used in the illicit manufacture of narcotic drugs or psychotropic substances, and to cooperate to that end. The Commission on Narcotic Drugs decides scheduling of precursor chemicals, with INCB assessment binding on scientific matters. It is the core legal authority for recognizing trafficking offences and precursor diversion.'),
    ('drug_incb_precursors',
     'INCB Precursor Control - Tables I/II, Pre-Export Notifications, and PEN Online',
     'UNODC UN Toolkit on Synthetic Drugs, Licit Trade Monitoring (PEN Online, INCB) - https://syntheticdrugs.unodc.org/syntheticdrugs/en/precursors/licit-trade-monitoring.html',
     'Under Article 12 of the 1988 Convention, precursor chemicals are listed in Table I and Table II, and competent national authorities monitor licit trade to keep them from being diverted to illicit drug manufacture. Pre-export notifications (PENs) inform an importing country of planned shipments so authorities can verify the transaction, the companies, and the intended end use, and investigate attempted diversions. PEN Online, provided free to governments by INCB, is the secure automated system for exchanging these notifications; suspicious shipments can be checked, suspended, or stopped before reaching illicit manufacturers. In October 2022 INCB launched PEN Online Light, a voluntary twin system for non-scheduled chemicals and designer precursors, responding to CND resolution 65/3. These are recognition and control tools, not chemistry guidance.'),
    ('drug_nps_scheduling',
     'New Psychoactive Substances (NPS) - Scheduling Challenges and Early Warning',
     'UNODC, The Challenge of New Psychoactive Substances and Early Warning Advisory - https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/scientists/the-challenge-of-new-psychoactive-substances.html',
     'New psychoactive substances (NPS) are substances of abuse not controlled under the 1961 or 1971 Conventions that may pose public-health threats, often marketed as legal alternatives to controlled drugs. UNODC reports that the number of individual NPS reported worldwide grew from roughly 254 a decade ago to more than 1,200 by 2024, spanning an expanding range of chemical groups. The conventions allow scheduling of new substances, but the speed at which NPS emerge makes timely control very challenging; new structures can be introduced faster than they are assessed and scheduled. The UNODC Early Warning Advisory and national and regional early-warning mechanisms generate the scientific evidence needed to identify emerging harmful substances. Recognizing NPS emergence and persistent legislative gaps is a legal-authority and public-health priority, not an invitation to operational detail.'),
    ('drug_financial_redflags',
     'Drug-Trafficking Financial Red Flags - Cash-Intensive and Trade-Based Laundering',
     'FATF/Egmont, Trade-Based Money Laundering: Risk Indicators (11 March 2021) - https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/Trade-based-money-laundering-indicators.html',
     'Proceeds of drug trafficking are commonly laundered through cash-intensive businesses and trade-based money laundering, which the FATF identifies as among the most complex and widely used laundering methods. The FATF/Egmont risk-indicator report groups red flags into business structure, trade activity, trade documents and commodities, and account and transaction activity. Recognition signals include a non-cash-intensive entity conducting excessive cash transactions, over- or under-invoicing, mismatched goods descriptions or quantities, opaque ownership and shell companies, and payment flows inconsistent with the stated trade. The FATF notes virtual assets are also used to launder drug-trade proceeds. These are recognition indicators for compliance and investigative use, supporting the anti-laundering legal framework; they contain no evasion methodology.'),
    ('drug_precursor_diversion_recognition',
     'Precursor-Diversion Recognition - Legitimate Industrial Chemicals Diverted to Illicit Use',
     'UNODC UN Toolkit on Synthetic Drugs, Licit Trade Monitoring - https://syntheticdrugs.unodc.org/syntheticdrugs/en/precursors/licit-trade-monitoring.html',
     "Many precursor and pre-precursor chemicals have legitimate industrial and pharmaceutical uses, so diversion from licit trade - not clandestine production of the chemicals - is the principal recognition concern. Under Article 12 of the 1988 Convention, authorities verify each transaction, the companies involved, and the intended end use, and investigate attempted diversions. Traffickers increasingly turn to non-scheduled substitute chemicals and designer precursors engineered to fall outside Table I and Table II; INCB maintains the International Special Surveillance List and the Precursors Incidents Communication System (PICS). Recognition signals include orders inconsistent with a buyer's legitimate business, vague or shifting end-use claims, and demand for chemicals with no plausible licit purpose. This is recognition and control only, with no synthesis detail."),
    ('drug_darknet_postal',
     'Darknet and Postal Drug-Trade Recognition - Parcel Red Flags',
     'UNODC UN Toolkit on Synthetic Drugs, Postal Security (Mail Parcel) - https://syntheticdrugs.unodc.org/syntheticdrugs/en/postal-security/tools/mail_parcel.html',
     'Synthetic drugs are sold on social media, clearnet sites, and darknet marketplaces, with criminals using encryption, VPNs, proxies, and cryptocurrency to evade detection and tracing. The high potency of synthetic opioids makes small concealments attractive to traffickers, so postal and courier channels are heavily exploited. Because courier-parcel volume is large, UNODC describes profiling - using intelligence and market knowledge to focus inspection on higher-risk articles. Recognition signals at the parcel level include mismatches between declared contents and sender or destination patterns, routing chosen to avoid customs scrutiny, and implausible shipping documentation. This material supports lawful interdiction and risk recognition within the international control framework; it provides no instructions for concealment, sourcing, or evasion.'),
    ('drug_forced_labour_nexus',
     'Forced-Labour and Coerced-Cultivation Nexus in Drug Supply Chains',
     'ILO, Forced labour, modern slavery and trafficking in persons; UNODC GLOTIP - https://www.ilo.org/topics-and-sectors/forced-labour-modern-slavery-and-trafficking-persons',
     'Illicit drug supply chains intersect with trafficking in persons and forced labour. The ILO estimates roughly 28 million people are in forced labour worldwide; the UNODC Global Report on Trafficking in Persons (2024) recorded almost 75,000 detected victims for the latest available year, with about 42 percent trafficked for forced labour. UNODC identifies forced criminality as an emerging category in which victims are coerced into drug production and related offences; reported cases include forced labour on illegal cannabis farms. Recognition signals include workers under coercion, debt bondage, confiscated documents, restricted movement, and inability to leave production sites. The remedy frame is victim identification, non-punishment of trafficked persons, and labour and criminal-justice cooperation - a protection and legal-authority lens, not operational supply-chain detail.'),
    ('drug_harm_reduction',
     'Harm Reduction and Public-Health Response (WHO/UNODC)',
     'WHO/UNODC, International Standards for the Treatment of Drug Use Disorders; UNODC harm-reduction policy note - https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/international-standards-for-the-treatment-of-drug-use-disorders',
     'WHO and UNODC frame drug use disorders as a health condition warranting evidence-based, ethical treatment rather than enforcement alone. Their International Standards for the Treatment of Drug Use Disorders set principles for organizing services across settings, modalities, and interventions, especially in less-resourced countries. Harm reduction protects people who use drugs and their families from infectious diseases and other adverse consequences through targeted interventions while supporting steps toward recovery; the no-wrong-door and no-judgement principles ensure access regardless of entry point. WHO, UNAIDS, and UNODC endorse harm reduction, citing evidence it is cost-effective and reduces HIV transmission. This is the public-health remedy lens complementing legal control: treatment, prevention, and harm reduction as a recognized international response.'),
    ('drug_opioid_supply',
     'Opioid and Fentanyl Supply-Chain and Overdose-Crisis Recognition (Public-Health Framing)',
     'UNODC World Drug Report 2025, Key Findings - https://www.unodc.org/documents/data-and-analysis/WDR_2025/WDR25_B1_Key_findings.pdf',
     'Synthetic opioids drive a severe overdose crisis, recognized by UNODC as a public-health emergency rather than only a law-enforcement problem. UNODC reports seizures of amphetamine-type stimulants at record highs alongside rising synthetic-opioid harm, with fentanyl overdoses concentrated in North America. A newer class of potent opioids, the nitazenes, has emerged - many unregulated under current treaties - and has been linked to substantial numbers of deaths in parts of Europe, evading routine detection and endangering users and first responders. Access to lifesaving naloxone remains uneven, reported in only 26 of 110 reporting countries. Recognition of these supply-and-harm trends supports scheduling, early warning, and overdose-response policy. The framing is public-health surveillance and remedy - naloxone access, treatment, and monitoring - with no sourcing or synthesis content.'),
    ('drug_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and Remedy - Controlled Delivery and Alternative Development (UNODC)',
     'UNODC, Alternative Development (Drug Control through Rural Development); 1988 Convention controlled-delivery provisions - https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/alternative-development/',
     'The international response pairs enforcement cooperation with development-based remedy. The 1988 Convention provides for cooperation including extradition, mutual legal assistance, transfer of proceedings, and controlled deliveries, in which suspect consignments proceed under surveillance so authorities can identify networks rather than only seize goods. On the supply side, UNODC alternative development promotes licit livelihoods to prevent and eliminate cultivation of coca, opium poppy, and cannabis by addressing poverty within a sustainable-development framework, requiring access to land, credit, infrastructure, and markets and aligning with the SDGs. UNODC alternative-development work in 2023 amounted to about 40 million US dollars across several countries. This is the legal-authority and remedy frame - cooperation, controlled delivery, and rural development - not tradecraft.'),
    ('election_iccpr_art25',
     'ICCPR Article 25 - Right to Genuine Periodic Elections and Universal Suffrage',
     'International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, Article 25 (UN General Assembly resolution 2200A (XXI), 16 Dec 1966) - OHCHR, https://www.ohchr.org/en/instruments-mechanisms/instruments/international-covenant-civil-and-political-rights',
     "ICCPR Article 25 establishes that every citizen has the right and opportunity, without unreasonable restrictions, to take part in the conduct of public affairs; to vote and be elected at genuine periodic elections held by universal and equal suffrage and by secret ballot guaranteeing the free expression of the will of the electors; and to have equal access to public service. The Human Rights Committee's General Comment 25 treats this as the core international benchmark for democratic legitimacy. Standard: elections must be genuine, periodic, universal, equal, secret and freely expressed. Recognition red flags: arbitrary disenfranchisement, suppressed candidacy, manipulated franchise, or results not reflecting voters' free will. Remedy framing: states must guarantee these rights in law and practice and provide effective redress. Standards reference, not operational guidance."),
    ('election_venice_code',
     'Venice Commission Code of Good Practice in Electoral Matters - Five Principles',
     'Council of Europe, Venice Commission, Code of Good Practice in Electoral Matters CDL-AD(2002)023rev2-cor (adopted 18-19 Oct 2002), https://www.venice.coe.int/webforms/documents/default.aspx?pdffile=CDL-AD(2002)023rev2-cor-e',
     "The Venice Commission Code codifies five underlying principles of Europe's electoral heritage: universal suffrage (all citizens may vote and stand, with only narrow, proportionate exceptions and accurate electoral registers); equal suffrage (equal voting rights, equal voting power, and equality of opportunity for parties); free suffrage (freedom to form an opinion and freedom to express it, including action against electoral fraud); secret suffrage (the ballot is secret and that secrecy is a duty); and direct suffrage. Conditions for implementation include respect for fundamental rights, stability of electoral law, and procedural guarantees: organisation by an impartial body, observation of elections, and an effective system of appeal. These are recognition and remedy standards against which electoral conduct can be assessed; the Code is non-partisan and country-neutral."),
    ('election_osce_odihr_observation',
     'OSCE/ODIHR Election Observation Methodology and Standards',
     'OSCE Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights, Election Observation Handbook, Sixth Edition, https://odihr.osce.org/odihr/elections/68439',
     "OSCE/ODIHR's election observation methodology, refined across more than 230 observed or assessed elections, sets impartial standards for evaluating elections against OSCE commitments (notably the 1990 Copenhagen Document) and international obligations. It emphasises independence, impartiality and professionalism, and deploys graduated formats: full and limited observation missions, assessment missions and expert teams. Observers assess the legal framework, administration, voter and candidate registration, campaign and media environment, voting, counting, tabulation and post-election developments. Recognition value: systematic, evidence-based observation distinguishes genuine elections from manipulated ones. Remedy framing: observation reports issue findings and recommendations to improve compliance. Oversight standards reference, not techniques to influence an election."),
    ('election_idea_integrity',
     'International IDEA Electoral Integrity Framework',
     'International IDEA, Protecting Elections / Electoral integrity definition and principles, https://www.idea.int/theme/electoral-processes',
     'International IDEA defines an election with integrity as any election based on the democratic principles of universal suffrage and political equality reflected in international standards, and that is professional, impartial and transparent throughout the electoral cycle. IDEA frames integrity across the full cycle, not election day alone, and articulates administration principles: respect for the law, impartiality and neutrality, transparency, accuracy and being voter-oriented, alongside fairness, secrecy of the vote and accountability. Its Integrated Framework for Protecting Elections consolidates a practitioner guide, assessment tool, training curriculum and remedies toolbox. Recognition: deviations from these principles signal compromised integrity. Remedy: the framework maps safeguards and corrective measures. Non-partisan, country-neutral standards reference only.'),
    ('election_campaign_finance',
     'Campaign-Finance Transparency, Disclosure and Limits',
     'U.S. Federal Election Commission, Contribution limits and Federal Election Campaign Act (FECA) disclosure requirements, https://www.fec.gov/help-candidates-and-committees/candidate-taking-receipts/contribution-limits/',
     'Campaign-finance integrity rests on transparency, disclosure and limits. Under the U.S. Federal Election Campaign Act, political committees must register with the FEC and file periodic reports itemising receipts and disbursements, including the identity, occupation and employer of any person contributing above a statutory threshold; reports are public so voters and watchdogs can scrutinise funding sources. Contribution limits cap how much an individual may give per candidate per election, while independent-expenditure-only committees may raise unlimited sums, creating disclosure gaps observers monitor. Recognition red flags: undisclosed or dark-money flows, straw-donor structuring, and contributions routed to evade limits or bans. Remedy framing: mandatory disclosure, independent oversight and enforcement. Transparency-norms reference, not advice on concealing political money.'),
    ('election_voter_suppression_redflags',
     'Voter-Suppression Recognition Red Flags',
     'International IDEA electoral integrity principles (equal access) and ICCPR Art 25 universal-suffrage standard, https://www.idea.int/theme/electoral-processes',
     "Voter suppression is any effort that limits eligible citizens' capacity to register, vote or cast an effective ballot, measured against the universal- and equal-suffrage standard. Recognition red flags, framed neutrally: intimidation or obstruction at polling places; deceptive communications that misstate voting dates, hours, locations or eligibility; inequitable allocation of polling resources producing burdensome lines in targeted areas; inaccurate purges of voter rolls; and registration rules that disproportionately exclude eligible groups. Disinformation-driven suppression uses messages and ads to deter turnout, and disparate impact on minorities, youth, the elderly and people with disabilities is a key signal. Remedy framing: independent administration, accurate registers, accessible polling and judicial review. Describes how to recognise suppression, never how to conduct it."),
    ('election_disinformation_recognition',
     'Election Disinformation Recognition - Coordinated Inauthentic Behavior',
     'EU Code of Practice on Disinformation, European Commission, https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/code-conduct-disinformation',
     'Election disinformation is the deliberate spread of false or misleading content to distort voter perception or depress participation. A predominant tactic is coordinated inauthentic behaviour: networks of accounts using synchronised posting, repeated reshares, hashtag sequences and link-sharing to amplify content and manufacture an illusion of widespread support. Coordination and inauthenticity are distinct - genuine grassroots activity is coordinated but authentic, while a deceptive account may be inauthentic yet uncoordinated; recognition needs both lenses. Common false narratives target election procedures, alleging machines change votes or that fraud is widespread, eroding trust in legitimate results. Remedy framing: platform transparency, fact-checking, provenance labelling and research access, as encouraged by the EU Code. Recognition standard only; no campaign instructions.'),
    ('election_foreign_interference',
     'Foreign Electoral Interference Recognition - Influence Operations and Illicit Funding',
     'International Foundation for Electoral Systems (IFES), Illicit Foreign Finance and Election Integrity, https://www.ifes.org/news/illicit-foreign-finance-and-election-integrity',
     "Foreign electoral interference is deliberate effort by a foreign state or its proxies to alter another country's election by non-military means. Analysts distinguish interference, which violates the target state's law (disrupting counts, illicit funding, cyberattacks), from influence by ostensibly legal means. Recognition indicators: covert funding of parties, candidates or campaigns; vote-buying; use of domestic parties, businesses or NGOs as proxies; opaque beneficial ownership, offshore routing and cryptocurrency to mask sources; and information manipulation paired with intimidation. Many jurisdictions lack foreign-donation bans, and even where bans exist much foreign money moves outside the law. Remedy framing: closing legal gaps, political-finance transparency, capable oversight bodies and cross-border information sharing. Recognition and oversight reference only."),
    ('election_deepfake_recognition',
     'Synthetic Media and Deepfake Election Manipulation Recognition',
     'Brennan Center for Justice, How to Detect and Guard Against Deceptive AI-Generated Election Information, https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/how-detect-and-guard-against-deceptive-ai-generated-election-information',
     'Synthetic media - AI-generated or AI-altered video, audio and images - can fabricate statements or actions by candidates and officials, or impersonate voices, to deceive voters or suppress turnout. The volume of deepfakes online rose sharply, and politically themed fakes have surfaced across many countries. Recognition cues highlighted by researchers include inconsistent lighting and shadows, implausible geometry, pixel-level artefacts and audio anomalies, alongside provenance and context checks against authenticated sources. Public concern is high. Remedy framing: disclosure and disclaimer requirements for election-related synthetic content adopted in several jurisdictions, content-provenance standards, platform labelling, and rapid authoritative rebuttal. Detection and standards reference; provides no instructions for creating deceptive synthetic media.'),
    ('election_eu_dsa_disinformation',
     'EU Code of Practice on Disinformation and Digital Services Act Platform Duties',
     'European Commission, Online platforms report on measures to protect European election integrity under the Code of Practice on Disinformation, https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/online-platforms-report-measures-protect-european-election-integrity-under-code-practice',
     'The EU Digital Services Act requires Very Large Online Platforms and Search Engines to assess and mitigate systemic risks, including negative effects on civic discourse and electoral processes. In February 2025 the voluntary Code of Practice on Disinformation was integrated into the DSA framework as a compliance benchmark, so full adherence can count as an appropriate risk-mitigation measure. The March 2024 Guidelines on Mitigation of Systemic Risks for Electoral Processes recommend best practices before, during and after elections. Recognition value: transparency reports, ad repositories and risk assessments expose manipulation patterns. Remedy framing: enforceable due-diligence duties, independent auditing, researcher data access and regulatory oversight. Platform-governance standards reference, not methods to evade them or to manipulate civic discourse.'),
    ('election_ballot_integrity_safeguards',
     'Ballot and Result-Integrity Safeguards - Audits and Chain of Custody',
     'U.S. Election Assistance Commission, Election Audits Across the United States, and risk-limiting audit practice, https://www.eac.gov/sites/default/files/bestpractices/Election_Audits_Across_the_United_States.pdf',
     'Result integrity depends on verifiable records and rigorous safeguards. Voter-verified paper ballots enable independent checking of electronic counts. A risk-limiting audit examines a statistically determined sample of paper ballots to give justified confidence the reported outcome is correct, expanding to a full hand count if evidence suggests a miscount, before certification; closer contests require larger samples. Chain-of-custody controls require containers to be identified, signed and sealed by at least two officials, with logs recording seal numbers and ballot counts, and seals compared whenever a container is opened. Recognition red flags: broken or mismatched seals, missing logs, unexplained discrepancies, and outcomes without auditable paper. Remedy framing: audits, ballot manifests, transparent reconciliation and observer access. Standards reference, not tampering guidance.'),
    ('election_remedy_oversight',
     'Remedy and Oversight - Electoral Management Bodies and Dispute Resolution',
     'International IDEA, Electoral Justice: The International IDEA Handbook, https://www.idea.int/sites/default/files/publications/electoral-justice-handbook.pdf',
     'Effective remedy and oversight are integral to electoral integrity. International IDEA describes electoral management bodies (EMBs) as the institutions mandated to organise or supervise core electoral functions, and stresses their structural and functional independence from government as a safeguard against capture. Free, fair and credible elections require efficient, transparent electoral dispute resolution; an essential guarantee is the right to a timely remedy by an independent, impartial body. Where EMBs decide challenges first, decisions should be appealable to higher EMBs or courts. Recognition red flags: politicised administration, opaque or absent complaint channels, and delayed or unreviewable decisions. Remedy framing: independent EMBs, layered appeals, published observation reports and enforceable rulings. Non-partisan, country-neutral oversight reference.'),
    ('food_codex_alimentarius',
     'Codex Alimentarius: International Food Standards (FAO/WHO)',
     'Codex Alimentarius Commission, About Codex (FAO/WHO Joint Food Standards Programme), https://www.fao.org/fao-who-codexalimentarius/about-codex/en/',
     "Codex Alimentarius (the 'Food Code') is the body of international food standards, guidelines, and codes of practice adopted by the Codex Alimentarius Commission, established in 1963 by FAO and WHO to protect consumer health and ensure fair practices in food trade. Standards cover food hygiene, additives, contaminants and toxins, pesticide and veterinary-drug residue limits, labeling, and sampling/analysis methods. WTO's Sanitary and Phytosanitary (SPS) Agreement references Codex as the international benchmark for trade disputes. Recognition framing: a product or claim that deviates from an applicable Codex maximum residue limit, additive list, or standard of identity, without scientific justification, is a credible integrity red flag. Remedy: Codex underpins national legislation, import controls, and SPS dispute resolution."),
    ('food_us_fsma',
     'US FSMA: Preventive Controls and Foreign Supplier Verification (FSVP)',
     'FDA, FSMA Final Rule on Foreign Supplier Verification Programs (FSVP) and Preventive Controls, https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/final-rule-foreign-supplier-verification-programs-fsvp-key-requirements',
     'The FDA Food Safety Modernization Act of 2011 shifted US food safety from reacting to contamination toward preventing it. Facilities must implement risk-based preventive controls built on a written hazard analysis. The Foreign Supplier Verification Programs (FSVP) rule requires importers to verify that foreign suppliers produce food meeting US safety standards and that the food is not adulterated or misbranded as to allergen labeling, using a qualified individual, hazard analysis, and supplier verification such as audits. Recognition framing: an importer with no documented FSVP, no named qualified individual, or no supplier verification records for a hazard-bearing food is non-compliant and a risk signal. Remedy: FDA enforces via inspections, warning letters, import refusal, and detention without physical examination.'),
    ('food_eu_general_food_law',
     'EU General Food Law (Regulation 178/2002): Traceability and RASFF',
     'EU Regulation (EC) No 178/2002 (General Food Law) and RASFF, European Commission, https://food.ec.europa.eu/food-safety/rasff_en',
     "Regulation (EC) No 178/2002 is the EU's foundational food law. It mandates traceability at all stages of production, processing, and distribution on a 'one step back, one step forward' basis, and obliges operators to withdraw or recall unsafe food and notify authorities. Article 50 establishes the Rapid Alert System for Food and Feed (RASFF), a round-the-clock network linking national authorities, EFSA, and the Commission to exchange notifications (alert, information, border rejection) on direct or indirect risks to human health. Recognition framing: an operator unable to identify its immediate supplier and customer for a lot, or a product subject to an open RASFF notification, is a clear integrity and safety red flag. Remedy: RASFF triggers rapid withdrawal, recall, and cross-border corrective action."),
    ('food_ema_food_fraud_redflags',
     'Economically Motivated Adulteration (EMA) and Food Fraud Red Flags',
     'FDA, Economically Motivated Adulteration (Food Fraud), https://www.fda.gov/food/compliance-enforcement-food/economically-motivated-adulteration-food-fraud',
     'EMA occurs when someone intentionally leaves out, removes, or substitutes a valuable ingredient, or adds a substance to make food appear better or of greater value; food fraud is a common type. FDA notes outside estimates that fraud affects about 1% of the global food industry at 10 to 40 billion USD a year. Beyond economic harm it can cause illness or death (e.g. lead poisoning from adulterated spices, hidden allergens). Recognition framing: prices implausibly below market, single-ingredient premium goods (olive oil, honey, saffron), opaque or shifting supply chains, and pure-product claims that resist verification are documented red flags. Remedy under the FD&C Act (sections 402(b)/403), FSMA economically-motivated-hazard controls, recalls, seizures, import alerts, and DOJ prosecution.'),
    ('food_haccp',
     'HACCP: Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points Framework',
     'FDA, HACCP Principles & Application Guidelines (Codex-derived), https://www.fda.gov/food/hazard-analysis-critical-control-point-haccp/haccp-principles-application-guidelines',
     'HACCP is a systematic, preventive food-safety management system that identifies, evaluates, and controls biological, chemical, and physical hazards across production. The seven principles are: (1) conduct a hazard analysis; (2) determine critical control points (CCPs); (3) establish critical limits; (4) establish monitoring; (5) establish corrective actions; (6) establish verification; (7) establish record-keeping and documentation. The Codex Alimentarius Commission set the foundational principles adopted worldwide; in the US, HACCP is mandatory for meat/poultry (FSIS, 9 CFR 417), seafood, and juice. Recognition framing: missing CCP monitoring records, undefined critical limits, or no corrective-action log indicate a control system that cannot assure safety. Remedy: regulatory plan review, reassessment, and enforcement against deficient plans.'),
    ('food_origin_substitution_fraud',
     'Food Origin and Species Substitution Fraud (Seafood, Olive Oil, Honey)',
     'FDA, Economically Motivated Adulteration (Food Fraud) - Seafood/Olive Oil/Honey examples, https://www.fda.gov/food/compliance-enforcement-food/economically-motivated-adulteration-food-fraud',
     'Species and origin substitution is among the most common food fraud. FDA documents cheaper fish sold as premium species (e.g. rockfish or other snappers sold as red snapper, Lutjanus campechanus), glaze (ice) added to frozen seafood sold by weight, extra-virgin olive oil diluted with cheaper oil, and honey or maple syrup cut with corn, rice, beet, or cane sugar. Oceana investigations found roughly a third of US seafood samples mislabeled. Recognition framing: market name not matching the FDA Seafood List, country-of-origin claims without documentation, premium grade at commodity price, and net weight inflated by glaze are red flags. Remedy: DNA barcoding, isotope/chemical authentication, import alerts, seizure, and criminal prosecution.'),
    ('food_allergen_mislabeling',
     'Allergen Mislabeling and Undeclared-Allergen Recognition',
     'FDA, Food Allergen Labeling and Consumer Protection Act of 2004 (FALCPA), https://www.fda.gov/food/food-allergensgluten-free-guidance-documents-regulatory-information/food-allergen-labeling-and-consumer-protection-act-2004-falcpa',
     "FALCPA (effective 2006) amended the FD&C Act to require foods to declare each major food allergen by its food-source name, either in parentheses after the ingredient or in a 'Contains' statement. The major allergens are milk, eggs, fish, crustacean shellfish, tree nuts, peanuts, wheat, and soybeans, with sesame added by the FASTER Act of 2021. Undeclared allergens are the leading cause of US food recalls. Recognition framing: a label missing a 'Contains' statement for a known allergenic ingredient, evidence of cross-contact without controls, or a reformulated product whose label was not updated are clear hazards. Remedy: such products are deemed misbranded or adulterated and are subject to recall, import refusal, and seizure."),
    ('food_horsemeat_lessons',
     'The 2013 Horsemeat Scandal: Elliott Review Supply-Chain Lessons',
     'Elliott Review into the Integrity and Assurance of Food Supply Networks: Final Report (UK Government, Prof. Chris Elliott, 2014), https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/elliott-review-into-the-integrity-and-assurance-of-food-supply-networks-final-report',
     "In 2013 horse DNA was found in products sold as beef across EU retail and food-service supply chains, exposing how long, complex, cost-driven sourcing invites fraud. The UK commissioned an independent review (led by a food-safety professor at Queen's University Belfast); the 2014 final report set out a national food crime prevention framework built on eight pillars: consumer first, zero tolerance, intelligence gathering, laboratory services, audit, government support, leadership, and crisis management, and led to a dedicated National Food Crime Unit. Recognition framing: the deeper and more opaque a supply chain, the greater its fraud vulnerability; insiders and legitimate-looking intermediaries were the main actors. Remedy: intelligence-led surveillance, authenticity testing, whole-chain audit, and criminal enforcement."),
    ('food_counterfeit_agro_inputs',
     'Counterfeit and Diluted Agro-Inputs (Fake Pesticides, Fertilizer) Recognition',
     'CropLife International, Anti-Counterfeiting Activities (with FAO, Interpol, Europol, WCO), https://croplife.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/CropLife-International_AntiCounterfeiting_2023.pdf',
     'Counterfeit and substandard agricultural inputs undermine food security and farmer livelihoods. African Union and FAO-cited estimates suggest up to 30 percent of agrochemicals in some African markets are fake or substandard; fake fertilizer supplies too few nutrients, cutting yields, while counterfeit pesticides may contain harmful or wrong actives that damage crops, soil, and water. Such trade is frequently linked to tax evasion, fraud, and labour exploitation. Recognition framing: missing or tampered authentication labels, prices far below genuine product, unregistered brands, poor packaging or spelling, and no traceable distributor are red flags. Remedy: anti-counterfeit serialization, registration verification, and enforcement coordinated by national authorities with Interpol, Europol, the World Customs Organization, and FAO.'),
    ('food_outbreak_recall',
     'Foodborne-Illness Outbreak Recognition and Recall Systems',
     'FDA, Investigations of Foodborne Illness Outbreaks (with CDC/NORS), https://www.fda.gov/food/outbreaks-foodborne-illness/investigations-foodborne-illness-outbreaks',
     'CDC, FDA, FSIS, and state/local partners run epidemiologic and traceback investigations and test product and environmental samples to find the cause of an outbreak and prevent further illness. Health departments report outbreak data to CDC through the National Outbreak Reporting System (NORS), complemented by NEARS for retail environmental assessments. A recall occurs when a firm removes a product believed to cause illness; FDA can request a voluntary recall or, under FSMA, order a mandatory recall when there is reasonable probability the food is adulterated and could cause serious illness or death. Recognition framing: a cluster of illnesses sharing a genetic or epidemiologic link to one food, or a positive pathogen sample, signals an outbreak. Remedy: public warning, recall, and traceback to the contamination source.'),
    ('food_certification_fraud',
     'Organic and Fair-Trade Certification Fraud Recognition',
     'USDA Agricultural Marketing Service, Organic Enforcement and Strengthening Organic Enforcement (SOE) Rule, https://www.ams.usda.gov/services/enforcement/organic',
     "The USDA National Organic Program (NOP) protects the organic label by posting fraudulent organic certificates and investigating complaints. The 2023 Strengthening Organic Enforcement (SOE) rule, the biggest update since 1990, expanded oversight: imports represented as organic must be declared to Customs and Border Protection, non-retail containers must be labeled, certifiers must be trained, and certified operations must maintain an organic fraud prevention plan and verify suppliers. Recognition framing: a certificate not matching the Organic Integrity Database, an expired or altered certificate, volumes exceeding a producer's certified capacity, or missing import documentation are red flags. Remedy: NOP works with CBP to detain, redirect, or destroy fraudulent shipments and can suspend or revoke certification."),
    ('food_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: Recall, RASFF/RFR, and Food-Fraud Networks',
     'FDA Reportable Food Registry and EU Agri-Food Fraud Network (AAC-FF), https://www.fda.gov/food/compliance-enforcement-food/reportable-food-registry-industry and https://food.ec.europa.eu/food-safety/eu-agri-food-fraud-network_en',
     'Multiple remedy channels exist. Under the US Reportable Food Registry, a responsible party must report to FDA via the electronic portal within 24 hours of determining a food is reportable (reasonable probability of serious adverse health consequences or death), and investigate the cause if it may have originated with them, helping FDA track adulteration patterns. In the EU, the Alert and Cooperation Network combines RASFF (risk notifications) with the Administrative Assistance and Cooperation system and the Agri-Food Fraud Network (established 2013, with Europol) using the AAC-FF IT tool to share cross-border fraud cases. Recognition framing: an unreported reportable food, or refusal to cooperate with a network request, indicates non-compliance. Remedy: recall, withdrawal, import refusal, seizure, and coordinated cross-border investigation and prosecution.'),
    ('heritage_unesco_1970',
     '1970 UNESCO Convention on Illicit Import, Export and Transfer of Cultural Property',
     '1970 UNESCO Convention on the Means of Prohibiting and Preventing the Illicit Import, Export and Transfer of Ownership of Cultural Property; https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/convention-means-prohibiting-and-preventing-illicit-import-export-and-transfer-ownership-cultural',
     'Adopted 14 November 1970 (in force 1972), this is the foundational treaty against illicit traffic in cultural property, with 146 States Parties. Article 1 defines cultural property by category (archaeological objects, antiquities over 100 years, manuscripts, art, ethnological material). Article 3 declares illicit any import, export or transfer of ownership effected contrary to the Convention. States Parties must require export certificates (Art. 6), prohibit import of objects stolen from a museum or public monument and documented in its inventory (Art. 7(b)(i)), and recover and return such property on request (Art. 7(b)(ii)), with just compensation to an innocent purchaser. Recognition signal: an antiquity offered without an export certificate or lawful collecting history predating 1970 warrants heightened scrutiny.'),
    ('heritage_unidroit_1995',
     '1995 UNIDROIT Convention on Stolen or Illegally Exported Cultural Objects',
     '1995 UNIDROIT Convention on Stolen or Illegally Exported Cultural Objects (Rome, 24 June 1995); https://www.unidroit.org/instruments/cultural-property/1995-convention/',
     "This treaty complements the 1970 UNESCO Convention with uniform private-law rules. It supports two claims: restitution of stolen cultural objects (Chapter II) and return of objects illegally exported contrary to a State's protection laws (Chapter III). Core rule: the possessor of a stolen object shall return it, and unlawfully excavated items are treated as stolen. A good-faith possessor is entitled to fair compensation only on proof they neither knew nor ought reasonably to have known the object was stolen and exercised due diligence at acquisition. Due-diligence assessment weighs the character of the parties, the price paid, and whether a register of stolen objects was consulted. Time limits: generally 3 years from discovery and 50 years from theft. Recognition signal: a buyer who skipped database checks or paid a price out of line with the market fails the due-diligence test."),
    ('heritage_hague_1954',
     '1954 Hague Convention - Cultural Property in Armed Conflict',
     '1954 Hague Convention for the Protection of Cultural Property in the Event of Armed Conflict, with First (1954) and Second (1999) Protocols; https://www.unesco.org/en/heritage-armed-conflicts/convention-and-protocols/1954-convention',
     'Adopted in 1954 under UNESCO, this is the first multilateral treaty dedicated to protecting cultural heritage in peace and war. It safeguards monuments, archaeological sites, works of art, manuscripts, books and scientific collections. States Parties must prepare safeguards, refrain from hostile acts against cultural property, and avoid using it for military purposes that expose it to destruction. Property may be entered in the International Register for Special Protection and marked with the Blue Shield emblem. The 1954 First Protocol addresses occupied-territory objects; the 1999 Second Protocol adds an enhanced-protection regime and individual criminal responsibility for theft, pillage and vandalism. Recognition signal: objects surfacing from a recent conflict zone without lawful export likely originate from wartime pillage.'),
    ('heritage_provenance_redflags',
     'Provenance Red Flags for Antiquities Acquisition',
     'Responsible Art Market (RAM) Antiquities Due Diligence Toolkit; https://www.responsibleartmarket.org/guidelines/art-transaction-due-diligence-toolkit/antiquities-due-diligence-toolkit/',
     "Provenance is the full ownership history of an object from discovery or creation to the present; for antiquities the recent record establishing lawful export is decisive. Recognition red flags drawn from market due-diligence standards: a suspiciously low price out of line with the segment; vague references such as 'private collection' that lack specificity; contradictory information about the same object; unexplained gaps in collecting history (which can signal theft, illicit excavation or forgery); recent presence in a conflict zone where lawful excavation was unlikely; payment requested in currencies or jurisdictions with no connection to the deal; and documents that may be fake, including counterfeit ICOM/UNESCO certificates. Absence of credible recent provenance for an archaeological object is itself a warning that warrants checking stolen-art databases before handling."),
    ('heritage_conflict_antiquities',
     'Conflict Antiquities and the Terrorist-Financing Nexus (UN Res 2199)',
     'UN Security Council Resolution 2199 (2015); UN press release SC/11775; https://press.un.org/en/2015/sc11775.doc.htm',
     'Adopted unanimously on 12 February 2015, Resolution 2199 recognises that ISIL and groups associated with Al-Qaida (including the Al-Nusra Front) generate income by looting and smuggling cultural heritage from archaeological sites, museums, libraries and archives in Iraq and Syria, using the revenue to recruit and strengthen operations. The Council imposed a legally binding requirement that all Member States take steps to prevent trade in Iraqi cultural property illegally removed since 6 August 1990 and Syrian cultural property illegally removed since 15 March 2011, including by prohibiting cross-border trade in such items. This extended to Syria the ban already in place for Iraq since 2003. Recognition signal: unprovenanced antiquities of Iraqi or Syrian type entering the market are presumptively conflict-looted and may finance terrorism.'),
    ('heritage_icom_red_lists',
     'ICOM Red Lists of Cultural Objects at Risk',
     'ICOM (International Council of Museums) Red Lists of Cultural Objects at Risk; https://icom.museum/en/resources/red-lists/',
     'The ICOM Red Lists are practical tools to curb illicit traffic. Critically, they are NOT inventories of stolen items: each list illustrates the categories of cultural goods most vulnerable to trafficking by showing inventoried objects from recognised institutional collections. They span material types (ceramics, gems, glass, metals, paper, textiles, wood) and object classes (architectural elements, currency, manuscripts, sculptures, weapons). Twenty-two lists exist, including emergency lists for Egypt, Iraq, Libya, Syria, Yemen and Ukraine, regional lists for Africa, Latin America and Southeast Europe, and country lists for Afghanistan, Cambodia, China, Colombia, Greece and Peru. Customs, police, dealers and museums consult them to recognise at-risk object types. Recognition signal: an object matching a Red List category from a listed region demands enhanced provenance verification.'),
    ('heritage_looting_recognition',
     'Recognising Looting and Illicit Excavation of Archaeological Sites',
     "UNESCO, 'Illegal excavations and online trade of cultural property looted in the midst of COVID-19'; https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/illegal-excavations-and-online-trade-cultural-property-looted-midst-covid-19",
     'Looting destroys the archaeological context that gives objects scientific meaning, so freshly excavated antiquities typically reach the market with no documented find-spot or ownership history. UNESCO reported that weakened surveillance of sites and museums (for example during COVID-19 lockdowns) created openings for illicit excavation, and that a substantial share of objects bought online and later seized by police came from archaeological excavations. Recognition signals of looted material include: no recorded find-spot or excavation documentation; soil residue or fresh breaks suggesting recent removal; pieces apparently detached from a larger monument; and emergence shortly after instability in a source country. UNESCO recommends checking the National Cultural Heritage Legislation Database, ICOM Red Lists and the INTERPOL Stolen Works of Art database.'),
    ('heritage_online_trafficking',
     'Recognising Online Antiquities Trafficking on Marketplaces and Social Media',
     "UNESCO, 'Illegal excavations and online trade of cultural property looted in the midst of COVID-19'; https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/illegal-excavations-and-online-trade-cultural-property-looted-midst-covid-19",
     "Looted antiquities increasingly move through social media and online marketplaces rather than traditional salerooms. UNESCO documented one antiquities-sales Facebook group that grew from about 300,000 members in March 2020 to nearly 500,000 within a month, prompting Facebook and Instagram to ban trade in historical artefacts. Recognition signals of illicit online listings include: objects offered with no provenance or only a vague 'old collection' claim; sellers posting many unprovenanced antiquities of conflict-zone type; private-channel or closed-group sales avoiding public records; and prices inconsistent with documented market value. UNESCO recommends specialised police units monitoring platforms and cross-checking listings against the ICOM Red Lists and the INTERPOL Stolen Works of Art database. This corpus supports recognition only, never how to sell or launder such objects."),
    ('heritage_due_diligence',
     'Museum and Dealer Acquisition Due Diligence (AAMD / ICOM Standards)',
     "Association of Art Museum Directors (AAMD), 'Strengthened Guidelines on the Acquisition of Archaeological Material and Ancient Art' (2013); https://aamd.org/for-the-media/press-release/strengthened-guidelines-on-the-acquisition-of-archaeological-material",
     "Professional ethics require rigorous acquisition due diligence. The AAMD Guidelines adopt the November 17, 1970 benchmark: a museum should not normally acquire archaeological material or ancient art without provenance demonstrating the object was outside its country of modern discovery before that date, or was legally exported afterward. Members must document all known ownership history and post new acquisitions to AAMD's public Object Registry, a requirement elevated into its Code of Ethics so claims and new research can surface. The ICOM Code of Ethics for Museums similarly bars acquiring objects without valid title and requires establishing full provenance before purchase. Recognition signal: an object lacking documented pre-1970 history or a lawful post-1970 export record, and not checked against stolen-art registers, warrants extreme caution and may be illicit."),
    ('heritage_import_restrictions',
     'Import Restrictions and Bilateral Agreements (US CPIA; EU Reg 2019/880)',
     'EU Regulation (EU) 2019/880 on the introduction and import of cultural goods; https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2019/880/oj/eng (and US Convention on Cultural Property Implementation Act, 19 U.S.C. 2601 et seq.)',
     "Market states impose import controls to block illicitly exported antiquities. EU Regulation 2019/880, Article 3(1), bans introducing into the EU any cultural good removed from its country of creation or discovery in breach of that country's laws. Archaeological objects and dismembered monument parts over 250 years old require an advance import licence with proof of lawful export; other historical, artistic and ethnological items over 200 years old and above EUR 18,000 require an importer statement. The US CPIA (19 U.S.C. 2601 et seq.) implements the 1970 UNESCO Convention through bilateral agreements (MOUs) and emergency actions that restrict import of designated archaeological and ethnological material for renewable five-year terms. Recognition signal: lack of lawful export documentation for an at-risk object can indicate a stolen or clandestinely excavated antiquity."),
    ('heritage_repatriation',
     'Repatriation and Restitution Mechanisms (UNESCO ICPRCP)',
     'UNESCO Intergovernmental Committee for Promoting the Return of Cultural Property to its Countries of Origin or its Restitution in case of Illicit Appropriation (ICPRCP); https://www.unesco.org/en/fight-illicit-trafficking/return-and-restitution',
     "Beyond the binding treaty routes, UNESCO's ICPRCP (established 1978) is a permanent, advisory intergovernmental body that facilitates return and restitution where the 1970 Convention does not formally apply (for example pre-1970 losses or non-ratifying states). A requesting State must first attempt bilateral negotiation; only if that fails may it bring the case to the Committee. Its 2010 Rules of Procedure for Mediation and Conciliation offer voluntary, non-binding dispute resolution. Documented outcomes include outright restitution, restitution with conditions, long-term loans, co-ownership, replicas, or compensation. Examples include the 2011 return of the Bogazkoy sphinx to Turkiye and pre-Columbian objects returned to Ecuador. Recognition framing: lawful restitution proceeds through state-to-state channels and registers, not through private resale of unprovenanced objects."),
    ('heritage_enforcement',
     'Enforcement: Customs, INTERPOL Stolen Works of Art, and the WCO',
     'World Customs Organization (WCO) Cultural Heritage Programme (https://www.wcoomd.org/en/topics/enforcement-and-compliance/activities-and-programmes/cultural-heritage-programme.aspx) and INTERPOL Stolen Works of Art Database (https://www.interpol.int/en/Crimes/Cultural-heritage-crime/Stolen-Works-of-Art-Database)',
     "International borders are the prime point to intercept trafficked cultural objects, so customs is central to enforcement. The WCO Cultural Heritage Programme runs ARCHEO, a secure network for exchanging intelligence on trafficking investigations (endorsed by UN Security Council Resolution 2347 of 2017), and the PITCH training handbook, and it coordinates operations such as Europe's Operation Pandora and the biennial global Operation Athena with INTERPOL and Europol. INTERPOL's Stolen Works of Art Database holds descriptions and images of almost 57,000 items and is the only international database with certified police information on stolen and missing art; authorised users can search it and the ID-Art app. Recognition signal: an object matching a database record, or one never checked against these registers, points to illicit origin and triggers reporting to competent authorities."),
    ('carbon_icvcm_ccp',
     'ICVCM Core Carbon Principles: high-integrity carbon credits',
     'Integrity Council for the Voluntary Carbon Market, The Core Carbon Principles, https://icvcm.org/the-core-carbon-principles/',
     'The Core Carbon Principles (CCPs) are ten benchmarks a carbon-crediting program must meet for credits to earn the CCP integrity label. They span governance (effective governance, tracking via a registry, transparency, robust independent third-party validation and verification), emissions impact (additionality, permanence, robust quantification, no double-counting), and sustainable development (benefits and safeguards, net-zero transition contribution). Recognition red flags: a credit asserting reductions that would have happened anyway (non-additionality), no buffer or reversal safeguard despite permanence risk, or no public registry serial number. Remedy: buyers should require CCP-Approved categories and ICVCM Assessment Framework conformity before purchase.'),
    ('carbon_greenwashing_eu_green_claims',
     'Greenwashing recognition under EU Green Claims and Empowering Consumers rules',
     'European Commission, Green claims, https://environment.ec.europa.eu/topics/circular-economy/green-claims_en; Directive (EU) 2024/825 Empowering Consumers for the Green Transition',
     'The EU found 53 percent of environmental claims vague, misleading or unfounded and 40 percent unsubstantiated. The Empowering Consumers Directive (EU 2024/825) bans generic claims such as eco-friendly, green, or climate friendly unless recognized excellent performance is proven, and bans neutral/reduced/positive-impact claims based on offsetting (climate neutral, CO2 neutral) outside the product value chain. The Green Claims proposal would require claims be substantiated with robust, science-based, verifiable methods checked by an accredited verifier. Recognition red flags: a label with no certification basis, an offset-based neutrality claim, or a future pledge with no verifiable plan. Remedy: independent verification and national consumer-protection enforcement.'),
    ('carbon_eu_csrd',
     'EU CSRD and ESRS sustainability reporting duties',
     'European Commission, Corporate sustainability reporting, https://finance.ec.europa.eu/capital-markets-union-and-financial-markets/company-reporting-and-auditing/company-reporting/corporate-sustainability-reporting_en',
     'The Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) requires large and listed companies to disclose risks, opportunities, and the impact of their activities on people and environment, reported under the European Sustainability Reporting Standards (ESRS) adopted July 2023. ESRS uses double materiality: both financial materiality and impact materiality. ESRS E1 governs climate, including transition plans and GHG metrics; disclosures are subject to phased assurance. Recognition red flags: selective impact-only reporting that omits financial risk, climate targets with no transition plan, or missing limited assurance. Remedy: standardized ESRS datapoints, mandatory assurance, and supervisory review give stakeholders comparable, auditable sustainability data rather than self-styled claims.'),
    ('carbon_credit_fraud_redflags',
     'Carbon-credit fraud red flags: double counting, phantom credits, non-additionality',
     'Integrity Council for the Voluntary Carbon Market, Core Carbon Principles (additionality, permanence, no double-counting, robust quantification), https://icvcm.org/the-core-carbon-principles/',
     'Core integrity failures recognizable in carbon markets: non-additionality (the activity would have occurred without credit revenue, so reductions are not real); double counting (the same tonne claimed toward two targets or sold twice); phantom or over-credited reductions from inflated baselines and non-conservative quantification; and reversal of stored carbon with no permanence safeguard. The CCPs require reductions be additional, permanent or reversal-protected, robustly and conservatively quantified, and counted only once. Recognition red flags: vague methodology, no registry retirement record, suspiciously large issuance versus credible baseline, no buffer reserve. Remedy: verify against an approved registry, conservative crediting, and independent validation before relying on a credit.'),
    ('carbon_eu_ets_vat_fraud',
     'EU ETS and the 2009-2010 carbon VAT carousel fraud lessons',
     'Europol, Carbon Credit fraud causes more than 5 billion euros damage for European Taxpayer (9 Dec 2009), https://www.europol.europa.eu/media-press/newsroom/news/carbon-credit-fraud-causes-more-5-billion-euros-damage-for-european-taxpayer',
     'Europol estimated VAT carousel (missing-trader intra-community) fraud in EU ETS allowances cost European taxpayers up to 5 billion euros in the 18 months to December 2009. Traders bought allowances cross-border VAT-free, resold them domestically with VAT added, then vanished without remitting the VAT, keeping it as profit. In some countries up to 90 percent of market volume was fraudulent. Recognition red flags: rapid round-tripping of allowances, shell intermediaries, volumes far exceeding real hedging need. Remedy: France, the Netherlands, the UK, and Spain applied a VAT reverse-charge so the buyer accounts for VAT, after which fraudulent volumes collapsed; registry and KYC controls remain the standing safeguard.'),
    ('carbon_paris_article6',
     'Paris Agreement Article 6: corresponding adjustments to avoid double counting',
     'UNFCCC, Article 6.2 Reference Manual for the accounting, reporting and review of cooperative approaches (April 2025), https://unfccc.int/sites/default/files/resource/Article_6.2_Reference_Manual.pdf',
     'Article 6 lets Parties cooperate on climate targets. Article 6.2 covers cooperative approaches transferring internationally transferred mitigation outcomes (ITMOs); Article 6.4 is a UNFCCC-supervised crediting mechanism (PACM). The cornerstone safeguard is the corresponding adjustment: when an ITMO is transferred, the seller adds the tonne back to its inventory and the buyer subtracts it, like double-entry bookkeeping, preventing two countries claiming one reduction. Parties must report authorization, ITMO metrics, and how environmental integrity is ensured. Recognition red flags: a unit used in a national target with no corresponding adjustment, missing authorization, or no transparent tracking. Remedy: robust accounting, initial reports, and technical review under the transparency framework.'),
    ('carbon_sec_tcfd_disclosure',
     'SEC climate disclosure rule and the TCFD framework',
     'U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, SEC Adopts Rules to Enhance and Standardize Climate-Related Disclosures for Investors (6 Mar 2024), https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2024-31',
     "The SEC's 2024 climate-disclosure rule, built on Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) pillars, requires registrants to disclose material climate risks, board and management governance of those risks, impacts on strategy and business model, risk-management processes, and material climate targets or goals. Large accelerated and accelerated filers must disclose material Scope 1 and Scope 2 GHG emissions under phased-in assurance; Scope 3 was dropped. Recognition red flags: a climate target announced with no governance or financial-effect disclosure, or emissions claims with no assurance. The rule's effective date was later delayed amid litigation. Remedy: standardized, investor-facing, assured disclosure deters selective or unverifiable climate claims."),
    ('carbon_esg_fund_labels_sfdr',
     'Greenhushing and misleading ESG fund labels under EU SFDR',
     "ESMA, Final Report - Guidelines on funds' names using ESG or sustainability-related terms (ESMA34-472-440), https://www.esma.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2024-05/ESMA34-472-440_Final_Report_Guidelines_on_funds_names.pdf",
     "Under the Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR), Article 8 funds promote environmental or social characteristics and Article 9 funds pursue a sustainable investment objective. ESMA's fund-naming guidelines require at least 80 percent of investments to meet the characteristics when ESG terms appear in a name, apply Paris-aligned or climate-transition benchmark exclusions, and demand that sustainability-named funds also hold a meaningful share of sustainable investments. Recognition red flags: a sustainable-named fund still heavily fossil-exposed, mass reclassification of Article 9 to Article 8 to dodge scrutiny, or strategic renaming to retain excluded holdings; greenhushing is under-reporting ESG action to avoid challenge. Remedy: naming thresholds, exclusions, and principal-adverse-impact disclosure."),
    ('carbon_redd_forestry_integrity',
     'REDD+ and forestry-offset integrity recognition',
     'UNFCCC REDD+ Info Hub, Forest reference emission levels fact sheet, https://redd.unfccc.int/fact-sheets/forest-reference-emission-levels.html',
     'REDD+ rewards reduced emissions from deforestation and forest degradation. Performance is measured against a forest reference emission level (FREL/FRL) expressed in tonnes CO2 equivalent per year, set transparently from historic data and assessed by UNFCCC LULUCF experts to be complete, consistent, and accurate. The Cancun safeguards and national forest monitoring (MRV) underpin results-based payments. Recognition red flags: a baseline inflated above credible historic trends (manufacturing paper reductions), leakage of deforestation to nearby areas, and reversal from fire or logging with no permanence buffer. Remedy: independent technical assessment of reference levels, conservative baselines, leakage accounting, buffer reserves, and safeguard information systems to avoid over-crediting.'),
    ('carbon_mrv_verification',
     'MRV measurement, reporting and verification safeguards',
     'UNFCCC REDD+ Info Hub, Forest reference emission levels / MRV, https://redd.unfccc.int/fact-sheets/forest-reference-emission-levels.html; ICVCM Core Carbon Principles (robust validation and verification), https://icvcm.org/the-core-carbon-principles/',
     'Measurement, reporting, and verification (MRV) is the evidence backbone of credible climate accounting: emissions and removals are quantified with transparent methods, reported against an assessed reference level, then checked by independent experts or accredited third-party auditors. The Core Carbon Principles require robust independent third-party validation and verification plus conservative quantification. Recognition red flags: self-reported reductions with no independent audit, methodology that is opaque or non-conservative, infrequent monitoring that hides reversals, or verifier conflicts of interest. Remedy: accredited independent verifiers, conservative and transparent quantification, periodic re-verification, and public registry records so claims are auditable rather than asserted.'),
    ('carbon_emerging_credit_risks',
     'Emerging plastic and biodiversity credit market risks',
     'The Royal Society, Proceedings B, Towards high-integrity biodiversity credits (2025), https://royalsocietypublishing.org/rspb/article/292/2053/20250990/234518/Towards-high-integrity-biodiversity-credits',
     'Plastic and biodiversity credit markets are young and largely unstandardized, so they risk repeating carbon-market failures. Researchers warn that without rigorous governance and independent verification, advances in metrics alone will not stop over-crediting, greenwashing, and inequitable benefit sharing; inconsistent additionality and permanence definitions and weak tracking allow the same recovered plastic or habitat outcome to be double counted. Recognition red flags: credits used to delay actual impact reduction, no third-party verification, vague baselines, and unclear data governance. Remedy: standardized rules, verification independence, transparent risk and data governance, and buyer demand for credits verified for real, durable, additional impact rather than volume alone.'),
    ('carbon_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and remedy: registry safeguards, buffers, and delisting',
     'Verra, Frequently Asked Questions (VCS registry, retirement, buffer pool, cancellation), https://verra.org/faq/',
     'Registry safeguards are the front line of enforcement. In the Verified Carbon Standard, each credit carries a registry serial number and is retired (taken out of circulation) when used, preventing resale and double counting. Land-based projects place a risk-adjusted share of credits in a global buffer pool that Verra controls and cancels when reversals occur; projects must report substantial reversals within 30 days. Non-conformance with MRV can trigger holds, late-to-verify status, buffer cancellation, or full project delisting, and registries can deny or cancel credits when integrity is impacted. Recognition red flags: credits with no retirement record or a delisted project. Remedy: independent audit oversight, public registry status, buffer-backed reversal cover, and cancellation authority.'),
    ('cft_fatf_rec5',
     'FATF Recommendation 5: Criminalising Terrorist Financing',
     'FATF, Guidance on Criminalising Terrorist Financing (Recommendation 5), https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/Criminalising-terrorist-financing.html',
     'FATF Recommendation 5 requires every country to make terrorist financing a distinct criminal offence, built on the 1999 Terrorist Financing Convention. The offence covers wilfully providing or collecting funds or assets, by any means, directly or indirectly, with the intention or knowledge they are to be used by a terrorist or terrorist organisation. Crucially it applies even with no link to a specific terrorist act and even if no attack ever occurs. Recognition signal: a financing or facilitation case need not show a completed bombing to be unlawful; the intent or knowledge behind the collection of funds is itself the offence. Remedy path: domestic prosecution with proportionate, dissuasive penalties, reaching legal entities as well as individuals.'),
    ('cft_fatf_rec6',
     'FATF Recommendation 6: Targeted Financial Sanctions for Terrorism',
     'FATF, International Best Practices: Targeted Financial Sanctions Related to Terrorism (Recommendation 6), https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/Bpp-finsanctions-tf-r6.html',
     "FATF Recommendation 6 obliges countries to implement targeted financial sanctions to comply with UN Security Council resolutions on terrorism, including UNSCR 1267 and successors and UNSCR 1373. Funds and assets of designated persons and entities must be frozen without delay and made unavailable. 'Without delay' means acting on reasonable grounds to suspect a person finances or supports terrorism, to prevent flight or dissipation of assets. Recognition signal: a properly listed name triggers an immediate freeze obligation, not a discretionary review. Designation reaches persons acting on behalf of, or owned or controlled by, listed parties. Remedy and safeguards: countries must provide procedures to designate, to de-list, and to protect the rights of those affected."),
    ('cft_fatf_rec8_npo',
     'FATF Recommendation 8: Protecting Non-Profit Organisations from Abuse',
     'FATF, Non-profit organisations (Recommendation 8) and Best Practices Paper, https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/topics/non-profit-organisations.html',
     'FATF Recommendation 8, revised in 2023, asks countries to protect non-profit organisations from terrorist-financing abuse using focused, proportionate, risk-based measures rather than blanket regulation. A country reviews its NPO sector, identifies the narrow subset at risk by virtue of activities or operating areas, and applies targeted oversight. The standard explicitly states NPOs need not be designated as reporting entities or subjected to full customer due diligence. Recognition signal: the risk is concentrated in service NPOs operating near active terrorist threats, not the sector as a whole. Remedy and safeguard: measures must not suppress legitimate civil society; FATF added a 2025 procedure for countries, the IMF and World Bank to raise undue-targeting consequences.'),
    ('cft_un_1267_1373',
     'UN Security Council 1267/1989/2253 and 1373 Sanctions Framework',
     "UN Security Council, ISIL (Da'esh) and Al-Qaida Sanctions Committee (resolutions 1267/1989/2253), https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/sanctions/1267",
     "The 1267 regime, established in 1999 and expanded by resolutions 1989 (2011) and 2253 (2015) to cover ISIL (Da'esh), imposes three measures on listed parties: an assets freeze, a travel ban, and an arms embargo. All states must freeze without delay the funds and economic resources of designated individuals and entities. Listing criteria cover participating in financing, planning, facilitating, or supporting acts of ISIL or Al-Qaida, including recruiting and supplying arms. Recognition signal: association with a listed group through funding or material support is itself a listing basis. UNSCR 1373 (2001) separately obliges every state to criminalise terrorist financing and freeze terrorist assets. Remedy: the Office of the Ombudsperson reviews de-listing requests."),
    ('cft_un_1999_convention',
     '1999 International Convention for the Suppression of Financing of Terrorism',
     'UN, International Convention for the Suppression of the Financing of Terrorism (1999), https://legal.un.org/avl/ha/icsft/icsft.html',
     'Adopted by the UN General Assembly on 9 December 1999 and in force since 10 April 2002, this Convention is the treaty foundation of terrorist-financing criminalisation. Article 2 makes it an offence to provide or collect funds, directly or indirectly, unlawfully and wilfully, with the intention or knowledge they will be used to carry out a terrorist act. The offence is complete on the collection or provision of funds, regardless of whether the funds were actually used and even if no attack occurs. Recognition signal: intent or knowledge, not a completed act, defines liability. Article 5 extends responsibility to legal persons. Remedy: states must identify, freeze, seize and confiscate such funds (Article 8) and either prosecute or extradite suspects (Article 10).'),
    ('cft_hawala_mvts',
     'Hawala and Informal Value-Transfer Systems Recognition',
     'FATF, Guidance for a Risk-Based Approach: Money or Value Transfer Services, https://www.fatf-gafi.org/content/dam/fatf-gafi/guidance/Guidance-RBA-money-value-transfer-services.pdf.coredownload.pdf',
     'Money or value transfer services (MVTS) accept cash or value in one place and pay a corresponding sum to a beneficiary elsewhere through a message or settlement network. Region-specific forms include hawala, hundi and fei-chen. Under FATF Recommendation 14, countries must ensure MVTS providers are licensed or registered and subject to monitoring; unregistered or hawala-and-other-similar-service-providers (HOSSPs) operating outside supervision are higher risk. Recognition signal: value moved with no regulated institution, settled by trust or offsetting rather than a wire trail, and concentrated in conflict zones or remote areas with limited financial infrastructure. Remedy: bring providers into licensing and supervision, and route suspicious activity to the financial intelligence unit; abuse does not make the whole sector illicit.'),
    ('cft_npo_abuse_redflags',
     'Charity and NPO Abuse Red Flags for Terrorism Diversion',
     'FATF, Best Practices: Combating the Terrorist Financing Abuse of Non-Profit Organisations (R.8), https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Financialinclusionandnpoissues/Bpp-combating-abuse-npo.html',
     'Terrorist actors can use charitable fundraising as cover, diverting donations intended for relief toward terrorism. FATF identifies service NPOs operating in close proximity to an active terrorist threat as the most exposed, because of the resources, geographic reach, and access to vulnerable populations involved. Recognition signals include mismatch between stated charitable purpose and where funds actually flow, opaque onward transfers to high-risk areas, lack of records reconciling money raised to programmes delivered, and links between organisers and listed parties. Remedy and safeguard: apply focused, proportionate oversight and report suspicions, while avoiding indiscriminate de-risking that suppresses legitimate humanitarian work; FATF stresses red-flag indicators must guide investigation, not blanket suspicion.'),
    ('cft_financing_typologies',
     'Terrorist Financing Typologies: Small-Value, Self-Funding, Crowdfunding',
     'FATF, Emerging Terrorist Financing Risks (2015) and Comprehensive Update on Terrorist Financing Risks (2025), https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/Emerging-terrorist-financing-risks.html',
     'Modern terrorist financing increasingly relies on small-scale methods: self-funding from salaries or loans, contributions from family and relatives, and crowdfunding, especially among small cells and lone actors. FATF reports operations becoming decentralised, with regional hubs and self-financed cells. Funds are raised, moved and stored through cash, hawala and MVTS, online payment services, formal banking, social media and crowdfunding features, and virtual assets, alongside abuse of legal entities. Recognition signal: low individual amounts that evade thresholds, hard to distinguish from ordinary activity, aggregated across many small transfers. Remedy: rely on patterns of payments, travel and online activity plus FATF risk indicators, and route suspicious-transaction reports to the FIU for analysis rather than expecting a single large flagged transfer.'),
    ('cft_sanctions_screening',
     'Designated-Party and Sanctions Screening Obligations',
     'FATF, International Best Practices on Targeted Financial Sanctions (R.6) and FATF Recommendations 2012, https://www.fatf-gafi.org/content/dam/fatf-gafi/guidance/BPP-Fin-Sanctions-TF-R6.pdf.coredownload.pdf',
     'Financial institutions must screen against lists of designated persons and entities, take freezing action, and prohibit transactions with them, including parties acting on their behalf or at their direction and entities they own or control. FATF notes name screening alone is not sufficient because sanctions reach indirect relationships, adding identification complexity. Relevant identifiers include alternate names and spellings, date and place of birth, address, nationality, and identification or passport numbers. Recognition signal: a customer, counterparty or beneficial owner matching or closely resembling a listed identity, or a front acting for one. Remedy: freeze without delay, report the match to competent authorities, and rely on FIU and law-enforcement guidance to confirm and act on screening hits.'),
    ('cft_crowdfunding_recognition',
     'Crowdfunding and Social-Media Terrorist Financing Recognition',
     'FATF, Crowdfunding for Terrorism Financing (October 2023), https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/crowdfunding-for-terrorism-financing.html',
     "FATF's 2023 report sets out the main ways crowdfunding is exploited for terrorist financing: abuse of humanitarian, charitable or non-profit causes; use of dedicated crowdfunding platforms or websites; and use of social media platforms and messaging apps to solicit donations, often interacting with virtual assets. Recognition signals include campaigns whose emotive humanitarian framing masks diversion, fundraising pages linked to known extremist actors, rapid solicitation across messaging apps, and conversion of donations into anonymity-enhancing crypto-assets. Remedy: platforms, payment processors and supervisors apply FATF risk indicators (Annex A), file suspicious-transaction reports, and support investigators, while avoiding unintended consequences that block legitimate charitable appeals."),
    ('cft_ftf_financing',
     'Foreign Terrorist Fighter Financing Recognition',
     'FATF, Emerging Terrorist Financing Risks (2015) and Comprehensive Update on Terrorist Financing Risks (2025), https://www.fatf-gafi.org/media/fatf/documents/reports/Emerging-Terrorist-Financing-Risks.pdf',
     'Foreign terrorist fighters (FTFs) predominantly use traditional, low-profile methods to fund travel to conflict areas, especially self-funding from wages, savings, loans and the sale of personal assets, supplemented by family contributions and crowdfunding. Money or value transfer services are used to move funds via remitters with agents near conflict-zone borders. Recognition signals include small transfers toward transit or border regions, sudden liquidation of assets ahead of travel, and payment patterns aligned with one-way movement to a conflict theatre. FATF notes these flows are extremely hard to distinguish from ordinary activity. Remedy: combine travel, payment and social-media indicators, and channel suspicious-transaction reports to the FIU so a terrorist-financing link can be established and assets restrained.'),
    ('cft_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and Remedy: Asset Freezing, Delisting and FIU Reporting',
     "UN, Office of the Ombudsperson to the ISIL (Da'esh) and Al-Qaida Sanctions Committee, https://main.un.org/securitycouncil/en/ombudsperson",
     'The terrorist-financing enforcement chain runs from detection to restraint to due-process review. Financial institutions file suspicious-transaction reports to the financial intelligence unit; FIUs (cooperating through the Egmont Group) analyse them and can establish a terrorist-financing link for law enforcement. Designated parties face an immediate assets freeze under UN and domestic sanctions, supported by confiscation and asset-recovery measures. Recognition signal: an enforced case shows a documented reporting trail, a freeze, and an evidentiary basis, not an arbitrary block. Remedy and safeguard: the UN Office of the Ombudsperson provides independent review of de-listing requests for the ISIL and Al-Qaida list, the only Security Council regime with such a procedure, protecting due process for those who should not be listed.'),
    ('marriage_cedaw',
     'CEDAW Article 16 equality in marriage and Joint General Recommendation 31 on harmful practices',
     'Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women, Art. 16; CEDAW/CRC Joint General Recommendation No. 31 / General Comment No. 18 (2019), OHCHR. https://www.ohchr.org/en/documents/general-comments-and-recommendations/joint-general-recommendation-no-31-committee',
     'CEDAW Article 16 obliges States parties to eliminate discrimination against women in all matters relating to marriage and family relations, guaranteeing the same right to enter marriage freely and to choose a spouse only with free and full consent. The Committee treats the betrothal and marriage of a child as having no legal effect. Joint General Recommendation No. 31 (with CRC General Comment No. 18) frames female genital mutilation, child marriage and forced marriage as harmful practices rooted in gender inequality, and requires States to prevent, prohibit and provide remedy. For an integrity reviewer, recognition centres on absence of free consent, a party below the lawful age, and family or community pressure - never on facilitating any such union.'),
    ('marriage_child_minimum_age',
     'Child marriage and the minimum-age-of-eighteen standard under the CRC and SDG 5.3',
     'Convention on the Rights of the Child, Art. 1; CEDAW/CRC Joint General Recommendation 31 / General Comment 18; SDG target 5.3; UNICEF child marriage data. https://data.unicef.org/topic/child-protection/child-marriage/',
     'The Convention on the Rights of the Child defines a child as every person below eighteen, and the CEDAW and CRC Committees recommend that States set the minimum age of marriage at eighteen for girls and boys, with no lower exception based on parental consent. Children cannot give free, prior and informed consent to a marriage partner or its timing, so any marriage of a minor is treated as a harmful practice. Sustainable Development Goal target 5.3 commits States to eliminate child, early and forced marriage by 2030; UNICEF estimates around 640 million girls and women alive today were married in childhood. Recognition focuses on a party under eighteen, abrupt withdrawal from school, or planned travel for a wedding.'),
    ('marriage_forced_trafficking_nexus',
     'Forced and servile marriage as a slavery and trafficking nexus for recognition',
     'Supplementary Convention on the Abolition of Slavery (1956), Art. 1(c); UN Protocol to Prevent, Suppress and Punish Trafficking in Persons (Palermo); UNODC. https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/frontpage/2020/October/The-UN-Trafficking-in-Persons-Protocol-A-Tool-To-Tackle-Forced-Marriage.html',
     "The 1956 Supplementary Slavery Convention lists servile marriage among institutions and practices similar to slavery: any practice whereby a woman, without a right to refuse, is promised or given in marriage on payment to her parents, guardian or family, transferred to another for value, or inherited on a husband's death. Where a marriage is achieved through coercion, deception or abuse of vulnerability and leads to exploitation, the Palermo Protocol act-means-purpose test can be met, making it human trafficking; the victim's apparent consent is irrelevant once coercive means are present. Recognition looks for sale or payment, no right to refuse, confinement and exploitation - identifying the pattern, never enabling it."),
    ('marriage_recognition_redflags',
     'Recognition red flags that may indicate a forced marriage situation',
     'HM Government Multi-Agency Statutory Guidance and Practice Guidelines on Forced Marriage; Forced Marriage Unit. https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-right-to-choose-government-guidance-on-forced-marriage/multi-agency-statutory-guidance-for-dealing-with-forced-marriage-and-multi-agency-practice-guidelines-handling-cases-of-forced-marriage-accessible',
     "UK multi-agency guidance lists indicators that, in combination, may signal forced marriage: extended absence or sudden withdrawal from school or work, a drop in performance, excessive parental control of movements, money or communications, and planned or unexplained travel to a family's country of origin. Other flags include reports of family conflict, running away, being accompanied and monitored at appointments, self-harm, depression or substance use, and threats framed around family honour after a relationship or pregnancy. Guidance cautions that many at risk show no outward signs, so any disclosure must be taken seriously and handled in private. A reviewer surfaces these patterns for safeguarding referral only; it never advises how to arrange or conceal such a marriage."),
    ('marriage_fgm_law',
     'Female genital mutilation as a harmful practice in law and recognition',
     'WHO classification of FGM (four types); CEDAW/CRC Joint General Recommendation 31; UNICEF FGM statistics 2024; SDG 5.3. https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/female-genital-mutilation',
     'The World Health Organization defines female genital mutilation as all procedures involving partial or total removal of the external female genitalia or other injury to the female genital organs for non-medical reasons, classified into four types including infibulation. FGM is recognised as a violation of the rights of girls and women, including the rights to health, physical integrity, and freedom from torture or cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment, and can cause death. CEDAW and CRC treat it as a harmful practice that States must prohibit and remedy under SDG target 5.3. UNICEF estimates over 230 million women and girls alive today have been subjected to FGM. Recognition centres on risk indicators and protection; this material is strictly non-operational.'),
    ('marriage_dowry_abuse',
     'Recognition of dowry-related abuse as a harmful practice',
     'Dowry Prohibition Act 1961 (India), as amended; Indian Penal Code s.304B (dowry death) and s.498A (cruelty); CEDAW harmful-practices framework. https://www.indiacode.nic.in/bitstream/123456789/5556/1/dowry_prohibition.pdf',
     "Dowry is property, goods or money given by or for either party in connection with a marriage. India's Dowry Prohibition Act 1961 prohibits the giving or taking of dowry, and the Penal Code addresses dowry-related cruelty and dowry death, where a woman dies unnaturally within years of marriage after harassment connected to a dowry demand. CEDAW treats dowry-linked violence as a harmful practice and a form of gender-based violence requiring prevention and remedy. For recognition, warning signs include escalating demands for money or goods from a spouse or in-laws after marriage, coercion, isolation, and threats. A reviewer flags these coercion and extortion patterns for protective response; it does not facilitate or normalise dowry demands."),
    ('marriage_uk_fmpo',
     'UK Forced Marriage Protection Orders and the criminal offence of forced marriage',
     'Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007; Anti-social Behaviour, Crime and Policing Act 2014 (forced marriage offence). https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2007/20/contents',
     'The Forced Marriage (Civil Protection) Act 2007 lets a court make a Forced Marriage Protection Order to prevent a marriage, stop an attempted one, or protect a person already in a forced marriage; terms can prohibit, restrict or require conduct, such as surrendering a passport or barring contact. Force includes coercion by threats or other psychological means, and the pressure may target the victim, another person, or the perpetrator. An application may be made by the person at risk, a relevant third party, or, with permission, others; courts may attach a power of arrest. Since 2014 it is also a criminal offence to force someone to marry. Recognition supports referral and protection only, never circumvention.'),
    ('marriage_bride_trafficking',
     'Recognition of bride trafficking and coerced transnational marriage',
     'UN Protocol to Prevent, Suppress and Punish Trafficking in Persons (Palermo); UNODC Issue Paper on Interlinkages between Trafficking in Persons and Marriage (2020). https://www.unodc.org/documents/human-trafficking/2020/UNODC_Interlinkages_Trafficking_in_Persons_and_Marriage.pdf',
     'Bride trafficking occurs when a person is recruited, transported or transferred into marriage through abduction, deception, abuse of vulnerability, or payment, often arranged by family, brokers or agencies for financial gain and leading to sexual exploitation, domestic servitude or forced labour. UNODC identifies young women from disadvantaged backgrounds, and cross-border movement, as common features; restricted movement and isolation from family are typical. Where coercive or fraudulent means are used, the Palermo Protocol applies and apparent consent is vitiated. Recognition indicators include a marriage brokered for a fee, removal to another country or region, confiscated documents, and inability to leave. This material aids identification and protection only and contains no operational guidance.'),
    ('marriage_consent_capacity',
     'Consent and capacity standards in marriage law',
     'Universal Declaration of Human Rights Art. 16(2); Convention on Consent to Marriage, Minimum Age for Marriage and Registration of Marriages (1962), Arts. 1-3, OHCHR. https://www.ohchr.org/en/instruments-mechanisms/instruments/convention-consent-marriage-minimum-age-marriage-and',
     'The 1962 Marriage Convention requires that no marriage be entered into without the full and free consent of both parties, expressed in person before the competent authority and witnesses; it requires States to set a minimum marriage age and to register all marriages. Consent must be genuine, informed, and free of duress, fraud, or incapacity, and a person below the lawful age cannot validly consent. Capacity also fails where a person cannot understand the nature of marriage, including some adults with cognitive impairment, who are entitled to protection rather than presumed agreement. Recognition focuses on signs that consent was absent, coerced, or impossible - a missing party, fear, or pressure - and routes such cases to safeguarding, never to facilitation.'),
    ('marriage_honour_based_abuse',
     'Recognition of so-called honour-based abuse linked to marriage',
     'Crown Prosecution Service legal guidance, So-Called Honour-Based Abuse. https://www.cps.gov.uk/legal-guidance/so-called-honour-based-abuse',
     "So-called honour-based abuse is described by the Crown Prosecution Service as an incident or crime involving violence, threats, intimidation, coercion or psychological, physical, sexual, financial or emotional abuse committed to protect or defend the perceived honour of an individual, family or community for an alleged breach of its code of behaviour. It is not a single offence but a context for crimes such as forced marriage, coercive control and, in extreme cases, killing, and is frequently perpetrated collectively by several family or community members. Recognition indicators include surveillance and control of a person's movements, contacts and clothing, threats after a relationship choice, and sudden travel. A reviewer flags these patterns for safeguarding and never endorses honour-based control."),
    ('marriage_remedy_protection',
     'Remedy and protection pathways for victims of forced marriage and harmful practices',
     'HM Government forced marriage guidance and Forced Marriage Unit; UN Women guidance on remedies for harmful practices. https://www.gov.uk/guidance/forced-marriage',
     "Survivors and people at risk are entitled to protection and remedy. Civil protection orders can bar a marriage, restrain contact, or require surrender of travel documents, and criminal law may apply to coercion and related offences. Support pathways generally include confidential helplines and safeguarding referral, emergency and longer-term safe accommodation, legal advice on protection orders, annulment, separation or custody, and emotional support and safety planning that respects the person's wishes and confidentiality. Specialist services and government units can coordinate response, including where a person has been taken abroad. Specific contact details change over time and should be confirmed against current official portals. This material supports access to protection and never assists abusers."),
    ('marriage_international_frameworks',
     'Key international frameworks against forced and child marriage',
     'Convention on Consent to Marriage, Minimum Age for Marriage and Registration of Marriages (1962), OHCHR; Council of Europe Convention on Preventing and Combating Violence against Women (Istanbul Convention), Arts. 37-38. https://rm.coe.int/168046031c',
     'Two frameworks anchor protection. The 1962 Marriage Convention requires full and free consent, a legislated minimum age, and registration of all marriages. The Council of Europe Istanbul Convention requires States to criminalise the intentional forcing of an adult or child into marriage and the luring of a person abroad for that purpose (Article 37), and to criminalise female genital mutilation (Article 38), with provision for extraterritorial jurisdiction over nationals and residents. Together with CEDAW, the CRC and the Palermo Protocol, they establish that forced and child marriage and FGM are rights violations States must prevent and remedy. Recognition aligns case patterns with these standards to support protection - it does not provide any operational instruction.'),
    ('sport_macolin_convention',
     'Macolin Convention on the Manipulation of Sports Competitions (CETS 215)',
     'Council of Europe Convention on the Manipulation of Sports Competitions (Macolin Convention, CETS No. 215), https://www.coe.int/en/web/sport/macolin',
     'The Macolin Convention is the first legally binding international instrument against the manipulation of sports competitions. Its stated purpose is to prevent, detect and punish manipulation, and to enhance information exchange and cooperation among public authorities, sports organisations and betting operators. Manipulation is defined as an intentional arrangement, act or omission aimed at improperly altering the result or course of a competition to remove its unpredictable nature for an undue advantage. It can involve athletes, officials, support staff or others. Recognition relevance: legal authority establishing manipulation as a defined wrong, not a sporting grey area. The Convention entered into force on 1 September 2019 and has been ratified by a growing number of states. Remedy: signatories must enable detection, sanctions and cross-border cooperation.'),
    ('sport_match_fixing_redflags',
     'Recognition Red Flags for Competition Manipulation and Match-Fixing',
     'Sport Integrity Australia, Competition Manipulation and Sports Wagering, https://www.sportintegrity.gov.au/what-we-do/competition-manipulation-and-sports-wagering',
     'Competition manipulation is an intentional act to improperly alter the course or result of a competition for a benefit, and can be carried out by competitors, whole teams, support staff, match officials or venue staff. Motivations split into betting-driven profit and sport-related advantage such as securing an easier draw or qualification. Recognition red flags include an unsolicited approach to underperform or alter play, requests to share team or lineup information, unexplained gifts or payments tied to results, and contact from individuals linked to betting. A core integrity duty is mandatory reporting: failing to promptly report an approach is itself a policy violation. Remedy: in some jurisdictions manipulation carries criminal liability and long bans from sport, alongside disciplinary sanction under national integrity frameworks.'),
    ('sport_betting_integrity',
     'Sports-Betting Integrity Monitoring and Suspicious-Alert Systems',
     'International Betting Integrity Association (IBIA), Global Monitoring and Alert Platform, https://ibia.bet/monitoring',
     'Betting-integrity monitoring uses regulated-operator intelligence to surface manipulation that on-field observation cannot. Operators share unusual and suspicious patterns including volume spikes, behavioural anomalies, surges of new accounts, geographic clustering, suspicious account histories and cross-operator linkage of activity. The workflow is alert-and-cross-check: a member raises an alert, others validate it against their own books, analysts perform customer-level review beyond simple odds movement, and confirmed cases are reported to sports bodies, regulators and law enforcement. Recognition relevance: an anomalous betting footprint, not the scoreline, is the primary detection signal. Remedy: alert reports become evidence supporting disciplinary, regulatory and criminal investigation of suspected manipulation.'),
    ('sport_wada_code',
     'World Anti-Doping Code Anti-Doping Framework and Sanctions',
     'World Anti-Doping Agency, World Anti-Doping Code 2021, https://www.wada-ama.org/sites/default/files/resources/files/2021_wada_code.pdf',
     'The World Anti-Doping Code harmonises anti-doping rules across sports and countries. It defines anti-doping rule violations including the presence of a prohibited substance in a sample, use or attempted use, evasion or refusal of sample collection, tampering, trafficking and administration. The strict-liability principle means a violation occurs when a prohibited substance is found, regardless of intent or fault; this has been consistently upheld by the Court of Arbitration for Sport. Recognition relevance: detection rests on analytical findings and whereabouts or testing failures, with a published Prohibited List as the reference standard. Remedy: sanctions range up to multi-year ineligibility, with possible reduction where an athlete proves no fault or no significant fault, and aggravating circumstances can extend a first offence.'),
    ('sport_governance_corruption',
     'Corruption in Sports Governance and Reform Lessons (FIFA and IOC)',
     'Basel Institute on Governance, Final Report by the Independent Governance Committee to the FIFA Executive Committee, https://baselgovernance.org/sites/default/files/2019-01/final_report_by_igc_to_fifa_exco_en.pdf',
     'Governance scandals in major sports bodies exposed weak oversight of bidding, funds and ethics. The FIFA Independent Governance Committee recommended reforms on transparency, financial control, accountability and ethical conduct, leading to commitments to good-governance and human-rights principles, a revised ethics code, disclosure of development funds, confidential reporting mechanisms, salary disclosure, term limits, and integrity checks for officials. The IOC adopted comparable reforms after the Salt Lake City scandal, including an ethics commission, a code of ethics and term limits. Recognition relevance: red flags include opaque bid processes, conflicts of interest among decision-makers, and undisclosed payments. Remedy: independent oversight bodies, ethics enforcement and disclosure requirements deter and sanction governance corruption.'),
    ('sport_money_laundering',
     'Money Laundering Through Sport (FATF Typologies)',
     'Financial Action Task Force, Money Laundering through the Football Sector, https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/Moneylaunderingthroughthefootballsector.html',
     'FATF examined why the sport sector attracts illicit money as it grew into a global industry whose regulation lagged its financial flows. Identified vulnerabilities include club ownership and investment, player-transfer and image-rights payments, sponsorship and broadcasting deals, and betting and gaming channels. A known betting typology is staking funds so as to recover the value as verifiable winnings, obscuring the source. Recognition relevance: red flags include opaque ownership and financing of clubs, inflated or circular transfer fees, payments routed through intermediaries, and gambling activity inconsistent with a customer profile. Remedy: applying anti-money-laundering controls, customer due diligence, beneficial-ownership transparency and suspicious-transaction reporting to sport-related finance, with referral to financial-intelligence units.'),
    ('sport_athlete_trafficking_nexus',
     'Trafficking and Exploitation of Young Athletes (Recognition)',
     'International Organization for Migration, IOM Backs Mission 89 to Strengthen Fight Against Child Trafficking in Sport, https://www.iom.int/news/iom-backs-mission-89-strengthen-fight-against-child-trafficking-sport',
     'Trafficking in the sport context exploits aspiring young athletes lured by false promises of academy places or professional contracts, intersecting with the broader transfer business and with child-protection failures. Children account for a large share of identified trafficking victims globally, and youth are recorded in increasing numbers misled under sporting pretexts. Recognition relevance: red flags include unregulated academies, undocumented international movement of minors, recruiters demanding fees from families, confiscated identity documents, and abandonment of players abroad after failed trials. Remedy: safeguarding standards, accountability for training academies, integration of protection measures into recruitment systems, and referral of suspected trafficking to child-protection and law-enforcement authorities consistent with anti-trafficking frameworks.'),
    ('sport_insider_courtsiding',
     'Inside Information and Courtsiding Recognition in Sport',
     'International Tennis Integrity Agency, Tennis Anti-Corruption Program (TACP), https://www.itia.tennis/anti-corruption/tacp/',
     "Courtsiding is the prohibited transmission of live event data to a third party for betting purposes, exploiting the latency between the venue and broadcast so bets can be placed before bookmakers update odds; it most often targets low-profile matches with minimal coverage. Inside information covers non-public knowledge about likely participation or performance, such as a player's health, fitness, court or weather conditions, known to a covered person. Recognition relevance: red flags include spectators relaying contemporaneous scoring, requests for confidential team or fitness information, and bets timed to non-public knowledge. Remedy: under anti-corruption programs these acts are disciplinary offences carrying suspensions and fines, with permanent bans confirmed on appeal for serious or repeated breaches."),
    ('sport_age_fraud',
     'Age Fraud in Youth Sport Recognition and Verification',
     'Age fraud in association football (overview of FIFA MRI eligibility protocol), https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_fraud_in_association_football',
     "Age fraud is the misrepresentation of an athlete's age to gain a competitive advantage in age-restricted youth competition, undermining fair play and child protection. Document-only checks proved unreliable, so governing bodies adopted radiological screening: an MRI eligibility protocol scanning the wrist growth plate, graded on a scale where full fusion indicates an athlete is likely over the youth age limit. The method is an eligibility screen rather than an exact-age determination and is most reliable below the upper teenage years. Recognition relevance: red flags include physical maturity inconsistent with stated age, document discrepancies across records, and refusal of screening. Remedy: failed verification leads to disqualification, squad ejection and disciplinary sanction against responsible officials or federations."),
    ('sport_whistleblower_reporting',
     'Confidential Whistleblower and Reporting Platforms in Sport Integrity',
     'World Anti-Doping Agency, Speak Up - Report Doping, https://www.wada-ama.org/en/athletes-support-personnel/speak-report-doping',
     "Confidential reporting platforms let athletes, support personnel and others disclose information about possible anti-doping rule violations, manipulation or organisational non-compliance through a secure channel handled by an independent intelligence-and-investigations function. Information can be submitted in confidence, with protection of the source so investigators do not need to know the reporter's identity, and reporters may choose whether to cooperate further. Parallel platforms exist across testing agencies and national bodies. Recognition relevance: credible insider tips with hard evidence frequently expose systemic schemes that testing alone cannot. Remedy: corroborated reports feed formal investigations and sanctioning processes; integrity frameworks also impose duties to report approaches and to protect those who come forward in good faith."),
    ('sport_national_platforms',
     'National Platforms and Integrity Units (Macolin National Platform Model)',
     'World Lotteries / The Macolin Convention, The National Platform: Exchanging Key Information, https://publications.world-lotteries.org/guides-articles/macolin-convention-the-national-platform',
     'Under the Macolin Convention, each state establishes a national platform as the hub coordinating the fight against the manipulation of sports competitions. The platform centralises, analyses and shares information among competent public authorities, sports organisations and betting operators, and channels intelligence to international counterparts. It is described as the cornerstone of the transnational framework because it links State and non-State enforcement actors around a single information flow, particularly for betting-related match-fixing. Recognition relevance: the platform aggregates alerts, approaches and betting anomalies into a coherent intelligence picture no single actor holds. Remedy: it routes confirmed concerns to disciplinary, regulatory and criminal-justice authorities and enables coordinated cross-border response.'),
    ('sport_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and Remedy in Sports Integrity (Sanctions, Bans, Referral)',
     'International Tennis Integrity Agency, CAS Upholds Lifetime Ban (Alekseenko brothers), https://www.itia.tennis/news/sanctions/cas-upholds-lifetime-ban-alekseenko-brothers/',
     'Sports-integrity enforcement operates on two parallel tracks. Disciplinary sanctions imposed by sport bodies are civil in nature and distinct from criminal penalties; they range from fines and fixed-term suspensions to permanent ineligibility, with the Court of Arbitration for Sport hearing appeals and confirming lifetime bans for serious match-fixing. In parallel, conduct such as competition manipulation and related corruption can be prosecuted criminally where national law provides an offence. Recognition relevance: a single fixing scheme can trigger both a sporting ban and a criminal case on the same facts. Remedy: outcomes include bans, financial penalties, voided results, and referral of evidence to law enforcement, with the Macolin framework enabling cross-border cooperation between sporting and criminal authorities.'),
    ('insurance_overview',
     'Insurance Fraud Overview and Economic Scale',
     'NAIC, Insurance Fraud topic page (citing Coalition Against Insurance Fraud and Colorado State University Global, 2022) - https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/insurance-fraud',
     'Insurance fraud is a deliberate deception against or by an insurer for an illegitimate gain, occurring while buying, using, selling, or underwriting coverage. The Coalition Against Insurance Fraud estimates it costs U.S. businesses and consumers about 308.6 billion dollars a year; the FBI estimates the average family pays 400 to 700 dollars more in annual premiums. Reported losses concentrate in life (74.7B), Medicare (60B), property and casualty (45B), health (36.3B), and workers compensation (34B) lines. Recognition framing: regulators distinguish hard fraud (a deliberately staged or fabricated loss) from far more common soft fraud (exaggerating a real claim or misstating an application). Control and remedy: insurers run special investigation units, predictive analytics, and red-flag screening, and refer suspected cases to state fraud bureaus for civil and criminal action.'),
    ('insurance_staged_accident',
     'Staged-Accident and Auto-Insurance Fraud Recognition Red Flags',
     'National Insurance Crime Bureau (NICB), Staged Auto Accident Fraud - https://www.nicb.org/prevent-fraud-theft/staged-auto-accident-fraud',
     'NICB documents organized staged-collision schemes designed to make an innocent motorist appear at fault so criminals can file injury and damage claims. In a swoop-and-squat, a squat vehicle pulls in front of the victim while a swoop vehicle cuts off the squat car, forcing a sudden stop and rear-end crash; the swoop car flees. Drive-down variants involve a driver waving the victim forward, then blocking the path while a confederate strikes the victim. Recognition red flags: occupants who all claim injury despite minor impact, vehicles that flee, unsolicited tow or medical referrals, and damage inconsistent with the event. Schemes cluster in urban or affluent areas and target well-insured new, rental, or commercial vehicles. Control and remedy: do not admit fault, photograph the scene, and report suspected staging to insurers and the NICB tip line for investigation.'),
    ('insurance_health_payer_fraud',
     'Health-Insurance Fraud from the Payer Side: Phantom Billing and Fake Providers',
     'National Health Care Anti-Fraud Association (NHCAA), The Challenge of Health Care Fraud - https://www.nhcaa.org/tools-insights/about-health-care-fraud/the-challenge-of-health-care-fraud/',
     'NHCAA reports that most health care fraud is committed by a small number of dishonest providers, and in some cases by people only posing as providers. Recognition patterns on the payer side include billing for services never rendered, padding claims, upcoding to a higher-paid service with an inflated diagnosis, unbundling one procedure into separate codes, medically unnecessary testing, misrepresenting non-covered cosmetic care as covered, and kickbacks for referrals. Fraud-doers spread false billings across many payers, including Medicare and Medicaid, to lessen detection. Control and remedy: compare claims to medical records, review Explanation of Benefits notices, and share data across the public-private partnership. Under HIPAA (18 U.S.C. 1347) health care fraud is a federal crime carrying up to 10 years, rising to 20 years or life if a patient is injured or dies, plus restitution.'),
    ('insurance_workers_comp',
     'Workers-Compensation Fraud Recognition Signals',
     "National Insurance Crime Bureau (NICB), How Workers' Compensation Fraud Games the System - https://www.nicb.org/news/blog/how-workers-compensation-fraud-games-system",
     'NICB describes three workers-compensation fraud streams: claimant fraud (faking or exaggerating an on-the-job injury for wage benefits), provider fraud through medical mills where doctors and attorneys recruit claimants via runners and bill for services not provided, and employer premium fraud via under-reported headcount or manipulated codes. Recognition red flags: an injury reported Monday morning or late Friday, no witnesses, a description that does not support the claimed injury, timing around a strike, layoff, termination, or end of seasonal work, a claimant hard to reach who often changes physicians and addresses, and prior litigated claims. When two or more appear together, fraud risk rises. Control and remedy: surveillance, open-source intelligence, data analytics, and referral to state fraud bureaus support investigation; premium fraud is separately prosecutable.'),
    ('insurance_application_fraud',
     'Application and Premium Fraud: Material Misrepresentation and Rate Evasion',
     'NAIC, Insurance Fraud topic page - https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/insurance-fraud',
     'The NAIC defines a fraudulent insurance act as a knowing, intent-to-defraud misstatement of a fact material to an application, policy rating, or claim. Application and premium fraud are recognition categories where a buyer omits or misrepresents underwriting facts to obtain coverage or a lower rate. Common patterns: rate evasion by misstating where a vehicle is garaged or the county or state of residence, and fronting, where an experienced driver names themselves as primary operator to hide a younger, higher-risk driver. Comp premium fraud (payroll under-reporting and class-code manipulation) drives a substantial share of comp losses. Control and remedy: the NAIC urges every insurer to maintain an antifraud plan and operates an Online Fraud Reporting System; material misrepresentation can void coverage and trigger fraud-bureau referral.'),
    ('insurance_claims_padding',
     'Claims Padding and Inflated-Claim Recognition',
     'NAIC, Insurance Fraud topic page (soft-fraud definition) - https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/insurance-fraud',
     'Claims padding is a form of soft fraud: a legitimate loss exists, but the claimant inflates its value or adds items that were not lost or damaged. The NAIC notes soft fraud, exaggerating an otherwise legitimate claim, is more common than hard fraud and is still prosecutable. Recognition red flags: altered or handwritten invoices, photos, or repair estimates; claimed property not substantiated by receipts or pre-loss records; pre-existing damage attributed to a new event; theft lists expanded with items never owned; and inflated medical or lost-wage components after a crash. A contractor or shop that offers to handle everything and absorb the deductible is a further signal. Control and remedy: insurers verify ownership and pre-loss condition, use analytics to flag anomalies, and refer suspected padding to state fraud bureaus; padding to recover a deductible is still fraud, not victimless.'),
    ('insurance_acfe_fraud_tree',
     'The ACFE Occupational-Fraud Tree: Three Branches of Internal Fraud',
     'Association of Certified Fraud Examiners (ACFE), Occupational Fraud 2024: A Report to the Nations - https://www.acfe.com/-/media/files/acfe/pdfs/rttn/2024/2024-report-to-the-nations.pdf',
     'The ACFE Fraud Tree classifies occupational fraud into three branches. Asset misappropriation (theft or misuse of assets such as skimming, cash larceny, false billing, payroll, and expense schemes) is the most common at about 89 percent of cases but least costly, with a median loss near 120,000 dollars. Corruption (conflicts of interest, bribery, kickbacks, bid rigging, economic extortion) appeared in about 48 percent of cases at a median loss near 200,000 dollars. Financial-statement fraud (intentional misstatement or omission in reported figures) was least common at roughly 5 percent but most costly, with a median loss near 800,000 dollars. Recognition: each branch maps to distinct red flags and ledgers. Control and remedy: segregation of duties, independent audits, hotlines, and proactive data monitoring shrink both loss and duration before referral to investigators and prosecutors.'),
    ('insurance_payroll_ghost',
     'Ghost Employees and Payroll-Fraud Recognition',
     'Association of Certified Fraud Examiners (ACFE), Occupational Fraud 2024: A Report to the Nations (payroll scheme classification) - https://www.acfe.com/-/media/files/acfe/pdfs/rttn/2024/2024-report-to-the-nations.pdf',
     'A ghost employee, per the ACFE Fraud Examiners Manual, is someone on the payroll who does not work for the organization: a fabricated identity, a relative, or a terminated worker never removed. Payroll schemes are a recurring asset-misappropriation type, often running for months undetected. Recognition red flags: hours that never vary, multiple paychecks in one pay period, a check endorsed under a name different from the payee, a thin personnel file, insistence on paper checks over direct deposit, duplicate bank-account or address details across staff, and headcount that does not reconcile to records. Control and remedy: enforce segregation of duties so no one person prepares, approves, and distributes pay; involve HR and the supervisor in onboarding and termination; reconcile payroll registers to active-employee lists; and run surprise audits, with restitution where theft is confirmed.'),
    ('insurance_vendor_fraud',
     'Vendor and Procurement Fraud: Kickbacks, Shell Vendors, and Bid Rigging',
     'Association of Certified Fraud Examiners (ACFE), Occupational Fraud 2024: A Report to the Nations (corruption branch) - https://www.acfe.com/-/media/files/acfe/pdfs/rttn/2024/2024-report-to-the-nations.pdf',
     'Procurement fraud sits in the ACFE corruption branch and manipulates purchasing for private gain via kickbacks, collusion, bid rigging, shell vendors, and overbilling. Recognition red flags: a procurement officer with undisclosed personal or family ties to a vendor; an employee living beyond their means; vendor prices inflated well above market; bidders submitting identical or patterned prices; phantom vendors with no physical address, weak online footprint, or invoices for vague services; and one staffer who both selects and approves a supplier. Kickbacks commonly run a percentage of each contract and may be disguised as gifts, travel, or payments to shell entities. Control and remedy: require competitive bidding, conflict-of-interest disclosure, vendor master-file vetting and de-duplication, three-way invoice matching, and buyer rotation; anonymous tips often surface these schemes.'),
    ('insurance_whistleblower',
     'Internal-Fraud Whistleblowing and Tip Reporting',
     'Association of Certified Fraud Examiners (ACFE), Occupational Fraud 2024: A Report to the Nations (detection findings) - https://www.acfe.com/-/media/files/acfe/pdfs/rttn/2024/2024-report-to-the-nations.pdf',
     'The ACFE consistently finds tips are the leading way occupational fraud is detected, about 43 percent of cases, roughly three times more than internal audit or management review. Employees provide most tips (about 52 percent), with customers and vendors also significant, and many anonymous. Organizations with reporting hotlines detect fraud faster and at lower loss; web-based and email channels now rival or exceed telephone hotlines. Recognition and control framing: an accessible, well-publicized, anonymous reporting channel is itself a primary anti-fraud control, and visible whistleblower routes deter wrongdoing. Remedy: route reports to an independent function for triage, with anti-retaliation protections; in financial institutions, suspected fraud may also require a Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) to regulators, and insurance matters may be referred to the state fraud bureau.'),
    ('insurance_controls',
     'Anti-Fraud Controls: Segregation of Duties, Data Analytics, and Audits',
     'Association of Certified Fraud Examiners (ACFE), Fraud Magazine, Top Internal Controls That Reduce Fraud Losses (2024) - https://www.acfe.com/fraud-magazine/all-issues/issue/article?s=top-internal-controls-that-reduce-fraud-losses-2024',
     'ACFE research links specific anti-fraud controls to lower losses and faster detection. Proactive data monitoring and analysis cut median scheme duration by roughly 58 percent (from 24 to 10 months) and median loss by about 52 percent; surprise audits cut duration by about 50 percent and loss substantially. Controls tied to at least a 50 percent reduction in both loss and duration include surprise audits, financial-statement audits, reporting hotlines, and proactive data analysis. A core preventive control is segregation of duties, where no single person controls an entire transaction. Recognition framing: surprise audits, job rotation, and mandatory vacation remain underused despite their value. Remedy: combine preventive controls (separation of duties, codes of conduct, training) with detective controls (monitoring, audits, hotlines) and a defined escalation path to investigation.'),
    ('insurance_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and Remedy: State Fraud Bureaus, Prosecution, and Restitution',
     'NAIC, Insurance Fraud topic page and Antifraud (D) Task Force materials - https://content.naic.org/insurance-topics/insurance-fraud',
     'Insurance fraud is addressed through a layered enforcement system. Most U.S. states plus the District of Columbia operate insurance fraud bureaus whose investigators work with federal, state, and local law enforcement to investigate and prosecute. The NAIC coordinates these efforts, maintains shared databases of fraudulent-activity intelligence, and runs an Online Fraud Reporting System so consumers and departments can refer suspected fraud. Under the NAIC Insurance Fraud Prevention Model Act, those with reason to believe fraud has occurred must report it to the fraud bureau. Recognition and remedy framing: confirmed cases can lead to civil penalties, criminal charges, license revocation, and restitution to victims; health care fraud is separately a federal felony. Reporting to the insurer, the state department of insurance, and law enforcement is the front-line path to enforcement.'),
    ('land_forced_eviction_cescr',
     'Forced Evictions Prohibited Under CESCR General Comment No. 7',
     'UN CESCR General Comment No. 7 (1997), right to adequate housing (Art. 11.1): forced evictions, https://www.refworld.org/legal/general/cescr/1997/en/53063',
     'The UN Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights defines a forced eviction as the permanent or temporary removal of individuals, families or communities, against their will, from homes or land they occupy, without access to appropriate legal or other protection. Such evictions are prima facie incompatible with the Covenant. Recognition cues: removal with no genuine consultation, no adequate notice, no compensation, no chance to challenge the order, or action that leaves people homeless. Protection requires due process, exploration of alternatives, legal remedies, and that no one be rendered homeless or vulnerable to other rights violations. Where eviction is unavoidable, it must follow the law and provide review and redress.'),
    ('land_grabbing_recognition',
     'Recognizing Large-Scale Land Acquisitions and Land Grabbing Risk',
     'IIED/FAO/IFAD, Land grab or development opportunity? Agricultural investment and international land deals in Africa, https://www.iied.org/12561iied',
     'Large-scale land acquisitions can displace rural communities whose legitimate, customary, or informal tenure is undocumented, raising the risk that poor people are evicted without consent or fair compensation. Recognition red flags: deals negotiated only with central authorities while local users are excluded, missing or hidden contract terms, no public record of size or location in land registries, weak or absent community consultation, and promises of jobs or infrastructure that are not in the agreement. Protection rests on transparent registers, public oversight, recognition of existing tenure rights, fair negotiation, and grievance channels. Affected communities can seek information, document their occupation, and invoke responsible-tenure-governance standards rather than accept opaque transfers.'),
    ('land_vggt',
     'FAO Voluntary Guidelines on the Governance of Tenure (VGGT)',
     'FAO Voluntary Guidelines on the Responsible Governance of Tenure of Land, Fisheries and Forests (2012), https://www.fao.org/tenure/voluntary-guidelines/en/',
     'The VGGT, endorsed by the Committee on World Food Security in 2012, set internationally accepted standards for responsible governance of tenure to support food security and the progressive realization of the right to adequate food. They cover public, private, communal, indigenous, customary, and informal tenure, with emphasis on vulnerable and marginalized groups. They serve as a benchmark to judge whether proposed actions respect legitimate tenure rights. Recognition value: actions that ignore existing legitimate rights, lack transparency, or exclude affected people fall short of the guidelines. Protection follows from safeguarding legitimate tenure holders, recording rights, consulting affected parties, and providing accessible dispute resolution before any change in tenure.'),
    ('land_fpic_indigenous',
     'Indigenous Land Rights and Free, Prior and Informed Consent',
     'OHCHR, Consultation and free, prior and informed consent (FPIC); UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (Arts. 10, 28, 32), https://www.ohchr.org/en/indigenous-peoples/consultation-and-free-prior-and-informed-consent-fpic',
     'Under the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, indigenous peoples shall not be forcibly removed from their lands, and no relocation occurs without their free, prior and informed consent and agreement on just compensation. States must consult in good faith to obtain consent before projects affecting their lands or resources. FPIC means free of coercion, sought in advance, based on full information, and a genuine collective decision. Recognition red flags: consent claimed after a project starts, rushed timelines, partial or misleading information, pressure on representatives, or no agreement on compensation. Protection includes restitution or redress for lands taken without consent and respect for indigenous decision-making institutions.'),
    ('land_tenure_fraud',
     'Recognizing Land-Title and Deed Documentation Fraud',
     'Barnstable County Registry of Deeds, Title Fraud Explained: Risks and Safeguards, https://www.capecod.gov/departments/registry-of-deeds/resources/title-fraud-explained-risks-and-safeguards/',
     'Title or deed fraud transfers ownership using forged signatures or documents, or by deceiving an owner into signing over title, often during financial distress. Vacant land, rental, and absentee-owned property are common targets because suspicious filings go unnoticed. Recognition warning signs: property tax, mortgage, or utility bills that stop arriving or change address; a new deed or transfer the owner did not authorize appearing in county records; unexpected loan documents or notices of default on a property owned free and clear. Protection: regularly review official property records, use registry fraud-alert or notification programs, keep title insurance, and pursue a quiet-title action since a forged deed is void from the start. Verify records through the official recorder, not third-party claimants.'),
    ('land_predatory_housing',
     'Recognizing Unlawful Tenant Eviction and Predatory Housing Tactics',
     'FindLaw, Illegal Evictions and Landlord Harassment, https://www.findlaw.com/realestate/landlord-tenant-law/illegal-evictions-can-get-you-in-trouble-for-landlord-harassment.html',
     "Lawful eviction requires a court process and an officer-executed order; landlords cannot lawfully use self-help to force a tenant out. Recognition red flags for unlawful eviction and predatory housing: changing locks, removing doors or windows, blocking entry, shutting off power, water, or heat, removing a tenant's belongings, or threats and harassment to coerce departure without a court order. These self-help tactics are prohibited under landlord-tenant law across jurisdictions and can expose a landlord to damages, daily penalties, and civil or criminal liability. Protection for tenants: the only lawful path is a court case and a writ executed by the sheriff or marshal; tenants facing lockouts, utility cutoffs, or intimidation can seek restraining orders, damages, and legal-aid assistance."),
    ('land_corruption_redflags',
     'Land-Corruption Red Flags: Registry Manipulation and Fraudulent Titling',
     'Transparency International, Land corruption (priority area), https://www.transparency.org/en/our-priorities/land-corruption',
     'Land corruption is the abuse of land administration and management for private gain. Transparency International reports that worldwide roughly one in five people has paid a bribe to access land services. Recognition red flags: bribes demanded during titling or registration, fraud in real estate registries, cadastres, and titling processes, collusion among officials, notaries, lawyers, and brokers, sexual extortion in exchange for a title, communities excluded from land deals, opaque speculation, and people evicted with unfair or no compensation. Women and young people are disproportionately denied rights. Protection rests on transparent, participatory, and accountable land governance, open registries, published deal terms, independent oversight, and complaint and whistleblowing channels so manipulated transfers can be challenged and reversed.'),
    ('land_displacement_safeguards',
     'Development-Induced Displacement Safeguards Under World Bank ESS5',
     'World Bank Environmental and Social Standard 5 (ESS5): Land Acquisition, Restrictions on Land Use and Involuntary Resettlement, https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/f25263d7a7f5415b79b69c5cc65a96cf-(verify current number)/original/ESS5-Land-Acquisition-Restrictions-on-Land-Use-and-Involuntary-Resettlement-English.pdf',
     'ESS5 governs project-related land acquisition and land-use restrictions that cause physical displacement (loss of shelter or residential land) or economic displacement (loss of assets, income, or access to livelihoods). Its objectives are to avoid or minimize involuntary resettlement, avoid forced eviction, and mitigate unavoidable impacts. Recognition value: a project that displaces people without exploring design alternatives, without replacement-cost compensation, or without livelihood-restoration support fails the safeguard. Protection requires that affected people, including those without formal title, receive timely compensation at replacement cost, resettlement assistance, and measures to at least restore living standards. Host communities receiving displaced people are also considered, and consultation and grievance mechanisms are required.'),
    ('land_housing_discrimination',
     'Recognizing Housing Discrimination and Spatial Segregation',
     'UN CESCR General Comment No. 4 and OHCHR report A/HRC/49/48 on spatial segregation and the right to adequate housing, https://www.ohchr.org/en/documents/thematic-reports/ahrc4948-spatial-segregation-and-right-adequate-housing-report-special',
     'The right to adequate housing must be enjoyed without discrimination. Prohibited grounds include race, color, sex, language, religion, political or other opinion, national or social origin, property, birth, disability, health status, and sexual orientation. Spatial segregation reflects compounded and intersecting discrimination and becomes a rights violation when states fail to prevent enclaves that deny groups equal access to housing, land, water, sanitation, services, jobs, and advancement. Recognition red flags: denial of housing or tenure based on identity, exclusionary allocation, neglected or under-serviced areas confined to marginalized groups, and forced concentration. Protection requires non-discriminatory laws and allocation, equal access to services, and remedies for those subjected to discriminatory or segregating housing practices.'),
    ('land_informal_settlements',
     'Informal-Settlement and Slum-Dweller Tenure Rights',
     'UN-Habitat, Slum Upgrading (topic) and right to adequate housing, https://unhabitat.org/topic/slum-upgrading',
     "Informal settlements are residential areas where inhabitants lack security of tenure for the land or dwellings they occupy, ranging from squatting to informal renting. Most residents hold no formal title or lease, which exposes them to constant eviction risk and limits access to credit, services, and investment. Recognition value: residents long established on land but without documents still hold rights deserving protection, and their lack of paperwork does not justify forced eviction. UN-Habitat's slum-upgrading approach prioritizes safe housing, water, sanitation, and secure tenure with community participation. Mapping and land-information tools have given hundreds of thousands of households tenure security and prevented forced eviction. Protection means in-situ upgrading, tenure regularization, and consultation rather than demolition."),
    ('land_climate_displacement',
     'Climate Displacement, Land Pressures, and Restitution Protections',
     'UN Principles on Housing and Property Restitution for Refugees and Displaced Persons (Pinheiro Principles, 2005), UNHCR, https://www.unhcr.org/media/principles-housing-and-property-restitution-refugees-and-displaced-persons-pinheiro-0',
     'Climate change is among the drivers of displacement, alongside conflict and development projects, intensifying competition over land and housing. The Pinheiro Principles, endorsed in 2005, give practical guidance to protect the housing, land, and property rights of refugees and displaced persons, affirming a right to restitution and, where return is impossible, restitution in lieu or alternative durable solutions. Recognition value: displaced people retain rights to their homes and land even after flight, and secondary occupation or opaque reallocation of abandoned property is a red flag. Protection includes safeguarding records of ownership and occupation, just compensation where restitution is not feasible, and inclusive processes that address the structural causes of displacement rather than erasing prior tenure.'),
    ('land_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: Land Courts, Restitution, and Regularization',
     'Action4Justice, Land rights: settling land disputes through legal action, https://action4justice.org/legal_areas/land-rights/land-disputes-taking-legal-action/',
     'Victims of tenure-security violations, especially forced evictions, are owed an effective remedy. Avenues include local customary forums, national or local courts where the land sits, administrative land offices and commissions, and, where domestic options fail, regional human-rights courts and UN mechanisms. Courts can declare ownership or occupation rights, issue injunctions to halt evictions or unauthorized occupation, order restitution or alternative land and rehousing, award compensation, and invalidate permits, decrees, or contracts that legitimized a land grab, including where required consultation was omitted or corruption is proven. Recognition value: a remedy is incomplete if judgments are not enforced. Protection depends on accessible justice, accurate records, tenure regularization, and an enforcement body ensuring decisions are implemented.'),
    ('maritime_unclos_flag_state',
     'UNCLOS Flag-State Duties and the Genuine-Link Requirement',
     'UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) Arts. 91 and 94, summarized at https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/legal/pages/registration-of-ships-and-fraudulent-registration-matters.aspx',
     'Legal authority: UNCLOS Art. 91 lets every State set conditions for granting its nationality to ships but requires a genuine link between State and vessel. Art. 94 obliges the flag State to effectively exercise jurisdiction and control over administrative, technical, and social matters: maintaining a register, overseeing seaworthiness, crew manning, labour conditions, and safe signalling. Recognition red flags: a flag State with no real connection to the owner, an inability or unwillingness to inspect or discipline its fleet, or a vessel whose paper registry exists only to escape oversight. Remedy: where the genuine link is hollow, coastal and port States may inspect, detain, and refer the vessel, and bodies like the IMO record false-flag status so other States act.'),
    ('maritime_flags_of_convenience',
     'Recognizing Flags of Convenience and Open Registries',
     'ITF Flags of Convenience program, https://www.itfglobal.org/en/sector/seafarers/flags-convenience',
     "Authority and definition: a flag of convenience (FOC) or open registry flies the flag of a State other than the owner's, with no nationality or residency requirement; the ITF has campaigned against the practice since 1948 and lists 48 such registries (2025). Recognition red flags: registries that charge fees but accept none of a genuine flag State's safety, crew-welfare, or enforcement duties; weak or absent inspection; no restriction on crew nationality; and rapid re-flagging to dodge fisheries or labour controls. IUU and exploited vessels disproportionately use FOCs precisely because monitoring is lax. Remedy: ITF inspectors examined over 9,000 FOC vessels across 109 ports in 2025 and recovered owed wages; port State control and de-flagging deny non-compliant vessels safe harbour."),
    ('maritime_iuu_fishing',
     'Recognizing Illegal, Unreported and Unregulated (IUU) Fishing',
     'FAO International Plan of Action (IPOA-IUU) definition, https://www.fao.org/iuu-fishing/background/what-is-iuu-fishing/en/',
     "Legal authority: the FAO IPOA-IUU (2001) sets the standard definition. Illegal fishing is conducted without a State's permission or against its laws, or against a regional fisheries management organization's (RFMO) conservation measures. Unreported fishing is catch not reported or misreported to the authority in breach of national rules. Unregulated fishing is by stateless vessels, by vessels flagged to non-RFMO-party States, or in areas with no applicable measures. Recognition red flags: missing or contradicted catch records, fishing in closed seasons or prohibited zones, and absent authorization. Harm: it depletes stocks, threatens food security, and undercuts honest fishers. Remedy: flag, port, coastal, and market States coordinate controls; IUU vessel lists and market bans cut off the catch."),
    ('maritime_transshipment',
     'Recognizing High-Risk At-Sea Transshipment',
     'Global Fishing Watch transshipment analysis, https://globalfishingwatch.org/rfmo-transshipment/',
     'Context and authority: transshipment, transferring catch from a fishing vessel to a carrier (reefer) at sea, is lawful but can mask IUU fishing by breaking the chain of traceability. Many RFMOs require it to be documented, with 13 mandating vessel tracking and 10 requiring an onboard observer. Recognition red flags: encounters far from port where a carrier and fishing vessel sit within roughly 500 metres for hours at low speed; about 60 percent of receiving carriers fly flags of convenience; and transfers that go unreported or unobserved let operators misreport or hide catch. Remedy: independent observers, mandatory vessel monitoring systems, RFMO authorization and reporting, and satellite encounter analysis surface unauthorized transfers for investigation and port denial.'),
    ('maritime_ais_gaps',
     'Recognizing AIS Gaps and Vessels Going Dark',
     'Global Fishing Watch, Going Dark: When Vessels Turn Off AIS, https://globalfishingwatch.org/data/going-dark-when-vessels-turn-off-ais-broadcasts/',
     "Context: the Automatic Identification System (AIS) broadcasts a vessel's identity and position; an increasing number of States require AIS within their waters. Recognition red flags: a vessel that disables AIS to avoid being seen doing something illegal or questionable, a clear pattern of switching off when leaving port and reappearing weeks later, or going dark near an exclusive economic zone boundary where it is not licensed. Not every gap is intentional, weak signals and poor satellite reception also cause dropouts, so the regularity of signals before and after a gap is analysed to separate deliberate disabling from technical loss. Remedy: machine-learning gap detection, radar and night-light imagery, and dark-vessel lists let authorities track and investigate vessels that hide their positions."),
    ('maritime_forced_labour_at_sea',
     'Recognizing Forced Labour and Abuse Aboard Fishing Vessels',
     'ILO Work in Fishing Convention, 2007 (No. 188), https://www.ilo.org/resource/c188-work-fishing-convention-2007-no-188',
     "Legal authority: ILO Convention No. 188 (in force 16 November 2017) sets binding minimum standards for all commercial fishing: a written fisher's work agreement, minimum age, hours of rest, occupational safety and health, medical care, social security, and a prohibition on charging fishers fees for recruitment or placement. Recognition red flags relevant to the forced-labour and trafficking nexus: crew not on the official crew list, no work agreement, debt tied to recruitment fees, withheld wages or documents, and prolonged time at sea via transshipment that prevents fishers from going ashore. Remedy: inspection against the C188 checklist, port State control, repatriation and wage-recovery support, and referral of trafficking indicators to enforcement bodies."),
    ('maritime_sts_sanctions_evasion',
     'Recognizing Ship-to-Ship Transfers Used for Sanctions Evasion',
     'IMO fraudulent-registration guidance on AIS manipulation, https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/legal/pages/registration-of-ships-and-fraudulent-registration-matters.aspx',
     "Context and authority: sanctioned cargo moves through multi-leg, multi-vessel chains designed to obscure origin and control; the IMO documents the broadcasting of falsified AIS data as intentional manipulation of a ship's identifying information. Recognition red flags: dark ship-to-ship transfers with both vessels' AIS off, AIS spoofing that places a ship in a false location, prolonged loitering in transfer zones, and transfers by a shadow fleet of older tankers operating outside Western ports. Compliance authorities now treat spoofing detection, not just transmission gaps, as a baseline expectation. Remedy: screening of high-risk and spoofing vessels, cross-checking AIS against satellite imagery, and denying port services and finance to vessels with manipulated identity or origin."),
    ('maritime_piracy',
     'Piracy and Armed Robbery Against Ships: The IMO Framework',
     'IMO Piracy and Armed Robbery framework (UNCLOS Art. 101; IMO Res. A.1025(26)), https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/security/pages/piracyarmedrobberydefault.aspx',
     "Legal authority: under UNCLOS Art. 101, piracy is an illegal act of violence, detention, or depredation for private ends, committed on the high seas by the crew of a private ship against another ship or persons or property aboard, plus voluntary participation with knowledge and any act of inciting or intentionally facilitating it. The distinction turns on location: equivalent acts within a State's internal, archipelagic, or territorial waters are armed robbery against ships under IMO Resolution A.1025(26), not piracy. Recognition red flags: approach by unidentified craft, boarding attempts, and threats to crew. Remedy: IMO's Code of Practice standardizes investigation; monthly and annual incident reports, regional agreements, and best management practices support prevention, reporting, and response."),
    ('maritime_vessel_identity_fraud',
     'Recognizing Vessel-Identity Fraud and False Flags',
     'IMO Registration of Ships and Fraudulent Registration Matters, https://www.imo.org/en/ourwork/legal/pages/registration-of-ships-and-fraudulent-registration-matters.aspx',
     "Legal authority: the IMO Ship Identification Number Scheme (SOLAS regulation XI-1/3) assigns a permanent unique number to support flag-State jurisdiction and control. The IMO documents three fraud patterns: vessels still flying a flag after registration was terminated, submission of fraudulent documentation to obtain IMO numbers without the flag State's knowledge, and broadcasting falsified AIS data to alter identifying information. When a ship is not legally registered under a claimed flag, the IMO records a false-flag designation in GISIS. Recognition red flags: name or IMO-number changes, mismatched registry records, and identity laundering that hides the true vessel. Remedy: the IMO Legal Committee is developing new registration guidelines (by 2027); data transparency and false-flag listings expose fraudulent identities."),
    ('maritime_port_state_control',
     'Port State Control and the Inspection Regime',
     'FAO Agreement on Port State Measures (PSMA) background, https://www.fao.org/port-state-measures/background/en/',
     'Legal authority: the FAO Agreement on Port State Measures (PSMA, in force June 2016) is the first binding international agreement targeting IUU fishing. Parties apply a minimum set of standard measures when foreign vessels seek port entry or remain in port, focused on detecting and investigating IUU fishing plus follow-up, reporting, and notification to other States and RFMOs. Recognition red flags handled at the port: vessels seeking to land or transship catch lacking valid authorization, inconsistent catch documentation, or appearing on an IUU list. Remedy: a vessel reasonably suspected of IUU fishing can be denied port entry or port services and refused permission to land or transship, so illegally caught fish are blocked from national and international markets.'),
    ('maritime_financial_flows',
     'Recognizing Fisheries-Crime Financial Flows',
     'FATF, Money Laundering from Environmental Crime (July 2021), https://www.fatf-gafi.org/content/dam/fatf-gafi/reports/Money-Laundering-from-Environmental-Crime.pdf',
     "Legal authority: the FATF July 2021 report treats environmental crime, including crimes associated with illegal fishing, as an anti-money-laundering priority; illegal fishing is estimated at roughly USD 10 to 23.5 billion per year and converges with corruption, fraud, trafficking, tax evasion, and document fraud. Recognition red flags drawn from the report's laundering methods: front companies used to co-mingle illegal proceeds with legal catch revenue, shell companies that hide beneficial owners, and trade-based fraud and money laundering across regional and international financial sectors. Remedy: applying environmental-crime red flags to suspicious-transaction reporting, following the money to beneficial owners, and closer coordination between financial-intelligence units and fisheries enforcement."),
    ('maritime_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and Remedy: PSMA, FISH-i Africa, and INTERPOL',
     'INTERPOL Global Fisheries Enforcement and Purple Notices, https://www.interpol.int/en/Crimes/Environmental-crime/Fisheries-crime',
     'Legal authority and mechanisms: INTERPOL Global Fisheries Enforcement helps member countries identify, deter, and disrupt fisheries-sector crime, issuing Purple Notices on the modus operandi of suspected IUU vessels, deceptive recruitment, fraudulent flag-registry abuse, and human trafficking at sea. Investigative Support Teams have produced detentions, convictions, fines, and other sanctions. Regional cooperation complements this: FISH-i Africa shares intelligence across fisheries, port, customs, police, and health authorities; de-flagging and license refusal have rendered offending vessels stateless and unable to fish legally anywhere. Remedy in practice: PSMA port denial, coordinated interdiction, IUU listing, de-flagging, and cross-border information sharing combine to stop catch reaching market and hold operators accountable.'),
    ('mining_oecd_due_diligence',
     'OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Mineral Supply Chains (3TG)',
     'OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals from Conflict-Affected and High-Risk Areas (3rd ed.) | https://www.oecd.org/en/topics/sub-issues/due-diligence-guidance-for-responsible-business-conduct/responsible-mineral-supply-chains.html',
     'Legal authority: the government-backed OECD Guidance (adopted 2011) sets the global standard for sourcing tin, tantalum, tungsten and gold (3TG) and other minerals, and underpins EU, US and African regulations. It recommends a five-step risk-management framework covering the whole chain from mine to end user. Recognition red flags (Annex II risks): minerals that finance armed groups, worst forms of child labour, forced labour, war crimes, bribery, money laundering, or sourcing from conflict-affected and high-risk areas without traceability. Remedy: establish a chain-of-custody and management system, identify and assess supply-chain risks, mitigate or suspend high-risk suppliers, obtain independent third-party audit of smelters/refiners, and publish an annual due-diligence report.'),
    ('mining_eu_conflict_minerals',
     'EU Conflict Minerals Regulation 2017/821 (Union importer due diligence)',
     'Regulation (EU) 2017/821 laying down supply chain due diligence obligations for Union importers of tin, tantalum, tungsten and gold | https://eur-lex.europa.eu/EN/legal-content/summary/responsible-trade-in-minerals-from-high-risk-or-conflict-areas.html',
     'Legal authority: Regulation (EU) 2017/821, applicable since 1 January 2021, binds Union importers of 3TG (tin, tantalum, tungsten, gold) above volume thresholds to mandatory OECD-aligned supply-chain due diligence. Recognition red flags: an importer with no supply-chain policy, no management system, undisclosed smelters/refiners, no independent third-party audit, or sourcing from conflict-affected and high-risk areas that could finance armed groups or security forces. Remedy: adopt a responsible-sourcing policy, identify and assess risks back to smelter/refiner, manage and report on risks, commission independent third-party audits, and disclose an annual report. Member State competent authorities perform compliance checks and impose corrective measures for breaches.'),
    ('mining_dodd_frank_1502',
     'US Dodd-Frank Section 1502 Conflict-Minerals Disclosure (Form SD)',
     'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act, Section 1502, and SEC Conflict Minerals Rule | https://www.sec.gov/rules-regulations/staff-guidance/compliance-disclosure-interpretations/dodd-frank-wall-street-reform-consumer-protection-act-frequently-asked-questions',
     'Legal authority: Section 1502 of the 2010 Dodd-Frank Act amended the Securities Exchange Act to require SEC-reporting issuers to disclose use of conflict minerals (cassiterite/tin, coltan/tantalum, wolframite/tungsten, gold) originating in the Democratic Republic of the Congo or adjoining covered countries, via an annual Form SD due each 31 May. Recognition red flags: a filer that skips the Reasonable Country of Origin Inquiry, cannot identify smelters, files no Conflict Minerals Report when due diligence is triggered, or labels products conflict-free without traceable evidence. Remedy: conduct the country-of-origin inquiry, perform supply-chain due diligence per the OECD framework where minerals may originate in covered countries, and file Form SD with a Conflict Minerals Report describing sources and product status.'),
    ('mining_eiti',
     'EITI Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative Standard',
     'EITI Standard 2023, Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative | https://eiti.org/eiti-standard',
     'Legal authority: the EITI is the global standard for open, accountable management of oil, gas and mineral resources; implementing countries must disclose information across the value chain from extraction to revenue use. Recognition red flags: licenses or contracts awarded outside published procedures, undisclosed beneficial owners, unreconciled gaps between company payments and government receipts, missing production or export data, or revenues that never reach published budgets. Remedy: disclose how licenses and contracts are allocated and registered, who beneficially owns operations, fiscal and legal terms, production volumes, taxes/royalties/payments reconciled against government receipts, and revenue allocation, validated by a multi-stakeholder group so civil society can scrutinize the numbers.'),
    ('mining_asm',
     'Artisanal and Small-Scale Mining (ASM) Formalization and Risk Recognition',
     'OECD, Conflict transformation and the role of responsible artisanal and small-scale mining (2023) | https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2023/04/conflict-transformation-and-the-role-of-responsible-artisanal-and-small-scale-mining_971a7282.html',
     'Legal authority: the OECD Guidance recognizes the concept of legitimate ASM and frames responsible artisanal and small-scale mining as compatible with due diligence; an estimated 15-30 million people work in ASM, producing a large share of global gold output, mostly outside formal regulatory control. Recognition red flags: unlicensed operation, no occupational health and safety, mercury or cyanide use, child or forced labour, illicit rent-seeking by armed actors, and minerals with no documented origin entering the chain. Remedy: support formalization through licensing, traceability, and recognized due-diligence schemes; reduce worst practices; integrate legitimate miners into audited supply chains; and treat unverifiable ASM material as high-risk until origin and conditions can be confirmed.'),
    ('mining_illegal_gold_mercury',
     'Illegal Gold Mining and Mercury under the Minamata Convention',
     'Minamata Convention on Mercury, provisions on artisanal and small-scale gold mining (ASGM) and National Action Plans | https://minamataconvention.org/en/topics/artisanal-and-small-scale-gold-mining/insights',
     'Legal authority: the Minamata Convention on Mercury obliges each Party that determines ASGM in its territory is more than insignificant to notify the Secretariat and develop a National Action Plan to reduce, and where feasible eliminate, mercury use. ASGM, present in over 80 countries with around 15 million miners, is the largest source of mercury emissions globally. Recognition red flags: open mercury amalgamation, whole-ore amalgamation, mercury burning without capture, mercury releases into rivers, and unlicensed gold leaving a site with no origin record. Remedy: implement the National Action Plan, set mercury-reduction targets, eliminate worst practices, promote mercury-free extraction, formalize and monitor the sector, and protect health and water from contamination.'),
    ('mining_origin_laundering',
     'Mineral Smuggling and Origin-Laundering Red Flags',
     'OECD, Free trade zones and illicit gold flows in Latin America and the Caribbean (2022) | https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/free-trade-zones-and-illicit-gold-flows-in-latin-america-and-the-caribbean_7536db96-en.html',
     "Legal authority: OECD analysis shows free trade zones and weak documentation enable gold laundering (concealing illegally mined origin) and gold-based money laundering, undermining the responsible-sourcing frameworks built on the OECD Guidance. Recognition red flags: large gaps between one country's recorded exports and another's recorded imports; short-lived or shell trading companies; falsified or recycled certificates of origin; gold routed through transit hubs and free trade zones to obscure source; declared volumes exceeding a region's plausible legal production. Remedy: reconcile mirror trade statistics, verify chain-of-custody back to a named mine or smelter, scrutinize trading intermediaries and beneficial owners, audit free-trade-zone transactions, and refuse consignments whose origin cannot be independently substantiated."),
    ('mining_forced_child_labour',
     'Forced and Child Labour in Mining (Trafficking Nexus)',
     'ILO, Child labour in mining and quarrying / The burden of gold | https://www.ilo.org/international-programme-elimination-child-labour-ipec/sectors-and-topics/child-labour-mining-and-quarrying',
     'Legal authority: ILO standards treat almost all child work in mining and quarrying as hazardous and a worst form of child labour; roughly one million children aged 5-17 work in small-scale mining, with documented forced labour, debt bondage, and trafficking in gold and cobalt supply chains. Recognition red flags: children at or near mine sites, recruitment fees or wage withholding creating debt bondage, confiscated identity documents, restricted freedom of movement, no contracts, and isolated camps controlled by intermediaries. Remedy: apply OECD Annex II due diligence to detect and remediate child and forced labour, remove children to education and safe livelihoods, refer suspected trafficking to protection and law-enforcement channels, and condition continued sourcing on verified remediation rather than supplier cut-and-run.'),
    ('mining_sand_mining',
     'Sand Mining and Ecological Harm Recognition',
     'UNEP, Sand and Sustainability / rising demand for sand calls for resource governance | https://www.unep.org/news-and-stories/press-release/rising-demand-sand-calls-resource-governance',
     'Legal authority: UNEP identifies sand as the most-extracted solid material after water and calls for governance because extraction is rising about 6 percent a year, an unsustainable rate with no global standard. Recognition red flags: in-river or coastal extraction causing bank erosion and aquifer salination; lowered groundwater and increased flooding; loss of storm-surge protection and fisheries; and illegal extraction by sand mafias often shielded by corruption and political ties. Remedy: require permits tied to measured replenishment, monitor and map extraction volumes, enforce no-go zones for critical ecosystems, prosecute illegal operators, and build transparent multi-stakeholder oversight of the sand value chain to expose and disrupt black-market flows.'),
    ('mining_kimberley_diamonds',
     'Kimberley Process and Conflict Diamonds',
     'Kimberley Process Certification Scheme (KPCS), established under UN General Assembly Resolution 55/56 | https://www.kimberleyprocess.com/about/what-is-kp',
     'Legal authority: the Kimberley Process Certification Scheme (2003) blocks conflict diamonds, rough diamonds used by rebel movements to finance war against legitimate governments, from the mainstream trade. Participants must trade rough diamonds only with other participants and certify each export. Recognition red flags: a rough-diamond shipment with no Kimberley Process certificate, an unsealed or tampered parcel, an uniquely-numbered certificate that does not match the consignment, import from or export to a non-participant, or undocumented country of origin. Remedy: route all rough-diamond imports and exports through the government authority that verifies source, seals parcels in tamper-resistant containers, and issues a forgery-resistant certificate; reject or quarantine any uncertified or mismatched shipment.'),
    ('mining_revenue_transparency',
     'Extractives Revenue Transparency and Beneficial Ownership',
     'EITI Standard 2023, strengthened beneficial-ownership requirements (Open Ownership analysis) | https://www.openownership.org/en/news/2023-eiti-standard-strengthened-requirements-on-beneficial-ownership-transparency/',
     'Legal authority: the 2023 EITI Standard strengthens beneficial-ownership transparency, requiring disclosure of who really owns and controls companies that bid for, operate, or invest in extractives, including full disclosure by politically exposed persons at any ownership level and disclosure of state ownership and control. Recognition red flags: hidden ultimate owners behind shell chains, undeclared PEP interests in license holders, license awards to anonymous entities, and revenues that cannot be traced to a beneficial owner. Remedy: maintain or contribute to public beneficial-ownership registers, adopt low ownership-reporting thresholds, disclose full legal and beneficial ownership chains, flag PEP conflicts of interest, and reconcile disclosed ownership against license and payment records for corruption investigations.'),
    ('mining_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and Remedy: Permit Integrity, INTERPOL, and Supply-Chain Audits',
     'INTERPOL, Illegal mining and environmental crime enforcement | https://www.interpol.int/en/Crimes/Environmental-crime/Illegal-mining',
     'Legal authority: INTERPOL treats illegal mining as transnational environmental crime tied to organized-crime networks, fraud, money laundering, and harm to communities, and runs coordinated multi-jurisdictional operations that have seized unprocessed gold, mercury, equipment and firearms and made hundreds of arrests. Recognition red flags: operation without a valid permit, forged or expired permits and certificates, mining outside permitted boundaries or in protected areas, no environmental safeguards, and supply chains lacking independent third-party audit. Remedy: verify permit and license authenticity against official registers, refer suspected illegal mining and laundering to law enforcement, require independent supply-chain audits of smelters and refiners, support cross-border cooperation, and provide remediation and protection for affected workers and communities.'),
    ('aid_diversion_recognition',
     'Recognizing humanitarian aid diversion: overview and risk factors',
     'Transparency International / U4, Corruption in humanitarian assistance in conflict settings, fetched https://knowledgehub.transparencycdn.org/helpdesk/Corruption-in-humanitarian-assistance-in-conflict-settings_2024_Final.pdf',
     'Aid diversion means relief intended for crisis-affected people is taken, taxed, resold, or steered to non-target groups. Transparency International notes diversion is not a rare breakdown but a common feature of operations where agencies lack in-country presence and monitor remotely. Recognition red flags include relief goods for sale in local markets, beneficiaries reporting short or missing rations, distribution records that never reconcile with stock leaving warehouses, access granted only after concessions, and pressure to inflate registration lists. Risk rises in conflict zones, with single intermediaries, weak commodity tracking, and no independent post-distribution monitoring. Safeguarding response: resource-tracking, identity checks, third-party monitoring, and confidential complaint channels. Remedy follows through investigation, fund recovery, and supplier or staff sanctions.'),
    ('aid_psea',
     'Protection from Sexual Exploitation and Abuse (PSEA) in aid delivery',
     'Inter-Agency Standing Committee (IASC), Six Core Principles Relating to Sexual Exploitation and Abuse, fetched https://psea.interagencystandingcommittee.org/resources/iasc-six-core-principles-relating-sexual-exploitation-and-abuse',
     'The IASC Six Core Principles treat sexual exploitation and abuse by aid personnel as gross misconduct and grounds for termination. Exchanging money, employment, goods, services, or assistance due to beneficiaries for sex is prohibited; sexual activity with anyone under 18 is barred regardless of local age of consent. A relationship involving improper use of rank over an assisted person is prohibited because it undermines aid integrity. Recognition red flags include staff demanding favors for registration, food, or shelter; aid given only to those who comply; gatekeepers controlling access to lists. Workers who suspect abuse must report through established mechanisms. Safeguarding requires codes of conduct, vetting, and survivor-centered complaint routes; remedy includes investigation, discipline, and referral to authorities.'),
    ('aid_ghost_beneficiaries',
     'Ghost beneficiaries and beneficiary-list fraud recognition',
     'Transparency International / U4, Corruption in humanitarian assistance in conflict settings, fetched https://knowledgehub.transparencycdn.org/helpdesk/Corruption-in-humanitarian-assistance-in-conflict-settings_2024_Final.pdf',
     'Beneficiary-list fraud inflates the number of recipients so surplus assistance can be skimmed. Ghost or fictitious beneficiaries, duplicate registrations, and names controlled by gatekeepers let aid be drawn for people who do not exist or never receive it. Recognition red flags include lists that grow without a matching rise in assessed need, many recipients sharing one address or collection point, identical or sequential identity records, refusal to allow independent verification, and an official insisting on managing the list. In Yemen, officials manipulated beneficiary lists and falsified records to remove food. Safeguarding response: independent needs assessment, biometric or identity verification, list de-duplication, and post-distribution checks with affected people. Remedy includes correcting the registry, recovering value, and sanctioning those responsible.'),
    ('aid_procurement_fraud',
     'Procurement fraud in humanitarian supply chains: warning signs',
     'Transparency International, Preventing Corruption in Humanitarian Operations: Handbook of Good Practices, fetched https://files.transparencycdn.org/images/2014_Humanitarian_Handbook_EN.pdf',
     'Humanitarian procurement is exposed to fraud at every stage. The Transparency International handbook catalogs manipulated tender specifications, bid-rigging and insider information, biased prequalification, manipulated bid evaluation and award, surplus procurement, and supply of sub-standard goods. Recognition red flags include only one qualified bidder, bids clustered at similar prices, specifications written to fit a single vendor, contracts split to stay under approval thresholds, late low bids suggesting leaked information, and vendors linked to staff. Emergency speed and waived controls heighten exposure. Safeguarding response: segregation of duties, competitive documented tendering, vendor due diligence, and resource-tracking audits. Remedy follows through audit-led investigation, contract cancellation, fund recovery, and supplier debarment.'),
    ('aid_food_diversion',
     'Food-aid diversion and resale: recognition and controls',
     'World Food Programme, Statement on strengthened actions to address food diversion in Ethiopia, fetched https://www.wfp.org/news/wfp-statement-strengthened-actions-address-food-diversion-ethiopia',
     'Food-aid diversion occurs when commodities meant for crisis-affected families are stolen, taxed, or resold instead of consumed by intended recipients. WFP detected widespread diversion in Ethiopia after finding evidence of significant food-aid sales in local markets, and paused distributions. Recognition red flags include branded relief commodities openly for sale, reported rations smaller than entitlements, warehouse releases that do not match what reaches families, and partners reluctant to allow monitoring. Safeguarding response per WFP: reinforce commodity tracking from warehouses to beneficiaries, strengthen beneficiary-list management and identity checks, run real-time needs assessment, and expand monitoring with community feedback. Remedy involves identifying those involved, closing registration loopholes, enforcing controls on partners, and recovering assistance.'),
    ('aid_cash_transfer_fraud',
     'Cash-transfer-programming fraud risks and safeguards',
     'CALP Network (ODI), Risk and Humanitarian Cash Transfer Programming, fetched https://www.calpnetwork.org/publication/risk-and-humanitarian-cash-transfer-programming/',
     'Cash and voucher assistance can be diverted like any aid; the CALP Network notes that in conflict environments parties may divert resources intended for humanitarian purposes to fuel the conflict, though evidence shows cash is not inherently riskier than in-kind aid. Recognition red flags include duplicate or fictitious registrations drawing multiple payments, recipients reporting that PINs, SIM cards, or cards were taken or shared under pressure, agents skimming a cut at cash-out, transfer values that do not reach the household, and collection concentrated under one controller. Safeguarding response: de-duplicated identity verification, secure delivery channels, reconciliation of transfer data against distribution lists, and post-distribution monitoring with confidential feedback. Remedy includes recovering misdirected funds, correcting registries, and sanctioning complicit agents.'),
    ('aid_extortion_armed_groups',
     'Taxation and extortion of aid by armed groups: recognition',
     'Transparency International / U4, Corruption in humanitarian assistance in conflict settings, fetched https://knowledgehub.transparencycdn.org/helpdesk/Corruption-in-humanitarian-assistance-in-conflict-settings_2024_Final.pdf',
     'In conflict settings armed groups extort and divert aid, using checkpoints both for control and as points of taxation; Transparency International documents per-kilogram transit fees on relief goods rising sharply over time. Recognition red flags include demands for payment, fuel, or a share of cargo to pass checkpoints; access granted only after a cut of the consignment is conceded; consignments arriving short after transiting controlled territory; and pressure to route aid through one armed-group-linked intermediary. These access trade-offs risk strengthening armed groups and are a recognized fiduciary concern. Safeguarding response: convoy and commodity tracking, access negotiation under clear red lines, independent monitoring of delivered versus dispatched quantities, and confidential reporting. Remedy includes documenting losses, donor notification, and suspending compromised routes.'),
    ('aid_ngo_financial_integrity',
     'NGO financial integrity and donor reporting safeguards',
     'Transparency International, Preventing Corruption in Humanitarian Operations: Handbook of Good Practices, fetched https://files.transparencycdn.org/images/2014_Humanitarian_Handbook_EN.pdf',
     "Financial integrity protects donor and beneficiary trust by ensuring funds are used for their intended purpose. Transparency International's handbook stresses internal controls, resource-tracking, audits, monitoring, transparency, and accountability. Recognition red flags include expenses unsupported by genuine documentation, falsified or altered receipts, payments to phantom vendors or staff, donor-restricted funds spent off-purpose, reconciliations that never balance, and one individual controlling a transaction end to end. Reporting that looks too clean or arrives without source records is itself a warning sign. Safeguarding response: segregation of duties, multi-tier approval for large expenditures, clear tracking of restricted funds, and independent audit. Remedy includes investigation, fund or administrative recovery, corrective controls, and disciplinary or legal action."),
    ('aid_conflict_of_interest',
     'Conflict of interest and kickbacks in aid: warning signs',
     'USAID Office of Inspector General, 12 Debarred Over Role in Syria Humanitarian Aid Fraud Scheme, fetched https://oig.usaid.gov/node/42',
     'Conflict of interest and kickbacks arise when private gain steers aid decisions. The USAID OIG Syria case exposed commercial vendors and NGO employees who colluded in bid-rigging and kickback schemes, including one company controlling several entities that bid against each other under the appearance of fair competition. Recognition red flags include staff with undisclosed ties to suppliers, related-party or family-linked vendors, gifts or payments from contractors, one owner behind multiple bidders, recurring awards to a favored vendor, and approvals of sub-standard goods. Behavioral signs include secrecy and resistance to oversight. Safeguarding response: mandatory disclosure, recusal from affected decisions, supplier beneficial-ownership checks, and segregation of approval roles. Remedy includes investigation, contract termination, fund recovery, vendor debarment, and staff discipline.'),
    ('aid_misconduct_reporting',
     'Aid-worker misconduct reporting channels and protections',
     "UNHCR, Report fraud, misconduct and sexual abuse (Inspector General's Office), fetched https://help.unhcr.org/global/reporting-misconduct-or-sexual-abuse/",
     "Affected people and staff can report fraud, corruption, sexual exploitation and abuse, and other misconduct by aid personnel, partners, or service providers. UNHCR routes reports to its Inspector General's Office and applies zero tolerance, whether the conduct is by its own personnel or partner staff. Cues that should prompt a report include anyone asking for money or favors for assistance, abusing position, or manipulating registration. Safeguarding features: reports are handled confidentially and the reporter's identity is not shared without consent; complaints may be anonymous; and reporting will not affect rights to protection and assistance. Channels include confidential helplines, online and email submissions, and complaint boxes at offices. These retaliation protections are part of the remedy pathway, since credible reports trigger investigation and disciplinary action."),
    ('aid_accountability_affected',
     'Localization and accountability to affected populations (CHS)',
     'CHS Alliance, Core Humanitarian Standard: Nine commitments to people affected by crises, fetched https://www.corehumanitarianstandard.org/the-standard',
     'The Core Humanitarian Standard sets nine commitments so organizations support crisis-affected people in ways that respect rights and dignity and promote their role in solutions. It addresses power imbalances and lets communities hold those who support them to account. Accountability-relevant commitments include that people can safely report complaints and have them addressed, and can expect resources to be managed ethically. A signal of weak accountability is the absence or distrust of complaint mechanisms; CHS notes the complaints commitment historically scores lowest, leaving diversion and abuse harder to surface. Safeguarding response: accessible, safe, community-based complaint channels, participation of affected people in decisions, and independent verification against the standard. Remedy follows when complaints are acted on, triggering correction, recovery, and discipline.'),
    ('aid_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: clawback, investigation, and debarment',
     'USAID Office of Inspector General, 12 Debarred Over Role in Syria Humanitarian Aid Fraud Scheme, fetched https://oig.usaid.gov/node/42',
     'When aid fraud is substantiated, remedy combines investigation, fund recovery, and exclusion of wrongdoers. In the USAID OIG Syria matter, investigation of bid-rigging and kickback schemes led to 12 vendors and individuals debarred, with broader regional results including 35 suspension or debarment decisions and 239 million dollars in suspended program funds. Debarred parties are listed government-wide and barred from future contracts; multilateral development banks apply similar sanctions, some subject to cross-debarment. Signs that enforcement is working include published sanctions lists, recovered funds, and corrective action on controls. Consequences of substantiated fraud can include fines, debarment from future funding, administrative recovery, civil suits, and criminal prosecution. Findings then feed back into stronger procurement, registration, and monitoring controls.'),
    ('education_diploma_mills',
     'Recognizing diploma mills and fake degrees (SDG 4)',
     'Council for Higher Education Accreditation (CHEA), Important Questions About Degree Mills, https://www.chea.org/important-questions-about-degree-mills',
     'A degree provider may be a diploma mill if several signs appear together: degrees can be purchased; accreditation is claimed with no evidence or from a questionable accreditor; it lacks state or federal licensure; little or no attendance or coursework is required; a degree is offered very fast or based solely on resume or life experience; only a post office box is given, no campus; no qualified faculty list is published; and the name mimics a well-known university. Not every unaccredited school is a mill - some are mid-process. To verify, check the CHEA and US Department of Education recognized-institution databases before accepting a credential; employers can use the National Student Clearinghouse. Mill degrees are routinely rejected for transfer, graduate study, and employment, leaving the holder without a usable credential.'),
    ('education_accreditation_fraud',
     'Recognizing accreditation mills and fake accreditors',
     'CHEA Statement in Response to Fake Accreditor and Higher Education Institution Websites (2025), https://www.chea.org/chea-statement-response-fake-accreditor-and-higher-education-institution-websites',
     "An accrediting body may be a mill if accredited status can be purchased; it lists institutions it claims to accredit without their knowledge; it falsely claims recognition by CHEA or the US Department of Education; it publishes few or no standards; status is granted fast or made permanent with no periodic re-review; reviews are document-only with no site visits; or its name imitates a recognized accreditor. CHEA warns that fraudulent accreditor sites - increasingly AI-generated - copy official seals and even impersonate CHEA itself to extract fees and sensitive personal data. To verify, consult only CHEA's and the US Department of Education's databases of recognized accreditors; one absent from both is unverified however official it looks. Report suspected fake accreditors to CHEA, which forwards them to the appropriate enforcing agency."),
    ('education_credential_fraud',
     'Recognizing forged transcripts and credentials',
     'Federal Trade Commission, College Degree Scams (Consumer Advice), https://www.consumer.ftc.gov/articles/0206-college-degree-scams',
     "Forged transcripts and diplomas often show uniformly perfect grades, fonts or seals differing from the issuer's standard, paper lacking genuine security features, inconsistent course terminology, dates or credit totals that do not add up, and a credential offered after only a resume review. The reliable recognition test is independent confirmation: instead of trusting the document the applicant hands over, request a fresh official transcript sent directly from the issuing registrar or via the National Student Clearinghouse, removing any chance of substitution. Confirm the issuing institution is itself accredited through the CHEA or US Department of Education databases - a genuine-looking transcript from a diploma mill is still worthless. The FTC notes presenting unearned credentials can lead to firing and prosecution; refer suspected document fraud to the institution and authorities."),
    ('education_contract_cheating',
     'Recognizing contract cheating and essay mills',
     'Quality Assurance Agency for Higher Education (QAA), Contracting to Cheat in Higher Education (3rd ed.), https://www.qaa.ac.uk/news-events/news/contracting-to-cheat-in-higher-education-updated-3rd-edition-of-contract-cheating-guidance-now-available',
     "Contract cheating is the outsourcing of a student's assessed work to a third party, often a commercial essay mill, for payment. Recognition signals in submitted work include a sudden unexplained jump in writing quality, a voice or vocabulary inconsistent with the student's prior work or in-class performance, references to sources the course never used, mismatched citation styles, generic content that misreads the brief, and metadata suggesting another author. QAA estimates well over a thousand essay mills operate. In England the Skills and Post-16 Education Act 2022 made it a criminal offence to provide or advertise these services as of 28 April 2022; other UK nations may follow. Remedy is institutional: clear academic-integrity rules, proportionate penalties, authentic assessment that is harder to outsource, and education on the risks of blackmail and worthless qualifications."),
    ('education_exam_fraud',
     'Recognizing exam fraud and impersonation',
     'ETS, Test Security of the TOEFL iBT Test, https://www.ets.org/toefl/institutions/ibt/about/test-security.html',
     'Exam fraud includes proxy or impersonation schemes where a paid stand-in sits a test, plus content theft and prearranged answers. Test providers recognize it through layered identity and behavior checks: government-ID validation with automated facial recognition before the test, biometric voice identification confirming the same person throughout, a mandatory second rear camera for remote sittings, AI gaze and video monitoring, and post-test statistical analysis flagging improbable score patterns by center, country, or region. Confirmed-fraud scores are cancelled and the test-taker barred; institutions can re-verify a score with the testing organization if it seems inconsistent with other evidence. Providers also work with law enforcement against professional impersonation rings - so a flagged or cancelled score is a strong red flag for admissions and licensing.'),
    ('education_student_visa_fraud',
     'Recognizing bogus enrollment and student-visa fraud (trafficking nexus)',
     "U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE), Indictment in 'pay-to-stay' school scam, https://www.ice.gov/news/releases/3-indicted-pay-stay-school-scam-allegedly-enabled-foreign-nationals-remain-us",
     "Sham schools issue enrollment documents (such as a Form I-20) to people who are not genuine students, enabling visa status without real study - a pathway that can mask unauthorized work, exploitation, and trafficking. In a documented case, records showed hundreds of enrolled foreign students while site visits found one class with a few attendees; students paid 'tuition' for paperwork only, and fabricated transcripts deceived immigration authorities. Recognition indicators: a large gap between claimed enrollment and observed attendance, students living far from campus, frequent unexplained transfers, addresses shared by many enrollees, empty facilities, and reluctance to attend orientation. Remedy: such schools lose enrollment-system access and certification, and operators face immigration-fraud and money-laundering charges. Verify a school's certification before relying on its documents."),
    ('education_paper_mills',
     'Recognizing research paper mills',
     'Committee on Publication Ethics (COPE), Paper Mills COPE Focus, https://publicationethics.org/cope-focus/paper-mills',
     "Paper mills manufacture and sell authorship or whole manuscripts, submitting fabricated research to journals for a fee to give buyers an easy publication. Editors and reviewers should watch for clusters of red flags rather than any single sign: 'tortured phrases' that evade plagiarism checks, data or images that appear templated or reused across unrelated papers, results too clean to be plausible, irrelevant or unsupportive references, mismatches between stated affiliation and the work, unusual author-addition requests, and coordinated or compromised peer review. COPE provides flowcharts to investigate systematic manipulation of the publication and peer-review processes. Remedy follows the evidence: investigation, expression of concern, correction or retraction, and reporting to institutions, coordinated cross-publisher through the COPE/STM United2Act initiative."),
    ('education_license_fraud',
     'Recognizing bogus professional licensing and certification',
     'National Council of State Boards of Nursing (NCSBN), License Verification (Nursys), https://www.ncsbn.org/nursing-regulation/licensure/license-verification.page',
     "Professional-credential fraud covers forged or stolen licenses and counterfeit certifications used to practice in regulated fields, creating direct public-safety risk - documented cases include people using another nurse's stolen license for months. Recognition relies on primary-source verification, not the document itself: confirm current status directly with the official board or national registry (for nursing, the Nursys database is the national source for license status, discipline, and practice privileges), and watch for mismatched names or numbers, licenses no board can confirm, and 'verification hotlines' the applicant supplies. Remedy: make primary-source verification a standing workflow at hire and re-check periodically, and report imposters to the board and law enforcement, since unlicensed practice is prosecutable."),
    ('education_predatory_journals',
     'Recognizing predatory journals and conferences',
     'Think. Check. Submit. journal checklist, https://thinkchecksubmit.org/journals/',
     'Predatory or deceptive journals and conferences exploit fee-paying authors while delivering little or no genuine peer review. The Think. Check. Submit. checklist helps recognize a trustworthy outlet: the publisher name and a real postal address are displayed; the journal is registered in the ISSN portal and indexed in a discoverable database with persistent identifiers; the peer-review process and external reviewers are described, with no guaranteed acceptance or implausibly fast turnaround; charges and waivers are stated transparently; and the journal or publisher holds membership in recognized bodies such as COPE, DOAJ, or OASPA. Warning signs: names mimicking established journals, false indexing claims, aggressive solicitation, and hijacked sites impersonating real journals. Remedy is preventive: verify before submitting, since fees are rarely recoverable.'),
    ('education_financial_aid_fraud',
     'Recognizing student financial-aid (Title IV) fraud',
     'U.S. Department of Education, Department Fights Fraud in Student Aid (press release), https://www.ed.gov/about/news/press-release/us-department-of-education-fights-fraud-student-aid-protect-american-taxpayer',
     'Title IV financial-aid fraud diverts Pell Grants and federal loans through stolen identities and organized rings that use automation to file mass applications, often targeting low-cost distance programs. The US Department of Education and its Office of Inspector General describe recognition indicators at the institution: application surges far beyond capacity, enrollees who never engage academically, a first-term student unusually well-informed about aid disbursement and anxious for a credit balance, reused identity details, and applications flagged for inconsistent FAFSA information. Countermeasures include resumed FAFSA identity verification and real-time cross-checks against Social Security records. Remedy: institutions must report suspected fraud and evidence to the Office of Inspector General hotline portal; confirmed disbursements are recovered and referred for prosecution.'),
    ('education_verification',
     'Credential verification and due diligence for qualifications',
     'ENIC-NARIC (Council of Europe / European Commission / UNESCO), Non-recognised HEIs and Diploma Mills, https://www.enic-naric.net/page-unrecognised-heis-diploma-mills',
     "Verifying a qualification means confirming both that the awarding institution is officially recognized and that the document is authentic. ENIC-NARIC - the joint Council of Europe, European Commission, and UNESCO network under the Lisbon Recognition Convention - publishes official lists of recognized institutions per country and warns that qualifications from unrecognized bodies are generally not entitled to recognition abroad. Diploma mills are a distinct subset that fraudulently claim recognized status and sell awards without examination. Recognition practice: check the country's recognized-institution list, and beware that some third parties falsely pose as ENIC/NARIC centres, so use only official sources. For authentication, credential evaluators such as NACES members verify by contacting the issuing institution directly - which is how forged and mill documents are caught."),
    ('education_enforcement_remedy',
     'Enforcement and remedy for education and qualification fraud',
     'Council of Europe, Recommendation CM/Rec(2022)18 on countering education fraud (via ENIC-NARIC), https://www.enic-naric.net/page-coe-recommendation-countering-education-fraud',
     "Education fraud is addressed through layered enforcement and remedy. The Council of Europe's Recommendation CM/Rec(2022)18, adopted by 46 member states via the ETINED platform, structures action around prevention, prosecution, international cooperation, and monitoring, and defines diploma, accreditation, and visa 'mills' and essay banks. In the United States, the Federal Trade Commission has obtained settlements and shutdowns against diploma-mill operators - including online 'high schools' that sold worthless diplomas while falsely claiming accreditation. Remedy avenues: report suspected mills and fake accreditors to consumer-protection and inspector-general bodies and recognition agencies, verify through recognized accreditor and credential-evaluation databases, and rely on criminal-fraud, immigration-fraud, and unlicensed-practice statutes. Reporting enables enforcement."),
    ('eldercare_crpd',
     'UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (CRPD): Legal Authority and Protection',
     'UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (CRPD), adopted 2006; UN Enable, Division for Inclusive Social Development. https://www.un.org/development/desa/disabilities/convention-on-the-rights-of-persons-with-disabilities/article-16-freedom-from-exploitation-violence-and-abuse.html',
     'The CRPD is the binding international instrument protecting persons with disabilities, including older adults with impairments. Recognition anchors: Article 12 affirms equal recognition before the law and legal capacity on an equal basis with others; Article 13 guarantees access to justice; Article 14 protects liberty and security of the person; Article 16 establishes freedom from exploitation, violence, and abuse inside and outside the home. States must provide age- and gender-sensitive support, educate people on how to recognize and report abuse, ensure independent monitoring of facilities, and promote recovery, rehabilitation, and reintegration in conditions that preserve dignity and autonomy. The Convention frames disability-based denial of rights as a protection failure, not a private matter.'),
    ('eldercare_elder_abuse_recognition',
     'Recognizing Elder Abuse: WHO Definition and Types',
     'World Health Organization, Abuse of older people fact sheet. https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/abuse-of-older-people',
     'WHO defines abuse of older people as a single or repeated act, or lack of appropriate action, occurring within any relationship where there is an expectation of trust, that causes harm or distress to an older person. Recognized forms include physical abuse, psychological or emotional abuse, financial and material abuse, sexual abuse, neglect, and abandonment or loss of dignity. WHO reports roughly one in six people aged sixty and older experienced abuse in community settings in the past year, with higher rates reported in institutions. Recognition red flags align with these forms: unexplained injuries, fearfulness or withdrawal, sudden financial changes, and unmet care needs. Protective responses WHO endorses include caregiver support, financial-management assistance, helplines, shelters, and multidisciplinary teams across health, justice, and protective services.'),
    ('eldercare_financial_exploitation',
     'Financial Exploitation of Elders and Vulnerable Adults: Red Flags and Protection',
     'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, Office for Older Americans, Data Spotlight: Suspicious Activity Reports on Elder Financial Exploitation (Nov. 2021). https://www.consumerfinance.gov/data-research/research-reports/data-spotlight-suspicious-activity-reports-on-elder-financial-exploitation/full-report/',
     "Elder financial exploitation (EFE) is the illegal or improper use of an older or vulnerable adult's funds, property, or assets. CFPB analysis of financial-institution Suspicious Activity Reports distinguishes scams by strangers from theft by someone known to the victim, such as family, caregivers, or fiduciaries; nearly half of reported cases involved a known person. Recognition red flags include unusual account activity, frequent large or unexplained withdrawals, new names added to accounts, sudden changes to wills or deeds, and patterns consistent with undue influence, where a trusted person uses a relationship to gain control over decisions. Protective measures include designating a trusted contact, advance financial planning for diminished capacity, bank monitoring and reporting, and referral to Adult Protective Services and law enforcement."),
    ('eldercare_guardianship_abuse',
     'Recognizing Guardianship and Conservatorship Abuse',
     'U.S. Department of Justice, Elder Justice Initiative, Mistreatment and Abuse by Guardians and Other Fiduciaries. https://www.justice.gov/elderjustice/mistreatment-and-abuse-guardians-and-other-fiduciaries',
     'A guardian or conservator is appointed to make decisions for a person a court finds unable to manage their own affairs; the role can be a remedy but can also become a vehicle for abuse. The DOJ Elder Justice Initiative notes mistreatment may be financial, physical, emotional, or neglectful, and most reported malfeasance involves financial exploitation. Recognition red flags include unexplained depletion of assets, unpaid bills despite available funds, unauthorized transactions, sudden estate-document changes, and isolation tactics that block family contact or visitation. Protection and remedy: courts can freeze assets, order accountings, enforce rights to communication and visitation, appoint a co-guardian, limit powers, remove the guardian, or terminate the guardianship. Adult Protective Services, advocacy systems, ombudsmen, and licensing boards can also respond.'),
    ('eldercare_nursing_home_neglect',
     'Nursing-Home Neglect and Abuse: Resident Protection Under the Elder Justice Act',
     'Administration for Community Living, The Elder Justice Act (2010). https://acl.gov/about-acl/elder-justice-act',
     "The Elder Justice Act of 2010 is the first comprehensive U.S. federal law addressing abuse, neglect, and exploitation of older adults. It supports Adult Protective Services systems, funds research and reporting, and provides additional protections for residents of long-term-care facilities, and it established the Elder Justice Coordinating Council. Recognition red flags for institutional neglect include unexplained injuries, pressure ulcers, rapid weight loss, dehydration, poor hygiene, untreated conditions, fearfulness or withdrawal around specific staff, and visible understaffing or unsanitary conditions. Protection framing centers on residents' rights, mandatory reporting by facility staff, protection from retaliation, facility oversight, and the long-term-care ombudsman program as an avenue to investigate and resolve complaints of abuse, neglect, and exploitation."),
    ('eldercare_disability_benefit_fraud',
     'Disability-Benefit Theft and Denial of Rights: Representative-Payee Misuse',
     'Social Security Administration, Office of the Inspector General, Fraud Categories. https://oig.ssa.gov/fraud-reporting/fraud-categories/',
     "Some people who cannot manage their own benefits have a representative payee appointed to receive funds on their behalf; the payee holds a fiduciary duty to use benefits only for the beneficiary's needs. The SSA Office of the Inspector General lists representative-payee misuse and deceased-payee fraud among disability and retirement program fraud categories, alongside benefit scams that impersonate officials and demand payment or personal information. Recognition red flags include a beneficiary lacking essentials despite benefits being paid, unexplained shortfalls, denial of access to one's own money, and pressure or threats around benefits. This is both theft and a denial of rights for a vulnerable adult. Protection: misuse can be investigated, payees removed, restitution sought, and exploitation reported to oversight and protective authorities."),
    ('eldercare_caregiver_abuse',
     'Recognizing Caregiver Abuse and Coercion of Older and Disabled Adults',
     'World Health Organization, Abuse of older people fact sheet; National Adult Protective Services Association, Neglect & Self-Neglect. https://www.napsa-now.org/neglect-and-self-neglect/',
     'Abuse by a caregiver occurs within a relationship of trust and can be physical, emotional, sexual, financial, or neglectful. Coercion and control are recognition signals: a caregiver who isolates the person, blocks visitors or phone contact, speaks for them, is verbally aggressive or demeaning, or is overly controlling of money or decisions. Emotional abuse uses threats, intimidation, and humiliation; financial coercion includes pressuring someone into signing contracts, wills, or powers of attorney. Other red flags include unexplained behavior or personality change, fearfulness, withdrawal, unexplained injuries, and a caregiver who abandons duties. Caregiver stress and isolation are risk factors, not excuses. Protection: report concerns to Adult Protective Services, which can assess safety and offer services; the adult may decline services unless at imminent risk.'),
    ('eldercare_mandatory_reporting',
     'Mandatory Reporting of Vulnerable-Adult Abuse and the Role of APS',
     'National Adult Protective Services Association (NAPSA), Neglect & Self-Neglect / Mandatory Reporting. https://www.napsa-now.org/neglect-and-self-neglect/',
     'Adult Protective Services (APS) is the lead public agency responding to suspected abuse, neglect, self-neglect, and exploitation of older adults and adults with disabilities. In most U.S. states certain people are mandatory reporters required by law to report suspected mistreatment; some states require all citizens to report, and there can be legal consequences for failing to report. Recognition cues that warrant a report include sudden inability to meet essential needs, disappearance from contact with others, signs of hunger or weight loss, soiled clothing, hazardous living conditions, and a caregiver who fails to provide scheduled care. Protection framing: a report triggers screening by trained professionals and, where criteria are met, a face-to-face safety assessment. A reporter does not need to prove abuse, only to raise a good-faith concern; reports are confidential.'),
    ('eldercare_institutional_abuse',
     'Institutional Abuse in Care Settings: Oversight and Protection',
     'UN CRPD Article 16 (independent monitoring of facilities); U.S. DOJ Elder Justice Initiative (long-term-care ombudsman). https://www.justice.gov/elderjustice/mistreatment-and-abuse-guardians-and-other-fiduciaries',
     'Institutional abuse occurs in care settings such as nursing homes, assisted-living facilities, group homes, and day programs, and includes physical, emotional, sexual, and financial abuse plus neglect and abandonment. CRPD Article 16 requires that all facilities and programs serving persons with disabilities be effectively monitored by independent authorities to prevent exploitation, violence, and abuse. Recognition red flags include residents who are fearful of staff, unexplained injuries, untreated medical needs, poor hygiene, restraint or over-medication, and barriers to family contact. Protection and remedy: the long-term-care ombudsman investigates and resolves complaints of abuse, neglect, and exploitation in facilities, with resident consent; Adult Protective Services, licensing and survey agencies, advocacy systems, and law enforcement provide further avenues.'),
    ('eldercare_capacity_decision_making',
     'Capacity and Supported Decision-Making as a Rights-Preserving Alternative',
     'Special Needs Alliance, Supported Decision-Making in the US: History and Legal Background (2022), discussing CRPD Article 12. https://www.specialneedsalliance.org/the-voice/supported-decision-making-in-the-us-history-and-legal-background/',
     "CRPD Article 12 establishes that disability may not be used to diminish a person's legal capacity and that states must provide the support a person needs to exercise it. Supported decision-making (SDM) is the rights-preserving alternative to full guardianship: the individual chooses trusted supporters to help gather information, weigh options, and communicate choices while retaining the legal right to decide. Recognition concern: removing all of a person's rights through guardianship carries stigma and can enable abuse, so capacity should be assessed for specific decisions, not assumed absent. Protection framing: under reform statutes, courts must consider less-restrictive alternatives such as SDM before appointing a guardian, and supporters owe a duty of good faith and may not self-deal, guarding against undue influence and exploitation while preserving autonomy and dignity."),
    ('eldercare_self_neglect',
     'Recognizing Self-Neglect and Social Isolation in Vulnerable Adults',
     'National Adult Protective Services Association (NAPSA), Neglect & Self-Neglect. https://www.napsa-now.org/neglect-and-self-neglect/',
     'Self-neglect occurs when an older adult or adult with a disability can no longer meet essential physical, psychological, or social needs in a way that threatens health, safety, or well-being. It is among the most common matters Adult Protective Services addresses and is strongly associated with social isolation and cognitive or physical decline. Recognition red flags include sudden inability to meet basic needs, disappearance from contact with neighbors and family, hunger or rapid weight loss, disorientation, disheveled appearance or soiled clothing, failure to take needed medication, and living in squalor, hoarding, or hazardous conditions lacking heat, power, or running water. Protection framing: anyone observing these signs can report a good-faith concern to APS, which assesses safety and offers voluntary services; competent adults retain the right to decline help absent an emergency.'),
    ('eldercare_remedy_protection',
     'Remedy and Protection: APS, the Ombudsman, and Protective Orders',
     'U.S. DOJ Elder Justice Initiative; National Adult Protective Services Association. https://www.justice.gov/elderjustice/mistreatment-and-abuse-guardians-and-other-fiduciaries',
     "Protection for abused older and disabled adults flows through several complementary avenues. Adult Protective Services screens reports, assesses safety, and arranges voluntary services. The long-term-care ombudsman investigates and resolves complaints of abuse, neglect, and exploitation for people in facilities, with the resident's consent. Protection-and-advocacy systems safeguard the rights of people with disabilities. Courts provide remedies including protective orders that restrict an abuser's contact, orders enforcing rights to communication and visitation, asset freezes, removal of an abusive guardian or agent, restitution, and voiding of fraudulent deeds. Licensing boards and inspectors general address professional and fiduciary misconduct. Recognition-to-action principle: a good-faith report, not proof, is enough to start protection, and reporting is confidential."),
    ('identity_synthetic_fraud',
     'Recognizing synthetic identity fraud (fabricated and combined identities)',
     'FinCEN, Financial Trend Analysis on synthetic identity fraud, and FinCEN Alert on deepfake media in fraud schemes (2024), via fincen.gov; Equifax business insight, equifax.com/business/blog',
     'Standards authority: FinCEN, the US financial-intelligence unit, treats synthetic identity fraud as a Bank Secrecy Act concern. A synthetic identity is built fully from fabricated attributes, or by combining a real identifier (often a Social Security number) with an invented name and birthdate, then nurtured to pass checks. Recognition red flags: an application identifier with no verifiable history before a recent date; one identifier tied to many names or addresses; a new account with little activity but rapid transactions, high volumes to risky payees, or many chargebacks; and device or location data inconsistent with the claimed identity. FinCEN reports such identities on credit applications rose roughly 50 percent over four years. Remedy: file suspicious activity reports, apply phishing-resistant multifactor and live verification, and refer suspected accounts to financial-crime.'),
    ('identity_deepfake_fraud',
     'Recognizing deepfake and synthetic-media fraud (defensive)',
     "FinCEN Alert on Fraud Schemes Involving Deepfake Media Targeting Financial Institutions (2024), fincen.gov; NIST AI 100-4, 'Reducing Risks Posed by Synthetic Content', nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-4.pdf",
     'Standards authority: FinCEN warns that criminals use generative AI to create falsified photographs, videos, and documents, including images placed on driver licenses and passport cards, to open accounts and launder proceeds. NIST AI 100-4 frames content provenance and detection as complementary defenses. Recognition red flags, stated defensively: a submitted photo or video that shows alteration artifacts, matches a gallery of AI-generated faces, or is flagged by detection software; media inconsistent with other identity documents; and device or geographic data that does not match the customer. NIST notes provenance signals can indicate whether content is synthetic but do not by themselves prove trustworthiness. Remedy: adopt live verification requiring audio or video confirmation, layer provenance and detection tooling, file suspicious activity reports, and route flagged cases to fraud.'),
    ('identity_kyc_standards',
     'Identity-verification assurance standards (NIST SP 800-63 Digital Identity Guidelines)',
     'NIST Special Publication 800-63 Digital Identity Guidelines, pages.nist.gov/800-63-3/sp800-63-3.html',
     'Standards authority: NIST SP 800-63 is the US federal reference for digital identity and underpins many KYC programs. It splits assurance into three independent components: Identity Assurance Level (IAL) for proofing, Authenticator Assurance Level (AAL) for authentication strength, and Federation Assurance Level (FAL) for federated assertions. IAL1 is self-asserted, IAL2 requires remote or in-person verification, IAL3 requires in-person proofing with document examination. AAL2 mandates multifactor authentication; AAL3 requires hardware authenticators with verifier-impersonation resistance. Recognition use: a process collecting full identity with no proofing, or relying on a single knowledge factor, operates below the assurance its risk demands. Remedy and good practice: select proofing and authentication levels by transaction risk, request only minimal verified attributes to limit.'),
    ('identity_account_opening_fraud',
     'Recognizing new-account and account-opening identity fraud',
     "FTC, Consumer Sentinel Network Data Book 2024, ftc.gov/reports/consumer-sentinel-network-data-book-2024; FTC, 'What To Know About Identity Theft', consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-about-identity-theft",
     'Standards authority: the FTC runs the national identity-theft reporting system and publishes the Consumer Sentinel Data Book. In 2024 it received more than 1.1 million identity-theft reports; credit-card identity theft, including fraudulent new-card applications, was the most reported type. Recognition red flags for an individual: an unexpected new account, card, or bill in your name; a credit report showing accounts you did not open; a hard inquiry you did not initiate; mail or statements that stop arriving (a possible address change); and credit denial for unknown reasons. For institutions, an applicant identifier with a thin or fabricated history is a classic new-account signal. Remedy: place a free credit freeze with all three bureaus to block new accounts, add a fraud alert, dispute fraudulent accounts, and report at the FTC identity-theft site for a recovery plan and pre-filled.'),
    ('identity_document_forgery',
     'Recognizing forged and tampered identity documents',
     'GSA / IDManagement.gov, Identity Fraud Detection Playbook, idmanagement.gov/playbooks/idfraud/; ISO/IEC machine-readable-document standards referenced therein; FinCEN deepfake-document alert, fincen.gov',
     'Standards authority: the US federal IDManagement.gov Identity Fraud Detection Playbook and machine-readable-document standards define how genuine credentials carry layered security features. Genuine government IDs typically include watermarks, optically variable ink, holograms, microtext, laser engraving, and a machine-readable zone with check digits; an e-passport adds an embedded chip. Recognition red flags, framed defensively: inconsistent fonts or spacing, misaligned holograms or watermarks, a portrait whose lighting does not match the document surface, machine-readable-zone check digits that fail validation, and metadata showing the image was edited in photo software. Counterfeits reproduce a document from scratch; altered documents change one field such as a date on an otherwise authentic credential. Remedy: cross-check documents against reference templates, validate check digits.'),
    ('identity_biometric_spoofing',
     'Recognizing biometric spoofing and the role of liveness detection (defensive)',
     'NIST FATE / FRVT Presentation Attack Detection program, pages.nist.gov/frvt/html/frvt_pad.html; ISO/IEC 30107 Biometric presentation attack detection, iso.org/standard/79520.html',
     'Standards authority: ISO/IEC 30107 defines a presentation attack as presenting an artefact or human characteristic to a biometric capture device to interfere with system policy, and NIST runs ongoing independent evaluation of presentation attack detection (PAD) under its Face Analysis Technology Evaluation. Attacks aim at impersonation (authenticating as a target identity) or evasion (avoiding recognition). Recognition framing for system owners: unsupervised remote enrollment and authentication are the highest-risk settings, so PAD and liveness detection are essential controls there. Performance is reported as Attack and Bona Fide Presentation Classification Error Rates, letting buyers set risk-appropriate thresholds. Remedy: require tested liveness or PAD meeting ISO/IEC 30107-3 reporting, combine software and hardware signals where feasible, calibrate thresholds to transaction risk.'),
    ('identity_sim_swap',
     'Recognizing SIM-swap and authentication-takeover fraud',
     "FCC, 'Protecting Consumers from SIM Swapping and Port-Out Fraud' rules (effective 2024), fcc.gov/document/protecting-consumers-sim-swapping-and-port-out-fraud",
     "Standards authority: the FCC adopted rules requiring wireless carriers to securely authenticate customers before moving a number to a new SIM or provider and to notify customers immediately of any SIM-change or port-out request. In a SIM swap an account is fraudulently moved to a device on a different SIM; in port-out fraud the number is transferred to an account a bad actor controls at another carrier. The harm is that the attacker then intercepts calls and texts used as authentication factors and takes over linked accounts. Recognition red flags for an individual: sudden loss of cell service or a 'SIM not provisioned' state with no explanation; carrier notices of a SIM change or port you did not request; and being locked out of email, bank, or social accounts. Remedy: contact your carrier at once to reverse the change, add a port-freeze or account PIN, move high-value accounts off SMS."),
    ('identity_voice_clone',
     'Recognizing AI voice-clone and vishing impersonation fraud',
     "FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3) reporting and PSAs on AI-enabled vishing, ic3.gov and fbi.gov; FBI 'Take a Beat' guidance, fbi.gov/news/press-releases/cryptocurrency-and-ai-scams-bilk-americans-of-billions",
     'Standards authority: the FBI, through its Internet Crime Complaint Center, warns that criminals increasingly use AI-generated audio to impersonate public figures and personal relations, and now reports a dedicated AI fraud category with hundreds of millions in losses. Recognition red flags, stated defensively: an urgent call where a familiar voice claims an emergency, arrest, or kidnapping; unusual pauses or unnatural fluctuations in the voice; pressure to act immediately and secretly; and demands for hard-to-trace payment such as wire transfer, gift cards, or cryptocurrency. A cloned voice can be built from short public audio, so a convincing voice alone is not proof of identity. Remedy: hang up and call the person back on a known number before sending anything; agree on a private verification phrase in advance; never move money under time pressure; and report to the FBI complaint.'),
    ('identity_digital_id_inclusion',
     'Inclusive and rights-protective digital identity (World Bank ID4D Principles on Identification)',
     'World Bank ID4D, Principles on Identification for Sustainable Development (2nd ed.), idprinciples.org and id4d.worldbank.org/principles; aligned with SDG 16.9 (legal identity for all)',
     'Standards authority: the Principles on Identification, endorsed by many international organizations, organize good identity-system practice under three pillars: inclusion, design, and governance, in support of SDG 16.9, legal identity for all including birth registration. Inclusion requires universal coverage from birth to death free of discrimination and removal of access barriers. Design requires a unique, secure, accurate identity, interoperability, open standards, and privacy by design, with authentication ideally returning only a minimal or yes/no answer. Governance requires enforceable legal frameworks, data-subject rights, clear mandates, and independent oversight. Recognition red flags: systems that exclude marginalized groups, lack legal safeguards, enable surveillance or profiling, or offer no correction path. Remedy: the Principles call for administrative (not judicial) error.'),
    ('identity_data_broker_risk',
     'Recognizing data-broker and PII-aggregation risk to identity',
     "CFPB, 'Protecting Americans from Harmful Data Broker Practices (Regulation V)' proposed rule and fact sheet (2024), consumerfinance.gov; Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA)",
     'Standards authority: the CFPB proposed to clarify that data brokers selling certain sensitive consumer information act as consumer reporting agencies under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, subjecting them to accuracy, access, and safeguarding duties. The agency described how aggregated identifiers such as Social Security and phone numbers and financial profiles fuel identity theft, enable foreign surveillance and blackmail of officials and service members, and let stalkers locate survivors. Recognition framing: broadly available dossiers of personal identifiers raise the baseline risk of synthetic identity creation, account takeover, and targeted impersonation, because the raw material for those frauds is cheap to obtain. Remedy: individuals can use FCRA rights and state privacy laws to access, correct, and request deletion of data, opt out where offered, and freeze credit; policy remedy.'),
    ('identity_impersonation_deepfake',
     'Recognizing impersonation and romance deepfake fraud (fraud nexus)',
     'FTC, supplemental rulemaking on impersonation of individuals and AI deepfakes (2024), ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/02/ftc-proposes-new-protections-combat-ai-impersonation-individuals; FTC impersonation loss data, ftc.gov',
     "Standards authority: the FTC, citing surging impersonation complaints and more than a billion dollars in reported losses, finalized a government-and-business impersonation rule and proposed extending protections to impersonation of individuals, expressly noting AI-generated deepfakes can turbocharge these harms. Recognition red flags, framed defensively: an online contact or 'official' who builds trust then steers to secrecy and urgency; a profile or video persona that resists live, unscripted verification; spoofed names, seals, logos, or lookalike addresses; and any request for money, gift cards, or crypto tied to a relationship or claimed authority. A realistic face or voice is not identity verification. Remedy: independently verify through an official channel you locate yourself, refuse pressure to act quickly, preserve messages and records, and report to the FTC, which can seek."),
    ('identity_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: identity restoration and the FTC impersonation rule',
     'FTC Trade Regulation Rule on Impersonation of Government and Businesses, 16 CFR Part 461 (effective April 1, 2024), federalregister.gov/documents/2024/03/01/2024-04335; FTC IdentityTheft.gov, identitytheft.gov',
     'Standards authority: the FTC impersonation rule (16 CFR Part 461) makes it an unfair or deceptive practice to falsely pose as, or misrepresent affiliation with, a government entity or business, and was crafted to give the FTC a faster federal-court path to recover money for injured consumers and impose civil penalties after AMG Capital Management v. FTC limited prior remedies. For individuals, IdentityTheft.gov is the official recovery service. Recognition of legitimate process: the FTC itself never threatens consumers or demands payment in cash, gift cards, or gold; such demands are the scam. Remedy steps: report at IdentityTheft.gov for a personal recovery plan and pre-filled letters to bureaus, businesses, and collectors; place credit freezes and fraud alerts; dispute fraudulent accounts; and keep an FTC Identity Theft Report.'),
    ('water_human_right',
     'The human right to water and sanitation: legal basis and recognition',
     'UN GA Resolution 64/292 (2010); CESCR General Comment 15 (2002), E/C.12/2002/11, via globalhealthrights.org/instrument/cescr-general-comment-no-15-the-right-to-water/ and unwater.org/water-facts/human-rights-water-and-sanitation',
     'Legal authority: UN General Assembly Resolution 64/292 (2010) recognized the right to safe drinking water and sanitation as a human right essential to life and dignity; CESCR General Comment 15 entitles everyone to sufficient, safe, acceptable, physically accessible and affordable water for personal and domestic use, without discrimination. Red flags to recognize: a service that exists on paper but is too costly, unreliable, unsafe to reach, or denied to marginalized groups is a rights gap, not mere inconvenience. Disconnection without due process, charges that crowd out food or housing, and exclusion by caste, gender, or migration status are recognizable violations. Remedy: states are duty-bearers; rights-holders may seek redress through regulators, ombuds offices, courts, and human-rights bodies, with priority for the most underserved.'),
    ('water_corruption_recognition',
     'Recognizing corruption in the water and sanitation sector',
     "Water Integrity Network, Water Integrity Global Outlook; Water Knowledge Hub / WIN, 'Water Integrity and Anti-corruption', waterknowledgehub.org/learn/iwrm-tools/water-integrity-and-anti-corruption",
     'Standards authority: the Water Integrity Network defines water integrity as honest, accountable, transparent and participatory use of entrusted power (the TAP-A principles), and estimates at least 10 percent of water-sector investment is lost to corruption. Recognition red flags: single-bidder or repeatedly re-awarded contracts, inflated unit costs, budgets fragmented to obscure totals, monopoly suppliers steering public decisions, unpublished procurement records, and officials with undisclosed interests in awarded firms. Falsified progress claims and gaps between funds spent and infrastructure delivered are warning signs. Remedy: integrity assessment tools (the Annotated Water Integrity Scan and Integrity Management Toolbox), mandatory disclosure, independent oversight, audit, whistleblower protection, and referral of suspected misuse to anti-corruption institutions.'),
    ('water_theft_recognition',
     'Recognizing illegal water abstraction and theft of water resources',
     "WWF, 'Illegal water use in Spain' (2006), wwfeu.awsassets.panda.org/downloads/illegal_water_use_in_spain_may06.pdf; CESCR General Comment 15",
     'Legal authority: most jurisdictions require a licence to abstract surface water or groundwater, and General Comment 15 obliges states to protect traditional and shared water sources from unlawful encroachment. Recognized offense categories: abstraction without a licence; unauthorized construction or alteration of wells, boreholes, dams or weirs, or drilling by an unlicensed driller; and breaching the volume, location, or conditions of a permit. Red flags: undeclared boreholes, basins drawn far below sustainable yield (in some basins roughly half of boreholes are unauthorized), metered points showing implausibly low use, and works appearing without records. Remedy: register and declare abstraction points by deadline, basin-authority inspection and metering, proportionate penalties, and consistent prosecution, since sporadic enforcement fails to deter.'),
    ('water_pollution_discharge',
     'Recognizing water pollution and illegal discharge',
     "US EPA, 'Criminal Provisions of Water Pollution' (Clean Water Act, 33 U.S.C. 1319), epa.gov/enforcement/criminal-provisions-water-pollution; Clemson HGIC, 'Illicit Discharges and Water Pollution'",
     'Legal authority: under the US Clean Water Act it is an offense to discharge a pollutant from a point source into protected waters without a permit or in violation of one; knowing endangerment that puts a person at risk of death or serious injury carries the gravest penalties. Recognition red flags: a pipe or ditch running on a dry day with no rain for several days; water that is cloudy, discolored, oily, or foul-smelling; excessive algae; dead fish; and unpermitted connections from sinks, washing machines, or floor drains into storm drains that flow untreated to streams. Sudden fish kills or a chemical sheen after dark signal illicit dumping. Remedy: report to the local stormwater or public-works authority with location, time, and photo evidence; regulators can fine, suspend permits, and pursue criminal charges, and citizen reports are central because inspectors cannot be everywhere.'),
    ('water_ghost_infrastructure',
     'Recognizing ghost sanitation infrastructure and service fraud',
     'Comptroller and Auditor General of India audit of school toilets (2020), reported via Down To Earth, downtoearth.org.in/water/shocking-irregularities-found-by-cag-among-toilets-constructed-in-govt-schools-73533',
     "Oversight authority: supreme audit institutions verify whether publicly funded sanitation assets actually exist and function. A government audit of school toilets found only about one-third of toilets claimed built were complete: roughly 40 percent surveyed were non-existent, partly built, or unused, most lacked running water, and large sums were unaccounted for. Recognition red flags: completion certificates without site verification, toilets or pipes built on paper but missing on the ground, facilities with no water connection, beneficiary lists that cannot be confirmed, and 'spent but not constructed' complaints concentrated in particular districts. Remedy: physical verification and geo-tagging of assets, third-party and social audits, withholding payment until functional handover, public asset registers, and referral of fabricated claims to anti-corruption bodies."),
    ('water_quality_falsification',
     'Recognizing falsification of water-quality monitoring data',
     'US EPA Clean Water Act tampering/false-statement provisions, 33 U.S.C. 1319(c)(4), epa.gov/enforcement/criminal-provisions-water-pollution; Colorado lab data-manipulation case reported by Colorado Public Radio (2024)',
     "Legal authority: under the Clean Water Act it is a crime to knowingly make a false statement in a required record, or to falsify, tamper with, or render inaccurate a monitoring device or method; a laboratory's certification can be revoked for falsifying quality-control data. A documented case saw a public-health-lab chemist manipulate quality-control data across thousands of water tests, triggering revocation of certification for affected methods. Recognition red flags: results improbably clean or identical across samples, gaps or back-dating in chain-of-custody, quality-control checks skipped or overwritten, sampling logs inconsistent with lab records, and pressure to report compliant numbers. Remedy: independent re-testing and split samples, accreditation audits, protected reporting for analysts, regulator certification action, and criminal referral for tampering."),
    ('water_transboundary',
     'Transboundary watercourses and equitable, reasonable use',
     'UN Convention on the Law of the Non-Navigational Uses of International Watercourses (1997, in force 2015), summarized via unwatercoursesconvention.org and unwater.org SDG 6 materials',
     "Legal authority: the 1997 UN Watercourses Convention sets three core rules for states sharing a river or aquifer: use the watercourse in an equitable and reasonable manner, take all appropriate measures to prevent significant harm to other riparian states, and give prior notification of planned measures that may affect them. Recognition red flags: unilateral large abstractions, dams, or diversions begun without notifying downstream states; refusal to share flow or water-quality data; and pollution or flow reduction that degrades a neighboring state's supply. Global progress on transboundary cooperation remains limited despite many shared basins. Remedy: joint river-basin commissions, data exchange, environmental and transboundary impact assessment, prior-notification procedures, and dispute settlement through negotiation, mediation, or adjudication rather than unilateral action."),
    ('water_billing_fraud',
     'Recognizing utility billing fraud and meter tampering',
     'Municipal utility-theft ordinances (e.g., NC Gen. Stat. 14-151.1; Ohio Rev. Code 4933.18) and non-revenue-water guidance, summarized via aquatechtrade.com and silverblaze.com utility loss reporting',
     "Legal authority: tampering with, bypassing, relocating, or reconnecting a water meter without authorization is a theft-of-service offense under utility statutes and ordinances, even when done by a licensed tradesperson. Non-revenue water averages roughly 30 to 35 percent of supply globally, part of it lost to theft. Recognition red flags: a meter that reverses, registers far below evident usage, or has magnets, broken seals, or signs of physical interference; reconnection after disconnection for non-payment; unmetered taps off mains or hydrants; and sudden unexplained drops in a customer's billed consumption. Clandestine taps also endanger other users and water quality. Remedy: smart-meter tamper alerts and flow analytics, seal and field inspections, back-billing and reconnection of legal service, and prosecution as theft of service; meter problems should be reported, never self-altered."),
    ('water_scarcity_governance',
     'Water scarcity, climate stress, and accountable governance',
     'UN-Water SDG 6 Progress materials and UN Sustainable Development, Goal 6, un.org/sustainabledevelopment/water-and-sanitation/',
     'Authority and context: SDG 6 commits states to universal safely managed water and sanitation and to integrated water-resources management (IWRM). UN reporting notes that in 2024 about 2.2 billion people lacked safely managed drinking water and 3.4 billion lacked safely managed sanitation, roughly half the world faced severe water scarcity for part of 2022, and IWRM implementation rose only from 49 to 57 percent. Recognition red flags under scarcity: allocation favoring politically connected or large users while cutting off the poor, opaque rationing, unmonitored over-abstraction, and weak governance leaving most wastewater untreated. Remedy: transparent allocation rules, metering and sustainable-withdrawal limits, integrated basin planning with transboundary cooperation, civil-society oversight, and inclusion of women, youth, and indigenous communities.'),
    ('water_wash_humanitarian',
     'WASH minimum standards in humanitarian and displacement settings',
     'Sphere Handbook, Minimum Standards for Water Supply, Sanitation and Hygiene Promotion, spherestandards.org/handbook/; UNHCR Emergency Handbook WASH guidance',
     'Standards authority: the Sphere Handbook expresses the right to water and sanitation as minimum standards for humanitarian response, covering water access and quantity, water quality, and hygiene promotion, with widely cited planning figures such as a basic minimum near 15 liters per person per day, short round-trip collection time, and capped users per latrine. Recognition red flags in camps and crises: supply far below the minimum, untested or contaminated water, latrines that are unsafe, unlit, or not gender-segregated, no hygiene promotion, and exclusion of the elderly, disabled, women, or children. Diarrheal and infectious disease tracks poor WASH. Remedy: measure against Sphere indicators, conduct water-quality testing and surveillance, prioritize safe and private sanitation, and hold responders accountable through coordinated WASH-cluster monitoring.'),
    ('water_stewardship',
     'Corporate water stewardship under the AWS Standard',
     'Alliance for Water Stewardship, International Water Stewardship Standard (AWS Standard), a4ws.org/aws-standard/',
     'Standards authority: the AWS Standard is a site- and catchment-based framework for major water users, built on a five-step cycle (gather and understand, commit and plan, implement, evaluate, communicate and disclose) and five outcomes: good water governance, sustainable water balance, good water quality, healthy freshwater ecosystems, and safe water, sanitation and hygiene for all. Recognition red flags of weak stewardship: a facility that ignores catchment-shared risks, abstracts beyond sustainable balance, degrades water quality, does not engage local communities, and makes unverifiable claims. A logo without certified, audited performance is a red flag. Remedy: pursue independent AWS certification (Core, Gold, Platinum) under an ISEAL-aligned assurance system, evaluate performance against the plan, and disclose transparently so claims are accountable to the shared catchment.'),
    ('water_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: regulators, audits, and integrity pacts',
     'Transparency International, Integrity Pacts (transparency.org/en/tool-integrity-pacts) incl. the K-III Greater Karachi Water Supply Scheme; WIN TAP-A integrity tools via waterknowledgehub.org',
     'Governance authority: remedies for water-sector wrongdoing combine regulators, audit institutions, courts, and preventive instruments. An Integrity Pact, developed by Transparency International, binds a contracting authority and bidders to transparency commitments under independent civil-society monitoring that reports irregularities to oversight bodies; it has been used for major water-supply procurement in dozens of countries. Recognition that remedy is available: complaints can run to utility regulators and ombuds offices, audit institutions, anti-corruption agencies, environmental enforcement bodies, and human-rights mechanisms. Red flags that remedy is being blocked: retaliation against whistleblowers, sealed records, and unmonitored sole-source contracts. Remedy toolkit: mandatory disclosure, independent monitoring, protected reporting, audit follow-up, and proportionate sanctions.'),
    ('benefits_ilo_floors',
     'ILO Recommendation 202: social protection floors as a rights baseline',
     'ILO Social Protection Floors Recommendation, 2012 (No. 202), Paras. 2-7 - https://normlex.ilo.org/dyn/nrmlx_en/f?p=NORMLEXPUB:12100:0::NO::P12100_INSTRUMENT_ID:3065524',
     'Recommendation No. 202 frames social protection as a human right, not a discretionary favour. Social protection floors are nationally defined sets of basic social security guarantees that prevent or alleviate poverty and exclusion, covering at least four life-cycle guarantees: essential health care with maternity care, and basic income security for children, for working-age persons unable to earn sufficient income, and for older persons. Core principles include universality, entitlement to benefits prescribed by national law, non-discrimination and adequacy. Laws must specify qualifying conditions and benefit levels plus impartial, rapid, accessible and free complaint and appeal procedures. Wrongful denial of an entitlement set by law is therefore an integrity failure on the rights side, just as undue claiming is on the loss side.'),
    ('benefits_issa_eef',
     'ISSA guidelines distinguishing error, evasion and fraud',
     'ISSA Guidelines on Error, Evasion and Fraud in Social Security Systems (objectives and definitions) - https://www.issa.int/guidelines/eef/180186',
     'The ISSA Guidelines on Error, Evasion and Fraud (EEF) give social security institutions a shared vocabulary for recognising deviations from correct entitlement. Error is marked by the lack of awareness of its commission and the absence of intention. Evasion comprises actions that increase benefits or reduce contributions by exploiting applicable laws, regulations or gaps in control systems. Fraud comprises intentional acts that breach the rules, by a beneficiary, contributor or service provider, to obtain undue benefits. The guidelines promote an integrated, governance-led approach across the administration cycle. Recognition rests on separating unintentional error from deliberate evasion or fraud, so genuine claimants are protected while undue claims are addressed proportionately and lawfully.'),
    ('benefits_improper_payments',
     'Improper payments versus fraud in the US payment-integrity framework',
     'US GAO, Improper Payments and Fraud: How They Are Related but Different (GAO-24-106608) - https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-24-106608',
     'GAO defines improper payments as payments that should not have been made or were made in an incorrect amount, including those that cannot be verified as proper for lack of documentation. Fraud is obtaining a thing of value through wilful misrepresentation. The two are related but not interchangeable: all fraudulent payments are improper, but most improper payments arise from administrative error, and some fraud produces no improper payment at all. The decisive distinction is intent, and the estimation process is not designed to measure fraud, so estimates undercount fraud-driven loss. The Payment Integrity Information Act requires agencies to identify risks, estimate and report improper payments, and take corrective action. Recognition means classifying a deviation by intent and documentation, never assuming an incorrect amount equals deliberate wrongdoing.'),
    ('benefits_identity_theft',
     'Identity-based benefit theft and synthetic claimants',
     "SSA Office of the Inspector General, testimony on SSA's role in combatting identity fraud (2023) - https://oig.ssa.gov/congressional-testimony/2023-05-25-social-security-administration%E2%80%99s-role-in-combatting-identity-fraud/",
     "Identity-based benefit theft uses stolen personal information to file fraudulent applications, take over online accounts, or redirect benefit payments to unauthorised accounts. Synthetic identity fraud is a distinct, harder-to-detect pattern that combines a real identifier with fabricated names and dates of birth to create a claimant who does not exist as a whole person. Oversight bodies recognise these schemes through audit and pattern analysis, for example earnings reported under children's identifiers, clusters of misused numbers belonging to older adults, and surges of account-takeover allegations. Recognition signals include mismatches between an identifier and the person presenting it, abnormal direct-deposit changes, and histories inconsistent with the stated claimant. The rights-side concern is the genuine identifier-holder, a victim entitled to remediation and restored access."),
    ('benefits_ghost_pension',
     'Recognising ghost and deceased beneficiaries in pension systems',
     'ISSA analysis, Proof of life in international social security: progress and persistent challenges - https://www.issa.int/analysis/proof-life-international-social-security-progress-and-persistent-challenges',
     'Ghost beneficiaries are non-existent persons fraudulently inserted into a pension payroll, or deceased pensioners retained on the payroll so payments continue after death. Proof-of-life or life-certification processes let institutions confirm a beneficiary is alive and still entitled before continuing periodic payments. Recognition relies on authoritative death data and timely records updating: agreements for exchanging information on deceased persons let institutions reconcile beneficiary rolls against death registrations and stop payments promptly. Large verification exercises routinely surface unverified cohorts, indicating records needing reconciliation rather than automatic guilt. The goal is accurate, current rolls, not exclusion of legitimate survivors.'),
    ('benefits_eligibility_fraud',
     'Recognising eligibility misrepresentation versus honest claimant error',
     'SSA Social Security Ruling SSR 22-1p, fraud and similar fault standards - https://www.ssa.gov/OP_Home/rulings/oasi/33/SSR2022-01-oasi-33.html',
     'Eligibility-misrepresentation concerns occur when a claim rests on inaccurate or incomplete information material to entitlement. SSA defines fraud narrowly: it exists only when a person, with intent to defraud, makes a false statement or misrepresentation of a material fact, or conceals a material fact, used in determining rights. It separates this from similar fault, where an incorrect or incomplete material statement is knowingly made or material information knowingly concealed, without intent to defraud. Information is material when it could influence the determination of entitlement or eligibility. Recognition therefore turns on materiality plus a graded mental state, applied through documented evidentiary standards, protecting honest claimants who erred while addressing deliberate misstatement.'),
    ('benefits_wrongful_denial',
     'Wrongful denial and exclusion error as an integrity failure',
     'World Bank, Social Registries as Digital Platforms for Inclusive Service Delivery (2025) - https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2025/11/19/social-registries-digital-platforms-inclusive-service-delivery',
     'Benefits integrity is two-sided: it covers both undue payment and the wrongful denial of an entitlement to an eligible person. Exclusion errors occur when qualifying households are not enrolled and lose benefits they are due, while inclusion errors give transfers to the ineligible. Where a single registry gates many programmes, a person wrongly omitted from it can be denied multiple benefits at once. Standards-aligned practice treats lowering exclusion error as a primary objective, since no targeting method delivers fair outcomes without sound implementation of each step of the delivery chain. The remedy framing is correction and reinstatement with dignity, consistent with the legal entitlement the registry exists to honour, not merely to police.'),
    ('benefits_registry_integrity',
     'Social-registry and targeting integrity safeguards',
     'World Bank, Social Registries as Digital Platforms for Inclusive Service Delivery (2025) - https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2025/11/19/social-registries-digital-platforms-inclusive-service-delivery',
     'A social registry is a centralised information system that collects household and individual data to identify potential beneficiaries and connect them to programmes. Targeting integrity means the registry includes those who qualify and excludes those who do not, while protecting the personal data it holds. Static registries that are rarely refreshed drift out of date, producing both inclusion and exclusion error; dynamic registries linked to civil-identity systems allow continuous updating so eligibility reflects current circumstances. Standards-aligned integrity pairs verification and de-duplication with strong data protection and transparency, so the same controls that deter manipulation of records also defend genuine applicants against arbitrary or opaque exclusion.'),
    ('benefits_insider_collusion',
     'Recognising collusion and insider benefit fraud',
     'ISSA Guideline 53, Prevention and control of error, evasion and fraud in contributions and benefits - https://www.issa.int/guidelines/gg/174532',
     'Insider benefit fraud involves staff or service providers who misuse authorised access to create, approve or sustain undue payments. Collusion is especially damaging because it can override segregation of duties, the foundation of internal control, when people responsible for separated steps deliberately combine to defeat the cross-checks. Recognition does not rely on a single red flag; it looks for patterns such as one actor able to both initiate and approve a transaction, records altered without independent review, and beneficiaries or vendors added to master files without dual control. The aim is to make undue payment require improbable cooperation among controls, while protecting legitimate beneficiaries from being implicated in misconduct committed by insiders without their knowledge.'),
    ('benefits_disaster_relief_fraud',
     'Disaster and emergency relief payment integrity',
     'US GAO, A Framework for Managing Improper Payments in Emergency Assistance Programs (GAO-23-105876) - https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-23-105876',
     "Emergency and disaster assistance carries heightened improper-payment and fraud risk because the need to provide help quickly can hinder effective controls, and programmes are often built or scaled at speed. The recognised tension is speed versus controls: rapid disbursement saves lives but invites error, while excessive friction delays aid to genuine victims. GAO's framework manages this balance by committing to manage payment errors, identifying and assessing risks, designing controls such as eligibility verification, monitoring whether controls work, and sharing information to enable learning and preparedness for the next emergency. Standards-aligned practice plans controls before the crisis so legitimate survivors receive aid quickly while undue claims are screened, rather than choosing between the two."),
    ('benefits_verification_controls',
     'Data-matching and verification controls before payment',
     'US GAO blog, How Social Security Death Data is Helping Prevent Federal Payment Errors - https://www.gao.gov/blog/how-social-security-death-data-helping-prevent-federal-payment-errors',
     "Preventive verification checks a claimant's or recipient's data against authoritative datasets before money is paid, so eligibility is confirmed rather than assumed. A central data-matching service lets agencies screen payments against multiple sources, including a comprehensive death records file, because eligibility can change over time and stale records drive error. Recognition of payment risk surfaces when applicant details fail to match trusted records, such as an identifier linked to a registered death or to circumstances inconsistent with the claim. Reported pilots show meaningful prevention and recovery of payment errors. The same verification that stops undue payment also protects genuine claimants by catching data errors that would otherwise wrongly reduce their benefits."),
    ('benefits_remedy_appeal',
     'Remedy, appeal and grievance redress with due process',
     'World Bank, Grievance Redress Service and grievance redress mechanisms in social protection - https://www.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/products-and-services/grievance-redress-service',
     'Integrity requires accessible remedy, not just enforcement. A grievance redress mechanism is the set of principles, structures, rules and procedures through which queries, complaints and appeals about a social protection programme are resolved. Well-designed mechanisms move through uptake, sorting, acknowledgement, verifying-investigating-acting, monitoring and feedback, with explicit space for appeals. Redress typically starts at community level and escalates through local administrative entities to national institutions or an independent ombudsman when it cannot be resolved locally. Beneficiary grievances commonly involve missed or delayed payments, payment errors, wrongful exclusion, or burdensome access to payment points. Due process means recovery and termination follow notice, evidence and a genuine opportunity to be heard.'),
    ('customs_wco_safe',
     'WCO SAFE Framework: Secured Supply Chain and Trusted Traders',
     'WCO SAFE Framework of Standards to Secure and Facilitate Global Trade (adopted 2005). https://www.wcoomd.org/en/topics/facilitation/instrument-and-tools/frameworks-of-standards/safe_package.aspx',
     "Authority: the WCO SAFE Framework (2005) is the global standard for securing supply chains, on three pillars - Customs-to-Customs networks, the Customs-to-Business Authorized Economic Operator (AEO) partnership, and Customs-to-other-agencies cooperation. It mandates advance electronic cargo information and risk management to target high-risk shipments. Recognition red flags that a movement evades SAFE controls: cargo with no or contradictory advance data; consignees or routings that do not match the manifest; a 'trusted trader' claim not verifiable against a real AEO authorization; pressure to bypass pre-arrival screening. Remedy: customs apply risk-based examination, suspend release pending verification, and may revoke AEO status for integrity breaches; report suspected manifest falsification to the importing administration rather than acting on the shipper's claim."),
    ('customs_revised_kyoto',
     'Revised Kyoto Convention: Harmonized, Audit-Based Customs Procedures',
     'WCO Revised Kyoto Convention (International Convention on the Simplification and Harmonization of Customs Procedures), in force 2006. https://www.wcoomd.org/en/topics/facilitation/instrument-and-tools/conventions/pf_revised_kyoto_conv.aspx',
     "Authority: the Revised Kyoto Convention is the blueprint for modern customs procedures, requiring transparency, predictability, standardized goods declarations, maximum use of information technology, proportionate controls, risk management, audit-based control, and partnership with trade. Because legitimate procedures are published and standardized, deviations are detectable. Recognition red flags: a broker or official directing goods around the published declaration process; requests to use a non-standard form, off-book channel, or undocumented 'fast lane'; refusal to issue a written, reviewable decision; controls that appear arbitrary rather than risk-based. Remedy: insist on the standardized declaration and a written, appealable decision; rely on post-clearance audit; escalate procedures that contradict the convention's transparency obligations to the administration's review channel."),
    ('customs_aeo',
     'Authorized Economic Operator: Validated Trust and Mutual Recognition',
     'WCO AEO Compendium and SAFE Framework Pillar 2. https://www.wcoomd.org/en/topics/facilitation/instrument-and-tools/tools/aeo-compendium.aspx',
     "Authority: AEO programs under SAFE Pillar 2 certify operators that demonstrate compliance history, sound record-keeping, financial solvency, and supply-chain security and safety standards; benefits include expedited clearance and reduced inspections, and Mutual Recognition Arrangements let one administration accept another's AEO status. AEO status is granted by customs, not self-declared. Recognition red flags: a counterparty asserting AEO benefits with no certificate number, an expired or mismatched authorization, or a claim of mutual recognition where no MRA exists; use of an AEO 'badge' to pressure a partner to skip due diligence. Remedy: verify the authorization against the issuing administration's records and the AEO Compendium; customs may suspend or revoke status for integrity or security failures, so report misuse of an AEO claim to the certifying administration."),
    ('customs_valuation_fraud',
     'Customs Valuation: Transaction Value and Undervaluation Red Flags',
     'WTO Agreement on Customs Valuation (Implementation of Article VII of GATT 1994); WCO Customs Valuation overview. https://www.wto.org/english/tratop_e/cusval_e/cusval_e.htm ; https://www.wcoomd.org/en/topics/valuation/overview/what-is-customs-valuation.aspx',
     "Authority: the WTO Customs Valuation Agreement establishes a fair, uniform, neutral system, using transaction value - the price actually paid or payable plus required adjustments (assists, royalties, commissions, packing, proceeds) - as the primary basis, with five further methods in sequence; it bars arbitrary or fictitious values. Over 90 percent of trade uses transaction value, so anomalies are detectable. Recognition red flags: declared prices far below comparable goods; split or dual invoicing; omitted assists, royalties, or commissions; related-party prices not at arm's length; mis-invoicing surfaced by mirror analysis. Remedy: customs use valuation databases and risk analysis to question doubtful values, reassess duty, and impose penalties; the importer can seek a written, appealable valuation determination rather than an off-record undervaluation deal."),
    ('customs_origin_fraud',
     'Rules of Origin: Substantial Transformation and Transshipment Fraud',
     'ITA / U.S. CBP rules of origin and substantial transformation guidance. https://www.trade.gov/rules-origin-substantial-transformation',
     "Authority: country of origin is determined by 'wholly obtained' or 'substantial transformation' (a fundamental change in name, character, or use; under FTAs often a tariff shift, regional value content, or specified processing). Minor processing - repackaging, dilution, simple assembly, relabeling - does not confer new origin. Origin fraud and illegal transshipment misrepresent where goods were made to dodge duties or trade-remedy orders. Recognition red flags: goods routed through a third country that only repackages or relabels them; origin certificates inconsistent with the claimed country's manufacturing capacity; 'origin' based solely on a transit point. Remedy: verify substantial transformation against actual production; CBP enforces via origin verification, penalties, and the Enforce and Protect Act; suspected false origin can be reported via CBP e-Allegations."),
    ('customs_misclassification',
     'HS Misclassification: Wrong Tariff Code and False Description Red Flags',
     'U.S. CBP e-Allegations trade violations (misclassified / falsely described merchandise). https://www.cbp.gov/trade/e-allegations/violations',
     "Authority: imported goods must be classified under the correct Harmonized System / HTSUS code, which sets the duty rate and whether antidumping or countervailing orders apply; importers owe reasonable care. Deliberate misclassification or false description moves goods to a lower (sometimes free) rate or outside a trade-remedy order. Recognition red flags: a declared HS code that does not match the goods' true form, material, or function; vague or euphemistic descriptions; a code chosen to fall just outside an AD/CVD scope; classifications that change without any change in the product. Remedy: confirm classification against the tariff schedule and binding rulings; CBP may reclassify, collect the duty difference, and assess fraud-statute penalties; doubtful or abusive classifications can be reported via CBP e-Allegations and resolved through a written ruling."),
    ('customs_transit_fraud',
     'Customs Transit and Duty Suspension: Diversion Recognition',
     'European Commission - Customs transit (Union/Common Transit, TIR). https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/customs/customs-procedures-import-and-export/customs-transit_en',
     "Authority: transit moves goods under suspension of duties and taxes between two points (Union/Common Transit, TIR carnet), secured by a guarantee held by the procedure holder, with cargo sealed and identifiable; it is discharged only when goods reach the declared destination or exit the territory. Because revenue loss on any diverted shipment is total, transit is a high fraud risk. Recognition red flags: a transit consignment that never arrives at the office of destination; broken or substituted seals; quantities or descriptions that change in transit; goods surfacing on the domestic market without a discharge or import entry; missing or invalid guarantee. Remedy: customs require timely discharge, enforce against the guarantee on non-arrival, and investigate diversion; insist on proper sealing, a valid guarantee, and confirmed discharge rather than informal 'in-transit' disposal."),
    ('customs_duty_evasion',
     'Duty and Tariff Evasion and Drawback Fraud Recognition',
     'U.S. CBP e-Allegations duty-evasion methods and drawback program. https://www.cbp.gov/trade/e-allegations/violations',
     'Authority: lawfully owed duties, fees, and taxes must be paid on imports; the duty-drawback program refunds up to 99 percent of duty when qualifying merchandise is exported. Evasion schemes CBP catalogs include undervaluation, false description, misclassification, false origin and manufacturer declarations, false preference claims, quota evasion, and false drawback claims. Recognition red flags: refunds claimed on goods never imported, never exported, or not matching the drawback documentation; double drawback claims; entries engineered to evade a safeguard or trade-remedy duty; declarations inconsistent with shipping and commercial records. Remedy: CBP recovers unpaid duty, denies improper refunds, and pursues fraud-statute and False Claims Act penalties; suspected evasion or false drawback can be reported through CBP e-Allegations and is validated by import specialists and auditors.'),
    ('customs_tfa',
     'WTO Trade Facilitation Agreement: Integrity and Due-Process Provisions',
     'WTO Agreement on Trade Facilitation, in force 2017 (Articles 1, 3, 4, 6, 7). https://www.wto.org/english/tratop_e/tradfa_e/tradfa_e.htm',
     "Authority: the WTO Trade Facilitation Agreement requires members to publish customs procedures and fees (Article 1), issue advance rulings (Article 3), provide a right of appeal or review (Article 4), discipline fees and charges and require proportionate, transparent penalties (Article 6), and expedite release while permitting post-clearance audit (Article 7). These make arbitrary or off-book demands recognizable. Recognition red flags: fees or 'facilitation' charges that are unpublished or exceed the cost of service; penalties imposed without written explanation or any avenue of appeal; denial of a binding advance ruling; clearance conditioned on an informal payment. Remedy: request the published rate and a written, appealable decision; rely on advance rulings and audit-based control; escalate undisclosed charges or denied appeals through the administration's review mechanism."),
    ('customs_cargo_security',
     'Cargo Security and Supply-Chain Integrity: CTPAT',
     'U.S. CBP Customs Trade Partnership Against Terrorism (CTPAT). https://www.cbp.gov/border-security/ports-entry/cargo-security/ctpat',
     "Authority: CTPAT is a voluntary, no-cost public-private partnership in which importers, carriers, brokers, and manufacturers meet CBP Minimum Security Criteria and document a supply-chain security profile; members are treated as low risk and receive reduced examinations, front-of-line processing, and possible foreign mutual recognition. Membership is validated by CBP, not self-asserted. Recognition red flags: a partner claiming CTPAT status with no member profile or specialist of record; security gaps such as unsealed or unmonitored conveyances, unvetted sub-carriers, or no chain-of-custody at transfer points; use of a 'trusted' claim to discourage inspection. Remedy: verify membership and security criteria; CBP can suspend or remove members for security failures, so report unsealed cargo, custody gaps, or false trusted-trader claims to a CTPAT field office or security specialist."),
    ('customs_ftz_diversion',
     'Free-Trade-Zone Diversion: Duty-Deferred Goods Leaking into Commerce',
     'U.S. CBP Foreign-Trade Zones overview (19 U.S.C. 81a-81u; 19 CFR Part 146). https://www.cbp.gov/border-security/ports-entry/cargo-security/cargo-control/foreign-trade-zones/about',
     'Authority: Foreign-Trade Zones are secure areas under CBP supervision, generally treated as outside CBP territory; merchandise is admitted on CBP Form 214 and formal entry and duty are deferred until goods enter CBP territory for consumption. Zone goods stay under customs control and records face spot checks. Diversion occurs when duty-deferred or re-export goods leak into the domestic market without proper entry and duty. Recognition red flags: inventory that cannot be reconciled to admission and removal records; product leaving the zone without a CBP entry or transportation bond; re-export claims unsupported by export evidence; status manipulation to dodge an antidumping order. Remedy: CBP reconciles zone records, demands the deferred duty, and pursues penalties; insist on documented Form 214 admission, proper entry on removal, and verifiable export before treating goods as duty-free.'),
    ('customs_enforcement_remedy',
     'Customs Enforcement and Remedy: Audit, Penalties, and Mutual Assistance',
     'U.S. CBP Enforce and Protect Act (EAPA), TFTEA 2015; WCO Nairobi Convention on mutual administrative assistance (1977). https://www.cbp.gov/trade/eapa ; https://www.wcoomd.org/en/topics/enforcement-and-compliance/instruments-and-tools/wco-and-international-instruments-on-mutual-administrative-assistance.aspx',
     'Authority: customs enforce process integrity through post-clearance audit, penalties, seizure, and recovery of evaded duty. Under the U.S. Enforce and Protect Act, an interested party may allege antidumping/countervailing-duty evasion; CBP must decide interim measures within 90 days and a final determination within 300 days, may apply adverse inferences against non-cooperating parties, and the determination is reviewable at the Court of International Trade. The WCO Nairobi Convention enables mutual administrative assistance so administrations exchange information and evidence on customs offences. Recognition: unresolved valuation, origin, classification, transit, or drawback anomalies trigger the remedy path. Remedy: file a documented allegation via CBP e-Allegations, cooperate with audit, and pursue statutory appeal and inter-administration assistance - not informal settlement.'),
    ('energy_electricity_theft',
     'Recognizing electricity theft and non-technical losses (World Bank)',
     'World Bank PPP Resource Center, Managing Non-Technical Losses and Theft in Utility Contracts (fetched https://ppp.worldbank.org/public-private-partnership/legislation-regulation/laws/theft-nontechnical-loss)',
     "Non-technical loss (NTL) is the World Bank's euphemism for theft of utility service: power consumed but never billed. Recognition signals are a persistent gap between energy generated or purchased and energy billed, NTL rates that can exceed 50 percent of output, and transformer zones where master-meter readings far outrun the sum of customer meters. Drivers are illegal connections, meter tampering, and refusal to pay; honest customers, taxpayers, and the underserved poor absorb the cost through higher tariffs and deficient service. Legal authority: many states codify utility theft as a distinct offense (e.g. India's Electricity Act 2003, ss.135-139, with Special Courts). Remedy runs through utility revenue-protection units, metered balancing at distribution transformers, community engagement, and prosecution under those statutes rather than ordinary theft law."),
    ('energy_meter_tampering',
     'Recognizing meter tampering and bypass on electricity meters',
     'CLOU Global, Tampering of Electric Energy Meters (fetched https://clouglobal.com/tempering-of-electric-energy-meters/)',
     'Meter tampering is a non-technical loss in which a customer manipulates the meter to under-record consumption. Recognition signals include a broken or missing meter seal, a meter found inverted or jammed, optical-port or terminal-cover seals that do not match installation records, and consumption that drops sharply with no change in occupancy or load. Balancing each customer against a transformer master meter exposes zones of indirect tampering; guidance treats loss above roughly 12 percent as likely theft, not technical line loss. Authority sits with the utility revenue-protection function and statutes making tampering an offense. Remedy stresses recognition not method: tamper-resistant enclosures, split meters, sealing schemes, random installation checks, and AMI tamper-event logging. This lists warning signs only and gives no instructions for defeating a meter.'),
    ('energy_fuel_adulteration',
     'Recognizing fuel adulteration and dilution in the supply chain',
     'Authentix, Fuel Marking Programs (fetched https://authentix.com/solutions-by-product/fuel-marking/); ENACT/ISS, Illicit fuel trade on the rise (fetched https://enactafrica.org/research/trend-reports/illicit-fuel-trade-on-the-rise)',
     "Fuel adulteration is the covert blending of cheaper hydrocarbons - kerosene, solvents, or waste oils - into branded motor fuel for profit, harming engines, the environment, and public health. Recognition relies on authentication: national fuel-marking programs add an invisible chemical marker at the terminal, and field analyzers or lateral-flow kits at terminals, borders, and stations confirm in minutes whether the marker is present at the correct concentration. A marker absent, diluted below range, or paired with an off-spec density or chromatography result flags dilution. Forensic GC-MS confirms intent to evade tax, abuse subsidy, or sell off-spec fuel. Authority lies with petroleum regulators, revenue authorities, and brand owners. Remedy: marking plus inspection - Ghana's program cut adulteration by 78 percent. Recognition guidance only, no formulation detail."),
    ('energy_fuel_smuggling',
     'Recognizing fuel smuggling and subsidy-price arbitrage',
     'UNODC, Fuel Trafficking in the Sahel - TOCTA Sahel (fetched https://www.unodc.org/documents/data-and-analysis/tocta_sahel/TOCTA_Sahel_fuel_2023.pdf); ENACT/ISS (fetched https://enactafrica.org/research/trend-reports/illicit-fuel-trade-on-the-rise)',
     "Fuel smuggling is the illegal movement of subsidized or low-tax fuel into a higher-price jurisdiction to capture the price gap (arbitrage). The illicit fuel trade is estimated near USD 133 billion a year across theft, adulteration, fraud, tax evasion, and subsidy abuse. Recognition signals at borders include large pump-price differentials between neighboring states, retail volumes inconsistent with legitimate supply, jerrycan and roadside sales near frontiers, and customs declarations whose value, volume, or commodity do not match inspection (trade misinvoicing). UNODC documents per-truck bribes exceeding officials' monthly salaries and proceeds that fund armed groups. Authority spans customs, petroleum regulators, INTERPOL-supported task forces, and anti-corruption bodies. Remedy: fuel marking, volume reconciliation, and customs cooperation. Recognition only - no routing methods."),
    ('energy_subsidy_diversion',
     'Recognizing fossil-fuel subsidy diversion and leakage (IEA)',
     'International Energy Agency, Fossil Fuel Subsidies topic page (fetched https://www.iea.org/topics/fossil-fuel-subsidies)',
     'Fossil-fuel consumption subsidies hold end-user prices below the full cost of supply; the IEA measures them with the price-gap method (reference price minus end-user price, times units consumed). In 2022 they exceeded USD 1 trillion for the first time, and governments still spent about USD 620 billion in 2023, far above the roughly USD 70 billion for consumer clean-energy support. The recognition concern is leakage: many subsidies are poorly targeted and disproportionately benefit higher-income groups who use the most subsidized fuel, while distorting price signals and enabling smuggling. Red flags that a subsidy is captured rather than reaching the poor include benefits skewed to wealthier households and price gaps that invite arbitrage. Authority and remedy lie with finance and energy ministries and the G20/COP reform commitments the IEA supports.'),
    ('energy_renewable_subsidy_fraud',
     'Recognizing renewable-energy subsidy and incentive fraud',
     'U.S. DOJ, District of Colorado press release - renewable energy company settles ARRA fraud allegations (fetched 2026-06-05 https://www.justice.gov/usao-co/pr/renewable-energy-company-pays-387000-settle-allegations-fraud-american-recovery-and)',
     'Renewable-energy incentive fraud occurs when applicants misstate facts to draw more public money than a project warrants from grant, tax-credit, or feed-in-tariff schemes. A documented U.S. case under the American Recovery and Reinvestment Act involved a wind developer that inflated installation costs to several times the true cost while concealing the originals, so grants reimbursed far more than the intended share; it settled for a six-figure sum. Recognition signals include cost figures inconsistent with invoices or benchmarks, undisclosed commission payments, output that cannot be reconciled with meter data, and claims for projects not actually built or energized. Authority runs through grant administrators, treasuries, auditors, and False Claims Act / qui tam whistleblower channels. Remedy: clawback, civil penalties, and debarment. Detection of misrepresentation only.'),
    ('energy_market_manipulation',
     'Recognizing energy-market manipulation (FERC anti-manipulation rule)',
     'U.S. Federal Energy Regulatory Commission, Enforcement and Prohibition of Energy Market Manipulation (fetched https://www.ferc.gov/enforcement)',
     "Under the Federal Power Act and Natural Gas Act, as amended by the Energy Policy Act of 2005, FERC's Anti-Manipulation Rule (18 C.F.R. 1c) makes it unlawful to use any device, scheme, or artifice to defraud in jurisdictional electricity or gas transactions. Recognized manipulative conduct includes wash trades (offsetting buy/sell pairs with no economic risk that create false volume or price), matched orders, and physical trades placed to benefit a related financial position - the pattern FERC penalized in major bank and trading-firm cases. Recognition signals include money-losing trades that only make sense as support for a larger derivatives position, volume that misrepresents genuine supply or demand, and prices moving against fundamentals near settlement. Fraud and manipulation is FERC's top priority; remedy from its Office of Enforcement includes disgorgement and civil penalties."),
    ('energy_procurement_fraud',
     'Recognizing procurement and construction fraud in energy projects',
     'World Bank Group press release, Debarment of Two Chinese Construction Companies on an Energy Project (fetched https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2018/07/19/world-bank-group-announces-debarment-of-two-chinese-construction-companies-working-on-energy-project)',
     "Energy infrastructure projects are exposed to fraud and corruption in bidding and execution. In a World Bank energy-efficiency project, two contractors were debarred after falsifying documents certifying work was complete to draw advance payments, and after misrepresenting subcontracted work as done in-house. Other Bank energy cases involved improper payments to officials and undisclosed agent commissions to influence awards. Recognition signals include completion certificates not matched by physical progress, advance-payment requests tied to unverified milestones, undisclosed commissions or agents, bids reflecting leaked information, and collusive bidding. Authority sits with the Bank's Integrity Vice Presidency and equivalent anti-corruption bodies. Remedy: debarment (cross-recognized among development banks), integrity-compliance programs, and contract sanctions."),
    ('energy_emissions_misreporting',
     'Recognizing carbon-intensity and emissions misreporting',
     "European Public Prosecutor's Office, Bulgaria EPPO probes multi-million euro fraud regarding greenhouse gas emissions (fetched https://www.eppo.europa.eu/en/media/news/bulgaria-eppo-probes-multi-million-euro-fraud-regarding-greenhouse-gas-emissions)",
     "Emissions misreporting is the submission of false monitoring data to under-declare a facility's greenhouse-gas output, evading compliance costs and distorting markets. In a EU Emissions Trading System case, a company accredited to verify thermal and heating plants allegedly filed falsified annual reports from 2017 onward, under-declaring emissions and causing losses of millions of euro to EU and national budgets while worsening local air quality. Recognition signals include verifier independence conflicts, reported emissions inconsistent with fuel-use or output records, and monitoring data that cannot be reconciled with continuous-emission sensors. This links to the carbon-integrity vertical. Authority spans environmental regulators, accreditation bodies, and prosecutors (e.g. EPPO). Remedy: investigation, allowance repayment, and loss of accreditation."),
    ('energy_offgrid_integrity',
     'Recognizing integrity risks in off-grid and mini-grid programs',
     'Brookings, Lessons from the proliferating mini-grid incentive programs in Africa (fetched https://www.brookings.edu/articles/lessons-from-the-proliferating-mini-grid-incentive-programs-in-africa/)',
     'Off-grid and mini-grid electrification programs deliver public subsidy - often results-based financing of roughly USD 350-500 per connection or auctions covering 60-80 percent of upfront capital - to extend access where the main grid does not reach. A Brookings review of 20 sub-Saharan programs found integrity risk concentrated in opacity: many run without a regulatory framework, and budget non-transparency lets cost per connection range from USD 348 to USD 2,500. Recognition signals include connection or output claims not verified by smart-meter data, capital costs outside the observed USD 4,400-6,200 per kW band, payments released without verification of energized connections, and tariffs set without disclosed cost basis. Authority lies with energy regulators, donors, and auditors. Remedy: payment tied to verified output, transparent budgets, and bankable rules.'),
    ('energy_eiti_transparency',
     'EITI energy-sector revenue transparency as an integrity baseline',
     'Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative, eiti.org and the 2023 EITI Standard (fetched https://eiti.org/)',
     'The Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative (EITI) is a global standard, implemented in dozens of countries, for open and accountable management of oil, gas, and mineral resources. The EITI Standard requires disclosure along the value chain: how licenses and contracts are allocated, who the beneficial owners are, fiscal terms, production volumes, payments to government, and how revenue reaches the public. The 2023 Standard strengthens beneficial-ownership and commodity-trading disclosures. As an integrity baseline, the signal of elevated risk is the gap from EITI: undisclosed beneficial owners, unpublished contracts, company payments that do not reconcile with government receipts, and opaque commodity-trading flows. Authority is the multi-stakeholder group in each country plus the international EITI Board. Remedy: publish reconciled data and act on discrepancies.'),
    ('energy_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: regulators and revenue-protection units',
     'U.S. FERC Office of Enforcement (fetched https://www.ferc.gov/enforcement); World Bank PPP on utility revenue protection (fetched https://ppp.worldbank.org/public-private-partnership/legislation-regulation/laws/theft-nontechnical-loss)',
     "Recognizing energy fraud only matters if it routes to a remedy. Market-side conduct (manipulation, wash trades, fraud in power and gas markets) is handled by regulators such as FERC, whose Office of Enforcement makes fraud its top priority and seeks disgorgement plus civil penalties; many regulators run confidential enforcement hotlines. Utility-side theft and non-technical loss route to revenue-protection units and to statutes making meter tampering a distinct offense, with bodies such as South Africa's SARPA coordinating practice. Project corruption routes to integrity and audit bodies with debarment and clawback powers; fuel and subsidy crime to customs and INTERPOL-supported task forces; emissions fraud to environmental prosecutors. The general remedy chain: detect, document with evidential testing or reconciled data, report to the authority, recover funds, and impose penalties."),
    ('sci_ffp_definition',
     'Research Misconduct: Fabrication, Falsification, Plagiarism (US ORI, 42 CFR 93)',
     'US Office of Research Integrity, Definition of Research Misconduct (42 CFR 93.103/93.104) - https://ori.hhs.gov/definition-research-misconduct',
     "The US federal standard defines research misconduct as fabrication, falsification, or plagiarism (FFP) in proposing, performing, or reviewing research, or in reporting results. Fabrication is making up data or results. Falsification is manipulating materials, equipment, or processes, or changing or omitting data so the record is not accurately represented. Plagiarism is appropriating another's ideas, processes, results, or words without appropriate credit. A finding requires a significant departure from accepted practices of the relevant community, an act committed intentionally, knowingly, or recklessly, and proof by a preponderance of the evidence. Honest error and good-faith differences of opinion are excluded. Recognition signal: data that cannot be reconciled with the described methods. Remedy: institutional inquiry under ORI oversight, then correction or retraction."),
    ('sci_singapore_statement',
     'Singapore Statement on Research Integrity: Four Global Principles',
     'Office of Research Integrity / 2nd World Conference on Research Integrity (2010), Singapore Statement - https://ori.hhs.gov/singapore-statement-research-integrity',
     'The Singapore Statement on Research Integrity, developed at the 2nd World Conference on Research Integrity in 2010, is a global reference for responsible conduct of research. It rests on four principles: honesty in all aspects of research; accountability in the conduct of research; professional courtesy and fairness in working with others; and good stewardship of research on behalf of others. It sets fourteen responsibilities covering integrity, adherence to regulations, sound methods, accurate records, honest reporting and authorship, responsible peer review, disclosure of conflicts, and reporting of irresponsible practice. It is a non-regulatory framework, not a substitute for national law. Recognition value: it is the shared yardstick reviewers and institutions use to judge conduct. Remedy framing: transparent institutional processes for investigating and correcting breaches.'),
    ('sci_peer_review_fraud',
     'Peer-Review Fraud: Fake Reviewers and Rigged Reviews',
     'Retraction Watch reporting on fabricated peer review; COPE estimates - https://retractionwatch.com/2015/08/17/64-more-papers-retracted-for-fake-reviews-this-time-from-springer-journals/',
     'Peer-review fraud subverts the independent evaluation that protects the scientific record. Documented schemes include fabricated reviewer identities tied to controllable email addresses, stolen identities of real experts, authors writing favourable reviews of their own work, and third-party agencies that rig reviews. Mass retractions across major publishers have removed dozens of papers per incident; COPE estimates roughly one in ten retractions involves faked reviewing. Recognition signals: suggested reviewers reachable only at non-institutional addresses, suspiciously rapid or uniformly glowing reports, and contacts that cannot be independently verified. Standard: reviewers must be genuinely independent and disclose conflicts. Remedy: journals verify reviewer identity through institutional channels, retract or correct affected papers, and notify institutions.'),
    ('sci_image_manipulation',
     'Recognizing Image Manipulation and Duplication in Figures',
     'ASM Journals Image Integrity policy; Frontiers analysis of gel/Western blot cropping - https://journals.asm.org/image-integrity',
     'Image integrity standards require that figures faithfully represent the original data. Inappropriate manipulation includes undisclosed splicing of non-adjacent gel or blot lanes, copy-move duplication of bands between experiments, selective erasure of features, and brightness or contrast adjustments applied to only part of an image. Acceptable practice: adjustments applied evenly to the whole image and disclosed, loading controls run on the same gel, spliced lanes clearly marked, and full uncropped images available on request. Recognition signals: repeated identical bands, sharp tonal seams, and backgrounds that do not match. Image-related problems appear in thousands of database-logged retractions, with gel and Western blots prominent. Remedy: editors request original unprocessed files, apply forensic screening, and issue correction or retraction where misrepresentation is confirmed.'),
    ('sci_data_fabrication',
     'Recognition Signals for Data Fabrication and Falsification',
     'ORI guidance on statistical/forensic detection; review of statistical methods to detect data anomalies (PMC) - https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12121900/',
     "Fabricated or falsified data depart from the natural variability of genuine measurement. Recognition signals discussed in the integrity literature include implausibly consistent results, terminal-digit and digit-preference anomalies, distributions that deviate from expectations such as Benford's Law, duplicated rows or blocks of values, results that exceed the stated capabilities of the equipment or setting, and summary statistics inconsistent with the reported sample. These are screening flags, not proof; honest error and legitimate variation must be ruled out. The US Office of Research Integrity has documented statistical and forensic analyses of questioned data in misconduct cases. Standard: raw data must support the reported record. Remedy: request primary data and lab records, perform forensic review, and refer confirmed cases for investigation and correction."),
    ('sci_authorship_abuse',
     'Authorship Abuse: Gift and Ghost Authorship (COPE Guidance)',
     'Committee on Publication Ethics (COPE), authorship guidance and flowcharts - https://publicationethics.org/guidance/flowchart/ghost-guest-or-gift-authorship-submitted-manuscript',
     "Authorship should reflect genuine, substantial contribution. COPE describes gift or guest authorship as listing a person who did not contribute meaningfully, sometimes added as a favour or due to seniority, and ghost authorship as omitting someone who did contribute significantly, such as an undisclosed industry writer. Both misrepresent accountability for the work. Recognition signals: implausibly prolific authors, department heads added without a defined role, and undisclosed professional writing. Standard: contributorship should be agreed in writing before the paper is drafted, and all who meet authorship criteria are named while others are acknowledged. Remedy: where a problem is suspected the editor suspends review, seeks clarification from the corresponding author, follows the COPE flowchart, and corrects the authorship list based on the journal's findings."),
    ('sci_trial_registration',
     'Clinical-Trial Registration and Selective-Reporting Controls (ICMJE)',
     'International Committee of Medical Journal Editors (ICMJE), Clinical Trial Registration recommendation - https://www.icmje.org/recommendations/browse/publishing-and-editorial-issues/clinical-trial-registration.html',
     'ICMJE member journals require prospective registration of clinical trials in a public, WHO-approved registry at or before first patient enrolment as a condition of publication. Prospective registration exists to prevent selective publication and selective outcome reporting; retrospective registration at submission defeats that purpose and is discouraged. Trials must also include data-sharing statements describing whether de-identified participant data will be shared and under what terms. Recognition signals: outcomes in the manuscript that differ from the registered primary outcomes, missing or post-hoc registration, and unexplained omission of pre-specified endpoints. Standard: the published report must align with the registered protocol. Remedy: editors compare manuscript to registry, require explanation of discrepancies, and may decline, correct, or flag selective reporting.'),
    ('sci_conflict_of_interest',
     'Conflict-of-Interest Disclosure Standards (ICMJE)',
     'ICMJE, Author Responsibilities - Conflicts of Interest - https://www.icmje.org/recommendations/browse/roles-and-responsibilities/author-responsibilities--conflicts-of-interest.html',
     "Conflict-of-interest disclosure lets readers judge whether relationships could bias reported work; ICMJE notes that perceived conflicts can erode trust as much as actual ones. Authors must disclose all relevant relationships and activities, including employment, consultancies, stock ownership, honoraria, patents, paid testimony, and full funding sources plus the sponsor's role in design, analysis, and publication decisions. Reviewers must disclose conflicts, recuse where bias is possible, and not use unpublished knowledge for gain; editors recuse from conflicted decisions. A standardized ICMJE disclosure form is used. Recognition signals: undisclosed industry funding, sponsor control over data or publication, and reviewer ties to the work. Remedy: require disclosure before review, publish it, and correct the record where material conflicts were concealed."),
    ('sci_retractions',
     'Retractions and Corrections as a Self-Correction Mechanism',
     'Retraction Watch, database and leaderboard methodology - https://retractionwatch.com/the-retraction-watch-leaderboard/',
     'Retractions and corrections are the formal mechanism for repairing the published record when work is found to be unreliable. Retraction Watch maintains an independent database and an unofficial leaderboard of authors with the most retractions, tracking withdrawals as a window into the scientific process. Papers are withdrawn for reasons ranging from data fabrication and image manipulation to fraudulent peer review and, increasingly, undisclosed AI-generated content. A retraction is not in itself a misconduct verdict; some are due to honest error. Recognition value: a retraction or expression of concern signals that downstream work relying on the paper should be reassessed. Standard: notices should state the reason and who initiated them. Remedy: prompt, transparent retraction or correction keeps the literature trustworthy and lets the community discount compromised findings.'),
    ('sci_predatory_citation',
     'Predatory Publishing and Citation Manipulation',
     'COPE discussion document on citation manipulation; Wikipedia entry on coercive citation - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coercive_citation',
     "Predatory journals exploit fee-based publishing while skipping genuine editorial and review controls; recognition signals include names mimicking established titles, fabricated or misleading impact metrics, promises of rapid publication, and conspicuous errors. A related abuse is citation manipulation: coercive citation, where an editor or reviewer pressures authors to add irrelevant references to inflate a journal's impact factor, and citation cartels, where authors or journals coordinate to cite each other. Removing artificial self-citations can sharply drop a manipulated impact factor, exposing the distortion. Standard: citations should be relevant and editorially justified, and metrics should reflect genuine influence. Remedy: editors must not demand unjustified self-citation, the community treats coercion as misconduct, and indexing bodies can suspend cartel-like journals."),
    ('sci_reproducibility',
     'Reproducibility and Open-Data Integrity Standards',
     'TOP (Transparency and Openness Promotion) Guidelines and FAIR data principles, via reproducibility-toolkit and open-science sources - https://blog.scholasticahq.com/post/research-integrity-toolkit-reproducibility-replicability/',
     'Reproducibility standards address the replication crisis by making methods, data, and code transparent enough for independent verification. The FAIR principles call for data that are Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable, and the TOP Guidelines define eight transparency standards, including data and code transparency and preregistration, across escalating compliance levels. Preregistration reduces undisclosed analytic flexibility, and computational reproducibility requires access to the original data and code. Recognition signals: results that cannot be regenerated from supplied data, missing code or materials, and outcomes that shift from a preregistered plan. Standard: claims should be reconstructable from shared artefacts. Remedy: journals adopt data-availability and preregistration policies and require deposit of data and code.'),
    ('sci_oversight_remedy',
     'Investigation, Oversight, and Remedy (Institutional Inquiry and ORI)',
     'US Office of Research Integrity, Handling Misconduct process (42 CFR 93) - https://ori.hhs.gov/handling-misconduct',
     'US research-misconduct oversight follows a staged process: receipt and assessment of an allegation, an institutional inquiry to decide whether a full investigation is warranted, the investigation, an institutional decision, ORI oversight review, the PHS decision, and an opportunity to contest findings before an administrative law judge. Findings require proof by a preponderance of the evidence that conduct was committed intentionally, knowingly, or recklessly. The institution carries the primary investigative duty under ORI oversight. Recognition value: a documented, impartial process distinguishes confirmed misconduct from honest error and protects the accused and whistleblowers. Administrative actions are proportionate to seriousness and may include correction or retraction notices to journals and repositories, supervision or certification requirements, and funding restrictions.'),
    ('telecom_irsf',
     'International Revenue Share Fraud (IRSF): Inflated Traffic to Premium Numbers',
     "Sahin & Francillon et al., 'Understanding and Detecting International Revenue Share Fraud', NDSS Symposium. https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss-paper/understanding-and-detecting-international-revenue-share-fraud/",
     'Authority: IRSF exploits carrier revenue-sharing settlements. An International Premium Rate Number (IPRN) provider passes part of the per-minute charge back to whoever drives traffic to that number, so fraudsters working with a rogue operator artificially inflate call volume to harvest the share; the Communications Fraud Control Association attributes billions in annual losses to it. Recognition red flags: spikes of long-duration international calls to rarely dialed high-cost destinations or specific test ranges; calls outside business hours from a single account, PBX, or SIM block; traffic concentrated on number ranges flagged in IPRN hot-lists. Remedy: operators apply rate-limiting, velocity rules, and CDR analytics, block suspect ranges, and share high-risk numbers through industry exchanges rather than settling the disputed traffic blindly.'),
    ('telecom_sim_box_bypass',
     'SIM-Box and Interconnect Bypass Fraud Recognition',
     'ITU / industry analysis of interconnect bypass and SIM-box grey routes (LATRO). https://latro.com/blog/what-is-bypass-fraud-in-telecom-and-how-to-prevent-it/',
     'Authority: interconnect bypass fraud terminates international calls as if they were local traffic, using SIM-box devices loaded with many prepaid SIMs to dodge the lawful international termination fee and pocket the rate difference; the ITU has urged new protocols to address it. This creates grey traffic that hides true origin and degrades call quality. Recognition red flags: international calls arriving with a local or no calling-line identity (CLI); poor voice quality, latency, jitter, or one-way audio on calls that should be carrier-grade; a single SIM placing abnormal call volumes with no incoming calls or mobility. Remedy: operators run test-call generation to confirm grey routes, analyze SS7/SIP/Diameter signaling and CDRs, deactivate offending SIMs, and work with regulators and law enforcement who use RF geolocation to seize equipment under strong SIM-registration (KYC) rules.'),
    ('telecom_wangiri',
     "Wangiri 'One-Ring' Callback Fraud: FCC Recognition Guidance",
     "U.S. Federal Communications Commission, 'One Ring Phone Scam' consumer guide. https://www.fcc.gov/consumers/guides/one-ring-phone-scam",
     "Authority: the FCC warns that if a phone rings once from an unrecognized number and stops, calling back may connect to a number outside the U.S. that charges a connection fee plus high per-minute fees for as long as the caller stays on the line - charges that surface on the bill as premium services, international, or toll calling. Wangiri ('one ring and cut') is a one-ring variant of revenue-share fraud. Recognition red flags: a single-ring missed call from an unfamiliar number; three-digit codes that resemble a U.S. area code but route internationally (the FCC cites 232 for Sierra Leone and 809 for the Dominican Republic). Remedy: the FCC advises not returning unknown one-ring calls, checking whether the code is international, asking the carrier to block outbound international calling if unneeded, disputing the charge with the carrier first, and reporting the call to the FCC."),
    ('telecom_subscription_fraud',
     'Subscription / Application Fraud Recognition (Identity Abuse)',
     'Communications Fraud Control Association, Global Fraud Loss Survey 2023 announcement. https://cfca.org/telecommunications-fraud-increased-12-in-2023-equating-to-an-estimated-38-95-billion-lost-to-fraud/',
     "Authority: the CFCA's 2023 survey found subscription (application) fraud and subscription (credit-mule) fraud rose to the top of telecom fraud, with account-manipulation methods making up 42 percent of the top ten and an estimated 1 in 9 applications believed fraudulent. The fraudster opens service using a stolen, borrowed, or synthetic identity with no intent to pay, often to obtain devices for resale (Asia and South America are top stolen-device destinations). Recognition red flags: identity data that does not aggregate to a real person; mismatched name, address, and device-financing details; rapid high-value device orders on a brand-new account; a 'credit mule' whose real identity is used by another party. Remedy: operators strengthen identity proofing and KYC at onboarding, run synthetic-identity and velocity checks, and refer confirmed cases to authorities."),
    ('telecom_pbx_toll_fraud',
     'PBX / IP-PBX Hacking and Toll (Dial-Through) Fraud Recognition',
     'FCC liability principles and industry guidance on PBX hacking / dial-through fraud (IPComms). https://www.ipcomms.net/blog/pbx-security-guide/',
     'Authority: PBX hacking, also called dial-through fraud, is unauthorized use of a business phone system to place long-distance or international calls billed to the compromised company; the CFCA ties billions in losses to it, and FCC tariff principles generally make customers liable for calls originating from their number while apportioning fault to whoever took the least reasonable steps to prevent fraud. Recognition red flags: a surge of after-hours, weekend, or holiday international calls to high-cost destinations; outbound calls placed through voicemail, DISA, or maintenance-port access; a sudden large call-charge spike over one weekend. Remedy: organizations patch PBX software, disable unused DISA and remote-access features, enforce strong credentials, restrict international dialing, monitor call patterns, and dispute charges with the carrier under the FCC liability framework.'),
    ('telecom_robocall_spoofing',
     'Robocall, Caller-ID Spoofing, and STIR/SHAKEN (FCC)',
     "U.S. Federal Communications Commission, 'Combating Spoofed Robocalls with Caller ID Authentication'. https://www.fcc.gov/call-authentication",
     'Authority: STIR (Secure Telephone Identity Revisited) and SHAKEN (Signature-based Handling of Asserted Information Using toKENs) let originating providers sign caller ID in an encrypted PASSporT carrying the provider name, originating number, and an attestation level - A, B, or C - for how well the provider knows the customer and their right to use the number; carriers validate it downstream. Mandated under the TRACED Act, the FCC required STIR/SHAKEN in the IP portions of networks by June 30, 2021. Recognition red flags: a call with low or no attestation, a CLI that fails verification, or a number that mismatches the claimed entity - hallmarks of illegal spoofing. Remedy: providers block calls likely illegally spoofed, the FCC moves to close non-IP authentication gaps, and consumers report spoofed robocalls to the FCC.'),
    ('telecom_premium_rate_scam',
     'Premium-Rate-Service Scam Recognition and Consumer Remedy',
     "Ofcom, 'Complain about premium rate services'. https://www.ofcom.org.uk/make-a-complaint/complain-about-premium-rate-services",
     "Authority: in the UK, premium-rate services (PRS) are charged to the phone bill or pre-pay account; Ofcom took regulation of Controlled PRS in-house on 1 February 2025 under its Regulation of Premium Rate Services Order 2024, replacing the former Phone-paid Services Authority code. A common harm is unauthorized direct-carrier-billing subscriptions or reverse-premium-rate texts that consumers deny ever consenting to. Recognition red flags: recurring charges or 'subscription' texts the user did not knowingly sign up for; charges appearing after a web interaction or pop-up; difficulty stopping the service. Remedy: the consumer should first ask the PRS provider that levied the charge for a refund and to stop the service, can use a network keyword-stop or bar-PRS option, and may complain to Ofcom via its PRS complaints route if the provider does not resolve it."),
    ('telecom_mobile_money_fraud',
     'Mobile-Money and Airtime Fraud Recognition',
     "GSMA, 'Mobile Money Fraud Typologies and Mitigation Strategies' (Mobile for Development). https://www.gsma.com/solutions-and-impact/connectivity-for-good/mobile-for-development/gsma_resources/mobile-money-fraud-typologies-and-mitigation-strategies/",
     "Authority: GSMA research identifies impersonation, identity fraud, social engineering, agent fraud, and SIM swap as the most prevalent mobile-money fraud types, in a sector that processed over a trillion dollars of transaction value; most schemes rely on social engineering - phishing calls, fake SMS, and impersonation of a bank or telco agent. Recognition red flags: an unsolicited contact claiming to be an operator or agent asking for a PIN, one-time code, or SIM-update details; pressure to reverse or 'confirm' a transfer; a request to share airtime or move funds urgently; an agent-initiated transaction the customer did not authorize. Remedy: GSMA urges operators to invest in anti-fraud programs, AI-based monitoring, agent oversight, and third-party scrutiny; customers should never share PINs or one-time codes and should report suspected agent fraud to the provider."),
    ('telecom_account_takeover',
     'Account Takeover, SIM-Swap, and Port-Out Fraud (Identity Link)',
     "U.S. Federal Communications Commission, Report and Order FCC 23-95, 'Protecting Consumers from SIM-Swap and Port-Out Fraud'. https://docs.fcc.gov/public/attachments/FCC-23-95A1.pdf",
     "Authority: in SIM-swap fraud a bad actor convinces a carrier to move a victim's service to a new SIM; in port-out fraud the number is transferred to a carrier the bad actor controls - both seize the phone number that banks and services use for identity verification. The FCC's 2023 order (FCC 23-95) requires secure customer authentication before SIM changes, bars predictable identifiers like national ID or birthdates, mandates immediate customer notification of change requests, and requires multi-year tracking of failed authentications. Recognition red flags: sudden loss of mobile service or an 'SOS only' state; an unrequested SIM-change or port notification; password-reset or login alerts the user did not initiate. Remedy: the customer should contact the carrier immediately to reverse the change, set an account PIN or passcode, and report identity theft to the relevant authority."),
    ('telecom_gsma_cfca_standards',
     'GSMA and CFCA Fraud-Control Standards and Landscape',
     'Communications Fraud Control Association, Global Fraud Loss Survey 2023; GSMA Fraud & Security Group. https://cfca.org/telecommunications-fraud-increased-12-in-2023-equating-to-an-estimated-38-95-billion-lost-to-fraud/',
     'Authority: the CFCA, surveying carriers worldwide since 1999, estimated 2023 telecom fraud at $38.95 billion (up 12 percent from 2021, about 2.5 percent of revenues), with subscription/application, credit-mule, PBX, account-takeover, and service/equipment abuse the leading methods. The GSMA Fraud & Security Group sets industry practice on roaming, interconnect, and signaling security and runs a Fraud Intelligence Service giving operators shared high-risk-number data and IR.21 validation. Recognition value: these bodies define the typologies and shared hot-lists operators use to flag anomalous traffic and identities. Remedy: participation in collaborative intelligence exchange, KYC and authentication standards, and CDR/signaling analytics; the CFCA also notes an enforcement gap - many organizations refer few cases to authorities - which standards bodies push operators to close.'),
    ('telecom_roaming_fraud',
     'Roaming and Wholesale Fraud Recognition (NRTRDE)',
     'GSMA roaming-fraud control via Near Real Time Roaming Data Exchange (NRTRDE); Oracle NRTRDE documentation. https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E16754_01/doc.75/e16717/rmg_nrtrde_about.htm',
     "Authority: roaming fraud exploits the delay between a subscriber roaming on a visited network and the home network receiving the usage records, letting losses accumulate before detection. The GSMA mandates Near Real Time Roaming Data Exchange (NRTRDE), which replaced the older High Usage Report in 2008 and requires the visited network to send an extract of each roaming call record to the home network within four hours. Recognition red flags: a roaming subscriber generating abnormally high-value or high-cost-destination usage soon after attaching to a visited network; concentrated traffic toward premium or revenue-share ranges; usage inconsistent with the customer's history. Remedy: home operators monitor NRTRDE feeds and velocity thresholds to suspend or bar a roaming account quickly, and wholesale carriers police partner traffic, removing operators with poor fraud controls."),
    ('telecom_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: Fraud Management Systems and Regulator Action',
     'Industry guidance on telecom Fraud Management Systems and law-enforcement action (LATRO). https://latro.com/blog/what-is-a-fraud-management-system-in-telecom-unlock-revenue-security/',
     'Authority: a Fraud Management System (FMS) centralizes detection across the network - standardizing Call Detail Records and signaling data, combining fixed rules with dynamic thresholds, and applying supervised learning for known threats and unsupervised learning for novel ones - to alert on suspicious behavior and quickly block or deactivate fraudulent SIMs. Recognition value: the FMS surfaces the red-flag patterns (traffic spikes, abnormal CLI, velocity anomalies) described across IRSF, bypass, PBX, and roaming fraud. Remedy and enforcement: operators deactivate offending accounts and dispute settlements; regulators and law enforcement use RF geolocation to locate, seize, and prosecute SIM-box and SMS-blaster operations; and strong SIM-registration (KYC) plus cross-jurisdiction collaboration between carriers, regulators, and police raises the cost and risk for fraud operators.'),
    ('agri_cap_fraud',
     'EU CAP subsidy fraud and OLAF investigations: authority and recognition',
     "European Court of Auditors, Special Report 14/2022 'Anti-fraud measures in the CAP' (fetched https://op.europa.eu/webpub/eca/special-reports/fraud-in-cap-14-2022/en/)",
     "Legal authority: the Common Agricultural Policy is protected by the EU Financial Regulation and investigated by OLAF, which probes fraud affecting the EU budget across the EAGF and EAFRD funds. Recognition red flags from real OLAF cases: 'false farmers' inserted into a paying-agency database so ineligible applicants draw aid; land claimed for years with no valid lease; declarations on public land or land under judicial seizure; a beneficiary secretly controlled by an undisclosed parent; and bribes paid to officials to approve rural-development projects. Treat first-time claims on public land, overlapping parcel claims, and missing farm infrastructure as warning signs. Remedy: OLAF issues recovery recommendations (Italy's false-farmer scheme drove roughly 32 million euro), authorities prosecute, and funds are clawed back. Recognition guidance only."),
    ('agri_phantom_land',
     'Recognizing phantom and ineligible-land subsidy claims under area-based aid',
     "European Court of Auditors, Special Report 14/2022 'Anti-fraud measures in the CAP' (fetched https://op.europa.eu/webpub/eca/special-reports/fraud-in-cap-14-2022/en/)",
     "Legal authority: area-based direct payments are controlled through the Integrated Administration and Control System (IACS) and Land Parcel Identification System (LPIS), so aid is paid only on eligible, actually-farmed land. Recognition red flags for phantom or ineligible-land claims: leasing contracts where the supposed tenant has died or never agreed; concessions declared over public land the claimant does not control; parcels claimed by several parties at once; declarations of non-existent livestock; and land under criminal seizure. Auditors also flag claims on land lacking required infrastructure or claimed for the first time. Remedy: paying agencies use satellite 'checks-by-monitoring' and risk-scoring to detect overstatement, then recover overpayments (one Slovak case exceeded 1 million euro) and refer for prosecution. Detection framing only."),
    ('agri_crop_insurance_fraud',
     'US federal crop-insurance fraud: USDA RMA recognition and enforcement',
     "USDA Risk Management Agency, 'Department of Justice Prosecutions' (fetched https://www.rma.usda.gov/about-rma/department-justice-prosecutions)",
     "Legal authority: the Federal Crop Insurance Act governs the program and the False Claims Act enables civil recovery against false claims; wire-fraud and money-laundering counts are often charged alongside. Recognition red flags in RMA's prosecution record: false 'prevented planting' claims on fields never planted; inflated or fabricated low-production and weather-damage claims; hiding or moving harvested production between fields to manufacture a loss; and agents or adjusters colluding to certify questionable claims. Watch for yields or losses inconsistent with regional patterns, claims clustered around specific agents, and discrepancies between adjuster reports and producer records. Remedy and enforcement: prison sentences, multi-million-dollar restitution (one case exceeded 6.5 million dollars), and multi-year program debarment. Recognition guidance, not a how-to."),
    ('agri_input_subsidy_diversion',
     'Fertilizer and input-subsidy diversion: ghost beneficiaries and resale',
     "allAfrica, 'Malawi: Kawale Unveils Tough New Fertilizer Access Rules to End AIP Corruption' (fetched https://allafrica.com/stories/202509110067.html)",
     "Context and authority: national farm-input subsidy programs (such as Malawi's Farm Input Subsidy Program) are governed by eligibility rules and audited by national authorities, with reported findings of misappropriation. Recognition red flags for diversion: subsidised fertilizer redirected to traders, officials, or political loyalists instead of poor smallholders; 'ghost beneficiaries' who never appear on lists yet collect on distribution day; 'briefcase farmers' registered in several villages to grab extra bags for resale; inflated supplier contracts, fake invoices, and dubious vendors. Flag duplicate registrations, beneficiaries lacking national-ID linkage, and suppliers without verifiable delivery. Remedy: digital IDs tied to national identity and GPS farm coordinates, electronic payments, audit recovery, and prosecution. Recognition framing only; no diversion method is described."),
    ('agri_organic_cert_fraud',
     'Organic certification fraud: conventional product sold as organic',
     "USDA Agricultural Marketing Service, 'Fraudulent Organic Certificates' / National Organic Program rule (fetched https://www.ams.usda.gov/services/enforcement/organic/fraudulent-certificates)",
     'Legal authority: the Organic Foods Production Act of 1990 and USDA National Organic Program rules govern organic labeling, with the 2023 Strengthening Organic Enforcement rule adding traceability and oversight. Recognition red flags for organic fraud: conventionally grown grain marketed and priced as organic; fraudulent or altered organic certificates; supply chains where volumes sold as organic exceed certified acreage; and high-risk imported or brokered ingredients with weak traceability. Verify certificates against certifier databases, reconcile certified acreage to claimed volumes, and watch for wealth inconsistent with legitimate yields. Remedy and enforcement: the largest US case ended in an 11-year sentence for a multi-hundred-million-dollar feed-grain scheme; other brokers faced indictment, asset forfeiture, and per-violation civil penalties. Detection guidance only.'),
    ('agri_landuse_deforestation',
     'Land-use and deforestation-linked subsidy abuse: perverse incentives',
     "Fern, 'Agriculture and deforestation: The EU Common Agricultural Policy' / FAO-UNDP-UNEP repurposing report (fetched https://www.fern.org/fileadmin/uploads/fern/Documents/Fern%20CAP%20FULL%20REPORT%20FINAL.pdf)",
     'Authority and framing: the European Court of Auditors and FAO-UNDP-UNEP analyses warn that coupled, price-distorting agricultural support can subsidise land conversion and deforestation; FAO finds roughly 470 billion dollars per year of support is price-distorting and environmentally or socially harmful. Recognition red flags: payments tied to crops or biofuel feedstocks that incentivise clearing forested or high-biodiversity land; subsidies flowing to plantations in sensitive forest frontiers; and monitoring gaps that let illegal land grabbing capture area-based aid. Cross-check satellite land-cover change against subsidised parcels and flag aid on newly cleared land. Remedy: repurpose harmful coupled support toward decoupled payments and public goods, tighten satellite monitoring, and exclude land obtained through illegal conversion. Recognition and reform framing only.'),
    ('agri_quota_fraud',
     'Production-quota and levy fraud: sham purchasers and evaded levies',
     "DairyReporter, 'Two farmers jailed for milk quota fraud in Italy' (fetched https://www.dairyreporter.com/Article/2010/10/13/Two-farmers-jailed-for-milk-quota-fraud-in-Italy/)",
     "Legal authority: historical EU production-control regimes (milk and sugar quotas) imposed levies on output exceeding a quota, enforced with OLAF support to national prosecutors on the technicalities of the levy legislation. Recognition red flags for quota and levy evasion: farmers creating cooperative 'first purchaser' companies that conduct no real business but exist on paper to disguise who buys the milk and dodge the super-levy; networks of shell purchasers routing volume to avoid declared overproduction; and unexplained gaps between farm output and declared deliveries. Flag purchasers with no genuine trading activity and quantities that do not reconcile across the chain. Remedy: levy recovery (Italian schemes left over 100 million euro unpaid), criminal convictions, and tightened first-purchaser verification. Recognition guidance only."),
    ('agri_export_refund_fraud',
     'Agri-export refund and drawback fraud: false destination and fictitious export',
     "OLAF / European Commission, 'Customs fraud' and OLAF investigative reporting (fetched https://anti-fraud.ec.europa.eu/policy/policies-prevent-and-deter-fraud/customs-fraud_en)",
     "Legal authority: EU rules grant export refunds on agricultural products only on objective criteria, the quantity, nature, characteristics, and real geographical destination of goods exported; OLAF investigates customs and refund fraud affecting EU revenue. Recognition red flags for export-refund and drawback fraud: goods declared for export to a refund-qualifying destination but diverted or never leaving; transit declarations claiming onward movement while goods actually stay in the EU; commodity substitution; and fictitious or quantity-inflated 'exports'. Reconcile declared destination against arrival evidence and flag missing proof of export. Remedy: OLAF traces the diversion (one transit scheme evaded roughly 118 million euro in duties plus 79 million euro VAT), recommends recovery of unduly paid refunds, and supports prosecution. Recognition framing only."),
    ('agri_subsidy_capture',
     'Land concentration and subsidy capture: recognizing skewed CAP distribution',
     "Greenpeace European Unit, 'Welfare for the rich: up to 40% of CAP money goes to 1% of recipients' (fetched https://www.greenpeace.org/eu-unit/issues/nature-food/47864/welfare-for-the-rich-up-to-40-of-cap-money-goes-to-1-of-recipients/)",
     'Authority and framing: the European Court of Auditors and policy analyses criticise area-based CAP direct payments for poor targeting, because aid tied to land area concentrates among the largest landholders. Recognition red flags for subsidy capture: in some member states the top 1 percent of recipients draw up to 40 percent of payments, and across several countries the top tenth receive roughly two-thirds; capping thresholds widely unapplied; and direct payments capitalising into higher land rents that entrench large holders. Flag highly concentrated payment distributions, beneficiaries whose scale dwarfs local family farms, and weak or absent capping. Remedy: apply capping and degressivity, redistribute toward small and medium farms, publish recipient and beneficial-owner data, and target support to income need. Policy-reform framing only.'),
    ('agri_disaster_relief_fraud',
     'Disaster and drought-relief agricultural fraud: false loss claims',
     "USDA Farm Service Agency, 'Livestock Forage Disaster Program (LFP)' and CRS disaster-assistance overview (fetched https://www.fsa.usda.gov/resources/disaster-recovery/livestock-forage-disaster-program-lfp)",
     'Legal authority: USDA disaster programs (LFP for drought-affected grazing, ELAP for adverse-weather livestock losses) operate under farm-bill authority and Farm Service Agency rules, with payments tied to verified qualifying conditions; the USDA Inspector General flagged LFP and NAP with improper-payment rates above 10 percent in FY2023. Recognition red flags for disaster-relief fraud: claimed grazing or forage losses where no qualifying drought trigger exists; inflated or non-existent livestock head counts; duplicate claims across programs for the same loss; and eligibility that cannot be substantiated. Reconcile claimed losses against independent drought data, verify animal inventories, and flag repeat anomalous claimants. Remedy: improper-payment recovery, eligibility audits, debarment, and referral for prosecution under false-claims authorities. Recognition framing only.'),
    ('agri_controls_ownership',
     'Beneficial ownership and controls in agri-funds: transparency as a safeguard',
     'European Commission, Arachne risk-scoring charter and CAP integration reporting (fetched https://employment-social-affairs.ec.europa.eu/document/download/a43cb969-8a8d-492b-b1c6-938edfb1f63a_en?filename=Arachne-Charter.pdf)',
     'Legal authority and tooling: EU controls for agricultural funds rest on IACS/LPIS verification plus the Arachne risk-scoring tool, which combines payments, beneficiaries, contracts, contractors, and beneficial-owner data with public sources to flag fraud, conflict-of-interest, and irregularity risk. Recognition red flags from beneficial-ownership analysis: hidden links between a beneficiary and the parties it contracts with; final recipients controlled by undisclosed parents; and conflicts of interest between applicants and officials. Require ultimate beneficial-ownership disclosure and reconcile who actually controls each payment. Control limit: Arachne flags risk but does not judge conduct or auto-exclude, so flags must trigger human follow-up. Remedy: publish recipient and owner data, route high-risk flags to investigation, recover, and exclude. Recognition and control framing only.'),
    ('agri_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: clawback, OLAF recovery, and EDES debarment',
     "European Court of Auditors, Special Report 11/2022 'Blacklisting economic operators to protect the EU budget' (fetched https://op.europa.eu/webpub/eca/special-reports/blacklisting-economic-operators-11-2022/en/)",
     'Legal authority: under the EU Financial Regulation, the Early Detection and Exclusion System (EDES, since 2016) lets the EU exclude and financially penalise untrustworthy operators, and OLAF recommends recovery of misused funds (about 600 million euro recommended for recovery in 2025). Exclusion grounds include fraud, corruption, grave professional misconduct, money laundering, and non-payment of taxes. Recognition and remedy: a confirmed agricultural fraud should trigger clawback of the unduly paid subsidy, OLAF recommendations, national prosecution, and blacklisting so the operator cannot draw further EU funds. Limit to note: the Court found blacklisting weak for shared-management funds like CAP because exclusion is not mandatory there; the recommended fix is extending EDES to member-state-managed funds and better data tools. Enforcement guidance only.'),
    ('gambling_fatf_aml',
     'AML in casinos: the FATF risk-based approach',
     'FATF, Vulnerabilities of Casinos and Gaming Sector (fetched https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/Vulnerabilitiesofcasinosandgamingsector.html)',
     "When the FATF revised its 40 Recommendations, casinos were added as Designated Non-Financial Businesses and Professions (DNFBPs), so member jurisdictions must apply customer due diligence, transaction monitoring and suspicious-transaction reporting to gambling. The FATF report finds the sector cash-intensive and vulnerable to criminal exploitation, with gaps in awareness of laundering typologies, weak controls over junket and VIP programs, and uneven global AML/CFT coverage. Recognition frame: a casino that cannot explain a customer's source of funds, applies only relaxed checks to high-value players, or has no risk-rating of patrons is exhibiting the exact control gaps regulators flag. Remedy: a risk-based program concentrates due diligence on high-risk customers and reports concerns to the national financial intelligence unit."),
    ('gambling_ml_recognition',
     'Recognizing money laundering through gambling',
     'Alessa / FINTRAC and APG indicators, Casino Money Laundering Red Flag Indicators (fetched https://alessa.com/blog/casino-money-laundering-red-flag-indicators/)',
     "Laundering through gambling shows recognizable patterns rather than ordinary play. FinCEN reporting found 'minimal gaming with large transactions' the single most-reported suspicious casino activity. Documented red flags: funds deposited then withdrawn shortly after with little or no actual wagering; transactions kept just below reporting thresholds (structuring); chips bought and cashed out as a check with minimal play; unknown source of chips; two or more people working together; aliases or mismatched account names; and casino accounts used like savings accounts. Chip-dumping describes one player deliberately losing to a co-conspirator so dirty money re-emerges as 'winnings.' Recognition frame: these are detection signals, not instructions. Remedy: regulated operators must file suspicious-activity reports and may freeze or refuse payouts pending source-of-funds verification."),
    ('gambling_illegal_unlicensed',
     'Recognizing illegal and unlicensed gambling operators',
     'UK Gambling Commission, How we tackle illegal gambling (fetched https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/about-us/guide/how-we-tackle-illegal-gambling)',
     "Under the Gambling Act 2005 it is illegal to offer gambling to consumers in Great Britain without a Gambling Commission operating licence, regardless of where the operator is based; a foreign licence does not authorize GB service, and advertising unlawful gambling is itself an offence. Illegal operators undermine the objectives of keeping crime out of gambling, ensuring fair play, and protecting the vulnerable. Recognition frame: warning signs include a site absent from the Commission's licensee register, no UK licence number, offshore licensing, oversized bonuses with no visible terms, crypto acceptance, and the targeting of self-excluded players. Such sites lack social-responsibility and AML safeguards. Remedy: check the public register and report suspected illegal operators through the regulator's confidential reporting service."),
    ('gambling_lottery_fraud',
     'Lottery fraud, insider rigging and claim fraud',
     'Wikipedia, Lottery fraud, documented cases incl. Hot Lotto / OLG retailer fraud (fetched https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lottery_fraud)',
     "Lottery fraud is any act to defraud a lottery or a legitimate winner. Recognized forms: forged or altered tickets; presenting a stolen genuine ticket; insider fraud where employees or agents tamper with the draw or claim prizes themselves; and retailers falsely telling a customer a ticket lost, then claiming it. Concrete cases show the pattern: in the Hot Lotto scandal a security insider rigged the random-number generator to produce predictable numbers on set dates; Ontario's lottery saw widespread retailer 'insider win' fraud between 1999 and 2006. Recognition frame: red flags include improbable clusters of retailer wins, draw anomalies, and prize claims an operator pays without verification. Remedy: regulators can fine operators that fail to investigate suspect claims, and players should check tickets independently and retain purchase receipts as proof of ownership."),
    ('gambling_problem_gambling',
     'Problem gambling as a public-health harm',
     'National Council on Problem Gambling, FAQs: What is Problem Gambling? (fetched https://www.ncpgambling.org/help-treatment/faqs-what-is-problem-gambling/)',
     'NCPG defines problem gambling as gambling behavior that damages a person or family and disrupts daily life and work; gambling disorder is a recognized mental-health diagnosis, and it is an emotional problem with financial consequences, not merely a money problem. Recognition frame: warning signs include preoccupation with gambling, needing to bet more and more often, chasing losses to win money back, restlessness when cutting down, loss of control, and continuing despite negative consequences; in severe cases bankruptcy, lost jobs or relationships, and suicidal thoughts. Children and teens are at higher risk, and faster speed of play may worsen harm. NCPG estimates the annual U.S. social cost at about 14 billion dollars. Remedy: the harm is treatable, with confidential helpline and counseling support, and the industry must run problem-gambling programs.'),
    ('gambling_safer_duties',
     'Safer-gambling duties and self-exclusion (UK LCCP)',
     'UK Gambling Commission, Self-exclusion in social responsibility (LCCP) (fetched https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/licensees-and-businesses/page/self-exclusion-in-social-responsibility)',
     'Under the LCCP social-responsibility code, operators that interact with customers must offer self-exclusion to anyone wanting to stop gambling. The expected commitment is a minimum of six months, and a customer who is certain must be able to self-exclude immediately, without being forced to wait to speak to staff first; operators should still offer a conversation with trained personnel and ensure the person understands what self-exclusion means. Remote operators must additionally support the multi-operator scheme so an exclusion can apply across licensed sites. Recognition frame: a self-excluded person who is still able to deposit, or who is re-marketed to, signals a control failure; targeting self-excluded users is a hallmark of illegal operators. Remedy: breaches of these conditions can lead the Commission to suspend, revoke, or financially penalize a licence.'),
    ('gambling_match_fixing_betting',
     'Match-fixing and sports-betting integrity red flags',
     'Crowe UK and ACGCS integrity analysis; Sportradar monitoring data (fetched https://www.crowe.com/uk/insights/match-fixing-threats)',
     'Match-fixing corrupts a sporting event for betting gain and is a recognized integrity threat linking the gambling and sport sectors. Recognition frame: red flags appear in the betting market, including large pre-match odds movements with no sporting explanation, high-volume wagers on unusual outcomes such as a particular half-time score in a low-tier match, and erratic in-play odds suggesting inside knowledge. Sportradar has flagged well over a thousand suspicious matches in a single year, with football most affected. Human-intelligence signals include players reporting the same suspicious approach or social-media requests for inside information. Remedy: integrity bodies and regulators run confidential reporting hotlines, monitor markets in real time, and refer suspected manipulation for disciplinary and criminal action; accepting a fixing approach is itself an offence.'),
    ('gambling_underage_protection',
     'Underage and vulnerable-player protection',
     'UK Gambling Commission, New age and identity verification rules - LCCP changes (fetched https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/new-age-and-identity-verification-rules-changes-to-the-lccp-from-tuesday-7)',
     "A core licensing objective is protecting children and vulnerable people from gambling harm. Under updated LCCP rules the previous 72-hour verification window was removed: operators must verify a customer's age and identity (name, address, date of birth) before that customer can deposit, access free-to-play games, or gamble. Licensees must maintain and act on policies to prevent underage gambling, review age-verification systems as technology advances, and train staff in secondary identification when initial checks fail. Recognition frame: signs of weak protection include sites allowing deposits before verification, no robust age gate, or free-play access without an age check; illegal operators commonly skip these safeguards. Remedy: breaches expose an operator to licence review, suspension, revocation, and financial penalty, and consumers can report underage access to the regulator."),
    ('gambling_affordability_marketing',
     'Affordability checks and marketing-consent rules',
     'UK Gambling Commission, New rules boosting safety and consumer choice; LCCP financial-vulnerability condition (fetched https://www.gamblingcommission.gov.uk/news/article/new-rules-boosting-safety-and-consumer-choice)',
     'To catch acutely financially vulnerable customers, the Gambling Commission introduced light-touch financial-vulnerability checks for online customers above a net monthly deposit threshold, phased from 500 pounds to 150 pounds, using only publicly available data rather than postcode or job title. Frictionless financial-risk assessments target cases where customers lost large sums quickly with no checks at all. On marketing, a double opt-in requires customers to consent by both product and channel, ending automatic cross-promotion of new products. Recognition frame: red flags include unprompted large losses with no operator intervention, pressure to deposit, or misleading messaging that blames technical issues on mandated checks. Remedy: these are licence conditions, so non-compliance can trigger enforcement, and customers can report misleading affordability messaging to the regulator.'),
    ('gambling_rng_integrity',
     'Game and RNG integrity testing standards',
     'Gaming Labs International and eCOGRA RNG certification overviews (fetched https://gaminglabs.com/services/igaming/random-number-generator-rng/)',
     "Fair online games depend on a certified random number generator (RNG) verified by an independent test lab such as Gaming Labs International, eCOGRA, or iTech Labs, with no affiliation to the operator. Certification rests on common principles: statistical randomness and uniform distribution (tests such as chi-squared or diehard), unpredictability and independence of outcomes, source-code review for sound design, and confirmation the RNG is correctly integrated into the game. RNGs are checked against the rules of regulators such as the UK Gambling Commission or Malta Gaming Authority. Recognition frame: warning signs include no published certification, an in-house or unaudited RNG, or claimed payout rates with no independent test. Remedy: licensed games carry verifiable certification; players or partners can confirm a lab's seal and report games that lack one."),
    ('gambling_online_crypto_risk',
     'Online-gambling and crypto-casino laundering risks',
     'FATF, Targeted report on Stablecoins and Unhosted Wallets (fetched https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Virtualassets/targeted-report-stablecoins-unhosted-wallets.html)',
     "Crypto-accepting online casinos heighten laundering risk because they can serve the layering stage, appeal to actors seeking anonymity, and often run relaxed know-your-customer checks. FATF reporting finds stablecoins account for most illicit virtual-asset volume, frequently moved through unhosted wallets, mixers, and cross-chain transfers to obscure origin. Recognition frame: red flags include gambling activity inconsistent with a customer's profile, rapid conversion of winnings into stablecoins with no economic purpose, deposit-and-withdraw cycles with little or no real play, and crypto acceptance with no identity checks, a pattern common to illegal operators. Remedy: FATF urges AML obligations on stablecoin issuers and tools such as wallet freezing; licensed operators must still apply due diligence and report suspicious crypto activity to the financial intelligence unit."),
    ('gambling_regulation_remedy',
     'Regulation, enforcement and player remedy',
     'Chambers Gaming Law 2025 (USA) and gaming-litigation guidance (fetched https://practiceguides.chambers.com/practice-guides/gaming-law-2025/usa)',
     "Gambling is licensed and supervised by regulators such as state gaming commissions and control boards in the United States and the Gambling Commission in Great Britain, which license operators, enforce compliance, and oversee responsible-gambling programs. Enforcement escalates with severity: fines, operational restrictions, suspension, and licence revocation, and courts may order disgorgement of profits from unlicensed activity, with some jurisdictions levying fines above one million dollars per violation alongside criminal charges. Recognition frame: an operator facing repeated penalties, refusing payouts without lawful basis, or operating with no traceable licence signals elevated risk. Remedy: licensed players have redress through the regulator's complaint process and, where available, an ombudsman; on illegal channels regulators generally cannot recover funds or intervene."),
    ('procurement_oecd_recommendation',
     'OECD Recommendation on Public Procurement: integrity standard',
     'OECD Recommendation of the Council on Public Procurement, OECD/LEGAL/0411 (2015), via https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/en/instruments/OECD-LEGAL-0411',
     'Authority: the 2015 OECD Recommendation sets twelve integrated principles for public procurement, naming integrity, transparency and accountability as core. It calls procurement the government activity most vulnerable to waste, mismanagement and corruption because of the money at stake, transaction volume, and close public-private contact. Recognition: weak internal controls, undisclosed conflicts of interest, opaque award decisions, and absence of any complaint channel are integrity warning signs. Remedy: it asks states to embed risk management, conflict-of-interest declarations, internal control and audit, transparent disclosure across the procurement cycle, and accessible mechanisms for suppliers to challenge decisions and for staff to report wrongdoing.'),
    ('procurement_bid_rigging_guidelines',
     'OECD Guidelines for Fighting Bid Rigging in Public Procurement',
     'OECD Guidelines for Fighting Bid Rigging in Public Procurement (2025 Update), referencing OECD Recommendation OECD/LEGAL/0396, via https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2025/09/oecd-guidelines-for-fighting-bid-rigging-in-public-procurement-2025-update_127880ea/full-report/component-3.html',
     'Authority: the OECD treats bid rigging (collusive tendering) as among the most egregious competition-law violations, illegal in all adherents and often criminal. It occurs when firms that would otherwise compete conspire in secret to raise prices or lower quality in a bidding process, injuring the public purchaser and taxpayers. Recognition: the Guidelines list market conditions that ease collusion (few suppliers, high barriers to entry, identical goods, predictable repetitive tenders) and a detection checklist of bidding, pricing, statement and behaviour red flags. Remedy: procurement officials should preserve records, avoid tipping off suspects, review tender histories for patterns, and report serious suspicions to the competition authority, which can sanction under leniency-backed enforcement.'),
    ('procurement_collusion_recognition',
     'Recognizing collusion and cover bidding in tenders',
     'OECD Guidelines for Fighting Bid Rigging in Public Procurement (2025 Update), Bid-Rigging Detection List, via https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2025/09/oecd-guidelines-for-fighting-bid-rigging-in-public-procurement-2025-update_127880ea/full-report/component-5.html',
     'Authority: the OECD defines the cartel forms. Cover (complementary, courtesy, token) bidding is when a competitor submits a bid known to be too high, above the designated winner, or with terms known to be unacceptable, to fake genuine competition. Other forms are bid suppression, bid rotation (taking turns winning), and market allocation. Recognition red flags: the same firm always wins; certain firms always bid but never win; firms take turns; a regular supplier skips a tender then appears as subcontractor; identical errors, metadata, IP addresses or pricing formulae across rival bids; the winner subcontracts to losers. Remedy: log dates, persons and statements; do not alert bidders; and refer suspicions to the competition authority for investigation and sanction.'),
    ('procurement_uncitral_model_law',
     'UNCITRAL Model Law on Public Procurement framework',
     'UNCITRAL Model Law on Public Procurement (2011), Preamble and Chapters, via https://uncitral.un.org/sites/uncitral.un.org/files/media-documents/uncitral/en/2011-model-law-on-public-procurement-e.pdf',
     "Authority: the 2011 Model Law's preamble states objectives of economy and efficiency, participation regardless of nationality, competition, fair and equitable treatment of suppliers, integrity and public confidence, and transparency. Article 10 requires the subject matter to be described objectively so it does not create obstacles to competition; Article 20 lets entities reject abnormally low bids after written inquiry; Article 21 excludes suppliers tainted by inducements, unfair competitive advantage or conflicts of interest. Recognition: brand-locked or narrowed specifications and unjustified direct solicitation are abuse signals. Remedy: Chapter VIII (articles 64 to 69) gives suppliers a right to challenge and appeal, including reconsideration by the entity and review before an independent body, with effect-of-challenge protections."),
    ('procurement_uncac_art9',
     'UNCAC Article 9: procurement and public-finance integrity',
     'United Nations Convention against Corruption (2003), Article 9, via https://www.unodc.org/pdf/corruption/publications_unodc_convention-e.pdf',
     'Authority: UNCAC Article 9 binds each State Party to establish procurement systems based on transparency, competition and objective decision criteria that are effective in preventing corruption. It requires public distribution of tender information with sufficient bidding time; conditions of participation and selection and award criteria set and published in advance; objective predetermined criteria enabling later verification; an effective domestic review and appeal system for legal recourse; and personnel measures such as conflict-of-interest declarations, screening and training. Recognition: rules issued late, criteria changed after opening, or no appeal route are red flags. Remedy: Article 9(2) adds public-finance safeguards (budget procedures, timely revenue and expenditure reporting, accounting and auditing oversight, risk management and internal control, and corrective action).'),
    ('procurement_tailored_spec_redflags',
     'Tailored specifications and single-bidder red flags',
     'IACRC, The Most Common Procurement Fraud Schemes and their Primary Red Flags (rigged specifications; excluding qualified bidders), via https://iacrc.org/fraud-and-corruption/the-most-common-procurement-fraud-schemes-and-their-primary-red-flags/',
     "Authority: anti-corruption practice treats rigged specifications and exclusion of qualified bidders as recognized fraud schemes. Specifications are drafted too narrow to favour one firm, too broad to qualify an otherwise unqualified favourite, or too vague to enable later amendments. Recognition red flags: only one or a few bidders respond; close similarity between the specification and the winner's product; a brand name in the request; specifications markedly narrower or broader than past similar tenders; unreasonably short bid windows; weak publicity; a high share of awards to one supplier; or a contract not rebid despite too few bids. Remedy: independent review of evaluation and specification practices, audit of single-bid awards, and competition-authority or oversight referral where favouritism or collusion is indicated."),
    ('procurement_conflict_interest',
     'Conflict of interest and the revolving door in procurement',
     'IACRC, Most Common Procurement Fraud Schemes (conflict of interest), via https://iacrc.org/fraud-and-corruption/the-most-common-procurement-fraud-schemes-and-their-primary-red-flags/; OECD conflict-of-interest guidance, via https://www.oecd.org/en/topics/sub-issues/conflict-of-interest.html',
     'Authority: integrity standards require disclosure and management of conflicts of interest, including the revolving door where staff move between public roles and bidders. UNCAC Article 9 expressly contemplates declaration of interest, screening and training for procurement personnel. Recognition red flags: a procurement official has an undisclosed interest in a supplier; accepts gifts, travel or entertainment from vendors; holds undisclosed employment discussions with a current or prospective contractor; appears to run a side business; lives beyond apparent means; or shows unexplained favouritism toward one firm. Remedy: mandatory interest declarations, recusal, gift and post-employment cooling-off rules, audit of award decisions, and disciplinary or enforcement action; OECD notes sanctions exist on paper but are rarely applied in practice.'),
    ('procurement_ghost_supplier',
     'Ghost supplier and fictitious-invoice fraud signals',
     'IACRC, Most Common Procurement Fraud Schemes (fictitious vendor; false, inflated or duplicate invoices), via https://iacrc.org/fraud-and-corruption/the-most-common-procurement-fraud-schemes-and-their-primary-red-flags/',
     'Authority: phantom or fictitious vendor schemes are a recognized procurement fraud, often enabled when one insider can order, receive and approve payment. A non-existent supplier, frequently claiming services or consumables that cannot be verified, submits false or inflated invoices; cartels also use fake invoices to disguise side payments. Recognition red flags: a paid vendor missing from the approved list; a vendor absent from business directories; an address that is only a mail drop; invoiced goods or services that cannot be located or verified; weak segregation of duties; and altered, copied or missing supporting documents. Remedy: enforce three-way matching of invoice, purchase order and receiving record, independent vendor master verification, and audit or investigation referral for unverifiable payees.'),
    ('procurement_change_order_abuse',
     'Change-order and contract-variation abuse indicators',
     'IACRC, Most Common Procurement Fraud Schemes (change order abuse; unbalanced bidding), via https://iacrc.org/fraud-and-corruption/the-most-common-procurement-fraud-schemes-and-their-primary-red-flags/',
     'Authority: change-order abuse is a recognized scheme because variations receive far less scrutiny than the original award, often needing only one approver. A contractor, sometimes colluding with an official, submits a low bid to win, then recovers profit through inflated or unjustified change orders, or uses variations to extend a contract that should be rebid. Unbalanced bidding manipulates line-item prices when later quantity changes are foreseen. Recognition red flags: a low-bid award followed by price- or scope-increasing change orders; vague or preliminary specifications later changed; numerous variations to one contractor approved by the same employee; and variations split below approval thresholds. Remedy: segregation of duties, independent justification and approval of variations above thresholds, and audit of low-bid-then-amend patterns.'),
    ('procurement_open_contracting',
     'Open Contracting Data Standard and transparency',
     'Open Contracting Partnership, Open Contracting Data Standard and Red Flags guidance, via https://www.open-contracting.org/data-standard/ and https://www.open-contracting.org/resources/red-flags-in-public-procurement-a-guide-to-using-data-to-detect-and-mitigate-risks/',
     'Authority: the Open Contracting Data Standard (OCDS) is a common data model for disclosing contracting information across the whole cycle: planning, tender, award, contract and implementation. Open, comparable data is the basis for detecting irregularity. Recognition: the Partnership maps red-flag indicators (drawn from research and practice) to OCDS phases, covering risks such as single-bid awards, short bidding windows, repeated winners, post-award scope inflation, and missing or late disclosure. Remedy: publishing structured contracting data lets auditors, oversight bodies, journalists and civil society spot and challenge anomalies, and lets authorities target monitoring; absence of disclosure or non-standard data is itself a transparency warning that should prompt scrutiny and a records request.'),
    ('procurement_world_bank_debarment',
     'World Bank debarment and MDB cross-debarment',
     'World Bank Group / IFC Sanctions System: Sanctionable Practices, via https://www.ifc.org/en/about/combating-fraud-and-corruption/sanctionable-practices; MDB Agreement for Mutual Enforcement of Debarment Decisions (2010)',
     'Authority: the World Bank Group sanctions five sanctionable practices in financed projects: corrupt, fraudulent, collusive, coercive and obstructive practices. The Integrity Vice Presidency (INT) investigates; a Suspension and Debarment Officer reviews and recommends; and an independent Sanctions Board, mostly external members, decides contested cases. Recognition: these definitions label misconduct such as kickbacks, falsified bids, secret bidder agreements, threats, and frustrating investigations. Remedy: sanctions include public reprimand, debarment, debarment with conditional release (the baseline, requiring an integrity-compliance program), conditional non-debarment, and restitution; sanctioned parties are publicly listed. Under the 2010 cross-debarment agreement, qualifying debarments of a year or more are mutually enforced across major development banks, preventing forum shopping.'),
    ('procurement_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: bid protest, review and sanctions',
     'WTO Agreement on Government Procurement (GPA 2012), Article XVIII domestic review, via https://www.wto.org/english/tratop_e/gproc_e/gp_gpa_e.htm; UNCITRAL Model Law Chapter VIII',
     "Authority: the WTO GPA 2012 (Article XVIII) requires parties to provide a timely, effective, transparent and non-discriminatory domestic review system letting suppliers challenge breaches, heard by a court or an impartial independent review body with no interest in the procurement and power to order remedies. The UNCITRAL Model Law gives a parallel right to challenge and appeal. Recognition: denial of any protest route, retaliation against a complainant, or refusal to suspend an award pending review signals a failing integrity system. Remedy: available relief includes rapid interim measures and award stays to preserve a supplier's chance to compete, re-solicitation, contract termination, and compensation for loss; enforcement also runs through audit institutions, competition authorities, debarment regimes and, internationally, WTO dispute settlement."),
    ('realestate_ml_fatf',
     'Money Laundering Through Real Estate: FATF Risk Indicators',
     'FATF, Guidance for a Risk-Based Approach: Real Estate Sector (July 2022), https://www.fatf-gafi.org/content/dam/fatf-gafi/guidance/RBA-Real-Estate-Sector.pdf.coredownload.pdf',
     "The Financial Action Task Force (FATF), the global anti-money-laundering standard-setter, identifies real estate as a high-value, low-scrutiny channel for laundering criminal proceeds. Recognition red flags: a buyer who pays in cash or through a legal entity or trust; a price markedly above or below market value with no commercial explanation; rapid buy-and-resell with no renovation; a mismatch between the owner's recorded wealth and the property value; funds routed through overseas accounts, virtual assets, or abrupt financing changes; and use of gatekeepers (agents, lawyers, notaries) to distance the true owner. FATF expects customer due diligence and identification of the beneficial owner. Remedy: regulated professionals must apply risk-based due diligence and file suspicious transaction reports; jurisdictions should bring all property professionals within AML supervision."),
    ('realestate_fincen_rule',
     'US FinCEN Residential Real Estate Transfer Reporting Rule',
     'FinCEN, Fact Sheet: Final Rule to Increase Transparency in Residential Real Estate Transfers (Aug. 2024), https://www.fincen.gov/system/files/shared/RREFactSheet.pdf',
     "FinCEN's Residential Real Estate Rule under the Bank Secrecy Act requires settlement, title, escrow agents, or attorneys to report certain non-financed (all-cash) transfers of one-to-four-family residential property to a legal entity or trust, regardless of price. Rationale: illicit actors favor cash transfers held by shell entities to evade bank AML and Suspicious Activity Report screening. Reports must identify the transferee entity or trust, its beneficial owners (25 percent ownership or substantial control), the seller, the property, and the payment. Recognition signal: cash purchases by anonymous LLCs or trusts that escape lender scrutiny. Status note: implementation has faced legal challenge and effective-date shifts, so verify the current deadline. Remedy: mandatory reporting creates a paper trail letting law enforcement trace proceeds of crime through property."),
    ('realestate_beneficial_ownership',
     'Beneficial-Ownership Opacity and Shell-Company Property Buyers',
     'Transparency International & Anti-Corruption Data Collective, Opacity in Real Estate Ownership (OREO) Index (26 Mar. 2025), https://www.transparency.org/en/news/unveiling-opacity-in-real-estate-ownership-index',
     "Transparency International's Opacity in Real Estate Ownership Index finds that across most major economies, money launderers can buy and hold property anonymously through shell companies and trusts with little scrutiny. In a study of 78 African corruption cases, 85 percent used companies or trusts to obscure asset ownership, often via cross-border layers in secrecy jurisdictions. Recognition red flags: title held by an entity that never discloses its true owner; complex multi-jurisdiction corporate chains with no business purpose; registers that omit purchase price or intermediary details; deals completed without any agent, lawyer, or notary subject to AML duties. Remedy: public beneficial-ownership registers, mandatory AML coverage for all property professionals (including developers), and open, interoperable property data that investigators can cross-match."),
    ('realestate_title_fraud',
     'Title and Deed Fraud: Forged Conveyance Recognition',
     'FBI Boston Field Office, Quit Claim Deed Fraud Is on the Rise (1 Apr. 2025), https://www.fbi.gov/contact-us/field-offices/boston/news/fbi-boston-warns-quit-claim-deed-fraud-is-on-the-rise-',
     'Deed or home-title theft involves forging a transfer of ownership, typically via a quitclaim deed because it requires little documentation. The FBI reports that from 2019 through 2023, 58,141 victims reported 1.3 billion dollars in real-estate-fraud losses. Recognition red flags: a forged owner signature notarized with a fraudulent stamp; scammers mining public records for vacant or mortgage-free parcels then impersonating the owner to list them; deals pushed entirely through email or encrypted apps with no in-person identity check; an owner who stops receiving property-tax or utility bills, or sudden utility use on a vacant lot. Remedy: monitor county records and set up title alerts; insist on in-person identity verification; report fraud quickly so wires can be clawed back; ask a title insurer about forgery endorsements (ALTA 49 / 49.1).'),
    ('realestate_permit_corruption',
     'Building-Permit and Zoning Corruption Recognition',
     'New Jersey Office of the Attorney General, Housing and Zoning Inspector Charged with Issuing Fraudulent Permits for Kickbacks, https://www.njoag.gov/paterson-housing-and-zoning-inspector-charged-with-issuing-fraudulent-building-permits-other-records-in-exchange-for-kickbacks/',
     'Building-permit and zoning corruption ranges from small bribes to expedite a permit to large payments for favorable rezoning or variances. Enforcement actions have charged inspectors who issued fraudulent permits and records in exchange for kickbacks, and prosecutions have exposed bribery rings operating fixed fee schedules where minor violations were overlooked for set sums. Recognition red flags: permits or sign-offs issued with abnormal speed or no inspection record; expeditors or fixers who collect cash to guarantee approvals; undisclosed conflicts of interest, especially in small jurisdictions; opaque, standard-less land-use discretion. Remedy: transparent, time-bound, standards-based permitting cuts the incentive for under-the-table dealing; bribery of an official is a prosecutable crime; report suspected corruption to prosecutors or an inspector general.'),
    ('realestate_bid_rigging',
     'Construction Bid-Rigging and Collusion Recognition',
     'US DOJ Antitrust Division, Price Fixing, Bid Rigging, and Market Allocation Schemes, https://www.justice.gov/atr/price-fixing-bid-rigging-and-market-allocation-schemes',
     'Bid rigging in construction procurement is a per se criminal violation of the Sherman Act. The DOJ identifies four scheme types: bid suppression (a competitor withholds or withdraws a bid); complementary or cover bidding (token bids intentionally too high or defective to fake competition); bid rotation (firms take turns winning); and market or customer division. Recognition red flags: the same firm always wins while others routinely lose; bids far above cost estimates; the winner subcontracting to the losing bidders; fewer bidders than expected; identical wording or errors across competing bids; last-minute bid withdrawals. Remedy: the DOJ Procurement Collusion Strike Force investigates and prosecutes; leniency exists for the first conspirator to report; procuring agencies should report suspicious bidding patterns to antitrust authorities.'),
    ('realestate_cost_transparency',
     'CoST: Construction-Sector Infrastructure Transparency',
     'CoST - Infrastructure Transparency Initiative, Our Approach, https://infrastructuretransparency.org/our-approach/',
     'CoST, the Infrastructure Transparency Initiative, is the leading global program for transparency and accountability in public infrastructure, built on four features: disclosure, assurance, multi-stakeholder working, and social accountability. The CoST Infrastructure Data Standard requires procuring entities to proactively publish 40 data points across the project cycle. The World Bank estimates 10 to 30 percent of public infrastructure budgets are lost to corruption, so disclosure is a recognition tool: missing cost, scope, contractor, or beneficial-ownership data, or figures that cannot be reconciled against delivered work, are warning signs. Remedy: independent assurance teams validate disclosed data and flag discrepancies for oversight bodies; in one CoST case a revision saved a government 3.5 million dollars. Contractor beneficial-ownership disclosure deters anonymous shell bidders.'),
    ('realestate_building_safety_fraud',
     'Substandard Construction and Falsified-Inspection Fraud',
     'US public-works False Claims Act reporting, Whistleblower Law Collaborative, Public Works Fraud, https://www.whistleblowerllc.com/what-we-do/government-program-fraud/public-works-fraud/',
     'Building-safety fraud occurs when contractors substitute cheaper materials, skip specified work, or falsify quality-control and inspection records, creating real safety hazards. Documented False Claims Act cases include a 1 million dollar settlement where a firm falsified concrete-panel test records for a transit project. Recognition red flags: passing inspection results that do not match observed workmanship; an inspector with an under-the-table tie to the contractor; materials billed as high-grade but visibly inferior; test or certification documents that appear altered or signed without real testing; refusal to provide mill certificates or inspection logs. Remedy: false certification and product-substitution are prosecutable under the False Claims Act (treble damages) and state law; whistleblowers can file qui tam actions; report hazards to a building authority.'),
    ('realestate_mortgage_appraisal_fraud',
     'Mortgage, Valuation, and Appraisal Fraud Recognition',
     'FinCEN, Mortgage Loan Fraud resources, https://www.fincen.gov/mortgage-loan-fraud',
     "Mortgage fraud is committed by borrowers and by industry insiders to obtain or profit from a loan. Appraisal fraud inflates a property's value, often to support a property-flipping scheme: buy, appraise high soon after, resell, and pocket the inflated proceeds. Occupancy fraud (claiming a primary residence while not occupying it) is among the most commonly reported misrepresentations, alongside falsified income, employment, and debt. Recognition red flags: pressure to overstate income or sign blank documents; appraisals far above comparable sales with no support; unusual or last-minute wire instructions; guaranteed approval offers; altered pay stubs or inconsistent application data. Remedy: the FBI is the lead investigator; mortgage fraud is a federal crime carrying heavy fines and prison; lenders should escalate red-flag files and file Suspicious Activity Reports."),
    ('realestate_rental_escrow_scam',
     'Rental, Escrow, and Deposit Scam Recognition',
     'US FTC, Rental Listing Scams - Consumer Advice, https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/rental-listing-scams',
     "The Federal Trade Commission warns that rental scammers post fake or hijacked listings to collect application fees, deposits, or first month's rent for properties they do not own or that do not exist. Recognition red flags: pressure to pay before seeing the unit in person; a purported owner who is conveniently out of the country and cannot show the property; demands for payment by wire transfer, gift card, or cryptocurrency (irreversible, like cash); rent far below comparable listings; the same address advertised under different owner names; and early requests for a Social Security number, license, or pay stubs that enable identity theft. Remedy: never pay for an unseen property or to a person not met in person; pay by credit card for chargeback protection; withhold personal data until a deal is agreed; report rental fraud to the FTC."),
    ('realestate_change_order_fraud',
     'Change-Order and Cost-Inflation Fraud in Projects',
     'US DOJ Civil Division, The False Claims Act, https://www.justice.gov/civil/false-claims-act',
     'Change-order and cost-inflation fraud arises when contractors pad invoices with unnecessary or fictitious costs, overstate labor or materials, or manipulate change orders to extract extra payment on a project. On federally funded work this triggers the False Claims Act, which imposes treble damages plus per-claim penalties on anyone who knowingly submits or causes a false claim; bribing an official to approve a change order or payment is itself a crime. Recognition red flags: a pattern of change orders just after award that erode a low winning bid; billed quantities exceeding what was delivered; vague or duplicated cost line items; subcontractor invoices inflated above market; and change orders lacking independent justification. Remedy: the government recovered over 2.9 billion dollars under the FCA in 2024, much via whistleblower qui tam suits; report to an inspector general or the DOJ.'),
    ('realestate_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: Title Insurance, Regulators, Debarment',
     'US GSA / FAR Subpart 9.4, Debarment, Suspension, and Ineligibility, https://www.acquisition.gov/far/subpart-9.4',
     'Remedies for real-estate and construction integrity failures span civil, criminal, and administrative tracks. Suspension and debarment under FAR Subpart 9.4 let the government exclude non-responsible firms; causes include fraud, antitrust violations, forgery, bribery, false statements, false claims, and other conduct showing a lack of business integrity, with debarment typically running three years. A remedy is warranted on a conviction or strong evidence of fraud, collusion, or falsified records on public work. Other remedies: title insurance and forgery endorsements protect owners against fraudulent conveyance; the False Claims Act recovers treble damages with whistleblower awards; state contractor-licensing boards, attorneys general, and inspectors general discipline and prosecute. Administrative compliance agreements impose independent audits to prevent recurrence.'),
    ('transport_emissions_defeat_device',
     'Vehicle Emissions Defeat Devices Under the Clean Air Act',
     'US EPA, Learn About Volkswagen Violations (Clean Air Act sec. 203(a)(3)(B)); https://www.epa.gov/vw/learn-about-volkswagen-violations',
     'Legal authority: the Clean Air Act bars any defeat device that bypasses, defeats, or renders inoperative a required emissions control element, and bars selling or installing one. Recognition red flags: a vehicle that passes a laboratory or test-station check yet emits far above standard in normal driving; software that detects the test cycle and enables full controls only during it; large gaps between certified and on-road emissions. In the Volkswagen case roughly 590,000 diesels emitted nitrogen oxides up to 40 times the limit while appearing compliant on the test. Remedy and enforcement: EPA issues a Notice of Violation; outcomes have included criminal penalties, large civil settlements, mandatory emissions-modification recalls, a mitigation trust, and injunctive relief. Report suspected tampering to EPA.'),
    ('transport_odometer_fraud',
     'Recognizing Odometer Fraud and Mileage Rollback',
     'US NHTSA, Odometer Fraud and consumer advisory (Truth in Mileage Act of 1986, Pub. L. 99-579); https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle-safety/odometer-fraud',
     'Legal authority: odometer fraud is the disconnection, resetting, or alteration of an odometer with intent to change the miles indicated, barred under federal law implementing the Truth in Mileage Act, which requires the seller to disclose actual mileage on the title at transfer. NHTSA estimates over 450,000 vehicles are sold yearly with false readings. Recognition red flags: title mileage that is obscured, altered, or lower than service records; odometer digits that are crooked, gapped, or jiggle; pedal, tire, and interior wear inconsistent with displayed miles; a digital odometer with no service history; a history report showing a later reading below an earlier one. Remedy and enforcement: request a VIN-keyed history report, compare it against maintenance stickers, and report suspected rollback to NHTSA and the state enforcement agency, which can pursue civil and criminal action.'),
    ('transport_falsified_maintenance',
     'Falsified Aircraft Maintenance and Airworthiness Records',
     'US FAA Advisory Circular 43-9C, Maintenance Records (14 CFR parts 43 and 91); https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_43-9C_CHG_2.pdf',
     'Legal authority: a U.S. Standard Airworthiness Certificate stays valid only while maintenance and alterations are performed and recorded under 14 CFR parts 43 and 91; insufficient or nonexistent records can render it invalid. Section 43.9 requires a record entry describing the work, the date, and the signature and certificate number of the person returning the item to service. Recognition red flags: missing or back-dated entries, entries lacking a certificate number or signature, gaps that break record continuity, reused or photocopied logbook pages, claimed work exceeding approved time-since-overhaul or repair limits, and no FAA Form 337 for a major repair. Remedy and enforcement: the FAA can act against the certificate tied to a false statement; for used aircraft, demand original records and have an unbiased mechanic verify continuity. Report suspected falsification to the FAA.'),
    ('transport_unapproved_parts',
     'Suspected Unapproved and Counterfeit Aircraft Parts',
     'US FAA, Suspected Unapproved Parts (SUP) Program (AC 21-29; Aircraft Safety Act of 2000); https://www.faa.gov/aircraft/safety/programs/sups',
     'Legal authority: the FAA SUP Program identifies and removes parts that do not meet approved type-design or production requirements; a counterfeit part is an unapproved part made or altered to imitate an approved part with intent to mislead or defraud. The Fastener Quality Act and Aircraft Safety Act of 2000 underpin enforcement. Recognition red flags: paperwork that cannot be traced to an approval basis, a part not in the Parts Manufacturer Approval data, altered or copied FAA Form 8130-3 release tags, mismatched markings or serial numbers, prices far below market, and surplus parts without provenance. Remedy and enforcement: the FAA investigates every SUP report, and on confirming a part is unapproved it issues an Unapproved Parts Notification to owners, operators, and distributors so the part can be removed. Submit a Form 8120-11 report; do not install parts of unknown origin.'),
    ('transport_hours_of_service_fraud',
     'Hours-of-Service and Electronic Logging Device Fraud',
     "US FMCSA, 49 CFR 395.8 Driver's record of duty status (subpart B, ELDs); https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/part-395/section-395.8",
     'Legal authority: 49 CFR 395.8(e) states that no driver or motor carrier may make a false report in connection with duty status, and no one may disable, jam, reprogram, or otherwise tamper with an electronic logging device so it fails to accurately record required data, nor permit or require another person to do so. The ELD allows only limited annotated edits and preserves the original record. Recognition red flags: driving time logged as off-duty or sleeper-berth, location entries that do not match the route or mileage, unexplained unassigned-driving segments, repeated edits that erase violations, and fuel receipts that contradict the log. Remedy and enforcement: officials review ELD data to detect falsification; violations support out-of-service orders, civil penalties, and downgraded safety ratings. Keep records of duty status current, accurate, and certified.'),
    ('transport_inspection_fraud',
     'Safety-Inspection and Roadworthiness-Certificate Fraud',
     'US DOJ and DOT OIG enforcement actions on false inspection certification; https://www.justice.gov/usao-ct/pr/company-owner-employee-charged-falsely-certifying-bridge-inspection-vehicles-related',
     'Legal authority: knowingly issuing a false certificate of inspection or making a false statement to a federal agency is prosecutable, and falsely passing emissions inspections violates the Clean Air Act. Recognition red flags: an inspection sticker or certificate issued without the vehicle present, daily vehicle inspection reports certifying no defects when serious defects exist, results recorded after connecting test equipment to a substitute or surrogate vehicle, more inspections logged in a day than physically possible, and altered dates or pre-signed forms. Remedy and enforcement: outcomes have included debarment, guilty pleas, fines, and prison terms; programs revoke fraudulent certificates and re-inspect affected vehicles. Report suspected inspection fraud to the DOT Office of Inspector General or state authority.'),
    ('transport_recall_concealment',
     'Vehicle Recall and Safety-Defect Concealment',
     'US DOT, Honda TREAD Act civil penalty; NHTSA Early Warning Reporting (49 U.S.C. ch. 301); https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/us-department-transportation-fines-honda-70-million-failing-comply-laws-safeguard',
     'Legal authority: the TREAD Act requires manufacturers to report claims and notices of deaths and injuries allegedly caused by defects and to submit early-warning data on complaints, warranty and property-damage claims, and field reports; it raises civil penalties and adds criminal penalties for misleading the Secretary about defects that caused death or injury. Recognition red flags: a manufacturer aware of a pattern of failures, injuries, or warranty claims that delays reporting or a recall; foreign safety campaigns not disclosed to U.S. regulators; field reports inconsistent with public statements. Honda paid 70 million dollars for failing to report deaths, injuries, and warranty claims. Remedy and enforcement: NHTSA can compel a recall, and concealment supports large civil penalties and criminal exposure. Owners should check recalls by VIN and report suspected defects to NHTSA.'),
    ('transport_cdl_fraud',
     'Commercial Driver License and CDL Skills-Test Fraud',
     'US DOT OIG, third-party CDL examiner convictions for fraudulent skills testing; https://www.oig.dot.gov/library-item/36474',
     'Legal authority: falsifying CDL skills-test results is prosecuted as making false statements and wire fraud, because score sheets are submitted to state licensing agencies to issue a federally regulated credential. Recognition red flags: examiners accepting cash to skip the test and certify passing scores, pre-signed or pre-dated score sheets, more tests recorded in one day than feasible, hand-selected rather than randomized test forms, and CDL holders who cannot demonstrate basic vehicle-control skills. Documented schemes involved examiners taking payments from dozens of applicants for falsified score sheets used to obtain CDLs without testing. Remedy and enforcement: convictions have brought guilty pleas, wire-fraud counts, and prison terms; FMCSA recommends examiner training, background checks, and barring those with fraud convictions. Improperly issued CDLs can be re-tested.'),
    ('transport_freight_fraud',
     'Cargo, Freight, and Bill-of-Lading Fraud Recognition',
     'US FMCSA, Broker and Carrier Fraud and Identity Theft; https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/mission/help/broker-and-carrier-fraud-and-identity-theft',
     "Legal authority: using another carrier's assigned USDOT number without authorization, or brokering loads without FMCSA registration, is a criminal act; the USDOT number belongs to one legal person and may not be sold, rented, or transferred. Recognition red flags: a broker asking a carrier to present itself under a different name or telling a driver to lie about who they work for; a load described as a blind load when the destination is questioned; a broker quick to agree to pay above the market rate; and a bill of lading or shipping paperwork that has been altered or printed from data obtained by phishing for a fictitious pickup or double-brokering scheme. Remedy and enforcement: FMCSA inactivates misused USDOT numbers; victims should verify carrier identity in SAFER, stop suspect transactions, and report to the FMCSA National Consumer Complaint Database and law enforcement."),
    ('transport_vin_cloning',
     'Title Washing and VIN Cloning Recognition',
     'US National Insurance Crime Bureau, Buying a Vehicle; NMVTIS, VehicleHistory.gov; https://www.nicb.org/prevent-fraud-theft/buying-vehicle',
     'Legal authority: VIN cloning and title washing involve counterfeit VIN tags and fraudulent ownership documents; the federal National Motor Vehicle Title Information System tracks brands such as salvage, junk, and flood across states. Recognition red flags for cloning: a late-model vehicle with no lienholder said to be paid off, an out-of-state title surrendered with documents that do not match, and a price too good to be true. Red flags for title washing or flood damage: a clean title inconsistent with prior salvage history, water stains, mildew, silt under carpets, rust on screws where water should not reach, and grit in the spare-tire well. Remedy and enforcement: a cloned vehicle can be confiscated and returned to the true owner; run a VIN check and NMVTIS title search, have it inspected, and report suspected fraud to the NICB and law enforcement.'),
    ('transport_safety_data_falsification',
     'Aviation and Maritime Safety-Data Falsification',
     'US FAA final rule, Falsification, Reproduction, Alteration, Omission (14 CFR part 3 subpart D); IMO Port State Control; https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/09/03/2025-16902/falsification-reproduction-alteration-omission-or-incorrect-statements',
     'Legal authority: the FAA prohibits intentionally false or fraudulent statements, fraudulent reproduction or alteration of documents, and knowingly omitting a material fact in applications and records submitted to the agency, consolidated under 14 CFR part 3 subpart D with standardized sanctions. At sea, SOLAS makes the flag State responsible for certifying compliance, and port State control lets authorities inspect foreign ships on clear grounds of non-compliance. Recognition red flags: logbook or inspection entries with no verification trail, records that contradict equipment condition or voyage data, and certificates tied to fraudulent registration. Remedy and enforcement: the FAA can act against the related certificate or approval, and port State control is a second line of defense against substandard shipping, detaining vessels and flagging fraudulent certificates.'),
    ('transport_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: Recalls, Penalties, Certificate Revocation',
     'US NHTSA, Motor Vehicle Safety Act remedy without charge (49 U.S.C. 30120); https://www.nhtsa.gov/sites/nhtsa.gov/files/mvs01092008.pdf',
     'Legal authority: under 49 U.S.C. 30120 a manufacturer must remedy a reported safety defect or noncompliance without charge by repairing, replacing, or refunding when the vehicle is presented; if repair is not completed in a reasonable time the manufacturer must replace it or refund the price less depreciation. The free-remedy duty generally applies if the first purchaser bought within eight years of the recall notice, and pre-notification repair costs are reimbursable, with claims acted on within 60 days. Recognition that remedy is owed: an open recall keyed to the VIN, a defect notice, or a denied reimbursement without a clear written reason. Across modes, enforcement tools include compelled recalls, civil and criminal penalties, debarment, and revocation of certificates or approvals tied to false statements. Owners should check recalls by VIN and escalate unresolved defects.'),
    ('aigov_eu_ai_act',
     'EU AI Act: Risk Tiers and Prohibited AI Practices',
     'Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (AI Act), Article 5 - prohibited practices; https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/',
     'The EU AI Act sorts systems into unacceptable, high, limited, and minimal risk. Article 5 bans eight unacceptable-risk practices: subliminal or manipulative techniques that distort behavior; exploitation of age, disability, or socioeconomic vulnerability; social scoring leading to detrimental treatment; risk-scoring of criminality based solely on profiling; untargeted scraping of facial images; emotion recognition in workplaces or schools; biometric categorization inferring protected traits; and untargeted real-time remote biometric identification by law enforcement. Red flags: a system that scores people across unrelated contexts, infers protected traits, or claims to read emotions for hiring or discipline. Remedy: prohibited systems must not be placed on the market or used; affected persons may complain to market-surveillance authorities, which can order withdrawal and fines.'),
    ('aigov_nist_rmf',
     'NIST AI Risk Management Framework for Trustworthy AI',
     'NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0), Jan 2023; https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework',
     'The NIST AI RMF is a voluntary, consensus-built framework organized around four functions: Govern (policies and oversight), Map (context and intended use), Measure (assess performance and risk), and Manage (mitigate and monitor). It defines trustworthy AI as valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair with harmful bias managed. Recognition red flags: a deployer that cannot name who governs the system, has no documented mapping of intended use and affected groups, or never measures real-world performance and bias. Remedy: adopt the Govern-Map-Measure-Manage loop, document risks and controls, and use NIST sector profiles; the framework supplies a shared vocabulary to demand and verify accountability even where it is not legally mandatory.'),
    ('aigov_oecd_principles',
     'OECD AI Principles: First Intergovernmental AI Standard',
     'OECD Recommendation on Artificial Intelligence (2019, updated 2024); https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles',
     'The OECD AI Principles, adopted in 2019 and updated in 2024, were the first intergovernmental AI standard and the basis for the G20 AI Principles, with dozens of adherents. Five values-based principles require AI to support inclusive growth, sustainable development, and well-being; respect human rights and democratic values including fairness and privacy; provide transparency and explainability; be robust, secure, and safe across its lifecycle; and assign clear accountability to AI actors. Recognition red flags: an AI actor that disclaims responsibility for outcomes, cannot explain a consequential decision, or excludes affected communities from governance. Remedy: governments and operators are urged to embed the principles in policy and practice, ensure traceability, and provide mechanisms so accountability for AI outcomes rests with identifiable actors, not the tool.'),
    ('aigov_unesco_ethics',
     'UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence',
     'UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI (adopted Nov 2021, 194 member states); https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics',
     'Adopted in November 2021 by all 194 UNESCO member states, this is the first global standard-setting instrument on AI ethics. It is grounded in human rights and dignity and sets principles including proportionality and do no harm; safety and security; fairness and non-discrimination; privacy and data protection; human oversight and determination; transparency and explainability; responsibility and accountability; sustainability; awareness and literacy; and multi-stakeholder governance. It states AI systems should be auditable and traceable. Recognition red flags: a deployment with no human oversight over significant decisions, no auditability, or that widens discrimination. Remedy: states are expected to build ethical-impact assessment, oversight bodies, and redress channels; the Recommendation anchors demands for traceable, contestable, human-accountable AI worldwide.'),
    ('aigov_algorithmic_bias',
     'Identifying and Managing Algorithmic Bias and Discrimination',
     'NIST Special Publication 1270, Towards a Standard for Identifying and Managing Bias in AI (2022); https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2022/03/theres-more-ai-bias-biased-data-nist-report-highlights',
     "NIST SP 1270 frames AI bias as a socio-technical problem with three sources: statistical and computational bias from data and models (such as underrepresented groups); human and cognitive bias in how people design and interpret systems; and systemic or institutional bias where organizational practices disadvantage social groups. It warns that purely technical fixes fall short because AI helps make decisions affecting people's lives. Recognition red flags: selection or scoring rates that diverge sharply across race, sex, age, or disability; a vendor that audits only the dataset and ignores deployment context; outcomes that mirror historical discrimination. Remedy: manage bias across the full lifecycle and institutional context, document data and decisions, test for disparate impact on protected groups, and engage affected stakeholders rather than relying on code alone."),
    ('aigov_transparency_explainability',
     'Automated-Decision Transparency and the Right to Explanation',
     'Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR), Article 22 - automated individual decision-making; https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/',
     'GDPR Article 22 gives individuals the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing, including profiling, that produces legal or similarly significant effects. Where such processing is permitted (contract, consent, or law with safeguards), controllers must provide the right to obtain human intervention, to express a point of view, and to contest the decision; sensitive-category data carries extra limits, and controllers should give meaningful information about the logic involved. Recognition red flags: a consequential decision (credit, hiring, benefits) made with no human review, no stated reasons, and no way to challenge it. Remedy: demand human-in-the-loop review, an explanation of the decision logic, and a contestation channel; supervisory authorities can investigate and order compliance where automated decisions lack these safeguards.'),
    ('aigov_automated_hiring',
     'Automated Hiring Audits: NYC Local Law 144 and EEOC Title VII',
     'NYC Local Law 144 (AEDT bias audit) and EEOC technical assistance on AI under Title VII (2023); https://www.dciconsult.com/nyc-automated-employment-decision-tools-bill',
     "NYC Local Law 144 requires that automated employment decision tools used for hiring or promotion undergo an independent bias audit, that a summary of audit results be published, and that candidates receive notice at least ten business days before use with an option for alternatives; violations carry per-day penalties. The EEOC's Title VII technical assistance adds that employers can be liable for disparate (adverse) impact from algorithmic tools even when built by a vendor, and that passing the four-fifths rule does not guarantee compliance. Recognition red flags: a hiring tool used without a published bias audit, no candidate notice, or selection rates below 80 percent for a protected group. Remedy: obtain and publish an independent audit, give notice and alternatives, and replace tools that cause adverse impact."),
    ('aigov_deepfake_disclosure',
     'AI-Enabled Fraud and Deepfake Disclosure Duties',
     'FTC business guidance, Chatbots, deepfakes, and voice clones: AI deception for sale (Mar 2023); https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/blog/2023/03/chatbots-deepfakes-voice-clones-ai-deception-sale',
     "The FTC warns that the FTC Act's ban on deceptive or unfair conduct can reach anyone who makes, sells, or uses a tool effectively designed to deceive, even if deception is not its sole purpose. Synthetic media such as deepfakes and voice clones can power imposter scams, extortion, and financial fraud, and chatbots can mass-produce fake reviews, profiles, and phishing. Newer state statutes also require conspicuous disclosure when AI-generated synthetic performers appear in advertising. Recognition red flags: audio or video of a known person making out-of-character requests, urgency to move money, a likeness or voice used without consent, or undisclosed AI-generated endorsements. Remedy: do not deploy tools built to deceive, label synthetic media, and report imposter or fraud schemes to consumer-protection authorities for investigation and enforcement."),
    ('aigov_model_documentation',
     'Model Documentation: Model Cards and Datasheets for Datasets',
     'Mitchell et al., Model Cards for Model Reporting (2018), and Gebru et al., Datasheets for Datasets (2018); https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993',
     "Model cards document a model's intended use, evaluation conditions, performance across demographic, cultural, and intersectional groups, ethical considerations, and limitations, so practitioners know when a system is appropriate and where it may fail. Datasheets for datasets parallel this by recording a dataset's motivation, composition, collection process, recommended uses, and distribution, improving communication between creators and consumers. Both standardize transparency and accountability in high-stakes domains like hiring, lending, and medicine. Recognition red flags: a model with no documented intended use, no group-disaggregated performance, or a dataset with unknown provenance and consent. Remedy: require a model card and datasheet before deployment, review them for population coverage and limitations, and refuse undocumented systems where bias cannot be assessed."),
    ('aigov_credit_fairness',
     'AI in Credit and Lending: ECOA Adverse-Action Reasons',
     'CFPB Consumer Financial Protection Circular 2022-03 on ECOA/Regulation B adverse-action notices and complex algorithms; https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/circulars/circular-2022-03-adverse-action-notification-requirements-in-connection-with-credit-decisions-based-on-complex-algorithms/',
     "Under the Equal Credit Opportunity Act and Regulation B, creditors who take adverse action must give applicants specific, accurate reasons for the decision. The CFPB's Circular 2022-03 confirms that this duty applies fully when decisions rely on complex algorithms or machine-learning credit models, and that a creditor cannot excuse vague or generic reasons by claiming its own model is too opaque to explain; lack of understanding of one's own method is not a defense. Recognition red flags: a denial notice with boilerplate or no real reasons, a lender that cites a black-box score it cannot explain, or reasons that do not match the actual factors used. Remedy: applicants are entitled to specific, accurate reasons; use only models that can produce them, and supervisory authorities may enforce ECOA against lenders that cannot."),
    ('aigov_coe_convention',
     'Council of Europe Framework Convention on AI and Human Rights',
     'Council of Europe Framework Convention on AI and Human Rights, Democracy and the Rule of Law (CETS No. 225, opened Sep 2024); https://www.coe.int/en/web/portal/-/council-of-europe-opens-first-ever-global-treaty-on-ai-for-signature',
     'Adopted in May 2024 and opened for signature in September 2024, CETS No. 225 is the first legally binding international treaty on AI. It obliges parties to ensure that activities across the AI lifecycle - design, development, deployment, and decommissioning - are consistent with human rights, democracy, and the rule of law. It addresses transparency, accountability, oversight, equality and non-discrimination, privacy, and the availability of effective remedies, supported by risk and impact assessments. Recognition red flags: state or private AI deployments that escape oversight, lack accountability, or leave affected people with no avenue to challenge harm. Remedy: parties must adopt measures including accessible remedies and procedural safeguards so individuals can contest AI-driven decisions and obtain redress; the treaty creates an international baseline for rights-respecting AI.'),
    ('aigov_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: Conformity Assessment, Audits, Redress',
     'Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (AI Act), Article 99 - penalties and enforcement; https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/99/',
     'The EU AI Act backs its rules with conformity assessment for high-risk systems, market-surveillance oversight, and graduated penalties: up to 35 million EUR or 7 percent of worldwide annual turnover for prohibited practices; up to 15 million EUR or 3 percent for other obligation breaches; and up to 7.5 million EUR or 1 percent for supplying incorrect information. Fines must be effective, proportionate, and dissuasive, with lighter treatment for SMEs. Recognition red flags: a high-risk system on the market with no conformity assessment, no documentation, or a provider that withholds information from authorities. Remedy: affected persons can complain to market-surveillance authorities, who may order corrective action, withdrawal, or recall and impose fines; conformity assessment, audits, and redress channels turn standards into enforceable accountability.'),
    ('blue_wto_fisheries_subsidies',
     'WTO Agreement on Fisheries Subsidies: the three prohibited subsidy classes',
     'WTO Agreement on Fisheries Subsidies, Ministerial Decision of 17 June 2022 (MC12), entered into force 15 September 2025 - https://www.wto.org/english/tratop_e/rulesneg_e/fish_e/fish_e.htm',
     "Legal authority: the first binding multilateral WTO agreement centered on environmental sustainability. Articles 3, 4 and 5 prohibit subsidies (a) to a vessel or operator engaged in illegal, unreported and unregulated (IUU) fishing or support activities, (b) for fishing of overfished stocks unless paired with rebuilding measures, and (c) for fishing on the unregulated high seas outside any coastal State or RFMO competence. Recognition red flags: a State funds a vessel on an IUU list; subsidies flow to flag-of-convenience vessels not flying the granting member's flag; support targets unassessed stocks without due restraint. Remedy: members must notify subsidies and IUU determinations; the Committee on Fisheries Subsidies oversees implementation; disputes route through WTO dispute settlement. The agreement lapses if broader overcapacity disciplines are not adopted within four years."),
    ('blue_harmful_subsidies',
     'Recognizing harmful capacity-enhancing fisheries subsidies (OECD risk matrix)',
     'OECD, Fisheries subsidies topic page and Fisheries Support Estimate (FSE) database; OECD Review of Fisheries 2025 - https://www.oecd.org/en/topics/sub-issues/fisheries-subsidies.html',
     "Authority: OECD distinguishes low-risk support (management, monitoring, control and surveillance) from capacity-enhancing support that, where fisheries are not effectively regulated, drives overfishing and overcapacity. Payments tied to variable inputs (fuel) most provoke fishing effort; payments tied to fixed capital (vessel construction) most build excess capacity. Recognition red flags: a large share of support paid as fuel or vessel subsidies rather than stewardship; support to fleets targeting depleted or unassessed stocks; eligibility with no stock-health or management condition. OECD reports support of roughly USD 5,722 per fisher in OECD countries versus USD 283 elsewhere (2020-22). Remedy: OECD's risk matrix lets governments shift the policy mix away from distorting subsidies, improve enforcement, and meet SDG Target 14.6 to phase out harmful subsidies."),
    ('blue_iuu_subsidy_link',
     'Subsidies reaching IUU-fishing vessels: the recognition and determination gap',
     'WTO Agreement on Fisheries Subsidies Article 3; USTR Fact Sheet on the WTO Agreement on Fisheries Subsidies (2022) - https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/fact-sheets/2022/august/fact-sheet-wto-agreement-fisheries-subsidies',
     'Authority: Article 3 requires members to stop subsidies to any vessel or operator they know has engaged in IUU fishing. IUU determinations come from the flag State for its own vessels and from RFMOs for areas and species they oversee. Recognition red flags: public money continuing to flow to a vessel after an RFMO IUU listing; flag-of-convenience and reflagging used to obscure beneficial ownership and dodge high-seas enforcement; subsidies to non-flag vessels (linked by the agreement to illegal activity and forced labor). The enforcement gap: on the high seas only the flag State can declare IUU, and States are reluctant to indict their own fleet. Remedy: cross-check beneficiaries against RFMO and national IUU vessel lists, require ownership transparency, and use WTO notification plus the OECD IUU Recommendation to withdraw funds when a vessel is implicated.'),
    ('blue_fuel_subsidy',
     'Fuel-subsidy distortion that keeps unprofitable fishing fleets at sea',
     'Oceana, Assessing the spatial burden of harmful fisheries subsidies; peer-reviewed economics-of-high-seas-fishing analyses - https://oceana.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/18/OceanaDWF_FinalReport.pdf',
     "Authority: fuel subsidies are the single largest category of capacity-enhancing support and the most likely to provoke excess fishing effort. Evidence indicates roughly 54% of high-seas fishing grounds would be unprofitable without subsidies, and some distant-water fleets receive support worth up to 40% of catch value; deep-sea bottom trawling is often net-positive only because of subsidies. Recognition red flags: fuel-tax concessions or rebates that make otherwise loss-making distant-water and bottom-trawl trips viable; high-income States subsidizing fleets that fish in low-HDI nations' waters (about 40% of harmful subsidies in very-low-HDI waters originate from high-HDI States). Remedy: reclassify fuel subsidies as harmful under SDG 14.6, phase out fuel concessions, redirect support to monitoring and stewardship, and disclose fuel-subsidy beneficiaries."),
    ('blue_aquaculture_integrity',
     'Aquaculture integrity: mislabeling, escapes, antibiotics, and audit fraud',
     'FAO, Food fraud in the fisheries and aquaculture sector (FAO Fisheries and Aquaculture Division); independent certification-audit investigations - https://www.food-safety.com/articles/11122-fao-publishes-report-on-food-fraud-in-fishery-and-aquaculture-sector',
     'Authority: FAO finds up to roughly 20% of aquatic products are mislabeled or otherwise fraudulent, a higher rate than meat or produce, helped by more than 12,000 traded seafood species. Recognition red flags: farmed Atlantic salmon sold as wild-caught (a DNA study found 43% of U.S. salmon mislabeled); species, origin, or eco-certification misrepresented; weight or freshness adulteration; certified farms exceeding sea-lice or antibiotic limits yet keeping certification; facilities falsifying records to pass audits or never processing the certified product they label. Remedy: FAO advocates harmonized labeling, mandatory scientific (Latin) names, digital end-to-end traceability from vessel to plate, and independent unannounced audits rather than self-reported compliance, so certification claims are verifiable.'),
    ('blue_mpa_enforcement',
     'Marine protected area enforcement and the paper-park problem',
     'CBD Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (2022) 30x30 target; Marine Conservation Institute analyses of MPA effectiveness - https://marine-conservation.org/on-the-tide/ten-percent-protected-three-percent-effective/',
     "Authority: the CBD Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (2022) commits parties to conserve 30% of seas by 2030 through 'effectively conserved and well-managed' areas - not mere designation. Recognition red flags: an MPA decreed on paper but underfunded, with no monitoring, surveillance, or enforcement budget; bottom trawling or industrial extraction continuing inside the boundary; high reported coverage (near 10% of ocean) but only about 3% effectively protected; designations rushed to hit numeric targets for press releases. Remedy: pair designation with funded surveillance and management, transparent management-effectiveness scoring, inclusive stakeholder engagement, and independent verification so 'protected' status reflects real restriction of exploitation, not a paper park."),
    ('blue_carbon_bond_integrity',
     'Blue-bond and blue-carbon integrity: guarding against greenwashing',
     "ICMA Blue Bond guidance (September 2023) on market integrity and external verification; analyses of debt-for-nature 'blue bond' labeling - https://sustainablefutures.linklaters.com/post/102iqmk/icma-publish-paper-on-market-integrity-and-greenwashing-risks-in-sustainable-fina",
     "Authority: ICMA issued voluntary blue-bond guidance in 2023 to help issuers launch credible instruments, set eligible project categories, and define external-verification criteria that preserve market integrity. Recognition red flags: a 'blue bond' label where most proceeds buy back existing debt and only a small slice funds conservation; vague or unmeasurable performance targets; no independent verifier or impact reporting; instruments later reclassified by fund managers as non-aligned. Remedy: require alignment with ICMA principles, a credible project pipeline, external review by qualified verifiers, transparent use-of-proceeds and impact reporting, and clear consequences for missing targets - so blue and nature finance is held to verifiable standards rather than self-declared green claims."),
    ('blue_access_agreement_transparency',
     'Fisheries access agreement transparency and hidden joint ventures',
     'EU Common Fisheries Policy Sustainable Fisheries Partnership Agreements (SFPAs); Coalition for Fair Fisheries Arrangements analysis - https://www.cffacape.org/news-blog/45-years-of-eu-fisheries-access-arrangements',
     "Authority: the EU's 2014 CFP reform set a transparency standard for Sustainable Fisheries Partnership Agreements (SFPAs), disclosing vessel numbers, ownership, transhipment and landing locations. Recognition red flags: 'second-generation' private access deals and opaque joint ventures (for example reflagging EU-origin vessels under a coastal State) that are far less transparent than SFPAs and obscure who controls the resource; undisclosed fees, catch, or beneficial ownership; access negotiated without public catch or stock data. Remedy: require public registers of access agreements, vessel ownership and authorizations; subject joint ventures to the same disclosure as SFPAs; and use advisory-council oversight and WTO transparency obligations so coastal States and citizens can assess whether access is sustainable and fairly priced."),
    ('blue_seafood_traceability',
     'Seafood traceability and catch documentation against fraud and IUU product',
     'FAO Voluntary Guidelines for Catch Documentation Schemes; FAO seafood-traceability technical work and the Global Dialogue on Seafood Traceability (GDST) Standard - https://openknowledge.fao.org/server/api/core/bitstreams/0466ac36-4d7d-45da-adb9-997fd717f18a/content',
     'Authority: FAO Voluntary Guidelines for Catch Documentation Schemes and the GDST Standard establish how to verify legal, correctly labeled product from harvest onward. Recognition red flags: missing or unverifiable chain-of-custody documents; species or origin claims that cannot be traced to a catch certificate; transhipment that breaks the documented chain; product lacking scientific names; certificates not aligned with national monitoring, control and surveillance. Remedy: implement interoperable digital end-to-end traceability, mandatory catch documentation, and key-data-element capture aligned to GDST, so IUU and mislabeled product can be detected at import and market entry; align national CDS with port State measures so undocumented catch is refused entry.'),
    ('blue_ssf_equity',
     'Small-scale fisher equity and tenure rights under the FAO SSF Guidelines',
     'FAO Voluntary Guidelines for Securing Sustainable Small-Scale Fisheries (SSF Guidelines, 2014) - https://www.wto.org/english/tratop_e/rulesneg_e/fish_e/2015_fao_ssf.pdf',
     "Authority: the SSF Guidelines (FAO, 2014) are the first international instrument dedicated to small-scale fisheries, built from a participatory process with over 4,000 stakeholders and grounded in a human-rights approach. They protect secure, equitable tenure rights to fish and to land for landing sites, processing and housing, with priority for vulnerable and marginalized groups. Recognition red flags: blue-economy investment, large-scale concessions, MPAs, or access deals that displace coastal communities ('ocean grabbing') without consultation or compensation; subsidies and quotas captured by industrial fleets while small-scale fishers lose access; tenure rights stripped without legal protection. Remedy: apply the SSF Guidelines to secure tenure, ensure free prior and informed participation, protect equitable access, and embed community rights in fisheries and blue-economy governance."),
    ('blue_deepsea_mining_risk',
     'Deep-sea mining: emerging blue-economy risk and the precautionary gap',
     'International Seabed Authority (ISA) regime under UNCLOS; analyses of Clarion-Clipperton Zone exploration and moratorium calls - https://www.wri.org/insights/deep-sea-mining-explained',
     'Authority: the ISA regulates mineral activity in the seabed area beyond national jurisdiction under UNCLOS and is still finalizing exploitation rules. The Clarion-Clipperton Zone (about 4.5 million square kilometers) already hosts 17 exploration contracts covering roughly 1 million square kilometers. Recognition red flags: extraction approvals advancing before agreed exploitation regulations and baseline science exist; sediment plumes and possible release of seabed-stored carbon threatening fisheries and biodiversity that Pacific Island blue economies depend on; harms described as potentially immediate and irreversible. Remedy: a precautionary pause or moratorium (called for by many ISA members) until enforceable, science-based safeguards, transparency, and environmental-impact rules are in place, with independent monitoring before any commercial mining proceeds.'),
    ('blue_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: WTO disputes, RFMOs, and transparency registries',
     'WTO Committee on Fisheries Subsidies and dispute settlement; RFMO IUU vessel lists; Global Fishing Watch vessel-transparency datasets - https://globalfishingwatch.org/international-policy/',
     "Authority: enforcement of blue-economy integrity spans the WTO Committee on Fisheries Subsidies (notification and surveillance) and WTO dispute settlement; RFMOs that set catch limits, authorize vessels, and sanction IUU operators via official IUU vessel lists; and transparency registries. Recognition red flags: a subsidy or vessel that never appears in mandated notifications; beneficial ownership that cannot be matched across registries; activity gaps where vessels disable tracking. Remedy: scrutinize WTO subsidy notifications; cross-reference subsidy beneficiaries against RFMO and national IUU lists; use interoperable vessel registries and Global Fishing Watch's fusion of 40-plus public registries plus AIS to detect irregularities; combine vessel monitoring, observer programs, catch documentation, and port State measures so harmful subsidies and IUU operators face consequences."),
    ('charity_fake_charity_recognition',
     'Recognizing Fake Charities and Solicitation Scams',
     'FTC Consumer Advice, Charity Scams (fetched https://consumer.ftc.gov/all-scams/charity-scams)',
     'Fake-charity recognition rests on a short list of red flags rather than on the cause itself. Treat as suspicious any solicitor who pressures for an immediate gift, refuses to explain how funds are used, cannot give a verifiable address or registration, or uses a name that closely imitates a well-known charity. A demand to pay by cash, gift card, cryptocurrency, or wire transfer is a strong indicator of fraud, because those channels are hard to trace or reverse. Vague emotional appeals with no detail on programs, leadership, or past results are a further warning sign. Standard practice: verify the organization through a charity-rating body before giving, ask what share reaches program work, and never let urgency override checking. Remedy: a legitimate charity accepts a delayed gift; donors can report suspected charity scams to the FTC and the state charity regulator for investigation.'),
    ('charity_self_dealing',
     'Private Inurement and Self-Dealing in Tax-Exempt Charities',
     'IRS, Intermediate Sanctions and Excess Benefit Transactions under IRC 4958 (fetched https://www.irs.gov/charities-non-profits/charitable-organizations/intermediate-sanctions-excess-benefit-transactions)',
     'A 501(c)(3) charity may not let its earnings inure to the private benefit of insiders. An excess benefit transaction occurs when the charity gives an economic benefit to a disqualified person (someone with substantial influence, such as a director, officer, or major contributor) worth more than the value the charity receives in return. Recognition red flags: above-market compensation, charity loans, rent, or asset sales to insiders, and family members hired or paid without arms-length review. Remedy under section 4958 is the intermediate-sanctions excise tax: the disqualified person owes 25 percent of the excess benefit, rising to 200 percent if not corrected, and a complicit manager can owe 10 percent. Correction means repaying the excess plus interest. Boards reduce risk by approving insider deals with independent members, comparability data, and contemporaneous documentation.'),
    ('charity_form_990_transparency',
     'IRS Form 990 Transparency and Governance Red Flags',
     'National Council of Nonprofits, Financial Transparency and Public Disclosure Requirements (fetched https://www.councilofnonprofits.org/running-nonprofit/ethics-accountability/financial-transparency-and-public-disclosure-requirements)',
     'The annual IRS Form 990 is a public-accountability document: a charity must, on request, provide its three most recently filed returns and its exemption application, and many post them online. Reviewers use it to check whether stated programs match actual spending and whether governance basics exist. Red flags include an unsigned return, board rosters that shift inconsistently year to year, no written conflict-of-interest or whistleblower policy, program descriptions that do not match where dollars went, and refusal to honor inspection requests. Failure to file for three consecutive years triggers automatic loss of tax-exempt status. Remedy: boards should review the 990 before filing, adopt internal controls and disclosure policies, and publish financials; donors and regulators can request returns and report discrepancies to the state charity official.'),
    ('charity_uk_commission',
     'UK Charity Commission Governance and Serious-Incident Reporting',
     'GOV.UK / Charity Commission for England and Wales, How to report a serious incident in your charity (fetched https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-report-a-serious-incident-in-your-charity)',
     'Under the Charity Commission framework, trustees hold ultimate responsibility and must report serious incidents promptly, fully, and honestly. A serious incident is an actual or alleged event causing or risking significant harm to beneficiaries, assets, or reputation: fraud, theft, money laundering, safeguarding failures, unverified large or anonymous donations, links to proscribed organizations, and major data breaches. Financial loss is generally reportable at 25,000 pounds or more, or less if it exceeds 20 percent of annual income. Charities over the 25,000 pound income threshold must confirm in the annual return that all reportable incidents were declared; withholding a known one can be mismanagement. Remedy: prompt self-reporting plus corrective action to limit harm and fix root causes, with the Commission able to advise, investigate, or use statutory powers where governance fails.'),
    ('charity_donor_fraud',
     'Donor Fraud and Misrepresentation of How Funds Are Used',
     'FTC press release, FTC and 10 States Take Action Against Operator of Sham Cancer Charity for Deceiving Donors (fetched https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/03/ftc-10-states-take-action-against-operator-sham-cancer-charity-deceiving-donors)',
     'Donor fraud occurs when solicitations misrepresent how gifts will be used. In enforced cases, charities told donors money would directly help beneficiaries with basic needs, yet only about one percent reached that purpose while the majority went to for-profit fundraisers and insider pay. Recognition red flags for donors: pledges that gifts go directly to people in need but no published split of program versus fundraising and overhead; reliance on outside telemarketers; emotive letters promising impact without verifiable results; and tax filings that contradict the pitch. Standard check: compare the appeal against the Form 990 program-expense ratio and independent ratings before giving. Remedy: regulators bring deceptive-solicitation actions under consumer-protection and fundraising law, can dissolve the entity, ban operators from nonprofit work, and seek restitution of charitable assets.'),
    ('charity_disaster_scam',
     'Recognizing Disaster and Crisis Charity Scams',
     'FBI, Charity and Disaster Fraud (fetched https://www.fbi.gov/how-we-can-help-you/scams-and-safety/common-frauds-and-scams/charity-and-disaster-fraud); FTC Charity Scams alerts',
     'After disasters and crises, fraudulent appeals spike because urgency and sympathy lower donor scrutiny. Recognition red flags: brand-new fundraising pages or charities created right after an event, names that copy established relief organizations, unsolicited calls, texts, emails, or social posts pushing for instant gifts, and requests to pay by gift card, cryptocurrency, or wire transfer. Look for missing detail on who runs the appeal and exactly how funds reach victims. Standard practice: give to established relief charities, verify the organization independently rather than clicking a link in an unsolicited message, and never feel rushed by a deadline. Remedy: donate by credit card for chargeback protection, keep records, and report suspected disaster-charity fraud to the FTC, the FBI, and the state charity regulator so enforcement and warnings can follow.'),
    ('charity_fundraising_cost',
     'Excessive Fundraising Cost and Commercial Co-Venturer Abuse',
     'BBB Wise Giving Alliance, Standards for Charity Accountability (fetched https://give.org/bbb-standards-for-charity-accountability); state paid-solicitor and commercial co-venturer regulation',
     'Disproportionate fundraising costs signal that donor intent may be diverted. Accountability standards expect a charity to spend a reasonable share of contributions on program activity rather than on solicitation and administration, and to put fundraising agreements in writing under board oversight. Red flags: outside paid solicitors keeping most of each dollar raised, commercial co-venturer promotions (a for-profit selling goods and donating a share) with no clear disclosure of how much reaches the charity, and reluctance to state the program-expense ratio. Most US states regulate this: paid solicitors and co-venturers commonly must register, file contracts and reports, post bonds, and disclose paid-solicitor status before asking for a gift; non-disclosure is a deceptive practice. Remedy: donors can request the ratio and contracts; regulators enforce registration and disclosure rules.'),
    ('charity_board_governance',
     'Conflict of Interest and Board Governance Standards',
     'National Council of Nonprofits, Conflicts of Interests (fetched https://www.councilofnonprofits.org/running-nonprofit/governance-leadership/conflicts-interests)',
     "Sound governance turns on the board's duty of loyalty: directors must put the charity's interest ahead of personal gain. A written conflict-of-interest policy is widely treated as the most important board policy. It should require anyone with an actual or potential conflict to disclose it and bar interested members from voting; minutes should record the disclosure, discussion without that member present, and the abstention. Red flags: no written policy, no annual disclosure questionnaire, related-party deals approved by interested directors, and one person holding incompatible roles such as chair and treasurer. The IRS Form 990 asks both whether a policy exists and how conflicts are managed. Remedy: unmanaged conflicts can trigger intermediate-sanctions penalties on the benefiting person and the organization; boards prevent this through disclosure, recusal, and independent approval."),
    ('charity_grant_fraud',
     'Grant Fraud and Subgrantee Misuse Recognition',
     'Grants.gov, Grant Fraud (fetched https://www.grants.gov/learn-grants/grant-fraud); US GAO and Inspector General grant-fraud guidance',
     'Grant fraud is deception about how award funds are used, taking forms such as embezzlement, theft, bribery, and false claims or statements. Recognition red flags from oversight bodies: charging personal costs as grant expenses, billing for work not performed, inflated labor hours or fictitious employees and consultants, falsified application information, weak procurement controls without separation of duties, repeated high-dollar transactions with one vendor, and conflicts such as hiring family or buying from parties with a financial stake. Subrecipient misuse warrants close monitoring when pass-through controls are thin. These indicators do not alone prove fraud but justify scrutiny. Remedy: report concerns to the relevant agency Inspector General; consequences include debarment from future funding, recovery of funds, civil suits, and criminal prosecution.'),
    ('charity_civil_society_space',
     'Protecting Legitimate NGOs and Civil-Society Space',
     'ICNL, Legal Enabling Environment and Civic Space Initiative (fetched https://www.icnl.org/our-work/global-programs/legal-enabling-environment-program)',
     'Anti-fraud integrity must distinguish genuine wrongdoing from restrictions that suppress lawful civil society. ICNL, focused on the laws affecting nonprofits and public participation, documents measures that impede forming organizations, seeking resources, and assembling peacefully; since 2019 it has tracked hundreds of restrictive legal initiatives across dozens of countries. Recognition standard: legitimate oversight is proportionate, transparent, and applied evenly, protecting the freedoms of association, expression, and assembly, while abusive control uses vague registration denials, funding bans, or selective audits to silence dissent under an integrity pretext. Remedy framing is balance: regulators should target diversion, self-dealing, and deception with due-process safeguards, not broad burdens on compliant NGOs, so accountability does not become a tool for closing civic space.'),
    ('charity_crowdfunding_fraud',
     'Recognizing Crowdfunding and Online Fundraiser Fraud',
     'GoFundMe Safety, How GoFundMe Protects Donors From Fraudulent Fundraisers (fetched https://www.gofundme.com/c/safety/fraudulent-campaigns); FTC and state consumer alerts',
     'Crowdfunding fraud uses personal fundraising pages to mislead donors, though platforms report it affects a small fraction of campaigns. Red flags: an organizer with a brand-new social account and few followers, no comments from family or close friends of the beneficiary, story text copied from elsewhere or mismatched names, ages, or locations suggesting impersonation, an unresponsive organizer, and requests to route money off-platform via cryptocurrency, gift card, cash, or wire transfer. A page with no named organizer or beneficiary, or an odd look-alike domain, is suspect. Standard practice: give mainly where you know the organizer or beneficiary, verify the parties, and pay by traceable methods. Remedy: report suspected fraudulent fundraisers to the platform, which can suspend campaigns and may offer a refund guarantee where fraud is proven, and notify the relevant regulator.'),
    ('charity_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement Against Charity Misuse',
     'California DOJ Office of the Attorney General, Charities (fetched https://oag.ca.gov/charities); NAAG Charities Regulation guidance',
     'State attorneys general are the primary enforcers protecting charitable assets, acting against charities and fundraisers that misuse assets or solicit deceptively. Public checks include a searchable registry exposing filings and IRS returns so donors can vet a charity before giving, plus a complaint channel for reporting misconduct. Enforcement remedies include orders to stop soliciting, remove donation links, and notify donors (which need not first prove fraud), plus monetary penalties, injunctive relief, restitution of charitable assets, removal of board members, and involuntary dissolution. For charities formed to benefit insiders, regulators may bar those individuals from future charity work and refer them for prosecution. Donor recourse note: restitution typically restores assets to charitable use, often via cy-pres to a similar purpose, rather than refunding individual donors.'),
    ('media_ad_fraud_recognition',
     'Digital Ad Fraud and Invalid Traffic Recognition (IAB/MRC)',
     'IAB and Media Rating Council, Invalid Traffic (IVT) Detection and Filtration Guidelines Addendum (fetched https://www.iab.com/guidelines/mrc-invalid-traffic-ivt-detection-and-filtration-guidelines-addendum/)',
     'Standard: the MRC and IAB Tech Lab classify non-human or fraudulent ad traffic as Invalid Traffic, split into General Invalid Traffic (GIVT: known bots, spiders, crawlers, non-browser user agents, removable by list-based filtering) and Sophisticated Invalid Traffic (SIVT: hijacked devices, adware, malware, data-center traffic, human fraud farms, requiring advanced analytics and multi-point corroboration). Recognition red flags: impossible engagement (clicks far exceeding human norms), traffic spikes from data centers, near-100 percent click-through, conversions that never transact, and metrics that collapse when bot filters are applied. Remedy: use MRC-accredited measurement and filtration vendors, apply up-front filtration, demand IVT-adjusted reporting, and seek make-goods or refunds for documented invalid impressions.'),
    ('media_domain_spoofing',
     'Domain Spoofing, ads.txt and sellers.json Supply-Chain Integrity',
     'IAB Tech Lab, ads.txt / app-ads.txt / sellers.json Authorized Digital Sellers (fetched https://iabtechlab.com/ads-txt/)',
     "Standard: ads.txt and app-ads.txt let a publisher publicly declare, in a file at the root of its domain, exactly which companies are authorized to sell its inventory; sellers.json plus the OpenRTB SupplyChain object let buyers trace each intermediary back to the originating seller. Together they counter domain spoofing (impersonating a legitimate site) and unauthorized reselling of counterfeit inventory. Recognition red flags: a seller offering inventory for a domain that does not list it in ads.txt; bid requests whose declared domain mismatches the publisher's official declaration; gaps or unknown intermediaries in the supply-chain object; and inventory with no corresponding seller ID. Remedy: buy only against verified ads.txt/sellers.json records, block unauthorized paths, and report counterfeit inventory to the SSP and to IAB Tech Lab."),
    ('media_ftc_endorsement',
     'Undisclosed Paid Endorsements and the FTC Endorsement Guides',
     'U.S. Federal Trade Commission, Disclosures 101 for Social Media Influencers (fetched https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/disclosures-101-social-media-influencers)',
     'Standard: under the FTC Endorsement Guides, an endorser must clearly and conspicuously disclose any material connection to a brand. A material connection includes a financial, employment, personal, or family relationship, and is not limited to cash; free or discounted products, perks, or anything of value triggers disclosure even if no mention was requested. Recognition red flags: disclosures buried in an About page, hidden behind a More link, dropped at the end of a video, mixed into a block of hashtags, or expressed only as vague shorthand like sp, spon, or collab; tags, likes, and pins promoting a brand that omit any relationship notice. Remedy: place the disclosure with the endorsement itself in plain terms such as advertisement, ad, or sponsored; repeat it periodically in livestreams; both brand and endorser bear responsibility, and the FTC can pursue enforcement.'),
    ('media_native_disclosure',
     'Native Advertising and Sponsored-Content Disclosure (FTC)',
     'U.S. Federal Trade Commission, Native Advertising: A Guide for Businesses (fetched https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/native-advertising-guide-businesses)',
     'Standard: the FTC Act makes it deceptive to mislead consumers about the commercial nature of content. An ad is deceptive if it promotes a product yet is not readily identifiable as an ad, including any express or implied suggestion it comes from a party other than the sponsoring advertiser. The more a native ad mimics surrounding editorial in format and topic, the more a clear and prominent disclosure is required, both on the publisher page and on the click-into page. Recognition red flags: paid articles styled as independent journalism, deceptive door-opener headlines, content-recommendation widgets labeled From Around the Web, and ambiguous tags like Promoted. Remedy: disclose with understood terms (Ad, Advertisement, Paid Advertisement, Sponsored Advertising Content) placed in front of or above the headline; agencies and ad networks share liability.'),
    ('media_fake_reviews',
     'Fake Reviews, Astroturfing and the FTC Reviews Rule',
     'U.S. Federal Trade Commission, Final Rule Banning Fake Reviews and Testimonials, 16 CFR Part 465 (fetched https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/08/federal-trade-commission-announces-final-rule-banning-fake-reviews-testimonials)',
     'Standard: the FTC rule on consumer reviews and testimonials (16 CFR Part 465, effective 2024) prohibits creating, buying, or selling reviews from people who do not exist (including AI-generated reviews) or who never used the product; buying reviews conditioned on a particular sentiment; undisclosed insider reviews; company-controlled sites posing as independent; suppressing negative reviews while implying the displayed set is representative; and buying or selling fake followers, likes, or views. Recognition red flags: clusters of five-star reviews posted in a short window, near-identical phrasing, reviewers with no purchase history, all-positive ratings with vanished criticism, and sudden follower spikes. Remedy: the FTC can seek civil penalties per violation; platforms remove fraudulent reviews and inauthentic accounts.'),
    ('media_deceptive_advertising',
     'Deceptive Advertising and the FTC Substantiation Standard',
     'U.S. Federal Trade Commission, Policy Statement Regarding Advertising Substantiation (fetched https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/ftc-policy-statement-regarding-advertising-substantiation)',
     'Standard: under Section 5 of the FTC Act, an ad is deceptive if a material representation, omission, or practice is likely to mislead a reasonable consumer. Advertisers must hold a reasonable basis (prior substantiation) for every objective express and implied claim before it is made; for health, safety, and efficacy claims that basis must be competent and reliable scientific evidence, meaning tests or studies conducted objectively by qualified persons and generally accepted to yield accurate results. Recognition red flags: absolute cure or guaranteed-results claims, cherry-picked or fake testimonials standing in for proof, fine-print contradicting the headline, and bait-and-switch pricing. Remedy: the FTC can order claims pulled, require corrective advertising, impose injunctions, and refer matters for penalties; competitors may also challenge claims through self-regulation.'),
    ('media_viewability_mfa',
     'Ad Viewability Standards and Made-for-Advertising Site Recognition',
     'Media Rating Council, Viewable Ad Impression Measurement Guidelines (fetched https://www.iab.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/06/MRC-Viewable-Ad-Impression-Measurement-Guideline.pdf)',
     "Standard: the MRC defines a viewable display impression as at least 50 percent of the ad's pixels in view for a minimum of one continuous second (for large ads above roughly 242,000 pixels, 30 percent for one second); video requires 50 percent in view for at least two continuous seconds. Made-for-advertising (MFA) sites are low-quality, ad-cluttered pages built to harvest arbitraged traffic rather than serve readers. Recognition red flags: pages dense with auto-refreshing or stacked ad slots, thin or aggregated content, heavy paid-traffic sourcing, slideshow pagination, and viewability or attention metrics far below benchmarks. Remedy: buy against MRC-accredited viewability measurement, apply MFA exclusion lists and curated inventory, and shift spend toward verified, attention-quality placements."),
    ('media_brand_safety',
     'Brand Safety, Suitability and Misinformation Adjacency',
     'World Federation of Advertisers / Global Alliance for Responsible Media, Brand Safety Floor and Suitability Framework (fetched https://wfanet.org/knowledge/item/2022/06/21/GARM-announces-guidelines-on-misinformation-standards-on-ad-placements-and-expansion-to-cover-the-metaverse)',
     'Standard: brand safety frameworks distinguish a Brand Safety Floor (content never suitable for any monetization, such as illegal, exploitative, or harmful material) from a Suitability scale that lets advertisers set risk tolerance across sensitive categories, with explicit guidance on demonetizing misinformation and on ad placement adjacency in feeds, stories, and in-stream formats. Recognition red flags: ads served beside misinformation, hate, graphic, or extremist content; opaque placement reports; and adjacency in user-generated streams with weak controls. Remedy: apply standardized content categories and adjacency controls, use independent verification and pre-bid blocklists, demand placement-level transparency, and withhold spend from non-compliant inventory. (GARM was discontinued in 2024; its floor and suitability definitions remain a referenced baseline.)'),
    ('media_affiliate_disclosure',
     'Affiliate-Marketing Disclosure Compliance (FTC)',
     "U.S. Federal Trade Commission, The FTC's Endorsement Guides: What People Are Asking (fetched https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/ftcs-endorsement-guides-what-people-are-asking)",
     'Standard: when a publisher earns a commission on sales made through a link, that payment is a material connection that must be disclosed clearly and conspicuously near the recommendation and close to the affiliate link, because it can affect the weight consumers give the endorsement. Recognition red flags: commission disclosures relegated to a footer, sidebar, separate Disclosure page, or profile bio rather than beside the link; vague labels such as commissionable link or bare affiliate link that consumers may not understand; and review or ranking content that hides its pay-per-sale incentive. Remedy: use plain language such as I earn a commission on purchases made through links in this post, place it adjacent to the link, keep it visible without clicking through, and ensure any product claims remain truthful and substantiated.'),
    ('media_political_ad_transparency',
     'Political-Ad Transparency and Disclaimer Rules (FEC)',
     'U.S. Federal Election Commission, Advertising and Disclaimers (fetched https://www.fec.gov/help-candidates-and-committees/advertising-and-disclaimers/)',
     "Standard: FEC rules require a clear and conspicuous Paid for by disclaimer on public political communications, including ads placed for a fee on another person's website, app, or platform. The disclaimer must name who paid; if not authorized by a candidate it must give the payor's full name plus a permanent address or website and state it was not authorized by any candidate. Video disclaimers must be visible at least four seconds without user action; an adapted disclaimer with an indicator and one-click mechanism is allowed only when space is genuinely constrained. Recognition red flags: paid political messaging with no payor identified, vague or shell-named sponsors, and missing not-authorized statements. Remedy: require complete disclaimers, verify sponsor identity, and report non-compliant ads to the FEC and the hosting platform."),
    ('media_dark_patterns',
     'Subscription Dark Patterns and Negative-Option Marketing (FTC)',
     "U.S. Federal Trade Commission, Click to Cancel: The FTC's Amended Negative Option Rule (fetched https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/blog/2024/10/click-cancel-ftcs-amended-negative-option-rule-what-it-means-your-business)",
     "Standard: the FTC's negative-option framework requires sellers of recurring subscriptions to clearly and conspicuously disclose material terms before collecting billing information, obtain the consumer's express informed consent to the recurring charge, and provide a simple cancellation path that is at least as easy as sign-up and offered through the same medium used to enroll. Recognition red flags (dark patterns): hidden auto-renewal terms, pre-checked consent boxes, free trials that silently convert to paid, cancellation hidden behind mandatory phone calls or maze-like menus, and confirm-shaming. Remedy: file complaints with the FTC and state consumer agencies, dispute unauthorized charges, and seek refunds. (Note: a 2024 click-to-cancel rule provision was vacated by a 2025 appellate ruling on procedural grounds; the underlying deception and consent standards still apply.)"),
    ('media_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: FTC Orders, NAD Self-Regulation, Platform Action',
     'BBB National Programs, National Advertising Division (NAD) Challenge Process (fetched https://bbbprograms.org/programs/advertising/nad/challenges)',
     'Standard: advertising integrity is enforced on multiple tracks. The industry self-regulatory body, the National Advertising Division (NAD), reviews the truth and accuracy of national advertising via competitor challenges or its own monitoring; if an advertiser declines to comply with NAD recommendations, the case is referred to the FTC and other agencies. The FTC, in turn, can issue cease-and-desist and consent orders, require corrective advertising, and seek civil penalties under specific rules. Recognition cues that a remedy path exists: a documented deceptive claim, undisclosed material connection, fake-review pattern, or invalid-traffic loss. Remedy: challenge the claim at NAD, file an FTC complaint, pursue platform takedowns and ad-policy enforcement, demand make-goods or refunds from media partners, and, where applicable, support competitor or class actions.'),
    ('pharma_off_label_promotion',
     'Off-Label Promotion as Misbranding and False Claims Act Liability',
     'Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (misbranding) + False Claims Act, 31 U.S.C. 3729(a)(1)(A); Berger Montague, Off-Label Marketing as Misbranding, https://bergermontague.com/off-label-marketing-misbranding/',
     'Authority: The FDCA limits manufacturer promotion to FDA-approved uses; marketing a drug for unapproved indications renders it misbranded. When such promotion induces prescriptions reimbursed by Medicare or Medicaid, the manufacturer can cause false claims and incur False Claims Act liability, often surfaced by qui tam whistleblowers (frequently marketing staff). Red flags to recognize: sales scripts pushing unapproved indications, doses, or populations; ghostwritten journal articles; sham advisory boards; and incentive structures tied to off-label volume. Documented resolutions include multi-hundred-million to multi-billion dollar settlements. Remedy: report internally and to FDA/DOJ; civil and criminal penalties, restitution, and corporate integrity agreements follow. This is recognition guidance only, not promotional instruction.'),
    ('pharma_dscsa_supply_chain',
     'Drug Supply Chain Security Act Track-and-Trace Integrity Controls',
     'Drug Supply Chain Security Act, section 582 of the FD&C Act (Title II of the Drug Quality and Security Act); FDA, Drug Supply Chain Security Act (DSCSA), https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-supply-chain-integrity/drug-supply-chain-security-act-dscsa',
     'Authority: DSCSA (section 582 FD&C Act) builds an interoperable, electronic, package-level system to identify and trace prescription drugs. Trading partners must exchange transaction information, history, and a statement; manufacturers and repackagers serialize each package with a product identifier (GTIN/NDC, serial number, lot, expiration) in human- and machine-readable form. Red flags of an illegitimate or suspect product: missing or mismatched transaction data, broken chain of ownership, unauthorized trading partners, altered or unreadable 2D barcodes, and pedigree that fails verification. Remedy: quarantine suspect product, run verification with trading partners, and notify FDA within 24 hours after determining a product is illegitimate. Recognition guidance only.'),
    ('pharma_gmp_violations',
     'Recognizing Current Good Manufacturing Practice Violations in Drugs',
     'Current Good Manufacturing Practice, 21 CFR Parts 210-211; FDA Data Integrity and Compliance With Drug CGMP guidance and Warning Letters, https://www.fda.gov/inspections-compliance-enforcement-and-criminal-investigations/compliance-actions-and-activities/warning-letters',
     'Authority: 21 CFR 210-211 sets CGMP for finished pharmaceuticals; a drug not made under CGMP is adulterated under the FDCA. Data integrity is central: 211.180 requires retention of original or true-copy records, and all data including failing, passing, and suspect results must be preserved and reviewable. Red flags an observer can recognize: missing or disabled audit trails, shared or reused logins, backdated or rewritten batch records, discarded out-of-specification results, uncontrolled retesting into compliance, and unsanitary or unqualified facilities. These recur in FDA warning letters and Form 483 observations. Remedy: warning letters, import alerts, recalls, consent decrees, and injunctions. Recognition guidance only, not a manufacturing how-to.'),
    ('pharma_pharmacovigilance',
     'Pharmacovigilance Failures and Adverse-Event Underreporting',
     'Postmarketing reporting of adverse drug experiences, 21 CFR 314.80; Legal Information Institute mirror, https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/314.80',
     'Authority: 21 CFR 314.80 requires approved-drug applicants to promptly review adverse drug experiences from all domestic and foreign sources and to submit serious, unexpected events as 15-day Alert Reports, plus periodic reports and 10-year recordkeeping with written surveillance procedures. Red flags of pharmacovigilance failure: serious events never filed or filed late, downgrading serious cases to non-serious, suppressing foreign or literature signals, no written intake procedures, and selective coding that hides a safety pattern. Underreporting is severe, with estimates that only a small fraction of serious events reach FDA. Remedy: a reporting failure can support FDA withdrawal of approval and FDCA enforcement; report through MedWatch. Recognition guidance only.'),
    ('pharma_pricing_awp_fraud',
     'Average Wholesale Price and Reported-Pricing Fraud Recognition',
     'Anti-Kickback Statute + False Claims Act applied to inflated AWP/ASP reporting; Berger Montague, McKesson AWP/ASP Settlement, https://bergermontague.com/another-asp-case-resolved-mckesson-corporation-settles-false-claims-act-lawsuit-for-190-million/',
     'Authority: Government programs set reimbursement off reported benchmarks such as Average Wholesale Price and Average Sales Price. Reporting an inflated AWP, then promoting the gap between the inflated benchmark and the real acquisition cost (marketing the spread) to drive purchasing, can violate the Anti-Kickback Statute and cause false claims under the False Claims Act. Red flags to recognize: a wide unexplained spread between reported AWP and actual cost, spread used as a sales pitch, and pricing data feeds that diverge from invoices. Documented settlements include a publisher/distributor FCA resolution exceeding 190 million dollars. Remedy: FCA recovery, restitution, and integrity obligations. Recognition guidance only, not a pricing scheme manual.'),
    ('pharma_device_clearance_integrity',
     'Medical-Device 510(k)/PMA Clearance Integrity and Misrepresentation',
     'Premarket review under the FD&C Act (Medical Device Amendments of 1976); FDA, Medical Device Safety and the 510(k) Clearance Process, https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/510k-clearances/medical-device-safety-and-510k-clearance-process',
     'Authority: Devices reach market via risk-based pathways: PMA (independent demonstration of safety and effectiveness for high-risk devices), De Novo, or 510(k) (substantial equivalence to a legally marketed predicate). Integrity depends on truthful submissions, accurate predicate claims, and complete test data. Red flags of fraudulent clearance: claiming substantial equivalence while concealing material technological differences, citing an inappropriate or ineligible predicate, omitting failing bench or clinical data, or marketing beyond the cleared intended use. FDA may reclassify a type to class III, remove predicates, and require a PMA when safety concerns emerge. Remedy: refusal or rescission of clearance, recalls, warning letters, and FDCA misbranding/adulteration enforcement. Recognition guidance only.'),
    ('pharma_clinical_data_integrity',
     'Clinical-Data Integrity and the FDA Application Integrity Policy',
     'FDA Application Integrity Policy (AIP); FDA, Application Integrity Policy, https://www.fda.gov/inspections-compliance-enforcement-and-criminal-investigations/compliance-actions-and-activities/application-integrity-policy',
     'Authority: The AIP governs the integrity of data in applications submitted for FDA review. It is triggered by wrongful acts such as untrue statements of material fact or patterns of error producing widespread data-integrity problems, and data may be deemed unreliable through negligence, incompetence, or systemic failure. Red flags to recognize: fabricated or copied source data, impossible or duplicated subject records, undisclosed protocol deviations, and pressure to alter study results. Under the AIP, FDA defers substantive scientific review pending a validity assessment and may refuse or withdraw approval. Remedy: the applicant must complete a corrective-action plan, replace false data via a new application, and certify truthfulness; criminal exposure can follow. Recognition guidance only.'),
    ('pharma_counterfeit_supply',
     'Counterfeit and Substandard Medicines in the Legal Supply Chain',
     'WHO definitions of substandard and falsified (SF) medical products (70th World Health Assembly, 2017); WHO Fact Sheet, Substandard and falsified medical products, https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/substandard-and-falsified-medical-products',
     "Authority: WHO distinguishes substandard products (failing quality specifications from poor manufacturing or quality control) from falsified products (deliberately misrepresenting identity, composition, or source). At least one in ten medicines in low- and middle-income countries is affected, causing treatment failure, poisoning, and accelerated antimicrobial resistance. These products enter legitimate channels through long supply chains with many intermediaries, online sellers, and weak oversight. Red flags to recognize: purchase from unauthorized sources, prices far below market, packaging irregularities, and broken pedigree. Remedy: detection via track-and-trace and laboratory testing, plus reporting through WHO's Global Surveillance and Monitoring System and national regulators. Recognition guidance only."),
    ('pharma_prescriber_kickbacks',
     'Kickbacks to Prescribers Under the Anti-Kickback and Sunshine Acts',
     'Anti-Kickback Statute (42 U.S.C. 1320a-7b) + Physician Payments Sunshine Act / CMS Open Payments; DOJ, Takeda Settlement on Improper Payments to Physicians, https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/takeda-agrees-pay-136m-resolve-false-claims-allegations-relating-improper-payments',
     'Authority: The Anti-Kickback Statute bars offering or paying anything of value to induce referrals or use of items covered by federal health programs; resulting claims can violate the False Claims Act. The Sunshine Act created CMS Open Payments, a public database of manufacturer payments to physicians and teaching hospitals. Red flags to recognize: sham speaker programs with no real educational content, lavish meals, travel, or honoraria correlated with prescribing volume, consulting fees with no deliverable, and unreported or mischaracterized transfers of value. Documented resolutions span tens to hundreds of millions of dollars. Remedy: FCA recovery, criminal penalties, exclusion, and corporate integrity agreements; cross-check Open Payments. Recognition guidance only.'),
    ('pharma_compounding_safety',
     'Recognizing Compounding-Pharmacy Safety Failures (503A/503B)',
     'Compounding Quality Act / Drug Quality and Security Act (2013), FD&C Act sections 503A and 503B; FDA Compounding Program presentation, https://www.fda.gov/media/156360/download',
     'Authority: Section 503A covers state-licensed pharmacy compounding; section 503B covers FDA-registered outsourcing facilities making sterile drugs under CGMP. The framework followed the 2012 New England Compounding Center fungal meningitis outbreak, with more than 750 illnesses across 20 states and over 60 deaths from contaminated injectable steroids. Red flags to recognize: non-sterile conditions, failed environmental and air/surface monitoring, absent sterility or endotoxin testing, compounding copies of commercially available drugs, and shipping interstate without proper registration. Sterile injectables carry the highest patient risk including infection and sepsis. Remedy: FDA inspection, recalls, warning letters, and shutdown of unsafe operations. Recognition guidance only, not a compounding procedure.'),
    ('pharma_device_recall_concealment',
     'Device-Recall and Safety-Signal Concealment Recognition',
     'Medical Device Reporting, 21 CFR Part 803, and corrections/removals reporting, 21 CFR Part 806, under the FD&C Act; FDA, Medical Device Reporting (MDR), https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/medical-device-safety/medical-device-reporting-mdr-how-report-medical-device-problems',
     'Authority: Under 21 CFR Part 803, manufacturers and importers must report to FDA, generally within 30 days, when information reasonably suggests a device may have caused or contributed to a death or serious injury, or that a malfunction would likely do so if it recurred; Part 806 requires reporting certain corrections and removals. Red flags of concealment: known malfunctions left unreported, a field correction disguised as routine, a label change made without a required new submission, and patient harm not escalated. Public enforcement has charged manufacturers for concealing malfunctions or deaths and for late or omitted reports. Remedy: criminal misbranding charges, fines, mandated compliance reforms, recalls, and monitoring. Recognition guidance only.'),
    ('pharma_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement: Warning Letters, Recalls, Decrees, Debarment',
     'FDA enforcement under the FD&C Act (warning letters, recalls, injunctions/consent decrees, debarment); FDA Warning Letters, https://www.fda.gov/inspections-compliance-enforcement-and-criminal-investigations/compliance-actions-and-activities/warning-letters',
     'Authority: FDA enforces the FD&C Act through escalating tools. A warning letter cites specific violations and demands corrective action within a deadline; recalls (voluntary, requested, or classified by health hazard) remove unsafe product; consent decrees are court-supervised agreements after repeated or serious violations, often with third-party oversight and operating restrictions; debarment bars individuals or firms from FDA-regulated drug activity after serious convictions. Civil and criminal penalties, restitution, and corporate integrity agreements can accompany DOJ action. Red flags that a remedy is warranted: unresolved Form 483 observations, ignored warning letters, and recurring data-integrity or safety failures. Remedy path: respond, correct, document, and report. Recognition guidance only.'),
    ('biosec_bwc',
     'Biological Weapons Convention (BWC)',
     'Convention on the Prohibition of Biological and Toxin Weapons (BWC), opened for signature 1972, entered into force 26 March 1975; UN Office for Disarmament Affairs (fetched https://disarmament.unoda.org/biological-weapons/)',
     'Legal authority: The BWC is the first multilateral treaty banning an entire category of weapons of mass destruction. It prohibits the development, production, acquisition, transfer, stockpiling, and use of biological and toxin weapons, and obliges parties to take national measures to prevent misuse. An Implementation Support Unit and confidence-building measures support a network approach across international bodies. Recognition red flags: biological work without a peaceful, prophylactic, or protective justification; no national implementing legislation; refusal to file confidence-building declarations; or capabilities with no legitimate civilian rationale. Remedy: adopt national prohibition law, designate a point of contact, submit annual confidence-building measures, and raise unresolved compliance concerns through consultative meetings and, ultimately, the UN Security Council.'),
    ('biosec_durc',
     'Dual Use Research of Concern (DURC) Oversight Policy',
     'United States Government Policy for Oversight of Dual Use Research of Concern and Pathogens with Enhanced Pandemic Potential (May 2024); NIH Office of Science Policy (fetched https://osp.od.nih.gov/us-government-releases-policy-for-oversight-of-dual-use-research-of-concern-and-pathogens-with-enhanced-pandemic-potential/)',
     'Legal authority: DURC is life-sciences research that could reasonably be anticipated to provide knowledge or technologies readily misapplied to threaten public health, agriculture, the environment, or national security. The 2024 US policy unified three prior frameworks into Category 1 (DURC) and Category 2 (enhanced-pandemic-pathogen) oversight, requiring an Institutional Review Entity plus federal funding-agency review and risk-mitigation plans. Recognition red flags: research with listed agents and high-consequence experimental effects proceeding without institutional review; absence of a risk-benefit assessment; or attempts to bypass the funding-agency review tier. Remedy: route covered research to the institutional review entity, document a risk-mitigation plan, secure funding-agency concurrence before work begins, and report changes that raise the risk profile.'),
    ('biosec_gain_of_function',
     'Gain-of-Function and Enhanced-Pandemic-Pathogen Oversight (P3CO)',
     'Potential Pandemic Pathogen Care and Oversight (P3CO) framework, consolidated into the 2024 USG DURC-PEPP policy; Council on Strategic Risks analysis (fetched https://councilonstrategicrisks.org/2024/05/07/welcome-elements-of-the-new-us-policy-update-on-durc/) and CRS R47114 (fetched https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R47114)',
     'Legal authority: Oversight of research reasonably anticipated to enhance a pathogen toward enhanced pandemic potential is governed through Category 2 review. The former P3CO framework, now folded into the 2024 USG policy, adds a multidisciplinary department-level review above institutional review, with a Secretary-level waiver required to expedite work during a declared emergency. Periodic four-year policy review keeps scope current. Recognition red flags: enhancement work funded without department-level P3CO review; reliance on naturally-occurring exemptions to avoid scrutiny; or undisclosed transmissibility or virulence changes. Remedy: submit covered proposals for pre-funding department-level review, document containment and benefit justification, and pause work pending review rather than proceeding under an unverified exemption.'),
    ('biosec_biosafety_levels',
     'Laboratory Biosafety Levels and the WHO Laboratory Biosafety Manual',
     'WHO Laboratory Biosafety Manual, 4th edition (2020), risk-based approach; World Health Organization (fetched https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240011311)',
     'Legal authority: The WHO Laboratory Biosafety Manual sets the global reference for safe handling of biological agents. The 4th edition moves from prescriptive containment tiers toward an evidence- and risk-based model, balancing core requirements, heightened control measures, and maximum-containment measures against an agent- and activity-specific risk assessment, supported by monographs on facility design, decontamination, and program management. Recognition red flags: work assigned a containment level without a documented risk assessment; missing standard operating procedures or competency training; biosafety cabinets uncertified; or a facility operating above its design capability. Remedy: conduct and record a risk assessment, match controls to assessed risk, certify engineering controls, train and credential personnel, and convene local oversight before raising the scope of work.'),
    ('biosec_select_agents',
     'Pathogen and Select-Agent Security (US Federal Select Agent Program)',
     'Federal Select Agent Program (FSAP), 42 CFR Part 73, 7 CFR Part 331, 9 CFR Part 121; CDC and APHIS (fetched https://www.selectagents.gov/overview/index.htm)',
     'Legal authority: FSAP, jointly run by CDC and APHIS under post-2001 bioterrorism law, regulates possession, use, and transfer of 63 biological agents and toxins that could severely threaten public, animal, or plant health. Registered entities must keep biosafety, security, and incident-response plans; individuals undergo an FBI security risk assessment before access; Tier 1 agents carry heightened physical-security standards. Recognition red flags: possession of a listed agent by an unregistered entity; access by an individual without an approved risk assessment; unexplained inventory loss; or a release not promptly reported. Remedy: register the entity, complete personnel suitability screening, secure inventory, and immediately report any theft, loss, or release so corrective action, inspection, and enforcement can follow.'),
    ('biosec_australia_group',
     'Australia Group Export Controls for Biological Items',
     'The Australia Group (informal multilateral export-control arrangement established 1984), Common Control Lists; Australian DFAT Secretariat (fetched https://www.dfat.gov.au/publications/minisite/theaustraliagroupnet/site/en/index.html)',
     'Legal authority: The Australia Group is an informal forum of participants who harmonize national export controls so exports do not contribute to chemical or biological weapons, reinforcing the BWC and CWC. Participants apply common control lists for human, animal, and plant pathogens and toxins, and for dual-use biological equipment, technology, and software. Recognition red flags: attempts at venue shopping across suppliers to avoid licensing; orders for listed pathogens or equipment with no declared legitimate end use; transfers to entities on sanctions or denied-party lists; or mismatched end-user documentation. Remedy: apply common-control-list screening, require export licenses where listed, verify end-user legitimacy, and refuse or report transfers that fail screening to national authorities.'),
    ('biosec_dna_synthesis_screening',
     'Synthetic-Biology and DNA-Synthesis Order Screening (IGSC)',
     'International Gene Synthesis Consortium (IGSC) Harmonized Screening Protocol v3.0 (3 September 2024); IGSC (fetched https://genesynthesisconsortium.org/wp-content/uploads/IGSC-Harmonized-Screening-Protocol-v3.0-1.pdf)',
     'Legal authority: IGSC members, who represent a majority of global commercial gene-synthesis capacity, voluntarily screen both ordered sequences and customers to prevent misuse of synthetic nucleic acids, aligning with US Screening Framework Guidance, the 2024 OSTP Nucleic Acid Synthesis Screening Framework, and Executive Order 14110. Sequences are matched against a Restricted Pathogen Database; flagged orders go to human expert review; records are retained at least eight years. Recognition red flags: orders for sequences of concern with no bona fide peaceful use; customers refusing to verify identity or institution; residential or PO-box shipping; or venue shopping to evade screening. Remedy: verify customer legitimacy, screen sequences down to the 50-base-pair threshold, refuse and report suspicious orders to authorities, and retain screening records for audit.'),
    ('biosec_incident_recognition',
     'Biosecurity Incident and Lab-Safety-Failure Recognition',
     'GAO, High-Containment Laboratories oversight findings (GAO-16-642 and GAO-14-785T); U.S. Government Accountability Office (fetched https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-16-642)',
     'Legal authority: Federal reviews of US high-containment (BSL-3 and BSL-4) laboratories found oversight fragmented and largely self-policing, with no single agency responsible for aggregate risk and no national standards for design, commissioning, and operation. Accidents, while rare, recur primarily from human error and management or equipment failure, and incident referral criteria were inconsistent. Recognition red flags: laboratory-acquired infections, spills, needlesticks, incomplete pathogen inactivation, or inventory gaps that go unreported; gaps in mandatory theft, loss, or release reporting; and absence of a written incident-handling process. Remedy: report incidents promptly through the responsible program, apply consistent referral criteria, conduct root-cause review, implement corrective actions, and strengthen independent oversight and national operating standards.'),
    ('biosec_ihr',
     'International Health Regulations (IHR) and Outbreak Reporting',
     'International Health Regulations (2005), legally binding on 196 States Parties; World Health Organization (fetched https://www.who.int/health-topics/international-health-regulations)',
     'Legal authority: The IHR (2005) bind 196 States Parties to prevent, detect, assess, report, and respond to public health risks that may spread across borders. Parties must designate a National IHR Focal Point, build core surveillance and response capacities including at points of entry, and notify WHO of events that may constitute a public health emergency of international concern under the Annex 2 decision instrument. Recognition red flags: a potential cross-border health event detected but not assessed or notified; missing or untested core capacities; or refusal to verify information WHO receives from other sources. Remedy: assess events with the Annex 2 instrument, notify WHO through the focal point within required timelines, verify external reports, and request technical assistance to close core-capacity gaps.'),
    ('biosec_biorisk_management',
     'Biorisk-Management Standard (ISO 35001)',
     'ISO 35001:2019, Biorisk management for laboratories and other related organisations (with Amd 1:2024); International Organization for Standardization (fetched https://www.iso.org/standard/71293.html)',
     'Legal authority: ISO 35001 defines a management-system process to identify, assess, control, and monitor risks from hazardous biological materials for any organization that works with, stores, transports, or disposes of them, and is built to complement existing laboratory standards and regulatory frameworks. It applies a plan-do-check-act cycle linking leadership, risk assessment, operational control, performance evaluation, and continual improvement. Recognition red flags: a laboratory with no documented biorisk-management system; controls not tied to assessed risk; no internal audit or management review; or corrective actions never closed. Remedy: establish a biorisk-management system with defined roles, conduct risk assessments and set proportionate controls, audit performance, and drive continual improvement so safety and security keep pace with the work.'),
    ('biosec_responsible_publication',
     'Responsible-Science and Dual-Use Publication Norms',
     'National Science Advisory Board for Biosecurity (NSABB) guidance on responsible communication of dual-use research; NIH Office of Science Policy (fetched https://osp.od.nih.gov/policies/national-science-advisory-board-for-biosecurity-nsabb/)',
     'Legal authority: NSABB advises the US government and the research enterprise on the conduct, oversight, and responsible communication of dual-use research. It frames an ethical duty for authors, institutions, funders, and journals to weigh whether knowledge, data, models, or methods proposed for publication could be misapplied to cause harm, and supports risk-benefit review and structured-access paradigms for sensitive datasets and tools. Recognition red flags: manuscripts or repositories disclosing readily misusable methods without a biosecurity review; no institutional point of contact for dual-use concerns; or unconditional release of high-risk models or sequences. Remedy: conduct dual-use review before submission, redact or stage genuinely sensitive operational detail, use structured access for high-risk artifacts, and document the risk-benefit rationale for communication decisions.'),
    ('biosec_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Oversight: BWC Confidence-Building and Institutional Biosafety Committees',
     'BWC confidence-building measures (UNODA, fetched https://disarmament.unoda.org/biological-weapons/) and NIH Guidelines Institutional Biosafety Committee oversight (NIH OSP, fetched https://osp.od.nih.gov/policies/biosafety-and-biosecurity-policy/)',
     'Legal authority: Oversight runs from the international down to the local level. Under the BWC, parties exchange annual confidence-building measures and resolve concerns through consultation; at the institutional level, NIH Guidelines require an Institutional Biosafety Committee (IBC) to review recombinant and synthetic nucleic acid work, with minutes increasingly public and significant incidents reportable to oversight within set timelines. Recognition red flags: no IBC or an IBC that does not meet or minute its reviews; incidents in higher-containment work not immediately reported; or international concerns left unaddressed. Remedy: register and convene an IBC, document and publish review minutes, report exposures and releases promptly, and escalate unresolved state-level concerns through BWC consultative mechanisms toward the UN Security Council.'),
    ('border_smuggling_protocol',
     'UN Protocol against the Smuggling of Migrants: scope and migrant safeguards',
     'Protocol against the Smuggling of Migrants by Land, Sea and Air, supplementing UNTOC (2000), Arts 2, 3, 4, 6 - fetched https://www.unodc.org/documents/middleeastandnorthafrica/smuggling-migrants/SoM_Protocol_English.pdf',
     "The Smuggling of Migrants Protocol is the only global instrument addressing migrant smuggling. Article 3(a) defines smuggling as procuring, for financial or other material benefit, the illegal entry of a person into a State of which they are not a national or resident. The Protocol's stated purpose (Art 2) is to combat smuggling while protecting the rights of smuggled migrants. Recognition framing: the offence targets organized criminal groups (Art 4), not migrants. Article 6 criminalizes the smuggler, document fraud done to enable smuggling, and treats endangering life or inhuman or degrading treatment as aggravating circumstances. Protection and remedy: smuggled persons are crime objects owed humane treatment, not offenders to be punished for being smuggled."),
    ('border_smuggling_vs_trafficking',
     'Distinguishing migrant smuggling from human trafficking: consent and exploitation',
     'UNODC, Migrant Smuggling FAQs (How is migrant smuggling different from human trafficking?) - fetched https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/human-trafficking/faqs-migrant-smuggling.html',
     "Smuggling and trafficking are distinct but interconnected crimes. Smuggling is a crime against a State's borders: the smuggler profits from facilitating illegal border crossing, the relationship typically ends on arrival, and it is by definition transnational. Trafficking is a crime against a person: it aims at ongoing exploitation, can occur inside one country, and any apparent consent is void where coercion, deception, or abuse is present. Red flag for recognition: a person who agreed to be smuggled can become a trafficking victim when later deceived, threatened, or forced into exploitation, for example forced labour to repay transport debt. Protection response: screen every smuggled person for trafficking indicators so victims are identified and referred to assistance, never penalized."),
    ('border_document_visa_fraud',
     'Recognizing document and visa fraud at borders: impostor, FOG, and counterfeit',
     'Frontex, Document fraud (typologies); Protocol against Smuggling of Migrants Art 3(c) on fraudulent travel or identity documents - fetched https://www.frontex.europa.eu/what-we-do/fighting-crime/document-fraud/',
     'The Smuggling Protocol (Art 3(c)) defines a fraudulent travel or identity document as one falsely made or altered, improperly obtained through misrepresentation, corruption, or duress, or used by someone other than the rightful holder. Frontex identifies recurring fraud types: an impostor presents a genuine document belonging to a look-alike; a fraudulently obtained but genuine (FOG) document carries false data or a swapped portrait; counterfeits are full fakes. Recognition red flags: real documents obtained under false pretences, breeder documents (fake birth or marriage certificates) used to get genuine papers. Protection framing: document checks should detect organized facilitators while not penalizing coerced migrants; shared reference libraries such as FADO support first-line officers.'),
    ('border_corruption_recognition',
     'Border-control corruption as a facilitator of migrant smuggling',
     'UNODC, Issue Paper - Corruption and the Smuggling of Migrants (2013); Migrant Smuggling FAQs - fetched https://www.unodc.org/documents/human-trafficking/2013/The_Role_Of_Corruption_in_the_Smuggling_of_Migrants_Issue_Paper_UNODC_2013.pdf',
     'UNODC finds that most smuggling operations could not persist without corruption. Bribery of customs, visa, and border-control officials neutralizes immigration controls and lets smugglers sell a reliable service at a premium. Recognition red flags: officials directly involved in theft or falsification of documents, blank passports or visas diverted to the black market, selective non-enforcement at specific crossing points, and unexplained official wealth. Integrity safeguard: corruption is treated as an enabler of transnational organized crime, so anti-smuggling work must pair border enforcement with anti-corruption controls, asset tracing, and protection of whistleblowers. Protection framing: corruption harms migrants by exposing them to violence and exploitation, so accountability is part of remedy.'),
    ('border_migrant_protection',
     'Migrant protection and non-refoulement under the 1951 Refugee Convention',
     'Convention relating to the Status of Refugees (1951), Arts 31 and 33; Smuggling Protocol saving clause Art 19(1) - fetched https://www.unhcr.org/about-unhcr/overview/1951-refugee-convention',
     "Non-refoulement (1951 Convention Art 33) prohibits returning a refugee to territories where their life or freedom would be threatened; it binds States as customary law and does not depend on lawful residence. Article 31 bars penalizing refugees for irregular entry when coming directly from danger. The Smuggling Protocol's saving clause (Art 19) expressly preserves these obligations and forbids discriminatory treatment of smuggled persons. Recognition red flag: a border practice that summarily expels or pushes back people without examining protection claims violates this floor. Protection and remedy: anyone intercepted at a border must have access to an individual asylum or protection assessment before any return, and the principle of non-refoulement overrides border-enforcement convenience."),
    ('border_carrier_api',
     'Carrier sanctions and advance passenger information integrity at borders',
     'WCO/IATA/ICAO Guidelines on Advance Passenger Information (API); ICAO Doc 9944 PNR; Smuggling Protocol Art 11 - fetched https://www.iata.org/contentassets/18a5fdb2dc144d619a8c10dc1472ae80/api-guidelines-main-text_2014.pdf',
     'The Smuggling Protocol (Art 11) asks States to require commercial carriers to verify that passengers hold the travel documents needed for entry, with sanctions for breach. Internationally standardized Advance Passenger Information (API) and Passenger Name Record (PNR) data let border agencies screen travellers before boarding and against sanctions and watch lists. Integrity red flags: carriers boarding passengers with obviously deficient documents, or API data that is incomplete, altered, or not transmitted. Protection framing: carrier and API controls aim at organized facilitation and identity fraud, but must respect data-protection limits and must not be used to block bona fide asylum seekers; non-refoulement and the right to seek protection still apply at the border.'),
    ('border_smuggling_finance',
     'Following migrant-smuggling financial flows (FATF guidance)',
     'FATF, Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing Risks Arising from Migrant Smuggling (2022) - fetched https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Methodsandtrends/Migrant-smuggling.html',
     'FATF estimates migrant-smuggling proceeds exceed USD 10 billion per year, yet many States do not treat it as a high money-laundering risk and rarely investigate the flows. Recognition red flags: heavy use of cash, avoidance of formal banking, value moved through hawala and other informal channels, and proceeds integrated into legitimate-looking shops, travel agencies, and transport companies; profits are reinvested in real estate and high-value goods in origin and destination countries. Enforcement and remedy: FATF urges authorities to proactively follow the money, trace and seize criminal assets, and cooperate across borders and with the private sector. Following finance disrupts the profit motive that puts migrants in danger and exposes networks that bribe officials.'),
    ('border_vulnerable_minors',
     'Safeguarding unaccompanied minors and vulnerable migrants at borders',
     'Inter-Agency Guiding Principles on Unaccompanied and Separated Children; UNHCR child protection; Smuggling Protocol Art 16(4) - fetched https://migrationnetwork.un.org/resources/inter-agency-guiding-principles-unaccompanied-and-separated-children',
     'The Smuggling Protocol (Art 16) requires protection of smuggled persons and explicitly directs States to account for the special needs of women and children. Guiding principles anchor every decision in the best interests of the child and family unity. Safeguards: appoint an independent guardian for each unaccompanied or separated child as a coordination point, and as a general rule do not detain children. Recognition red flags: children moving with non-relatives, debt owed for transport, or signs of coercion are trafficking warning signs. Protection and remedy: minors get age-appropriate care, best-interests assessment, and alternatives to detention rather than enforcement-first handling, because vulnerable migrants are exposed to deception and abuse by smugglers and others.'),
    ('border_search_rescue',
     'Duty to render assistance and search-and-rescue at sea',
     'UNCLOS Art 98; SOLAS 1974 Reg V/33; Smuggling Protocol Art 9 safeguard clauses - fetched https://www.refworld.org/policy/legalguidance/unhcr/1997/36258',
     "UNCLOS Article 98 obliges States to ensure ship masters render assistance to any person found in danger of being lost and proceed with all speed to those in distress, so far as is reasonable without seriously endangering the rescuers. SOLAS Regulation V/33 imposes the same duty, and these operate alongside the SAR Convention's coordination architecture. The Smuggling Protocol's Article 9 safeguards require ensuring the safety and humane treatment of persons on board during interdiction at sea. Recognition red flag: failing to respond to a vessel in distress, or interdiction that endangers life, breaches the duty. Protection framing: rescue is not optional and is owed regardless of a person's migration status; disembarkation must respect non-refoulement."),
    ('border_return_integrity',
     'Return and readmission integrity: dignity, due process, and remedy',
     'IOM Policy on Return, Readmission and Reintegration; GCM Objective 21; Smuggling Protocol Art 18 - fetched https://www.iom.int/sites/g/files/tmzbdl486/files/documents/ioms-policy-full-spectrum-of-return-readmission-and-reintegration.pdf',
     'The Smuggling Protocol (Art 18) requires returns of smuggled persons to be carried out in an orderly manner with due regard for the safety and dignity of the person. IOM policy and the Global Compact (Objective 21) require due process, individual assessment, and effective remedy, and uphold the prohibition of collective expulsion and of return where there is a real and foreseeable risk of death, torture, or irreparable harm. Recognition red flags: collective or summary expulsions, returns without individual screening, or returns of people with protection or trafficking-victim claims. Protection framing: voluntary return is preferred over forced return, with reintegration support and specific safeguards for migrants in vulnerable situations.'),
    ('border_biometric_systems',
     'Biometric border systems and travel-document data integrity',
     'ICAO Doc 9303 Machine Readable Travel Documents; ICAO Traveller Identification Programme (TRIP) - fetched https://www.icao.int/sites/default/files/publications/DocSeries/9303_p9_cons_en.pdf',
     "ICAO Doc 9303 sets the global standard for electronic machine-readable travel documents (eMRTDs). Integrity rests on verifying that chip data matches the printed page and binds to the holder via the primary biometric, with authenticity confirmed through Passive Authentication and chip-binding mechanisms validated against ICAO's Public Key Directory. The Smuggling Protocol (Arts 12 and 13) likewise requires secure, hard-to-falsify documents and prompt verification of legitimacy on request. Recognition red flags: a chip that fails authentication, data inconsistent between chip and print, or a portrait that does not match the bearer. Protection framing: biometric identity assurance must follow data-protection principles, disclosing only required data, so it strengthens border integrity without exposing travellers to misuse."),
    ('border_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: prosecuting smugglers while protecting migrants',
     'UNODC, Migrant Smuggling FAQs (UNODC response; prosecutions); Smuggling Protocol Arts 5, 6, 16 - fetched https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/human-trafficking/faqs-migrant-smuggling.html',
     "Enforcement targets smugglers, not the smuggled. The Protocol's Article 5 shields migrants from prosecution for the mere fact of being smuggled, while Article 6 obliges States to criminalize smugglers, organizers, and enabling document fraud, with aggravation for endangering life or inhuman treatment. UNODC supports transnational investigations into smuggling rings and the tracing and seizure of illicit proceeds. Recognition red flag: systems that detect and penalize migrants instead of investigating organizers leave the low-risk, high-profit crime intact. Protection and remedy: smuggled persons should be encouraged to cooperate as witnesses and afforded assistance under Article 16, including consular notification on detention and special measures for women and children."),
    ('nuclear_npt',
     'Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons (NPT)',
     'Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons (NPT), INFCIRC/140, entered into force 5 March 1970, indefinitely extended 1995; IAEA (fetched https://www.iaea.org/topics/non-proliferation-treaty)',
     'Legal authority: The NPT is the centrepiece of non-proliferation, with 191 States parties. Article III obliges each non-nuclear-weapon State to conclude a comprehensive safeguards agreement (CSA) with the IAEA so the Agency can verify that nuclear material is not diverted from peaceful uses; Article IV protects peaceful uses; Article VI commits parties to disarmament negotiations. Recognition red flags: a non-nuclear-weapon State with no CSA in force, unexplained gaps between declared and verified material, or claims that civilian status exempts a programme from inspection. Remedy: bring a CSA into force, accept IAEA verification, and resolve discrepancies; unresolved diversion concerns are reported by the IAEA to the UN Security Council and General Assembly.'),
    ('nuclear_iaea_safeguards',
     'IAEA Safeguards and the Additional Protocol',
     'Comprehensive Safeguards Agreements (INFCIRC/153) and Model Additional Protocol (INFCIRC/540, approved 1997); IAEA (fetched https://www.iaea.org/topics/additional-protocol)',
     'Legal authority: IAEA safeguards are technical measures that independently verify nuclear material is used only for peaceful purposes. A CSA covers declared material and facilities; the Additional Protocol, after Iraq and the DPRK exposed gaps, grants the IAEA expanded access to information and locations across the whole fuel cycle to detect undeclared material and activities. As of end-2025 Additional Protocols are in force with 144 States plus Euratom. Recognition red flags: refusal to conclude an Additional Protocol, denied or delayed complementary access, incomplete declarations of mines, imports, or fuel-cycle plans, and obstruction of environmental sampling. Remedy: conclude and implement the Additional Protocol, provide full declarations, and grant access so the IAEA can draw a broader safeguards conclusion on the absence of undeclared activity.'),
    ('nuclear_physical_protection',
     'Physical Protection of Nuclear Material (CPPNM and 2005 Amendment)',
     'Convention on the Physical Protection of Nuclear Material (CPPNM), INFCIRC/274/Rev.1, in force 8 February 1987; 2005 Amendment in force 8 May 2016; IAEA (fetched https://www.iaea.org/publications/documents/conventions/convention-physical-protection-nuclear-material-and-its-amendment)',
     'Legal authority: The CPPNM and its 2005 Amendment are the only legally binding instruments on physical protection of peaceful-use nuclear material and facilities. The original convention covered material in international transport; the Amendment extends binding obligations to domestic use, storage, and transport, criminalizes theft and sabotage offences, and strengthens international cooperation and information sharing. Recognition red flags: facilities without graded physical-protection measures, missing implementing legislation, no criminalization of unlawful taking or sabotage, and no point of contact for recovery cooperation. Remedy: accede to the Amendment, enact implementing law, request an IPPAS peer review, and cooperate on recovery of material lost, stolen, or subject to a credible threat.'),
    ('nuclear_illicit_trafficking',
     'Illicit Trafficking of Nuclear and Radioactive Material (IAEA ITDB)',
     'IAEA Incident and Trafficking Database (ITDB), established 1995; IAEA (fetched https://www.iaea.org/resources/databases/itdb)',
     'Legal authority: The ITDB is the IAEA information system on incidents involving nuclear and other radioactive material outside regulatory control. States report trafficking, theft, unauthorized possession, transfer, loss, discovery of uncontrolled material, and scams offering benign material as radioactive. The data show that unsecured material remains available and that individuals are prepared to traffic it; about 55 percent of thefts reported since 1993 occurred during transport. Recognition red flags: material offered for sale or moved without authorization, missing inventory, undeclared cross-border movement, and inconsistent provenance documents. Remedy: detect at borders, secure recovered material, report the incident to the ITDB and national authorities, and apply CPPNM and Code of Conduct controls to restore regulatory control.'),
    ('nuclear_export_controls',
     'Nuclear Export Controls (Nuclear Suppliers Group and Zangger Committee)',
     'NSG Guidelines, INFCIRC/254 Part 1 (Trigger List) and Part 2 (dual-use); Zangger Committee Trigger List, INFCIRC/209; Nuclear Suppliers Group (fetched https://www.nuclearsuppliersgroup.org/index.php/en/guidelines/nsg-guidelines)',
     "Legal authority: The NSG's participating governments apply two control lists, the Trigger List of items especially designed or prepared for nuclear use and a dual-use list of items that could contribute to an unsafeguarded fuel cycle or explosive activity. Transfers require recipient comprehensive safeguards and physical protection; dual-use transfers are barred where diversion risk is unacceptable. The Zangger Committee maintains a complementary trigger list interpreting NPT Article III.2 export conditions. Recognition red flags: end-users that cannot justify a civilian use, transshipment through weak-control jurisdictions, false end-user certificates, and orders mismatched to declared activity. Remedy: deny or condition the licence, require IAEA safeguards as a condition of supply, and report suspected diversion to national enforcement authorities."),
    ('nuclear_source_security',
     'Radioactive Source Security and Orphan Sources (Code of Conduct)',
     'IAEA Code of Conduct on the Safety and Security of Radioactive Sources (2004) with Import/Export and Disused-Sources guidance; IAEA (fetched https://www.iaea.org/topics/codes-of-conduct and https://www.iaea.org/topics/disused-sources)',
     'Legal authority: The Code of Conduct is a non-legally-binding instrument with political support from more than 130 States; it helps regulators keep sealed sources within a cradle-to-grave control system, supplemented by import/export and disused-source guidance. An orphan source is one never under, or removed from, regulatory control by abandonment, loss, theft, or unauthorized transfer; disused sources no longer serve their authorized practice. Sources are graded Category 1 to 5 by potential harm. Recognition red flags: sources missing from inventory, transfers without proper authorization, abandoned high-activity devices, and no national register or import/export controls. Remedy: recover and secure orphan sources, return them to use or manage as disused, maintain a national inventory, and apply graded security per the Code.'),
    ('nuclear_safety_standards',
     'Nuclear Safety Standards (Convention on Nuclear Safety)',
     'Convention on Nuclear Safety (CNS), adopted 17 June 1994; related Joint Convention and Early Notification / Assistance Conventions; IAEA (fetched https://www.iaea.org/topics/nuclear-safety-conventions)',
     'Legal authority: The Convention on Nuclear Safety commits Contracting Parties operating land-based civil nuclear power plants to maintain a high level of safety through fundamental safety principles, enforced via a peer-review reporting process rather than sanctions. The Joint Convention extends similar principles to spent fuel and radioactive waste; the 1986 Early Notification and Assistance Conventions establish the international emergency framework adopted after Chernobyl. Recognition red flags: no independent regulator, missing periodic safety review, failure to submit national reports, and no transboundary accident-notification arrangement. Remedy: establish an effective independent regulator, submit national reports for peer review, implement findings, and join the emergency notification and assistance conventions to meet treaty obligations.'),
    ('nuclear_safeguards_evasion',
     'Safeguards Evasion and Undeclared-Activity Recognition',
     'IAEA strengthened safeguards (Programme 93+2) and Additional Protocol detection measures, INFCIRC/540; IAEA (fetched https://www.iaea.org/topics/additional-protocol)',
     "Legal authority: After Iraq and the DPRK showed that verifying only declared material could miss a covert programme, the IAEA's strengthened safeguards added early design-information provision, environmental sampling, satellite imagery, and broad fuel-cycle access to detect undeclared nuclear material and activities. Recognition red flags: discrepancies between declared and measured inventories, environmental-sampling results inconsistent with declarations, undeclared procurement of trigger-list or dual-use items, sanitized or demolished sites ahead of access, and refusal of complementary access. These are integrity-and-oversight indicators, not operational details. Remedy: the IAEA seeks clarification and access, applies the Additional Protocol, and where cooperation fails reports the matter through the Director General to the Board of Governors for non-compliance findings."),
    ('nuclear_proliferation_finance',
     'Proliferation Finance in Nuclear Procurement (FATF and UNSCR 1540)',
     'FATF Recommendation 7 and counter-proliferation-financing guidance; UN Security Council Resolution 1540 (2004); FATF (fetched https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Financingofproliferation/Guidance-counter-proliferation-financing.html)',
     'Legal authority: FATF Recommendation 7 requires countries to apply targeted financial sanctions implementing UN Security Council resolutions against WMD proliferation and its financing. UNSCR 1540 (2004) obliges States to prevent non-state actors from acquiring, financing, or transferring nuclear, chemical, or biological weapons and related materials. Together they extend the non-proliferation regime into procurement finance, linking to counter-terrorism-financing and arms controls. Recognition red flags: front companies and opaque intermediaries, trade-finance for dual-use goods routed through weak-control jurisdictions, mislabeled shipments, and end-users mismatched to stated business. Remedy: screen against sanctions lists, freeze designated assets, file suspicious-transaction reports, and refer suspected proliferation financing to financial-intelligence and enforcement authorities.'),
    ('nuclear_ctbt',
     'Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty (CTBT) Monitoring',
     'Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty (CTBT, opened for signature 1996) and its verification regime; CTBTO (fetched https://www.ctbto.org/our-work/verification-regime)',
     'Legal authority: The CTBT prohibits all nuclear-test explosions and is verified by a regime of three components: the International Monitoring System (planned 321 stations and 16 laboratories using seismic, hydroacoustic, infrasound, and radionuclide technologies), the International Data Centre in Vienna, and, after entry into force, on-site inspections, plus consultation-and-clarification and confidence-building measures. The IMS has detected all declared nuclear tests since 2006. Recognition red flags: an unexplained seismic or radionuclide signature, a State declining to host or operate designated stations, or failure to notify large chemical explosions under confidence-building measures. Remedy: States seek clarification through the consultation process or the Executive Council and, once the Treaty is in force, may request an on-site inspection to verify a suspected breach.'),
    ('nuclear_forensics',
     'Nuclear Forensics and Attribution',
     'IAEA nuclear forensics for nuclear security; IAEA Nuclear Security Series guidance and national nuclear forensic libraries; IAEA (fetched https://www.iaea.org/topics/nuclear-forensics)',
     'Legal authority: Nuclear forensics examines nuclear and other radioactive material using analytical techniques to determine its origin and history for law-enforcement investigations and to assess nuclear-security vulnerabilities, supported by IAEA technical assistance, training, and coordinated research. Isotopic, chemical, and physical characteristics, combined with conventional forensic evidence such as tool marks, can link a sample to places and events; national nuclear forensic libraries aid attribution. Recognition red flags: interdicted or found material with no documented provenance, a chain-of-custody break, or analyses inconsistent with a declared origin. Remedy: preserve the radiological crime scene, establish chain of custody, sequence non-destructive before destructive analysis, and use forensic findings to attribute the material and strengthen national security practices.'),
    ('nuclear_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and Enforcement (IAEA Board of Governors and UN Security Council)',
     'IAEA Statute Article XII.C non-compliance and reporting authority; referral to the UN Security Council; Arms Control Association analysis (fetched https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2010-01/safeguards-noncompliance-challenge-iaea-and-un-security-council)',
     'Legal authority: Under Article XII.C of the IAEA Statute, inspectors report non-compliance to the Director General, who reports to the Board of Governors. The Board calls on the State to remedy the non-compliance forthwith and may report it to all members and to the UN Security Council and General Assembly, and may suspend assistance and curtail membership privileges. Eight safeguards non-compliance cases, beginning with Iraq in 1991, have generated such special reports. Recognition red flags: an outstanding Board finding of non-compliance, unresolved verification questions, or refusal to remedy. Remedy: the State cooperates fully and restores compliance; failing that, the Board escalates and the Security Council may impose measures, providing the enforcement backstop for the safeguards system.'),
    ('space_outer_space_treaty',
     'Outer Space Treaty 1967: foundational principles for state conduct',
     'Treaty on Principles Governing the Activities of States in the Exploration and Use of Outer Space (Outer Space Treaty), 1967 - UNOOSA. https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/treaties/outerspacetreaty.html',
     'Legal authority: The 1967 Outer Space Treaty is the constitutional baseline for space governance. Article I: exploration and use serve all countries. Article II: outer space and celestial bodies are not subject to national appropriation by sovereignty claim, use, or occupation. Article IV: no nuclear weapons or weapons of mass destruction in orbit. Article VI: states bear international responsibility for national activities, including by private actors, and must authorize and continuously supervise them. Article VIII: the state of registry keeps jurisdiction over its objects. Red flags of an illegitimate claim or actor: assertions of sovereign ownership over orbital regions or celestial territory; a private operator claiming it needs no state authorization or supervision; weapons-of-mass-destruction payloads framed as civil. Remedy: raise non-compliance through the responsible state, which carries treaty liability, and through the UN Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space (COPUOS).'),
    ('space_debris_mitigation',
     'Space-debris mitigation guidelines (UN COPUOS and IADC)',
     'Space Debris Mitigation Guidelines of the Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space (UNGA-endorsed 2007); IADC Space Debris Mitigation Guidelines. https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/sd/COPUOS-GuidelinesE.pdf',
     'Legal authority: The COPUOS Space Debris Mitigation Guidelines (UN General Assembly endorsed, 2007) and the underlying IADC guidelines set seven measures: limit debris released in normal operations; minimize break-up potential; limit accidental collision; avoid intentional destruction; passivate stored energy post-mission; limit long-term presence in low-Earth orbit (de-orbit, historically a 25-year guideline); and move geostationary objects to a graveyard orbit above the GEO protected region. They are voluntary but reflected in ISO 24113 and many national licences. Recognition red flags: a mission plan with no end-of-life disposal or passivation; deliberate fragmentation or anti-satellite tests creating long-lived debris; abandoning a stage with residual propellant or charged batteries. Remedy: condition national authorization on a disposal plan; report non-conforming actors to the licensing regulator and to COPUOS.'),
    ('space_spectrum_orbital',
     'Satellite spectrum and orbital-slot integrity under the ITU',
     'ITU Radio Regulations, Articles 9 and 11; ITU Radio Regulatory Framework for Space Services. https://www.itu.int/en/ITU-R/space/snl/Documents/ITU-Space_reg.pdf',
     "Legal authority: Radio-frequency spectrum and satellite orbital positions are limited shared resources managed by the International Telecommunication Union (ITU) through the Radio Regulations. Operators coordinate via advance publication, coordination, and notification, with assignments recorded in the Master International Frequency Register. A 'first come, first served' coordination process, a seven-year bring-into-use deadline, and milestone deployment percentages for non-geostationary constellations guard against warehousing. Recognition red flags: 'paper satellites' (filings reserving spectrum or slots with no genuine intent to operate); claims of an exclusive orbital slot without recorded coordination; spectrum warehousing to block competitors; operations causing harmful interference outside a coordinated assignment. Remedy: challenge through the affected national administration and the ITU Radiocommunication Bureau, which can require interference elimination and can cancel assignments not brought into use."),
    ('space_dual_use_export',
     'Dual-use space technology and export-control integrity',
     'ITAR US Munitions List Category XV (Spacecraft and Related Articles), 22 CFR 121 / 124.15; Missile Technology Control Regime and Wassenaar Arrangement. https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2014-05-13/html/2014-10806.htm',
     'Legal authority: Spacecraft, launch vehicles, and key components have military and civil uses, so they are controlled as defense or dual-use items. Under US ITAR, US Munitions List Category XV covers militarily critical spacecraft (anti-satellite, space-to-ground weapons, certain remote sensing), while less sensitive items move to the Commerce Control List; launch-capable systems fall under Missile Technology Control Regime (MTCR) Category I restraint, and the Wassenaar Arrangement adds transparency for dual-use transfers. Recognition red flags: transfers of satellite or propulsion technology routed through front companies or non-aligned third countries; requests to omit technology-transfer control plans or end-use monitoring; civil framing for hardware with weapons-targeting capability. Remedy: deny or condition the licence, report suspected diversion to the export-control authority (e.g., the State Department DDTC or Commerce BIS), and flag MTCR-controlled items for the highest restraint.'),
    ('space_situational_awareness',
     'Space situational awareness and collision-avoidance integrity',
     'CCSDS Conjunction Data Message standard 508.0; US SPD-3 / Department of Commerce TraCSS; NASA Spacecraft Conjunction Assessment. https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/oce-51-nasa-spacecraft-conjunction-assessment.pdf',
     'Legal authority and practice: Safe operations depend on tracking objects and warning of close approaches. Conjunction assessment screens a protected asset against the catalog, estimates collision probability and time of closest approach, and shares results in a standardized Conjunction Data Message; operators then plan avoidance maneuvers. Civil services such as the US Traffic Coordination System for Space (TraCSS) and the space-track.org catalog distribute warnings. Recognition red flags: launching an untrackable spacecraft (below the trackability size threshold) with no tracking arrangement; an operator that ignores conjunction warnings or refuses to share ephemerides; stale orbital data passed off as current. Remedy: require trackability and ephemeris sharing as a licence condition; escalate non-cooperative operators to the SSA provider and the national regulator responsible for continuing supervision.'),
    ('space_artemis_accords',
     'Artemis Accords and space-resource governance principles',
     'The Artemis Accords: Principles for Cooperation in the Civil Exploration and Use of the Moon, Mars, Comets, and Asteroids (2020), Sections 3-12. https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/Artemis-Accords-signed-13Oct2020.pdf',
     "Legal authority: The Artemis Accords are a non-binding political commitment that operationalizes the Outer Space Treaty for civil exploration. Signatories commit to peaceful purposes, transparency, interoperability, emergency assistance, registration of space objects, open release of scientific data, preserving heritage sites, orbital-debris mitigation, and deconfliction via 'safety zones' with notification of harmful interference. Section 10 states that extracting space resources does not inherently constitute national appropriation under Article II of the Outer Space Treaty. Recognition red flags: a 'safety zone' asserted as exclusive territory or sovereignty rather than a temporary deconfliction area; resource activity hidden from the UN Secretary-General; refusal to consult after a claim of harmful interference. Remedy: invoke the Accords' consultation mechanism and Article IX of the Outer Space Treaty (due regard); raise systemic issues at COPUOS."),
    ('space_gnss_spoofing',
     'GNSS spoofing and satellite-signal fraud recognition',
     'ICAO Assembly 42 Working Paper on GNSS RFI; DHS Resilient PNT Reference Architecture; ITU/ICAO/IMO joint statement on protecting GNSS. https://www.icao.int/sites/default/files/Meetings/a42/Documents/WP/wp_034_en.pdf',
     "Legal authority and practice: Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) signals underpin aviation, maritime, and timing. Jamming blocks signals ('the receiver dies'); spoofing transmits deceptive signals so the receiver reports a false position or time ('the receiver lies'). ICAO Assembly resolutions urge states to refrain from jamming and spoofing affecting civil aviation and to keep terrestrial backups; ITU, ICAO, and IMO jointly call for protecting GNSS from harmful interference. Recognition red flags: sudden position jumps, implausible signal-to-noise ratios, clock or time discontinuities, divergence between GNSS and inertial or radar position, and unexplained NOTAMs of GNSS interference. Remedy: report interference to the ITU, the national spectrum regulator, and aviation/maritime authorities; deploy resilient receivers, navigation-message authentication (e.g., Galileo OSNMA), and conventional backups."),
    ('space_liability_convention',
     'Liability Convention 1972: damage and state responsibility',
     'Convention on International Liability for Damage Caused by Space Objects, 1972 - UNOOSA introduction page. https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/treaties/introliability-convention.html',
     "Legal authority: Elaborating Article VII of the Outer Space Treaty, the 1972 Liability Convention provides that a launching State is absolutely liable to pay compensation for damage caused by its space object on the surface of the Earth or to aircraft in flight, and liable on a fault basis for damage caused elsewhere (in space). 'Launching State' includes the state that launches, procures the launch, or from whose territory or facility the object is launched, so liability can be joint and several. Claims are pursued through diplomatic channels, with a Claims Commission if unresolved. No exoneration applies where the damage stems from activity contrary to international law. Recognition red flags: an operator or state denying launching-State status to dodge liability; uninsured high-risk re-entry; absence of any compensation channel for damage. Remedy: a claimant state presents a claim through diplomatic channels against the launching State and may invoke the Claims Commission procedure."),
    ('space_registration_convention',
     'Registration Convention and space-object transparency',
     'Convention on Registration of Objects Launched into Outer Space, 1975 - UNOOSA introduction and UN Register. https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/treaties/introregistration-convention.html',
     'Legal authority: The 1975 Registration Convention requires each State of registry to maintain a national registry and to furnish the UN Secretary-General with information on each space object, including an appropriate designator, date and territory or location of launch, basic orbital parameters (nodal period, inclination, apogee, perigee), and general function. UNOOSA maintains the public UN Register / Online Index. Transparency supports identification, liability attribution, and collision avoidance. Recognition red flags: an object operating with no registration entry; a launch where no state will accept State-of-registry status; missing or falsified orbital parameters or function; large gaps between objects launched and objects registered. Remedy: seek clarification from the presumed launching or controlling state (the COPUOS sustainability guidelines require a timely reply); raise persistent non-registration with UNOOSA and COPUOS, which promote enhanced registration practices.'),
    ('space_commercial_licensing',
     'Commercial-space licensing and safety integrity',
     'FAA 14 CFR Part 450 Launch and Reentry License Requirements; Commercial Space Launch Act, 51 U.S.C. 50901-50923; CRS R48582. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-III/subchapter-C/part-450',
     'Legal authority: Outer Space Treaty Article VI obliges states to authorize and continuously supervise national space activities, including private operators. Implementing this, the US FAA licenses commercial launch and reentry under 14 CFR Part 450 through five reviews: policy (national security and foreign policy), payload, safety, financial responsibility (maximum probable loss insurance), and environmental, plus space-object registration duties. Comparable national regulators exist worldwide. Recognition red flags: an operator launching or reentering without a licence or claiming none is needed; concealed payloads or undisclosed foreign ownership during payload review; no maximum-probable-loss insurance; ignored continuing-supervision conditions. Remedy: the regulator can deny, condition, suspend, or revoke a licence, inspect for compliance, and refer foreign-policy or security concerns to consulted agencies; affected parties petition the licensing authority.'),
    ('space_mining_resource',
     'Space-mining and resource-claim disputes',
     'Moon Agreement 1979 (Article 11, common heritage) - UNOOSA; US Space Resource Exploration and Utilization Act 2015, 51 U.S.C. 51303; CRS R48144. https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/treaties/moon-agreement.html',
     "Legal authority and dispute: International law on space mining is contested. The 1967 Outer Space Treaty bars national appropriation (Article II) but permits use; the 1979 Moon Agreement (Article 11) declares the Moon and its resources the common heritage of mankind, bars ownership by any state or private entity, and calls for an international regime with equitable sharing - but major space powers are not parties. National laws (US 2015 Space Resource Act; Luxembourg 2017) grant citizens ownership of resources they extract, and the Artemis Accords state extraction is not inherently appropriation. Recognition red flags: claims of sovereignty or exclusive territory over a celestial body or asteroid (versus title to extracted material); resource activity not reported to the UN Secretary-General; ignoring due-regard and harmful-interference duties. Remedy: contest sovereignty claims under Article II; pursue clarification and a governance regime through COPUOS; rely on the licensing state's continuing-supervision obligation."),
    ('space_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and oversight: COPUOS and national space regulators',
     'COPUOS Guidelines for the Long-term Sustainability of Outer Space Activities (2019), Guidelines A.1, A.3, A.5; UNGA Resolution 74/82. https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/PromotingSpaceSustainability/Publication_Final_English_June2021.pdf',
     'Legal authority: There is no single space police force; oversight is layered. Under Outer Space Treaty Article VI, each state authorizes and continuously supervises its national activities through a national regulator. The 2019 COPUOS Long-term Sustainability Guidelines direct states to adopt and amend national regulatory frameworks (A.1), supervise national space activities (A.3), and enhance space-object registration (A.5). COPUOS is the principal UN forum for dialogue; UNOOSA maintains the registry; the ITU governs interference; the Liability Convention provides a compensation route. Recognition red flags: an operator outside any national authorization; a state with no functioning licensing or supervision regime; ignored registration or interference duties; no point of contact for urgent coordination. Remedy: escalate to the responsible national regulator (it can suspend or revoke licences), pursue Liability Convention claims through diplomatic channels, request clarification via UNOOSA, and raise systemic concerns at COPUOS.'),
    ('cbam_eu_regulation',
     'EU CBAM Regulation 2023/956: scope, covered goods, and phased timeline',
     'Regulation (EU) 2023/956 (CBAM), Annex I; via https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A32023R0956',
     'Legal authority: Regulation (EU) 2023/956 prices greenhouse-gas emissions embedded in imports of iron and steel, cement, aluminium, fertilisers, hydrogen and electricity (Annex I), to prevent carbon leakage. It entered into force 16 May 2023, ran a report-only transitional period 1 Oct 2023 to 31 Dec 2025, and began definitive obligations 1 Jan 2026. Red flags for integrity review: importers claiming a good is out of scope despite an Annex I CN code; declared imports vanishing below the EUR 150 consignment threshold; product re-described to dodge coverage. Remedy: the importer of record carries declaration and certificate liability; national competent authorities and customs verify scope, and the Commission may close gaps by delegated act.'),
    ('cbam_embedded_emissions',
     'Embedded-emissions calculation and Commission default values under CBAM',
     'CBAM Implementing Reg. (EU) 2023/1773; Regs (EU) 2025/2547 and 2025/2621; via https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/carbon-border-adjustment-mechanism/cbam-legislation-and-guidance_en',
     'Legal authority: Implementing Regulation (EU) 2023/1773 set transitional reporting; Regulation (EU) 2025/2547 specifies methods for calculating emissions embedded in goods and 2025/2621 sets default values. Operators report actual measured emissions or, where actual data is unavailable, Commission default values, covering direct and (progressively) indirect emissions within defined system boundaries. Red flags: actual figures implausibly far below the sector default; system boundaries narrowed to exclude precursors; defaults claimed when actual data clearly existed; round numbers with no production records. Remedy: declarations rest on accredited-verifier checks; competent authorities may reject unsupported figures and require recalculation or substitution of conservative default values.'),
    ('cbam_carbon_leakage_wto',
     'Carbon leakage rationale and WTO-compatibility debate around CBAM',
     'Analysis of GATT Art. III/XX and CBAM (EU) 2023/956; via https://www.europeanpapers.eu/e-journal/international-law-reading-eu-carbon-border-adjustment-mechanism',
     "Legal authority: CBAM is framed to mirror the EU ETS carbon cost so imports face pricing equivalent to domestic goods, addressing carbon leakage. Its WTO defensibility turns on GATT non-discrimination (Most-Favoured-Nation, National Treatment) and the Article XX(b)/(g) environmental exceptions, including the chapeau bar on arbitrary or unjustifiable discrimination. Red flags for integrity: claims that CBAM is a banned tariff rather than an internal-charge equivalent; arguments that pricing structure hides discrimination between like products of different origins; selective exemptions applied where the same conditions prevail. Remedy: disputes route through WTO panels and the EU's own review clauses; transparent, origin-neutral methodology and credit for foreign carbon prices are the documented compliance path."),
    ('cbam_circumvention',
     'Recognizing CBAM circumvention: resource shuffling, transshipment, and re-routing',
     'Regulation (EU) 2023/956, Art. 27; Commission proposal COM(2025) 989; via https://www.tradecomplianceresourcehub.com/2025/12/19/commission-proposes-expansion-of-eu-cbam-to-downstream-goods-and-addresses-potential-cbam-abusive-practices/',
     "Legal authority: Article 27 defines circumvention as a change in trade patterns with insufficient due cause or economic justification other than avoiding CBAM. Named practices include slightly modifying goods to escape covered CN codes without altering essential characteristics, artificially splitting shipments below the de minimis value, routing goods through third countries, and resource shuffling (exporting cleaner output to the EU while keeping dirtier production elsewhere). The 2025 proposal adds Article 27(2)(c) on artificially adjusting supply chains to secure lower default values and an 'abusive practices' concept in Article 3(35). Red flags: sudden re-routing, post-rule CN reclassification, fragmented low-value consignments. Remedy: the Commission may extend scope by delegated act and direct intensified customs controls."),
    ('cbam_mrv_verification',
     'Monitoring, reporting and verification of imported-goods emissions under CBAM',
     'Commission Reg. (EU) 2025/2546 (verification) and Delegated Reg. (EU) 2025/2551 (verifier accreditation); via https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/carbon-border-adjustment-mechanism/cbam-legislation-and-guidance_en',
     "Legal authority: Regulation (EU) 2025/2546 sets principles for verifying declared embedded emissions, and Delegated Regulation (EU) 2025/2551 governs accreditation and oversight of independent verifiers; declarants must keep records substantiating emissions. Red flags for integrity review: an 'independent' verifier with undisclosed ties to the producer; verification statements lacking site data, monitoring plans, or source documents; figures that cannot be reproduced from primary records; verifier not accredited under the EU scheme. Remedy: declarations depend on accredited third-party verification; competent authorities may reject deficient verification, require re-verification, and escalate to penalties where embedded-emissions records cannot be substantiated."),
    ('cbam_declarant_obligations',
     'Authorised CBAM declarant obligations and certificate surrender',
     'Regulation (EU) 2023/956, Arts 20-24; via https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32023R0956',
     'Legal authority: only authorised CBAM declarants may import covered goods. They buy certificates on a common central platform priced weekly off the average EU ETS allowance price (Art. 20), must hold a sufficient quarterly balance (Art. 22(2): 31 Mar, 30 Jun, 30 Sep, 31 Dec), and surrender certificates by 31 May of the following year equal to embedded emissions less foreign carbon price and free-allocation reductions (Art. 22(1)). Certificates have limited validity and excess may be repurchased (Arts 23-24). Red flags: importing without authorisation; chronic quarterly under-holding; double-counting a foreign carbon price never actually paid. Remedy: competent authorities police authorisation, surrender, and deductions; shortfalls trigger penalties and corrective surrender.'),
    ('cbam_misdeclaration_fraud',
     'Recognizing trade-climate misdeclaration and fraud in CBAM reporting',
     'Regulation (EU) 2023/956, Arts 6-7 and 26-27; Commission proposal COM(2025) 989; via https://www.tradecomplianceresourcehub.com/2025/12/19/commission-proposes-expansion-of-eu-cbam-to-downstream-goods-and-addresses-potential-cbam-abusive-practices/',
     "Legal authority: CBAM declarations must reflect true origin, identification, and embedded emissions. The 2025 proposal strengthens this via Article 6(6a) (additional supporting documentation), mandatory EORI numbers under indirect representation, Article 6(7) high-risk product controls, and Article 7(5) record-keeping. Red flags for integrity review: origin certificates inconsistent with shipping or production records; emissions data copied across unrelated suppliers; a producer's stated output exceeding plausible installation capacity; documentation that materialises only after a query. Remedy: customs may intensify controls on flagged categories, demand substantiating records, and refer suspected fraud; penalties and circumvention measures (Art. 26-27) attach to false or unsupported declarations."),
    ('cbam_ets_interaction',
     'CBAM interaction with the EU ETS and free-allocation phase-out',
     'Regulation (EU) 2023/956 with EU ETS Directive 2003/87/EC reform; via https://cbamguide.com/carbon/free-allocation/',
     "Legal authority: CBAM and EU ETS free allocation are directly coupled. As CBAM phases in, ETS free allowances for covered sectors phase out from 97.5 percent in 2026 to 0 percent by 2034; the CBAM obligation applies only to the share of embedded emissions no longer covered by free allocation, so the CBAM factor equals the percentage of free allocation withdrawn (about 2.5 percent in 2026, 100 percent by 2034). Red flags for integrity: certificate counts that ignore the year's CBAM factor; claiming a free-allocation offset for imports (it applies to EU installations); double benefit from both free allowances and an asserted foreign price. Remedy: authorities reconcile the declared volume against the statutory CBAM factor and require corrective surrender."),
    ('cbam_equity_concerns',
     'Developing-country equity and CBDR concerns raised by CBAM',
     'UNCTAD, EU CBAM: Implications for developing countries; UNFCCC CBDR-RC principle; via https://unctad.org/publication/european-union-carbon-border-adjustment-mechanism-implications-developing-countries',
     'Legal authority and context: critics argue CBAM sits in tension with the UNFCCC principle of common but differentiated responsibilities and respective capabilities (CBDR-RC), because uniform charges and reporting fall equally on low- and middle-income exporters with limited MRV capacity. UNCTAD notes such measures should align with the Paris Agreement and account for partner capacity. Red flags for integrity review: equity framing used to excuse unverifiable or inflated low-emission claims; capacity gaps cited to justify skipping verification; selective relief asserted without a legal basis in the regulation. Remedy: relief, recognition of foreign carbon prices, and capacity support are policy and legislative questions decided by the EU co-legislators and review clauses, not by individual declarants self-exempting.'),
    ('cbam_other_jurisdictions',
     "Other jurisdictions' border-carbon measures: the UK CBAM from 2027",
     'UK Government CBAM factsheet (HM Treasury/HMRC), from 1 Jan 2027; via https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/factsheet-carbon-border-adjustment-mechanism-cbam/factsheet-carbon-border-adjustment-mechanism',
     'Legal authority: the UK CBAM starts 1 January 2027 on imports of aluminium, cement, fertiliser, hydrogen, and iron and steel (glass and ceramics excluded). Importers above a 50,000 GBP minimum registration threshold over the relevant period must register with HMRC; sector rates track the UK ETS and are reduced by a factor reflecting UK free-allowance phase-out. Liable persons use independently verified actual data or default values, and may deduct a qualifying overseas carbon price with evidence. Red flags: jurisdiction-shopping between EU and UK regimes; an overseas-price deduction claimed without supporting proof; under-stating import value to stay below the threshold. Remedy: HMRC registration, verification, and evidence requirements govern eligibility and corrections.'),
    ('cbam_data_integrity',
     'Embedded-emissions data integrity and supplier reporting under CBAM',
     'CBAM calculation/verification framework, Regs (EU) 2025/2547, 2025/2546 and Art. 7(5) record-keeping; via https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/carbon-border-adjustment-mechanism/cbam-legislation-and-guidance_en',
     'Legal authority: declarants must base embedded-emissions figures on auditable production data from third-country operators, calculated per Regulation (EU) 2025/2547 and verified under Regulation (EU) 2025/2546, with records retained under Article 7(5). Red flags for integrity review: supplier emission factors that never change across batches or sites; data that cannot be traced to a monitoring plan or measurement; figures conveniently at or just below a default threshold; missing precursor and energy-source data; a single template reused across unrelated producers. Remedy: where supplier data is unverifiable, conservative default values apply and competent authorities may reject the declaration; persistent data failures feed circumvention and penalty assessment rather than acceptance of the lower number.'),
    ('cbam_remedy_enforcement',
     'CBAM remedy and enforcement: penalties, customs controls, and review',
     'Regulation (EU) 2023/956, Art. 26 (penalties, referencing Dir. 2003/87/EC Art. 16) and Art. 27; via https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32023R0956',
     'Legal authority: Article 26 sets penalties for failing to surrender required certificates identical to the EU ETS excess-emissions penalty under Directive 2003/87/EC Article 16(3)-(4) (EUR 100 per certificate), with higher, dissuasive penalties where unauthorised persons import covered goods; paying a penalty does not extinguish the surrender obligation. Article 27 enables anti-circumvention extension of scope. Red flags for review: treating the EUR 100 penalty as a cheaper substitute for compliance; importing through an unauthorised party to dodge the regime; re-routing once a practice is flagged. Remedy: national competent authorities and customs enforce surrender, recover shortfalls, intensify controls on high-risk goods, and the Commission reviews and tightens scope by delegated act.'),
    ('chem_cwc_opcw',
     'Chemical Weapons Convention and the OPCW oversight regime',
     'Chemical Weapons Convention, Articles I and VIII; OPCW (https://www.opcw.org/chemical-weapons-convention)',
     'The Chemical Weapons Convention (CWC) entered into force on 29 April 1997 and is the first multilateral treaty to ban an entire category of weapons of mass destruction. It prohibits States Parties from developing, producing, acquiring, stockpiling, retaining, transferring, or using chemical weapons, and obliges them to destroy declared stockpiles and production facilities. Article VIII establishes the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) as the implementing body. The OPCW, with 193 States Parties and the 2013 Nobel Peace Prize, runs the verification and oversight regime. Recognition cue: legitimate chemical activity is declared, inspectable, and confined to purposes not prohibited under the Convention. Remedy path: compliance concerns move through consultation, fact-finding, the Executive Council, and the Conference of States Parties under Article XII.'),
    ('chem_schedules',
     'Scheduled chemicals: Schedules 1, 2, and 3 and declarations',
     'CWC Annex on Chemicals; OPCW Declaration Requirements (https://www.opcw.org/resources/declarations/declaration-requirements-scheduled-chemicals)',
     'The CWC Annex on Chemicals lists toxic chemicals and precursors in three Schedules by risk to the Convention. Schedule 1 covers chemicals with little or no use outside weapons and is subject to the strictest oversight: production is capped, restricted to declared single small-scale facilities, and verified by on-site inspection. Schedule 2 covers precursors and toxic chemicals with limited commercial use; Schedule 3 covers chemicals produced in large industrial volumes that also have weapons relevance. Recognition cue for oversight: Schedule 2 and 3 plant sites must file declarations above set thresholds and accept routine inspection; absent declarations, falsified aggregate national data, or refused access are red flags. Remedy: the OPCW Technical Secretariat reconciles declarations and reports discrepancies to the Executive Council.'),
    ('chem_verification',
     'OPCW verification, inspections, and industry declarations',
     'CWC Article VI and Verification Annex; OPCW (https://www.opcw.org/chemical-weapons-convention)',
     "Article VI governs activities not prohibited under the Convention, the non-proliferation and industry-verification regime. States Parties submit initial and annual declarations on scheduled-chemical plant sites, and OPCW inspectors visit declared facilities to confirm declarations are complete and accurate and that activity stays within permitted purposes. A unique oversight tool is the challenge inspection: any State Party in doubt about another's compliance may request a surprise, any-time-anywhere inspection with no right of refusal. The OPCW has conducted thousands of inspections since 1997. Recognition cue: complete, reconciled declarations plus accepted inspections signal compliance; refused access, undeclared production, or discrepancies between declared and observed activity are warning signs. Remedy: findings feed the Executive Council compliance process."),
    ('chem_use_recognition',
     'Chemical-weapons-use recognition and attribution by the OPCW IIT',
     'OPCW decision C-SS-4/DEC.3; OPCW Investigation and Identification Team (https://www.opcw.org/iit)',
     'The OPCW Investigation and Identification Team (IIT) was established by Conference decision C-SS-4/DEC.3 of 27 June 2018 to identify perpetrators of chemical-weapons use in Syria where the Fact-Finding Mission determined use occurred. The IIT is a fact-finding, not prosecutorial, body and applies a reasonable-grounds standard, drawing on environmental and biomedical sample analysis at independent OPCW Designated Laboratories, witness interviews, munition-remnant and meteorological analysis, and open-source data. Recognition framing: attribution rests on impartial scientific evidence and documented chain of custody, never on operational reconstruction. The IIT reports to the Executive Council and the UN Secretary-General and preserves information for UN accountability mechanisms such as the IIIM, the remedy pathway for individual criminal accountability.'),
    ('chem_riot_control_boundary',
     'Riot-control agents and the toxic-chemical-misuse boundary',
     'CWC Article I(5) and Article II; OPCW Article I (https://www.opcw.org/chemical-weapons-convention/articles/article-i)',
     'Under CWC Article I, paragraph 5, each State Party undertakes not to use riot-control agents as a method of warfare. Article II permits law enforcement, including domestic riot control, only in types and quantities consistent with such purposes. The CWC defines a riot-control agent as a chemical, not listed in a Schedule, that causes rapid sensory irritation or disabling effects which disappear shortly after exposure. The oversight boundary: any toxic chemical used to cause harm through its toxic properties as a method of warfare is a prohibited chemical weapon regardless of label. Recognition cue: deployment scale and battlefield context, not the agent name, distinguish permitted law-enforcement use from prohibited warfare use. Remedy: disputes over interpretation are raised in OPCW policy organs.'),
    ('chem_australia_group',
     'Australia Group chemical export controls and harmonisation',
     'Australia Group, Chemical Weapons Precursors Control List; DFAT (https://www.dfat.gov.au/publications/minisite/theaustraliagroupnet/site/en/index.html)',
     'The Australia Group is an informal forum of countries that harmonise export controls so exports do not contribute to chemical or biological weapons programmes. It maintains common control lists, including a Chemical Weapons Precursors list and lists of dual-use chemical manufacturing equipment, and participants are expected to deny export licences where there is concern an item could feed a weapons programme. The precursors list is individually enumerated and was expanded in February 2020 to add Novichok precursors after those agents entered CWC Schedule 1. Recognition cue for oversight: licit trade is licensed, declared, and end-use checked, while attempts to evade controls or obscure end-users are diversion red flags. Remedy: licence denial, national enforcement, and information-sharing among participating governments.'),
    ('chem_dual_use_diversion',
     'Dual-use chemical diversion recognition and red flags',
     'CWC Article VI verification regime; OPCW chemical security (https://www.opcw.org/our-work/preventing-re-emergence-chemical-weapons)',
     "Many scheduled and non-scheduled chemicals have legitimate commercial uses but can be diverted toward weapons purposes. The CWC verification regime addresses this by requiring declarations and inspections for scheduled-chemical plant sites of varying commercial significance, so inspectors can confirm declarations are accurate and activity stays lawful. Chlorine illustrates the concern: a widely used industrial chemical that has nonetheless been misused as a weapon. Recognition cues for diversion oversight, drawn from OPCW Know Your Customer guidance: orders inconsistent with a buyer's stated business, refusal to identify end-use, requests to bypass declaration thresholds, or routing through opaque intermediaries. The remedy path is licence refusal, declaration reconciliation, national enforcement, and referral of compliance concerns to the OPCW Executive Council."),
    ('chem_destruction',
     'Old and abandoned chemical weapons and verified destruction',
     'CWC Articles IV and V; OPCW (https://www.opcw.org/media-centre/news/2023/07/opcw-confirms-all-declared-chemical-weapons-stockpiles-verified)',
     "Articles IV and V oblige States Parties to destroy declared chemical weapons and production facilities, including old chemical weapons and weapons abandoned on another State's territory, all under OPCW verification. On 7 July 2023 the OPCW confirmed that all chemical-weapons stockpiles declared since 1997, totalling 72,304 metric tonnes of agent, had been verifiably and irreversibly destroyed, with the United States the last possessor to complete destruction. Recovery of abandoned weapons, such as Japanese-abandoned munitions excavated in China, continued under monitoring. Recognition cue for oversight: destruction is legitimate only when conducted under OPCW verification with documented, irreversible elimination. Remedy: incomplete or undeclared destruction is a compliance matter handled through the Executive Council and Conference of States Parties."),
    ('chem_precursor_control',
     'Chemical-weapons precursor control and trade oversight',
     'CWC Annex on Chemicals (Schedules 2 and 3 precursors); OPCW declarations (https://www.opcw.org/resources/declarations/declaration-requirements-scheduled-chemicals)',
     'Precursors are chemicals used to make weapons-relevant toxic chemicals, and the CWC controls them through the Schedules. Schedule 1 and Schedule 2 include precursors with limited legitimate use, while Schedule 3 captures larger-volume industrial chemicals with weapons relevance. States Parties must declare scheduled-precursor plant sites above set thresholds, report aggregate national data on quantities produced, imported, and exported, and notify transfers; Schedule 1 transfers require 30 days advance notice to the OPCW. Recognition cue for oversight: lawful precursor trade is declared with quantitative import and export specifications per country, while missing transfer notifications or mismatched aggregate national data are red flags. Remedy: the OPCW reconciles declarations and escalates unresolved discrepancies through its compliance organs.'),
    ('chem_industrial_security',
     'Toxic industrial chemicals and chemical-facility security',
     'OPCW Chemical Safety and Security Management Programme (https://www.opcw.org/resources/capacity-building/international-cooperation-programmes/chemical-safety-and-security)',
     'Beyond scheduled chemicals, the OPCW promotes chemical safety and security to reduce risks from toxic industrial chemicals, including misuse and terrorism. It distinguishes chemical safety, preventing accidental releases, from chemical security, preventing deliberate releases. Many toxic industrial chemicals such as chlorine are unscheduled yet of security concern because of wide availability and past misuse as weapons. The OPCW develops guidance, including Know Your Customer guidelines, to help facilities prevent diversion of hazardous chemicals. Recognition cue: secure facilities track inventories, vet customers, and report anomalies; weak access controls, unexplained inventory loss, or sales to unverified end-users are warning signs. Remedy: national chemical-security regulation, OPCW capacity-building, and compliance reporting through the Executive Council.'),
    ('chem_nonstate_1540',
     'Non-state actors and proliferation finance under UNSCR 1540',
     'UN Security Council Resolution 1540 (2004); UNODA (https://disarmament.unoda.org/en/our-work/weapons-mass-destruction/un-security-council-resolution-1540)',
     'UN Security Council Resolution 1540, adopted unanimously in 2004 under Chapter VII, places legally binding obligations on all States to prevent non-state actors, including terrorists, from acquiring nuclear, chemical, or biological weapons and related materials. States must refrain from supporting such actors, enact and enforce laws prohibiting proliferation, and maintain export, transit, and trans-shipment controls, including controls on financing and transport that would contribute to proliferation. The 1540 Committee, supported by a Group of Experts, monitors implementation. Recognition cue for oversight: lawful chemical commerce is licensed and traceable, while proliferation finance hides behind front companies, falsified end-use, and layered payments. Remedy: national criminal enforcement, Security Council reporting, and international assistance to close legal and control gaps.'),
    ('chem_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: OPCW Executive Council and UN Security Council',
     'CWC Article XII; OPCW (https://www.opcw.org/chemical-weapons-convention/articles/article-xii-measures-redress-situation-and-ensure-compliance)',
     "Article XII is the final stage of the Convention's compliance machinery. Where the Executive Council has asked a State Party to redress a compliance problem and it fails to act in time, the Conference of States Parties may, on the Council's recommendation, restrict or suspend that State's rights and privileges under the Convention until it conforms. Where serious damage to the object and purpose of the Convention may result, the Conference may recommend collective measures consistent with international law. In cases of particular gravity and urgency, the Executive Council brings the matter, with its information and conclusions, directly to the UN General Assembly and Security Council. Recognition framing: enforcement follows graduated, evidence-based steps; remedy escalates from consultation to suspension to UN referral, never bypassing due process."),
    ('contentmod_dsa_systemic_risk',
     'DSA Systemic-Risk Duties for Very Large Platforms and Search Engines',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act), Articles 34-35; consolidated text at https://www.eu-digital-services-act.com/Digital_Services_Act_Article_34.html',
     'Authority: Under DSA Article 34, providers reaching 45 million monthly EU users (VLOPs/VLOSEs) must annually assess systemic risks from their design and algorithmic systems across four categories: (a) dissemination of illegal content; (b) negative effects on fundamental rights, including dignity, privacy, expression and media pluralism; (c) negative effects on civic discourse, electoral processes and public security; and (d) effects on gender-based violence, public health, minors and mental well-being. Article 35 requires reasonable, proportionate, effective mitigation. Recognition red flags: a platform claiming influence-at-scale yet publishing no risk assessment, refusing to name algorithmic amplification risks, or treating safety as optional. Remedy: assessments are filed with regulators; the Digital Services Coordinator and Commission can compel mitigation and impose fines.'),
    ('contentmod_dsa_transparency',
     'DSA Transparency Reporting and Statements of Reasons Obligations',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act), Articles 15 and 17; consolidated text at https://www.eu-digital-services-act.com/Digital_Services_Act_Article_15.html and /Digital_Services_Act_Article_17.html',
     'Authority: DSA Article 15 requires intermediary providers to publish machine-readable transparency reports covering authority orders, notices received, own-initiative moderation, complaints handled, automated-tool accuracy and error rates, and median response times. Article 17 requires a clear statement of reasons to each affected user whenever content is removed, demoted or restricted, naming the type of restriction, the facts relied on, the legal or contractual ground, whether automated means were used, and available redress. Recognition red flags: opaque takedowns with no explanation, no public reporting, or boilerplate notices that hide whether a human or algorithm decided. Remedy: users invoke the statement to appeal internally, seek out-of-court dispute settlement, or pursue judicial review; regulators audit report completeness.'),
    ('contentmod_trusted_flaggers',
     'DSA Trusted Flaggers and Notice-and-Action Mechanism',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act), Articles 16 and 22; consolidated text at https://www.eu-digital-services-act.com/Digital_Services_Act_Article_22.html',
     'Authority: DSA Article 16 obliges hosting providers to run an accessible notice-and-action mechanism so any individual or entity can flag illegal content and receive a decision. Article 22 creates the trusted-flagger status, awarded by a Digital Services Coordinator to bodies showing particular expertise, independence from any platform, and diligent, accurate, objective work; their notices must be given priority and decided without undue delay. The Commission maintains a public trusted-flagger database. Recognition red flags: a platform with no working report channel, ignoring expert civil-society flags, or refusing to disclose how flagged items are handled. Remedy: complainants escalate unanswered notices to the Coordinator; abusive or negligent flaggers can have status suspended or revoked after investigation.'),
    ('contentmod_tco_regulation',
     'EU Terrorist Content Online Regulation: One-Hour Removal Duty',
     'Regulation (EU) 2021/784 on addressing the dissemination of terrorist content online, Articles 3, 5, 6, 18; EUR-Lex at https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32021R0784',
     "Authority: Regulation (EU) 2021/784 requires hosting providers to remove or disable terrorist content across all Member States within one hour of a competent authority's removal order (Article 3). Article 2 defines terrorist content as material inciting, soliciting, glorifying or instructing terrorist offences, excluding educational, journalistic, artistic or research uses. Providers exposed to such content must adopt specific protective measures and terms (Article 5) and preserve removed content for six months for review (Article 6). Recognition red flags: a service systematically ignoring lawful removal orders, lacking any reporting mechanism, or providing no challenge route to affected content providers. Remedy: content providers can contest removals; under Article 18, systematic failure to meet the one-hour deadline draws penalties up to 4 percent of global turnover."),
    ('contentmod_csam_duties',
     'Platform Duties to Detect and Report Child Sexual Abuse Material',
     '18 U.S.C. 2258A (Reporting requirements of providers); Legal Information Institute at https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2258A',
     'Authority: Under 18 U.S.C. 2258A, U.S.-based online providers must report apparent child sexual abuse material to the NCMEC CyberTipline as soon as reasonably possible after obtaining actual knowledge, supplying identifying, geographic and content data, and must preserve the report contents securely for one year. The 2024 REPORT Act broadened reportable offences and extended retention. The EU is finalising a parallel CSA Regulation with risk tiers and a dedicated EU Centre. Recognition red flags: a platform that detects abuse material but never reports it, deletes evidence rather than preserving it, or has no trust-and-safety reporting pipeline. Remedy: NCMEC routes reports to law enforcement; knowing failure to report exposes providers to fines up to 850,000 USD (first offence) and 1,000,000 USD for repeat violations.'),
    ('contentmod_age_verification',
     'Age Assurance and Protection of Minors Under the DSA',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065, Article 28(1); European Commission Guidelines on the protection of minors (14 July 2025) at https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/commission-publishes-guidelines-protection-minors',
     "Authority: DSA Article 28(1) requires platforms accessible to minors to ensure a high level of privacy, safety and security for children. The Commission's July 2025 guidelines recommend accurate, reliable, robust, non-intrusive and non-discriminatory age assurance; private-by-default minor accounts; recommender safeguards prioritising explicit child signals over behavioural ones to curb rabbit-hole effects; and disabling persuasive features such as autoplay, streaks and read receipts by default. An EU age-verification app and Digital Identity Wallet serve as benchmarks. Recognition red flags: services serving children with no age checks, default-public minor profiles, or addictive design aimed at engagement. Remedy: Coordinators and the Commission enforce against non-compliance; the guidelines set the benchmark used to assess Article 28 breaches."),
    ('contentmod_recommender_transparency',
     'Recommender-System Transparency and the DSA Dark-Pattern Ban',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act), Articles 25 and 27; consolidated text at https://www.eu-digital-services-act.com/Digital_Services_Act_Article_27.html and /Digital_Services_Act_Article_25.html',
     "Authority: DSA Article 27 requires platforms using recommender systems to set out in plain language the main parameters driving recommendations and the criteria that matter most, and to let users select and modify their preferred option directly from the interface. Article 25 prohibits designing or operating interfaces that deceive, manipulate or materially distort users' free and informed choices; flagged dark patterns include visual prominence tricks, repeated nagging pop-ups, and making cancellation harder than sign-up. Recognition red flags: an unexplained black-box feed, no option to turn off profiling-based ranking, or asymmetric opt-out friction. Remedy: users exercise the in-interface control; Coordinators and the Commission can order changes and fine non-compliant interface designs."),
    ('contentmod_moderation_appeals',
     'Content-Moderation Accountability, Appeals and the Santa Clara Principles',
     'Santa Clara Principles 2.0 on Transparency and Accountability in Content Moderation, at https://santaclaraprinciples.org/',
     'Authority: The Santa Clara Principles 2.0 set a civil-society standard: companies should publish clear rules, ground moderation in human rights and due process, apply automated tools only when confident, and disclose state involvement in enforcement. The operational pillars require Numbers (regular machine-readable transparency data by country and rule), Notice (telling each user the specific clause violated, the detection method and available appeals, in their language), and Appeal (human review by people not involved in the original decision, with reasoning given). Recognition red flags: silent removals, no stated rule, automated-only bans with no human appeal, or hidden government takedown requests. Remedy: users demand notice and a fresh human appeal; the principles are widely cited as the benchmark regulators and auditors measure platforms against.'),
    ('contentmod_inauthentic_behavior',
     'Coordinated Inauthentic Behavior and Platform-Integrity Duties',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065, Article 34(2) (manipulation/inauthentic use); EU Code of Practice on Disinformation; analysis at https://policyreview.info/articles/analysis/platforms-compliance-code-of-practice-on-disinformation-review',
     'Authority: Coordinated inauthentic behavior (CIB) is the orchestrated use of fake, duplicated or hijacked accounts as an adversarial network to deceptively amplify an agenda. DSA Article 34(2) directs VLOPs to assess how their services are intentionally manipulated, including inauthentic use and rapid artificial content spread, when evaluating systemic risk; the EU Code of Practice on Disinformation (a DSA co-regulatory code) commits signatories to detect and disrupt such networks and report on integrity actions. Recognition red flags: bot-driven trending topics, sock-puppet networks, paid engagement farms, and platforms that publish no integrity-enforcement data. Remedy: users and researchers report networks; regulators can open DSA proceedings where a platform fails to address CIB, leading to mitigation orders and fines.'),
    ('contentmod_independent_audits',
     'Independent Audits of Very Large Platforms Under the DSA',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act), Article 37; consolidated text at https://www.eu-digital-services-act.com/Digital_Services_Act_Article_37.html',
     'Authority: DSA Article 37 requires VLOPs and VLOSEs to undergo an annual independent audit, at their own expense, of compliance with Chapter III obligations and any codes of conduct. Auditors must be independent (with conflict-of-interest limits, a 12-month bar on non-audit services and a 10-year rotation cap), technically competent and objective. The audit report must describe methodology, key findings and an audit opinion rated positive, positive with comments, or negative; a non-positive opinion triggers an audit implementation report within one month. Recognition red flags: a large platform with no published audit, an audit by a conflicted or non-independent firm, or ignored audit recommendations. Remedy: the Coordinator and Commission use audit results to enforce; persistent non-compliance can escalate to fines.'),
    ('contentmod_global_landscape',
     'Global Platform-Duty Landscape: UK Online Safety Act and Beyond',
     'UK Online Safety Act 2023; UK Government explainer at https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/online-safety-act-explainer/online-safety-act-explainer',
     "Authority: The UK Online Safety Act 2023 imposes duties of care on user-to-user and search services to assess and reduce risks from illegal content (priority offences include child sexual abuse, terrorism, fraud and intimate-image abuse) and to protect children from primary-priority harms such as pornography and suicide or eating-disorder content, backed by children's risk assessments and consistent age checks. Ofcom is the independent regulator, issuing binding codes of practice and overseeing compliance regardless of where a service is based. Recognition red flags: services accessible to children with no risk assessment, no age assurance, or no illegal-content systems. Remedy: Ofcom can fine up to 18 million GBP or 10 percent of qualifying worldwide revenue, with senior-manager criminal liability for certain child-safety and information failures."),
    ('contentmod_remedy_enforcement',
     'DSA Remedy and Enforcement: Coordinators, Complaints and Fines',
     'Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act), Articles 49, 52 and 53; consolidated text at https://www.eu-digital-services-act.com/Digital_Services_Act_Article_49.html and /Digital_Services_Act_Article_52.html',
     'Authority: DSA Article 49 requires every Member State to designate competent authorities and a Digital Services Coordinator responsible for supervision and enforcement, coordinating with peers, the European Board for Digital Services and the Commission. Article 53 gives recipients the right to lodge a complaint with the Coordinator of their place of residence against any provider. Article 52 requires effective, proportionate, dissuasive penalties: fines up to 6 percent of annual worldwide turnover for infringements, up to 1 percent for supplying incorrect information, and periodic payments up to 5 percent of average daily worldwide turnover. Recognition red flags: a platform unresponsive to complaints or ignoring orders. Remedy: users complain to the Coordinator, seek out-of-court dispute settlement, and the Commission directly enforces against VLOPs/VLOSEs.'),
    ('disaster_sendai_framework',
     'Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction: governance and accountability baseline',
     'Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015-2030 (UN/UNDRR), https://www.undrr.org/implementing-sendai-framework/what-sendai-framework',
     "The Sendai Framework (adopted 2015, UN General Assembly endorsed) is the global standard for disaster risk reduction, organized around four priorities: understanding risk, strengthening risk governance, investing in resilience, and enhancing preparedness to Build Back Better. Progress is measured against seven global targets and 38 indicators self-reported by states. Integrity red flags: self-reported indicator data with no independent audit; governance gaps where the State's primary role is not matched by transparent budgets or local accountability; reconstruction spending that bypasses risk-informed standards. Remedy: align national strategies to the four priorities, publish loss data (DesInventar Sendai), and treat accountability as a stated framework theme protecting human rights."),
    ('disaster_relief_fraud',
     'Recognizing disaster-relief and benefit fraud after a declared disaster',
     'U.S. DOJ National Center for Disaster Fraud / FEMA guidance, https://www.fema.gov/press-release/20250807/protect-your-identity-be-alert-fraud-and-scams-after-disaster',
     "Federal and local disaster workers never ask for or accept money, and FEMA does not charge for assistance. Recognition red flags: callers demanding cash deposits or advance fees to 'release' a grant; people posing as inspectors who request a Social Security number or bank details; a badge, shirt, or jacket offered as the only proof of identity; unsolicited contact when you never applied. Legitimate inspectors schedule appointments, verify damage only, and do not ask for money or your full SSN. Remedy: verify any contact through an official helpline, demand official photo ID, give no personal data to unverified parties, and report suspected disaster fraud to the DOJ National Center for Disaster Fraud or the cognizant Inspector General hotline."),
    ('disaster_procurement_fraud',
     'Post-disaster procurement and reconstruction contracting fraud risks',
     'U.S. GAO, Disaster Response: Agencies Should Assess Contracting Workforce Needs and Purchase Card Fraud Risk (GAO-21-42), https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-21-42',
     "GAO found disaster response raises fraud risk because urgency and a higher risk tolerance weaken normal controls; agencies spent over $20 million via purchase cards across 2017-2018 disasters, yet most had not assessed how purchase-card fraud risk changes in a disaster environment. Recognition red flags: contracts awarded with no identified official to oversee performance (Katrina-era oversight gaps); too few technical monitors for the contract count; missing vendor or disaster-event data that blocks fraud analysis; split or repeated card charges below review thresholds. Remedy: apply GAO's Fraud Risk Framework, document a purchase-card fraud risk profile that accounts for disaster surge, staff contractor oversight adequately, and capture vendor-level transaction data for audit."),
    ('disaster_early_warning',
     'Early-warning-system integrity and coverage-gap recognition',
     'UNDRR/WMO, Global Status of Multi-Hazard Early Warning Systems 2025, https://www.undrr.org/news/early-warning-systems-reach-new-heights-critical-gaps-jeopardize-global-progress',
     "Disaster mortality is nearly six times lower where multi-hazard early warning systems are comprehensive, yet only about 60% of countries report having one and small island developing States reach just 43%. An effective system needs all four pillars working: risk knowledge, forecasting and detection, warning dissemination, and early action. Recognition red flags: a 'system' reported to exist on paper while one or more pillars fail; warnings that never reach last-mile communities; funding that buys hardware but is not sustained for operation and maintenance; risk-knowledge capability missing (the least-developed pillar). Remedy: target and sustain financing, co-develop systems with local communities, and use after-event reviews and monitoring (Target G of the Sendai Framework) to drive accountability."),
    ('disaster_building_resilience',
     'Building-code and disaster-resilience compliance fraud recognition',
     'World Bank/GFDRR, Building Regulation for Resilience: Managing Risks for Safer Cities, https://www.gfdrr.org/sites/default/files/publication/BRR%20Exec%20Summary.pdf',
     'Most disaster deaths come from structural collapse, not the hazard itself, so code enforcement is a life-safety control. The GFDRR report cites evidence that 83% of earthquake deaths over three decades occurred in countries Transparency International rated most corrupt, linking code-enforcement corruption and regulatory capture to building failure. Recognition red flags: permits or inspection sign-offs issued without site visits; certifications obtained by fee or favor rather than compliance; standards quietly lowered to benefit builders, or set unaffordably high to force informal evasion; plan-review steps so costly they push construction off-record. Remedy: transparent, advice-plus-rigorous-inspection regimes, accredited third-party review, materials testing, reduced arbitrary discretion in approvals, and disclosure of technical requirements.'),
    ('disaster_insurance_fraud',
     'Disaster insurance fraud and inflated-claims recognition',
     'National Insurance Crime Bureau (NICB), Disaster Fraud, https://www.nicb.org/prevent-fraud-theft/disaster-fraud',
     'After storms, floods, and wildfires, fraudsters target devastated policyholders. Common schemes per NICB: manufactured roof or water damage, inflated water-mitigation bills, abused assignment-of-benefits agreements, and tearing out unrelated areas to pad estimates. Recognition red flags: contractors who rush you, knock door-to-door offering unsolicited repairs, demand large upfront payment, carry out-of-state plates or licenses, or present blank-space contracts that can be altered later. Note that any offer to manipulate price to cover your deductible or bill for damage the disaster did not cause is itself insurance fraud and can void coverage. Remedy: get three written estimates, check credentials with the BBB or state attorney general, pay by traceable method, never sign blank contracts, and report suspected fraud to NICB.'),
    ('disaster_humanitarian_coordination',
     'Humanitarian coordination and accountability to affected people',
     'Core Humanitarian Standard on Quality and Accountability (CHS 2024), https://corehumanitarianstandard.org/the-standard',
     'The Core Humanitarian Standard sets nine commitments organizations use so people affected by crisis can hold responders to account, with assistance treated as a right, not charity, delivered without discrimination. Commitment 5 requires safe, responsive complaint mechanisms, yet self-assessments score it lowest among the nine, signaling a recurring accountability gap. Recognition red flags: aid programs with no functioning feedback or complaint channel; communities excluded from decisions about their own response; no way to safely report exploitation or diversion. Remedy: establish accessible complaint and feedback mechanisms, coordinate via inter-agency standards (IASC commitments on accountability and protection from sexual exploitation and abuse), and verify performance against the CHS commitments.'),
    ('disaster_ghost_claims',
     'Ghost-victim and duplicate-claim recognition in disaster assistance',
     'U.S. GAO, Hurricanes Katrina and Rita Disaster Relief: Continued Findings of Fraud, Waste, and Abuse (GAO-07-300), https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-07-300',
     "GAO documented control weaknesses that let improper and duplicate disaster payments flow: rental assistance paid while the same household lived in free FEMA housing, duplicate payments to the same person across different disasters for the same address, and payments to non-qualified applicants. Separately, criminals file using names, addresses, and Social Security numbers stolen from real survivors, creating ghost or duplicate applications. Recognition red flags: more than one application per household; an applicant told they 'already applied' when they did not; benefits claimed for the same damage twice; self-certified data never cross-checked. Remedy: cross-match registrant and housing data, flag duplicate applications, validate identities against authoritative records, and refer suspected fraud to the Inspector General."),
    ('disaster_price_gouging',
     'Price-gouging and disaster profiteering recognition',
     'Texas Office of the Attorney General, How to Spot and Report Price Gouging (DTPA Sec. 17.46(b)), https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/consumer-protection/disaster-and-emergency-scams/how-spot-and-report-price-gouging',
     "Once a governor or president declares a disaster, many states prohibit selling necessities, fuel, food, medicine, lodging, building materials, generators, water, at exorbitant or excessive prices. Enforcers typically compare the charged price to the average over a set window (often 30 days) before the emergency and look for a gross disparity. Recognition red flags: sudden steep markups on essential goods after a declaration; refusal to honor pre-disaster prices on necessities; bundling or 'mandatory' add-ons that mask the increase. High prices alone are not automatically unlawful, but exploiting the declaration to spike essential prices likely is. Remedy: document the price and date and file a complaint with the state attorney general's consumer-protection office, which can pursue civil penalties."),
    ('disaster_displacement_equity',
     'Climate-disaster displacement and relief-equity recognition',
     'UNDRR, Disasters are a threat to human rights (Human Rights Council statement), https://www.undrr.org/news/disasters-are-threat-human-rights',
     'Disasters expose and amplify inequality: marginalized groups, defined by gender, age, disability, or income, suffer disproportionately, and disregard of known risk (as in the Brumadinho tailings-dam disaster) reflects a disregard for human rights. Extreme-weather displacement (2.6 million people in sub-Saharan Africa in 2017) can expose people to trafficking, abuse, and harassment. Recognition red flags: relief allocation that tracks political connection rather than need; exclusion of persons with disabilities, women, or displaced people from aid and decisions; reconstruction that rebuilds the same unsafe exposure. Remedy: apply a rights-based, leave-no-one-behind approach, integrate disability and gender inclusion (Sendai Gender Action Plan) into DRR policy, and make equitable, non-discriminatory targeting an accountable design requirement.'),
    ('disaster_donation_scams',
     'Donation and crowdfunding disaster-scam recognition',
     'U.S. FTC Consumer Advice, Charity Scams / Donating Through Crowdfunding Platforms, https://consumer.ftc.gov/all-scams/charity-scams',
     "Charity and crowdfunding fraud spikes after high-profile disasters, with money going to the scheme's creator rather than survivors. Recognition red flags: pressure to give immediately; requests for cash, gift cards, cryptocurrency, or wire transfer; vague sentimental appeals with no specifics on how funds are used; charities that sprang up overnight or use names confusingly similar to real ones; spoofed caller ID. On crowdfunding pages, reused or stolen photos and copied stories (detectable via reverse image search) signal a scam. Remedy: give to established organizations with a disaster track record, donate directly through a verified website rather than a forwarded link, research via BBB Wise Giving Alliance, Charity Navigator, CharityWatch, or GuideStar, and report fraud to the FTC."),
    ('disaster_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: Inspectors General, clawback, and oversight',
     'U.S. DHS Office of Inspector General, AECOM to Pay $11.8 Million to Resolve False Claims Act Allegations in Connection with Hurricane Disaster Relief, https://www.oig.dhs.gov/news/press-releases/2023/10242023/aecom-pay-118-million-resolve-false-claims-act-allegations-connection-hurricane-disaster-relief',
     'Disaster fraud is recoverable. The False Claims Act lets the government claw back funds from contractors that bill falsely; a disaster-relief contractor agreed to pay $11.8 million to resolve hurricane-relief allegations, and DHS OIG works with FEMA investigators, SBA OIG, IRS Criminal Investigation, and U.S. Attorneys to prosecute those who defraud assistance programs. Recognition signals that drive remedy: questioned or improper payments flagged by Inspectors General; self-certifications with no verification repository; contract billings unsupported by deliverables. Remedy pathways: report to the cognizant Inspector General hotline, support qui tam / False Claims Act actions, pursue improper-payment recovery and clawback, and strengthen pre-payment controls so recovery is not the only line of defense.'),
    ('gig_misclassification',
     'Worker misclassification: employee versus independent contractor',
     'US DOL, Misclassification of Employees as Independent Contractors under the FLSA; FLSA Final Rule RIN 1235-AA43 (eff. 11 Mar 2024)  -  https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/flsa/misclassification/rulemaking',
     'Misclassification labels a de facto employee as an independent contractor, stripping minimum wage, overtime, unemployment insurance, and safety coverage. The DOL applies a totality-of-circumstances economic reality test: whether the worker is economically dependent on the firm or genuinely in business for themselves. Recognition red flags: a 1099 form, a signed contractor agreement, or no payroll withholding do NOT settle status; control over the work, integration into the core business, and economic dependence do. Rights and remedy: a misclassified worker may claim back wages, overtime, and liquidated damages and can seek enforcement through a labour agency or court rather than being confined to private contract remedies.'),
    ('gig_eu_platform_directive',
     'EU Platform Work Directive (2024): rebuttable presumption of employment',
     'Directive (EU) 2024/2831 on improving working conditions in platform work; European Parliament press release (24 Apr 2024)  -  https://www.europarl.europa.eu/news/en/press-room/20240419IPR20584/parliament-adopts-platform-work-directive',
     'Directive (EU) 2024/2831 entered into force 1 December 2024, with national transposition due by 2 December 2026. It creates a rebuttable legal presumption of employment when facts indicating direction and control are present, and it shifts the burden of proof onto the digital labour platform to show no employment relationship exists. Recognition red flags: a platform setting pay, dictating terms, monitoring acceptance rates, or controlling task delivery while calling workers self-employed. Rights and remedy: the presumption is a procedural facilitation benefiting the worker; workers, their representatives, or authorities can trigger reclassification proceedings in administrative and judicial fora.'),
    ('gig_abc_test',
     'The ABC test and California AB5 / Dynamex reclassification standard',
     'California DIR/DLSE, Independent contractor versus employee FAQ; Dynamex Operations West v. Superior Court (2018) 4 Cal.5th 903; AB5, Labor Code secs. 2775-2787  -  https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/faq_independentcontractor.htm',
     "The ABC test presumes every worker is an employee unless the hiring entity proves all three prongs: (A) the worker is free from control and direction; (B) the work is outside the usual course of the hiring entity's business; and (C) the worker is customarily engaged in an independently established trade. Failing any single prong means employee status. Recognition red flag: a courier or driver performing the firm's core service while labelled a contractor typically fails prong B. Rights and remedy: misclassified California workers may file a wage claim with the Labor Commissioner, report widespread violations, or sue; willful misclassification carries civil penalties of $5,000 to $25,000 per violation."),
    ('gig_algorithmic_management',
     'Algorithmic management and platform-worker rights to human oversight',
     'Directive (EU) 2024/2831, algorithmic-management chapter; ILO World Employment and Social Outlook 2021  -  https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/rapid-growth-digital-economy-calls-coherent-policy-response-0',
     'Algorithmic management lets platforms allocate, evaluate, monitor, and discipline workers through automated systems, often limiting autonomy and obscuring how decisions are made. The EU Directive requires human oversight of significant automated decisions and bars terminating or suspending a worker by automated decision alone; the ILO urges a human-in-command approach so final decisions affecting work are taken by people. Recognition red flags: opaque deactivation, scoring that penalises declined jobs, and surveillance without explanation. Rights and remedy: workers are entitled to meaningful information on the logic, significance, and consequences of automated decisions and to request human review and contest the outcome.'),
    ('gig_wage_benefit_denial',
     'Wage and benefit denial via misclassification: recognition signs',
     'California DIR/DLSE FAQ (status determines wage, overtime, break, safety, and unemployment coverage); US DOL FLSA misclassification guidance  -  https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/faq_independentcontractor.htm',
     "Misclassification is frequently a vehicle to avoid minimum wage, overtime, meal and rest breaks, unemployment insurance, workers' compensation, and the employer share of payroll taxes. Recognition red flags: pay-per-task models that fall below minimum wage once unpaid waiting and travel time is counted, no overtime premium for long shifts, no sick or holiday pay, and the worker bearing tool, vehicle, and insurance costs. Rights and remedy: an employee, unlike a true contractor, can seek enforcement before a state labour agency; relief includes restitution of unpaid minimum wage and overtime, penalties, and interest, and laws prohibit retaliation against workers who complain about misclassification."),
    ('gig_health_safety',
     'Gig-worker occupational health and safety risk and protection',
     'IOSH Magazine, Gig workers and safety standards (citing EU-OSHA 2021 review of 93 studies; UCL 2018 driver survey; ILO)  -  https://www.ioshmagazine.com/2022/12/19/gig-workers-and-safety-standards',
     'Platform and other non-standard workers face elevated occupational risk: an EU-OSHA review of 93 studies found 76 linked non-standard employment to worse injury rates, disease risk, or hazard exposure, and survey data show many delivery riders sustain collision damage while working. Time pressure, distracting apps, long lone-working shifts, and fatigue compound risk, and migrant, young, and women workers are disproportionately exposed. Recognition red flags: no rest breaks, employer-shifted PPE and insurance costs, and discouraged incident reporting. Rights and remedy: in the UK and EU non-standard workers have an equal right to health and safety protection, including free PPE for casual workers, and a duty of care applies regardless of label.'),
    ('gig_data_transparency',
     'Data access and transparency rights for platform workers',
     'GDPR Articles 15 and 22; Directive (EU) 2024/2831 (data-processing limits, transparency); Uber drivers v. Uber, Amsterdam Court of Appeal (2023)  -  https://legalblogs.wolterskluwer.com/global-workplace-law-and-policy/regulating-algorithmic-management-in-the-platform-work-directive-correcting-risky-deviations/',
     'Platform workers hold data and transparency rights under GDPR and the Platform Work Directive: access to personal data, meaningful information on the logic of automated decisions, and protection from solely automated decisions with legal effect under GDPR Article 22. The Directive also bars platforms from processing certain sensitive data, such as emotional or psychological state and beliefs. Recognition red flags: refusal of subject-access requests, no explanation of deactivation, and covert biometric or emotion monitoring. Rights and remedy: in the 2023 Amsterdam ruling the court ordered Uber to disclose decision logic and found it had not proven genuine human intervention before deactivating drivers.'),
    ('gig_collective_bargaining',
     'Collective bargaining and organising rights for platform workers',
     'ILO Right to Organise and Collective Bargaining Convention No. 98 (1949); ETUC resolution on platform and non-standard workers  -  https://www.etuc.org/en/document/etuc-resolution-protection-rights-non-standard-workers-and-workers-platform-companies',
     'Collective bargaining is a universal right recognised by the ILO, the European Court of Human Rights, and the Council of Europe regardless of employment status, and ILO Convention No. 98 does not exclude the self-employed. Workers on digital platforms can organise and bargain collectively whether presumed employed or self-employed. Recognition red flags: treating worker associations as cartels under competition law, blocking representation, or retaliating against organisers. Rights and remedy: EU guidance confirms collective agreements for solo self-employed platform workers should fall outside antitrust control, and workers may seek recognition, negotiate terms, and act collectively to remedy substandard conditions.'),
    ('gig_bogus_self_employment',
     'Bogus or disguised self-employment: recognition indicators',
     'European Labour Authority glossary, Bogus self-employment; Directive (EU) 2024/2831 indicator approach  -  https://www.ela.europa.eu/en/glossary/bogus-self-employment',
     "Bogus (false or disguised) self-employment registers a worker as self-employed while the relationship is in fact dependent employment, used to evade labour, tax, and social-security obligations. EU Labour Force Survey criteria flag a worker with a single or dominant client who controls working hours, and lacking the power to hire or set decisions. Recognition red flags: economic dependence on one platform, fixed schedules, and no genuine business of one's own. Rights and remedy: under the Platform Work Directive, where indicators of direction and control are met the relationship is presumed an employment relationship and the platform must prove otherwise; workers and representatives can alert authorities to trigger reclassification."),
    ('gig_crossborder_protection',
     'Cross-border platform work and social-protection gaps',
     'ILO World Employment and Social Outlook 2021 (platforms across jurisdictions); ISSA, platform workers and social protection  -  https://www.issa.int/analysis/platform-workers-and-social-protection-international-developments',
     'Platforms often operate across multiple jurisdictions while engaging workers as self-employed, leaving gaps in social protection, especially portable, long-term benefits such as pensions, that widen when worker and platform sit in different countries. The ILO notes half of online platform workers earn under US$2 per hour and many lack access to social security, association, and bargaining. Recognition red flags: no contributions to any social-security system, no sick or injury cover, and unclear applicable law. Rights and remedy: the ILO calls for coordinated frameworks extending adequate social security to platform workers and access to the courts of the jurisdiction where the worker is located, so coverage is not lost across borders.'),
    ('gig_uk_uber_ruling',
     'UK Uber ruling (Uber BV v Aslam) and worker-status case law',
     'Uber BV and others v Aslam and others [2021] UKSC 5 (19 Feb 2021); ILO summary of the judgment  -  https://www.ilo.org/media/260711/download',
     "In Uber BV v Aslam the UK Supreme Court unanimously held that drivers are 'workers', not independent contractors, entitled to minimum wage, paid holiday, and rest breaks, counting working time from logging on and being ready to work. The court refused to treat the written contract as the starting point, instead determining the true agreement from all circumstances. Recognition red flags identified: the platform fixing fares, dictating terms, monitoring and penalising trip-acceptance rates, controlling delivery via the app and ratings, and preventing driver-passenger relationships. Rights and remedy: status as a worker unlocks statutory protections and back-claims that a contractor label cannot lawfully waive."),
    ('gig_remedy_enforcement',
     'Remedy and enforcement: reclassification, back-pay, labour inspection',
     'California DIR/DLSE FAQ (wage claims, sec. 226.8 penalties, anti-retaliation); Spanish Labour Inspectorate enforcement of the Riders Law  -  https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/faq_independentcontractor.htm',
     "Remedies for misclassification include reclassification to employee status, restitution of unpaid minimum wage and overtime, liquidated damages, interest, and civil penalties; in California willful misclassification draws $5,000-$25,000 per violation under Labor Code section 226.8, and complainants are protected from retaliation. Enforcement runs through labour inspectorates and agencies as well as courts: Spain's Labour Inspectorate used the Riders Law to require delivery couriers be treated as employees and levied large fines on a non-compliant platform. Recognition red flags: firms ignoring a presumption or court ruling and continuing contractor labels. Rights and remedy: workers may file agency claims, group violation reports, or lawsuits to secure back-pay and reclassification."),
]
